window.QUIZ_DATA = {"version":"cf2a2465d3bb","papers":[{"id":"quine1951-two-dogmas-empiricism","title":"Two Dogmas of Empiricism","authors":["W. V. O. Quine"],"year":"1951","area":"language","topic":"analyticity & holism","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"our statements about the external world face the tribunal of sense experience not individually but only as a corporate body.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Any statement can be held true come what may, if we make drastic enough adjustments elsewhere in the system. … no statement is immune to revision.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Each attempt to explain analyticity, whether through meanings, definition, interchangeability salva veritate, or the semantical rules of artificial languages, either falls short or presupposes analyticity or something equally unclear; the analytic–synthetic boundary has simply not been drawn.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"The analytic–synthetic distinction and statement-by-statement confirmation rest on one mistake: splitting each statement's truth into linguistic and factual components. Only science as a whole faces experience, and no statement, even of logic, is immune to pragmatically guided revision.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"logical truth","aliases":["logically true","logical"],"definition":"If we suppose a prior inventory of logical particles, comprising 'no,' 'un-' 'if,' 'then,' 'and,' etc., then in general a logical truth is a statement which is true and remains true under all reinterpretations of its components other than the logical particles.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"cognitive synonymy","aliases":["cognitively synonymous","synonymous","synonymy"],"definition":"to say that 'bachelor' and 'unmarried man' are cognitively synonymous is to say no more nor less than that the statement: (3) All and only bachelors are unmarried men is analytic.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"reductionism","aliases":["reductionist"],"definition":"the belief that each meaningful statement is equivalent to some logical construct upon terms which refer to immediate experience.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"semantical rule","aliases":["semantical rules","Semantical Rules","Semantical rules"],"definition":"Semantical rules are distinguishable, apparently, only by the fact of appearing on a page under the heading 'Semantical Rules'; and this heading is itself then meaningless.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Consider 'creature with a heart' and 'creature with a kidney': as it happens, perhaps every creature with a heart is a creature with a kidney, and vice versa.","phenomenon":"coextensive but non-synonymous predicates","near":["cognitively synonymous predicates","necessarily coextensive predicates","empty general terms"],"avoid":["coextensive but non-synonymous terms"],"exact":false},{"id":"e2","text":"Truths which become false under substitution of 'unmarried man' for 'bachelor' are easily constructed … 'Bachelor' has less than ten letters.","phenomenon":"substitution failure in quotation contexts","aliases":["substitution"],"near":["substitution failure in modal contexts","substitution failure in belief contexts","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)"],"avoid":["referential opacity","referential opacity (substitution failure)","referential opacity (action under a description)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Revision even of the logical law of the excluded middle has been proposed as a means of simplifying quantum mechanics; and what difference is there in principle between such a shift and the shift whereby Kepler superseded Ptolemy, or Einstein Newton, or Darwin Aristotle?","phenomenon":"revisability of logic","aliases":["Revision","logical"],"near":["logical pluralism","conventionalism about logic","dialetheism"],"avoid":["confirmation holism","anti-exceptionalism about logic"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Physical objects are conceptually imported into the situation as convenient intermediaries — not by definition in terms of experience, but simply as irreducible posits comparable, epistemologically, to the gods of Homer. Let me interject that for my part I do, qua lay physicist, believe in physical objects and not in Homer's gods … But in point of epistemological footing the physical objects and the gods differ only in degree and not in kind.","phenomenon":"cultural posits","aliases":["posits"],"near":["fictionalism about physical objects","phenomenalist reduction of physical objects","Moorean common-sense certainty"],"avoid":["conceptual scheme organizing reality","scheme–content dualism"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2181906"},{"id":"grice1957-meaning","title":"Meaning","authors":["H. P. Grice"],"year":"1957","area":"language","topic":"speaker meaning & intention","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"\"A meantNN something by x\" is (roughly) equivalent to \"A intended the utterance of x to produce some effect in an audience by means of the recognition of this intention\"","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"for x to have meaningNN, … in some sense of \"reason\" the recognition of the intention behind x is for the audience a reason and not merely a cause.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"U meant something by uttering x ↔ U intended (1) x to produce a response r in an audience A, (2) A to recognize intention (1), and (3) that recognition to be (part of) A's reason for r.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Natural meaning is factive (spots meaning measles); non-natural meaning is not. Causal theories wrongly analyze non-natural meaning as a sign's conditioned tendency to produce attitudes; it should instead be analyzed through speakers' intentions on particular occasions.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"natural meaning","definition":"x meant that p and x means that p entail p. … I cannot argue from \"Those spots meant measles\" to any conclusion to the effect that somebody or other meant by those spots so-and-so.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"nonnatural meaning","aliases":["meantNN"],"definition":"\"A meantNN something by x\" is roughly equivalent to \"A uttered x with the intention of inducing a belief by means of the recognition of this intention.\"","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"primary intention","definition":"only what I may call the primary intention of an utterer is relevant to the meaningNN of an utterance. … if (say) I intend to get a man to do something by giving him some information, it cannot be regarded as relevant to the meaningNN of my utterance to describe what I intend him to do.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"timeless meaning","aliases":["timeless"],"definition":"\"x meansNN (timeless) that so-and-so\" might as a first shot be equated with some statement or disjunction of statements about what \"people\" (vague) intend (with qualifications about \"recognition\") to effect by x.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"\"Those spots mean (meant) measles.\" \"Those spots didn't mean anything to me, but to the doctor they meant measles.\" \"The recent budget means that we shall have a hard year.\" … I cannot say, \"Those spots meant measles, but he hadn't got measles,\" and I cannot say, \"The recent budget means that we shall have a hard year, but we shan't have.\"","phenomenon":"natural meaning","near":["meaningNN","speaker meaning","conventional meaning"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"\"Those three rings on the bell (of the bus) mean that the 'bus is full.'\" … I can use [it] and go on to say, \"But it isn't in fact full—the conductor has made a mistake\"","phenomenon":"non-natural meaning","near":["natural sign","conversational implicature","conventional implicature"],"avoid":["absence of the referent"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Herod presents Salome with the head of St. John the Baptist on a charger. … Feeling faint, a child lets its mother see how pale it is (hoping that she may draw her own conclusions and help). … Herod intended to make Salome believe that St. John the Baptist was dead and no doubt also intended Salome to recognize that he intended her to believe that St. John the Baptist was dead.","phenomenon":"deliberately and openly letting someone know","aliases":["lets"],"near":["non-natural meaning","covert intention to induce belief","conversational implicature"],"avoid":["natural meaning","sneaky intention (counterexample to Gricean meaning)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"I might leave B's handkerchief near the scene of a murder in order to induce the detective to believe that B was the murderer","phenomenon":"covert intention to induce belief","aliases":["induce"],"near":["deliberately and openly letting someone know","non-natural meaning","lying"],"avoid":["misleading without lying","sneaky intention (counterexample to Gricean meaning)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"I draw a picture of Mr. Y [displaying undue familiarity to Mrs. X] and show it to Mr. X. … it will make a difference to the effect of my picture on Mr. X whether or not he takes me to be intending to inform him (make him believe something) about Mrs. X, and not to be just doodling or trying to produce a work of art.","phenomenon":"non-natural meaning","near":["deliberately and openly letting someone know","covert intention to induce belief","natural sign"],"avoid":["non-conventional (communicative) illocutionary act"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2182440"},{"id":"grice1975-logic-conversation","title":"Logic and Conversation","authors":["H. P. Grice"],"year":"1975","area":"language","topic":"implicature","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Our talk exchanges do not normally consist of a succession of disconnected remarks, and would not be rational if they did. They are characteristically … cooperative efforts","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"initially at least, conversational implicata are not part of the meaning of the expressions to the employment of which they attach.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"The supposed divergences in meaning between the formal connectives and their natural-language counterparts ('and', 'or', 'if') are illusory: the extra content is implicated, not part of the words' meaning, arising from a presumption that speakers observe the Cooperative Principle.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Speakers convey more than they say: a hearer calculates an implicature as whatever the speaker must be supposed to think to reconcile her utterance, even a blatant breach of a maxim, with the presumption that she observes the Cooperative Principle.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"conversational implicature","definition":"to calculate a conversational implicature is to calculate what has to be supposed in order to preserve the supposition that the Cooperative Principle is being observed","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"Cooperative Principle","aliases":["principle"],"definition":"a rough general principle which participants will be expected (ceteris paribus) to observe, namely: Make your conversational contribution such as is required, at the stage at which it occurs, by the accepted purpose or direction of the talk exchange in which you are engaged.","exact":true},{"id":"t7","term":"maxim of Quantity","definition":"1. Make your contribution as informative as is required (for the current purposes of the exchange). 2. Do not make your contribution more informative than is required.","exact":true},{"id":"t8","term":"maxim of Quality","definition":"Try to make your contribution one that is true … 1. Do not say what you believe to be false. 2. Do not say that for which you lack adequate evidence.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"what is said","aliases":["say","said"],"definition":"In the sense in which I am using the word say, I intend what someone has said to be closely related to the conventional meaning of the words (the sentence) he has uttered.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"flouting","aliases":["flout"],"definition":"He may flout a maxim; that is, he may blatantly fail to fulfill it. … the hearer is faced with a minor problem: How can his saying what he did say be reconciled with the supposition that he is observing the overall CP?","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"generalized conversational implicature","aliases":["implicature"],"definition":"Sometimes one can say that the use of a certain form of words in an utterance would normally (in the absence of special circumstances) carry such-and-such an implicature or type of implicature.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"conventional implicature","aliases":["conventional","implicated"],"definition":"In some cases the conventional meaning of the words used will determine what is implicated, besides helping to determine what is said.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"A is writing a testimonial about a pupil who is a candidate for a philosophy job, and his letter reads as follows: 'Dear Sir, Mr. X's command of English is excellent, and his attendance at tutorials has been regular. Yours, etc.'","phenomenon":"flouting the maxim of Quantity","near":["clash between maxims (Quantity versus Quality)","opting out of the Cooperative Principle","conventional implicature"],"avoid":["conversational implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"X, with whom A has been on close terms until now, has betrayed a secret of A's to a business rival. A and his audience both know this. A says 'X is a fine friend'.","phenomenon":"verbal irony","near":["metaphor","meiosis (understatement)","hyperbole (overstatement)"],"avoid":["conversational implicature","sarcasm","echoic irony"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"A is standing by an obviously immobilized car and is approached by B; the following exchange takes place: … A: I am out of petrol. B: There is a garage round the corner.","phenomenon":"implicature from observing the maxim of Relation","near":["flouting the maxim of Relation","scalar implicature","conventional implicature"],"avoid":["conversational implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"A is planning with B an itinerary for a holiday in France. Both know that A wants to see his friend C, if to do so would not involve too great a prolongation of his journey: … A: Where does C live? B: Somewhere in the South of France.","phenomenon":"clash between maxims (Quantity versus Quality)","near":["flouting the maxim of Quantity","opting out of the Cooperative Principle","conventional implicature"],"avoid":["conversational implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"At a genteel tea party, A says Mrs. X is an old bag. There is a moment of appalled silence, and then B says The weather has been quite delightful this summer, hasn't it?","phenomenon":"flouting the maxim of Relation","near":["implicature from observing the maxim of Relation","flouting the maxim of Manner","clash between maxims (Quantity versus Quality)"],"avoid":["conversational implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e6","text":"If I say (smugly), He is an Englishman; he is, therefore, brave, I have certainly committed myself, by virtue of the meaning of my words, to its being the case that his being brave is a consequence of (follows from) his being an Englishman. But … I do not want to say that I have SAID (in the favored sense) that it follows from his being an Englishman that he is brave.","phenomenon":"conventional implicature","near":["generalized conversational implicature","particularized conversational implicature","entailment (part of what is said)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e7","text":"Anyone who uses a sentence of the form X is meeting a woman this evening would … implicate that the person to be met was someone other than X's wife, mother, sister, or perhaps even close platonic friend.","phenomenon":"generalized conversational implicature","aliases":["implicate"],"near":["particularized conversational implicature","conventional implicature","presupposition"],"avoid":["scalar implicature"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1163/9789004368811_003"},{"id":"davidson1967-truth-meaning","title":"Truth and Meaning","authors":["Donald Davidson"],"year":"1967","area":"language","topic":"theory of meaning & truth conditions","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"a theory of meaning for a language L shows \"how the meanings of sentences depend upon the meanings of words\" if it contains a (recursive) definition of truth-in-L.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"My objection to meanings in the theory of meaning is not that they are abstract or that their identity conditions are obscure, but that they have no demonstrated use.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A theory of meaning for L must, using no further semantic notions, entail every instance of 's is T ↔ p' (s a structural description of an L-sentence, p that sentence or its translation); T then coincides with truth-in-L.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Despite Tarski's pessimism, a truth definition can be an empirical theory of a natural language, tested by charitably mapping sentences speakers hold true onto ones the interpreter holds true; demonstratives require relativizing truth to speaker and time.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"theory of meaning","aliases":["theories of meaning"],"definition":"What we require of a theory of meaning for a language L is that without appeal to any (further) semantical notions it place enough restrictions on the predicate 'is T' to entail all sentences got from schema T when 's' is replaced by a structural description of a sentence of L and 'p' by that sentence.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"structural description","aliases":["structural descriptions"],"definition":"A 'structural description' of an expression describes the expression as a concatenation of elements drawn from a fixed finite list (for example of words or letters).","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"meaning holism","aliases":["holism","holistic"],"definition":"If sentences depend for their meaning on their structure, and we understand the meaning of each item in the structure only as an abstraction from the totality of sentences in which it features, then we can give the meaning of any sentence (or word) only by giving the meaning of every sentence (and word) in the language.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"principle of charity","aliases":["charity","charitable"],"definition":"Charity in interpreting the words and thoughts of others is unavoidable … just as we must maximize agreement, or risk not making sense of what the alien is talking about, so we must maximize the self-consistency we attribute to him, on pain of not understanding him.","exact":true,"avoid":["davidson1973-radical-interpretation/t4","davidson1974-very-idea-conceptual/t3"]}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose that 'R' and 'S' abbreviate any two sentences alike in truth value. Then the following four sentences have the same reference: (1) R (2) x̂(x=x.R)=x̂(x=x) (3) x̂(x=x.S) = x̂(x=x) (4) S For (1) and (2) are logically equivalent, as are (3) and (4), while (3) differs from (2) only in containing the singular term 'x̂(x=x.S)' where (2) contains 'x̂(x=x.R)' and these refer to the same thing if S and R are alike in truth value.","phenomenon":"slingshot argument","near":["Frege's puzzle about informative identity","Mates's puzzle about synonym substitution","the paradox of analysis"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"[We should not think] a theory any more correct that entails '\"Snow is white\" is true if and only if snow is white' than one that entails instead: (S) 'Snow is white' is true if and only if grass is green, provided … we are as sure of the truth of (S) as we are of that of its more celebrated predecessor. Yet (S) may not encourage the same confidence that a theory that entails it deserves to be called a theory of meaning.","phenomenon":"Foster's extensionality problem for T-theories","near":["slingshot argument","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","Liar paradox"],"avoid":["Tarskian T-sentence","coextensive but non-synonymous predicates","coextensive but non-synonymous terms","indeterminacy of interpretation","indeterminacy of translation","instance of the T-schema"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"'good' as it features in 'Bardot is a good actress' is another matter. … The problem is to frame a truth definition such that '\"Bardot is a good actress\" is true if and only if Bardot is a good actress' - and all other sentences like it - are consequences. Obviously 'good actress' does not mean 'good and an actress'.","phenomenon":"non-intersective (attributive) adjective modification","near":["intersective adjective modification","privative adjectives","emotive meaning of evaluative terms"],"avoid":["intensional (non-extensional) adjective"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"the same sentence may at one time or in one mouth be true and at another time or in another mouth be false. … 'I am tired' is true as (potentially) spoken by p at t if and only if p is tired at t. 'That book was stolen' is true as (potentially) spoken by p at t if and only if the book demonstrated by p at t is stolen prior to t.","phenomenon":"indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","aliases":["demonstrated"],"near":["lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","anaphoric dependence","essential indexical"],"avoid":["indexical (demonstrative) reference","same character, different content","Tarskian T-sentence","deictic (referential) use of tense","direct reference of demonstratives","instance of the T-schema"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00485035"},{"id":"putnam1975-meaning-meaning","title":"The Meaning of ‘Meaning’","authors":["Hilary Putnam"],"year":"1975","area":"language","topic":"externalism & mental content","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Traditional semantic theory leaves out only two contributions to the determination of extension - the contribution of society and the contribution of the real world!","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Cut the pie any way you like, \"meanings\" just ain't in the head!","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Two speakers can share every belief and inner state, the same narrow psychological state, while their word 'water' differs in extension; so no notion of meaning can both be fixed by what is in the head and determine extension.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Extension is fixed socially, through a division of linguistic labor with experts, and indexically, by the hidden nature of local paradigms; meaning is reconstructed as a vector of syntactic markers, semantic markers, stereotype and extension.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"division of linguistic labor","aliases":["division of labor","linguistic labor"],"definition":"everyone to whom gold is important for any reason has to acquire the word \"gold\"; but he does not have to acquire the method of recognizing if something is or is not gold. He can rely on a special subclass of speakers.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"stereotype","aliases":["stereotypical","stereotypes"],"definition":"a standardized description of features of the kind that are typical, or \"normal,\" or at any rate stereotypical. The central features of the stereotype generally are criteria - features which in normal situations constitute ways of recognizing if a thing belongs to the kind","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"methodological solipsism","definition":"the assumption that no psychological state, properly so called, presupposes the existence of any individual other than the subject to whom that state is ascribed.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"sameL","aliases":["same liquid as"],"definition":"x bears the relation sameL to y just in case (1) x and y are both liquids, and (2) x and y agree in important physical properties.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"indexical component","aliases":["indexical"],"definition":"words like \"water\" have an unnoticed indexical component: \"water\" is stuff that bears a certain similarity relation to the water around here.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"meaning vector","aliases":["vector"],"definition":"the normal form description of the meaning of a word should be a finite sequence, or \"vector,\" whose components should certainly include … (1) the syntactic markers …; (2) the semantic markers …; (3) a description of the additional features of the stereotype, if any; (4) a description of the extension.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"One of the peculiarities of Twin Earth is that the liquid called \"water\" is not H2O but a different liquid … XYZ. I shall suppose that XYZ is indistinguishable from water at normal temperatures and pressures. … Let Oscar1 be [a] typical Earthian English speaker, and let Oscar2 be his counterpart on Twin Earth. … Oscar1 and Oscar2 understood the term \"water\" differently in 1750 although they were in the same psychological state","phenomenon":"semantic externalism about natural-kind terms","near":["division of linguistic labor","social externalism (anti-individualism)","indeterminacy of translation"],"avoid":["natural-kind externalism","stereotype underdetermining extension","same character, different content","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","indexical (demonstrative) reference","argument from ignorance against descriptivism","causal theory of reference","causal-historical determination of reference","causal-historical chain of reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Consider our community as a \"factory\": in this \"factory\" some people have the \"job\" of wearing gold wedding rings, other people have the \"job\" of selling gold wedding rings, still other people have the job of telling whether or not something is really gold.","phenomenon":"division of linguistic labor","near":["vagueness (borderline cases)","stereotype as minimal linguistic competence","rigid designation of natural kinds"],"avoid":["social externalism (anti-individualism)","deferential use of a name","semantic externalism about natural-kind terms","natural-kind externalism"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Suppose, now, that I discover the microstructure of water - that water is H2O. … if you describe not another planet in the actual universe, but another possible universe in which there is stuff with the chemical formula XYZ which passes the \"operational test\" for water, we shall have to say that that stuff isn't water but merely XYZ.","phenomenon":"necessary a posteriori truth","near":["contingent a priori truth","analytic truth","world-relative (non-rigid) meaning"],"avoid":["modal argument against descriptivism","reference fixing by description","logical impossibility","semantic externalism about natural-kind terms","stereotype underdetermining extension","accidental satisfier of a name's descriptions"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"There were or are pieces of metal which could not have been determined not to be gold in Archimedes' day, but which we can distinguish from gold quite easily with modern techniques. Let X be such a piece of metal. Clearly X does not lie in the extension of \"gold\" in standard English; my view is that it did not lie in the extension of [χρυσός] in Attic Greek, either, although an ancient Greek would have mistaken X for gold","phenomenon":"stability of reference across theory change","near":["incommensurability of scientific theories","operational definition of meaning","vagueness (borderline cases)"],"avoid":["reference surviving a false associated theory","stereotype underdetermining extension","misidentification","the justification–truth distinction","argument from error against descriptivism","semantic externalism about natural-kind terms","causal theory of reference","accidental satisfier of a name's descriptions","causal-historical chain of reference"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"donnellan1966-reference-definite-descriptions","title":"Reference and Definite Descriptions","authors":["Keith Donnellan"],"year":"1966","area":"language","topic":"descriptions & definiteness","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"In the attributive use, the attribute of being the so-and-so is all important, while it is not in the referential use.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Using a definite description referentially, a speaker may say something true even though the description correctly applies to nothing.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Definite descriptions have two uses: attributive, saying something about whatever uniquely fits the description, and referential, a mere device for getting the audience to pick out a particular thing, which can succeed even if nothing fits.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Whether a description is used referentially or attributively depends on the speaker's intentions on an occasion, not on the sentence; referring is not denoting, and neither Russell's existence entailment nor Strawson's truth-value gap holds for referential uses.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"attributive use","aliases":["attributively","attributive"],"definition":"A speaker who uses a definite description attributively in an assertion states something about whoever or whatever is the so-and-so. … the definite description might be said to occur essentially, for the speaker wishes to assert something about whatever or whoever fits that description","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"referential use","aliases":["referentially","referential"],"definition":"A speaker who uses a definite description referentially in an assertion … uses the description to enable his audience to pick out whom or what he is talking about and states something about that person or thing.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"pragmatic ambiguity","aliases":["pragmatically ambiguous","pragmatically","ambiguous"],"definition":"Perhaps we could say that the sentence is pragmatically ambiguous: the distinction between roles that the description plays is a function of the speaker's intentions.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"denoting","aliases":["denotes","denotation","denote"],"definition":"a definite description denotes an entity if that entity fits the description uniquely … Thus whether or not a definite description is used referentially or attributively, it may have a denotation. Hence, denoting and referring, as I have explicated the latter notion, are distinct","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose first that we come upon poor Smith foully murdered. From the brutal manner of the killing and the fact that Smith was the most lovable person in the world, we might exclaim, \"Smith's murderer is insane.\" I will assume, to make it a simpler case, that in a quite ordinary sense we do not know who murdered Smith","phenomenon":"attributive use of a definite description","near":["referential use of a definite description","presupposition failure","generic use of a definite description"],"avoid":["merely de dicto (notional) belief"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose one is at a party and, seeing an interesting-looking person holding a martini glass, one asks, \"Who is the man drinking a martini?\" If it should turn out that there is only water in the glass, one has nevertheless asked a question about a particular person, a question that it is possible for someone to answer.","phenomenon":"referential use of a definite description","near":["attributive use of a definite description","presupposition accommodation","narrow-scope (de dicto) reading of a description"],"avoid":["speaker meaning independent of sentence meaning"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"If someone said, for example, in 1960 before he had any idea that Mr. Goldwater would be the Republican nominee in 1964, \"The Republican candidate for president in 1964 will be a conservative,\" … the definite description here would denote Mr. Goldwater. But would we wish to say that the speaker had referred to, mentioned, or talked about Mr. Goldwater?","phenomenon":"denotation without reference","aliases":["denote","referred"],"near":["reference without denotation","referential use of a definite description","empty definite description"],"avoid":["merely de dicto (notional) belief"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Suppose that I think I see at some distance a man walking and ask, \"Is the man carrying a walking stick the professor of history?\" … There is finally the case in which there is nothing at all where I thought there was a man with a walking stick … it was, perhaps, a trick of light that made me think there was a man there.","phenomenon":"reference failure","aliases":["refer"],"near":["reference via misdescription","attributive use of a definite description","empty name"],"avoid":["presupposition failure","truth-value gap from reference failure","absence of the referent"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2183143"},{"id":"kaplan1989-demonstratives","title":"Demonstratives","authors":["David Kaplan"],"year":"1989","area":"language","topic":"indexicals & demonstratives","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Indexicals, pure and demonstrative alike, are directly referential.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Character: Contexts → Contents; Content: Circumstances → Extensions; or, in more familiar language, Meaning + Context → Intension; Intension + Possible World → Extension","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Indexicals have two kinds of meaning: a character, fixed by convention and known to competent speakers, maps each context of use to a content, and that content is the referent itself rather than any descriptive condition.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"φ is valid iff, in every context c of every structure, φ's content in c is true at c's own world and time; so 'I am here now' is valid though □(I am here now) is not.","formal":true}],"pick":1,"avoid":["stalnaker1970-pragmatics"],"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"character","aliases":["characters"],"definition":"That aspect of the meaning of an expression which determines what its Content will be in each context, we call the Character of the expression. … the Character of each well-formed expression is determined by rules of the language … which are presumably known to all competent speakers.","exact":true,"avoid":["perry1977-frege-demonstratives/t1","lewis1980-index-context-content/t7"]},{"id":"t2","term":"content","aliases":["contents"],"definition":"Let us call this first kind of meaning—what is said—content. … It is contents that are evaluated in circumstances of evaluation. If the content is a proposition …, the result of the evaluation will be a truth-value.","exact":true,"avoid":["lewis1980-index-context-content/t6"]},{"id":"t3","term":"directly referential","aliases":["direct reference"],"definition":"in the case of a singular term which is directly referential, the constituent of the proposition is just the object itself. Thus it is that it does not just turn out that the constituent determines the same object in every circumstance, the constituent (corresponding to a rigid designator) just is the object.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"circumstance of evaluation","aliases":["circumstances of evaluation","circumstances","circumstance"],"definition":"we must distinguish possible occasions of use—which I call contexts—from possible circumstances of evaluation of what was said on a given occasion of use. Possible circumstances of evaluation I call circumstances or, sometimes, just counterfactual situations.","exact":true,"avoid":["lewis1980-index-context-content/t2"]},{"id":"t5","term":"monster","aliases":["monsters"],"definition":"Operators like 'In some contexts it is true that', which attempt to meddle with character, I call monsters. I claim that none can be expressed in English (without sneaking in a quotation device).","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"dthat","definition":"[A] special demonstrative which requires completion by a description and which is treated as a directly referential term whose referent is the denotation of the associated description","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"(6) I am here now. … Intuitively, (6) is deeply, and in some sense, which we will shortly make precise, universally, true. One need only understand the meaning of (6) to know that it cannot be uttered falsely. … It is certainly not necessary that I be here now. But for several contingencies, I would be working in my garden now, or even delivering this paper in a location outside of Portland.","phenomenon":"contingent a priori truth","aliases":["contingencies"],"near":["necessary a posteriori truth","Moore's paradox","answering-machine paradox"],"avoid":["indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","indexical (demonstrative) reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"We raise two identical twins, Castor and Pollux, under qualitatively identical conditions, qualitatively identical stimuli, etc. If necessary, we may monitor their brain states and make small corrections in their brain structures if they begin drifting apart. They respond to all cognitive stimuli in identical fashion. … Each sincerely says, ‘My brother was born before I was’ and the beliefs they thereby express conflict. In this, Castor speaks the truth, while Pollux speaks falsely.","phenomenon":"same character, different content","near":["same content, different character","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","monster (context-shifting operator)"],"avoid":["de se belief","self-locating belief","essential indexical","false de se belief","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","indexical (demonstrative) reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"on March 27 of this year, having lost track of the date, I may continue to hope to be finished by this March 26, without hoping to be finished by yesterday.","phenomenon":"same content, different character","near":["same character, different content","contingent a priori truth","monster (context-shifting operator)"],"avoid":["cognitive dynamics: losing track of time","temporal self-locating ignorance","essential indexical","self-locating belief","de se belief","irreducibly de se ignorance","substitution failure in belief contexts","referential opacity (substitution failure)","referential opacity","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","Frege's puzzle about co-referring names","Frege's puzzle about identity statements"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Suppose I point at Paul and say, ‘He now lives in Princeton, New Jersey.’ Call what I said … 'Pat'. … Suppose that Paul and Charles had each disguised themselves as the other and had switched places. If that had happened, and I had uttered as I did, then the proposition I would have expressed would have been false. But … Pat … would still be true … provided that Paul—in whatever costume he appeared—were still residing in Princeton.","phenomenon":"direct reference of demonstratives","near":["non-rigid descriptive designation","deferred reference","referential use of a definite description"],"avoid":["same character, different content","singular (Russellian) proposition","reference fixing by description","misidentification","modal argument against descriptivism","indexical (demonstrative) reference","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"A kidnapped heiress, locked in the trunk of a car, knowing neither the time nor where she is, may think 'It is quiet here now'. Could she plead that she could not have believed a proposition about that very place, since when thinking 'here' she did not know where she was? No! Ignorance of the referent is no excuse.","phenomenon":"singular thought without acquaintance","near":["descriptive (de dicto) thought","reference failure","knowledge by acquaintance"],"avoid":["singular (Russellian) proposition","perceptual de re belief","temporal self-locating ignorance","self-locating belief","self-locating knowledge","irreducibly de se ignorance","essential indexical","de se belief","indexical (demonstrative) reference","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)"],"exact":false}],"doi":null},{"id":"stalnaker1978-assertion","title":"Assertion","authors":["Robert Stalnaker"],"year":"1978","area":"language","topic":"speech acts & assertion","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"To make an assertion is to reduce the context set in a particular way, provided that there are no objections from the other participants in the conversation.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the essential effect of an assertion is to change the presuppositions of the participants in the conversation by adding the content of what is asserted to what is presupposed.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Asserting p, unless rejected, shrinks the context set C to C ∩ p. Rational assertion requires C ∩ p ≠ ∅ and C ⊈ p, and that the utterance express one proposition throughout C.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Where an utterance would express different propositions across the context set, hearers commonly reinterpret it as expressing its diagonal proposition—true at w iff what it expresses at w is true at w—explaining informative identities and singular negative existentials.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"avoid":["geach1965-assertion"],"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"context set","aliases":["context sets"],"definition":"the possible worlds compatible with what is presupposed. This set … is the set of possible worlds recognized by the speaker to be the “live options” relevant to the conversation.","exact":true,"avoid":["stalnaker1973-presuppositions/t2"]},{"id":"t2","term":"propositional concept","aliases":["propositional concepts"],"definition":"A propositional concept is … a function from an ordered pair of possible worlds into a truth value.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"diagonal proposition","aliases":["diagonal","diagonal propositions"],"definition":"the function from possible worlds into truth values whose values are read along the diagonal of the matrix from upper left to lower right … true at i for any i if and only if what is expressed in the utterance at i is true at i.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"I said You are a fool to O'Leary. O'Leary is a fool, so what I said was true, although O'Leary does not think so. Now Daniels, who is no fool and who knows it, was standing nearby, and he thought I was talking to him. So both O'Leary and Daniels thought I said something false: O'Leary understood what I said, but disagrees with me about the facts; Daniels … agrees with me about the fact … but misunderstood what I said.","phenomenon":"propositional concept (two-dimensional intension)","near":["rigid designation","essential indexical","contingent a priori truth"],"avoid":["indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","indexical (demonstrative) reference","same character, different content","misidentification"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"[In worlds i, j, and k, a metal bar is one, two, and three] meters long, respectively, at a certain time t. Now suppose an appropriate authority fixes the reference of the expression one meter by making the following statement in each of the worlds i, j, and k: This bar is one meter long. … The proposition expressed by the authority is one that might have been false, although he couldn't have expressed a false proposition in that utterance.","phenomenon":"contingent a priori truth","near":["necessary a posteriori truth","necessary a priori truth","contingent a posteriori truth"],"avoid":["reference fixing by description","descriptive name","descriptive name (name introduced by description)","analytic but not logical truth","non-logical analytic truth"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"hearing a woman talking in the next room, I tell you, That is either Zsa Zsa Gabor or Elizabeth Anscombe. Assuming that both demonstrative pronouns and proper names are rigid designators—terms that refer to the same individual in all possible worlds—this sentence comes out expressing either a necessary truth or a necessary falsehood, depending on whether it is one of the two mentioned women or someone else who is in the next room.","phenomenon":"diagonalization","near":["presupposition accommodation","contingent a priori truth","referential opacity"],"avoid":["necessary a posteriori truth","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","Frege's puzzle about identity statements","direct reference of demonstratives","indexical (demonstrative) reference","rigid designation","violation of the non-redundancy (informativity) constraint on assertion"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Suppose for example that you know that Jones won the election, believe mistakenly that I know it as well, and are prepared to take the truth of this proposition for granted if the occasion should arise, say by using it as a suppressed premiss in an argument, or by using the description the man who won the election to refer to Jones.","phenomenon":"defective context","near":["presupposition accommodation","presupposition failure","common knowledge"],"avoid":["referential use of a definite description","informative presupposition"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1163/9789004368873_013"},{"id":"montague1973-proper-treatment-quantification","title":"The Proper Treatment of Quantification in Ordinary English","authors":["Richard Montague"],"year":"1973","area":"language","topic":"quantification & formal semantics","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I regard it as one of the principal virtues of [a] treatment [in intensional logic] … that it enables us to deal directly with intensional locutions.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"We … see the virtue of having intransitive verbs and common nouns denote sets of individual concepts rather than sets of individuals","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"A fragment of English, including intensional verbs like 'seek', gets a rigorous truth-conditional semantics via systematic translation of each syntactic derivation into tensed intensional logic; alternative derivations yield genuine scope and de re/de dicto ambiguities.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Every term, proper names included, denotes a set of properties: ‘John’ ↦ λP.P{^j}, ‘every man’ ↦ λP.∀x[man′(x) → P{x}]; quantifying a term α in for the pronoun heₙ in φ yields α′(^λxₙ.φ′), generating scope readings.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"analysis tree","aliases":["analysis trees","tree","trees"],"definition":"To each node we attach a meaningful expression … with the index of that structural operation … which we understand as having been applied in obtaining the expression in question; the nodes dominated by any node are to be occupied by the expressions to which the structural operation is understood as having been applied …","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"logically determinate","aliases":["logically determinate"],"definition":"… proper nouns will be “logically determinate” according to the interpretations under consideration, that is, will have extensions invariant with respect to possible worlds and moments of time.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"individual concept","aliases":["individual concepts","individual-concept"],"definition":"An object of type ⟨s,e⟩: a function from world–time pairs to entities. Common nouns and intransitive verbs denote sets of these; for ‘horse’ they are constant functions, but ‘the temperature’ picks out one whose value varies with time.","exact":false},{"id":"t4","term":"pronoun of laziness","aliases":["pronouns of laziness","pronoun of laziness","laziness"],"definition":"… pronouns of laziness, that is, as “standing for” longer terms bearing a somewhat indefinite relation to other expressions in the sentence in question (or preceding sentences within the discourse in question).","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"The source of the ambiguity of John seeks a unicorn will perhaps be clarified if we compare that sentence with the intuitively synonymous John tries to find a unicorn … Here … the ambiguity is clearly a matter of scope, and indeed depends on the possibility of regarding either the component find a unicorn or the whole sentence as the scope of the existential quantification indicated by a unicorn.","phenomenon":"de re / de dicto ambiguity","aliases":["ambiguity"],"near":["free-choice indefinite","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","collective vs distributive reading"],"avoid":["quantifier scope ambiguity","specific vs non-specific indefinite","intensional transitive verb","non-specific reading of an intensional transitive verb","referential opacity","referential opacity (substitution failure)","failure of existential generalization","de dicto vs de re modality","de dicto vs de re reading","merely de dicto (notional) belief","quantifying into a modal context"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"‘a woman loves every man’ translates into two non-equivalent formulas, ∃u[woman′*(u) ∧ ∀v[man′*(v) → love′*(u, v)]] and ∀v[man′*(v) → ∃u[woman′*(u) ∧ love′*(u, v)]], although the sentence contains no intensional expression.","phenomenon":"quantifier scope ambiguity","near":["de re / de dicto ambiguity","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","collective vs distributive reading"],"exact":false},{"id":"e3","text":"From the premises the temperature is ninety and the temperature rises, the conclusion ninety rises would appear to follow by normal principles of logic; yet there are occasions on which both premises are true, but none on which the conclusion is.","phenomenon":"Partee's temperature paradox","aliases":["temperature"],"near":["Frege's puzzle about informative identity","substitution failure in belief contexts","problem of temporary intrinsics"],"avoid":["referential opacity","referential opacity (substitution failure)","substitution failure in modal contexts"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"in each of the following examples only one reading is possible, and that the referential: (1) John seeks a unicorn and Mary seeks it, (2) John tries to find a unicorn and wishes to eat it … This is, according to my intuitions … as it should be; but David Kaplan would differ, at least as to (2).","phenomenon":"intentional identity","near":["bridging (associative) anaphora","cataphora (backward anaphora)","sloppy identity under ellipsis"],"avoid":["pronoun of laziness","bound-variable anaphora","bound-variable pronoun","bound-variable reading of a pronoun","modal subordination","de dicto vs de re reading","de re / de dicto ambiguity","non-specific reading of an intensional transitive verb","donkey anaphora","E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora","cross-sentential anaphora","cross-sentential (discourse) anaphora","discourse anaphora","inaccessible discourse referent (scope-limited lifespan)","accessibility constraint on anaphora","de dicto vs de re modality"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/978-94-010-2506-5_10"},{"id":"lewis1979-scorekeeping-language-game","title":"Scorekeeping in a Language Game","authors":["David Lewis"],"year":"1979","area":"language","topic":"context & common ground","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I suggest that many components of conversational score obey rules of accommodation, and that these rules figure prominently among the rules governing the kinematics of conversational score.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Say something that requires a missing presupposition, and straightway that presupposition springs into existence, making what you said acceptable after all.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Contextual parameters (what is presupposed, which individual is most salient, how precise we are being, which possibilities are under consideration) tend, within limits, to shift so that whatever is said counts as acceptable.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Schema: if at t an utterance requires contextual parameter sᵢ to lie in range r to be true or otherwise acceptable, sᵢ ∉ r just before t, and further conditions hold, then sᵢ ∈ r at t.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"conversational score","aliases":["score"],"definition":"Like the components of a baseball score, the components of a conversational score at a given stage are abstract entities. They may not be numbers, but they are other set-theoretic constructs: sets of presupposed propositions, boundaries between permissible and impermissible courses of action, or the like.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"rule of accommodation","definition":"Baseball has no rule of accommodation to the effect that if a fourth ball is required to make correct the play that occurs, then that very fact suffices to change the score so that straightway there are four balls. Language games are different.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"true enough","aliases":["enough"],"definition":"we treat a sentence more or less as if it is simply true, if it is true over a large enough part of the range of delineations of its vagueness. (For short: if it is true enough.)","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"definite description","definition":"“the F” denotes x if and only if x is the most salient F in the domain of discourse, according to some contextually determined salience ranking.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"In the room is a cat, Bruce, who has been making himself very salient by dashing madly about. He is the only cat in the room, or in sight, or in earshot. I start to speak to you: The cat is in the carton. The cat will never meet our other cat, because our other cat lives in New Zealand. … And there he’ll stay, because Miriam would be sad if the cat went away.","phenomenon":"incomplete definite description resolved by salience","aliases":["salient"],"near":["attributive use of a definite description","donkey anaphora","bridging (associative) anaphora"],"avoid":["quantifier domain restriction","reference shift within a conversation","referential use of a definite description"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Peter Unger has argued that hardly anything is flat. … You think the pavement is flat - but how can you deny that your desk is flatter? But “flat” is an absolute term: it is inconsistent to say that something is flatter than something that is flat. Having agreed that your desk is flatter than the pavement, you must concede that the pavement is not flat after all.","phenomenon":"accommodating rise in standards of precision","near":["sorites paradox","scalar implicature","faultless disagreement"],"avoid":["context-dependent standards of precision","vague predicate","penumbral connection","loose (relaxed) use of an expression"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"The commonsensical epistemologist says: “I know the cat is in the carton - there he is before my eyes - I just can’t be wrong about that!” The sceptic replies: “You might be the victim of a deceiving demon”. … The commonsensical epistemologist must concede defeat. And yet he was not in any way wrong when he laid claim to infallible knowledge.","phenomenon":"accommodation widening the range of relevant possibilities","near":["Gettier case","lottery paradox","Moore's paradox"],"avoid":["context-supplied conversational background (modal base)","context-dependent standards of precision","accommodating rise in standards of precision","epistemic modality","shifting standards for knowledge attributions"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"It is peculiar to say, out of the blue, “All Fred’s children are asleep, and Fred has children.” The first part requires and thereby creates a presupposition that Fred has children; so the second part adds nothing to what is already presupposed when it is said; so the second part has no conversational point. It would not have been peculiar to say instead “Fred has children, and all Fred’s children are asleep.”","phenomenon":"presupposition accommodation","aliases":["presupposition","presupposed"],"near":["presupposition cancellation","presupposition failure","Moore's paradox"],"avoid":["violation of the non-redundancy (informativity) constraint on assertion","informative presupposition","presupposition filtering","local accommodation","global accommodation","symmetric presupposition filtering","pleonasm (redundancy)"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00258436"},{"id":"perry1979-problem-essential-indexical","title":"The Problem of the Essential Indexical","authors":["John Perry"],"year":"1979","area":"language","topic":"attitude reports & de se","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"belief states must be distinguished from objects of belief, cannot be individuated in terms of them, and are what is crucial for the explanation of action","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[T]here is not an identity, or even an isomorphic correspondence, but only a systematic relationship between the belief states one is in and what one thereby believes.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Indexical beliefs like 'I am making a mess' explain action in a way no non-indexical substitute can, and classifying beliefs by the proposition believed—Fregean, de re, or relativized—cannot capture what does the explaining.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Belief has two tiers: belief states, classified by the indexical sentences one would accept, which explain action; and the de re propositions believed, fixed by state plus context, so people in one state can believe different things.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"avoid":["perry1977-frege-demonstratives"],"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"essential indexical","aliases":["indexicals","indexical","essential","essentially"],"definition":"In these three cases the subjects in explaining their actions, would use indexicals to characterize certain beliefs they came to have. These indexicals are essential, in that replacement of them by other terms destroys the force of the explanation, or at least requires certain assumptions to be made to preserve it.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"locating beliefs","aliases":["locating belief","locating"],"definition":"I shall use the term \"locating beliefs\" to refer to one's beliefs about where one is, when it is, and who one is. Such beliefs seem essentially indexical.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"missing conceptual ingredient","aliases":["conceptual ingredient"],"definition":"There is a missing conceptual ingredient: a sense for which I am the reference, or a complex of properties I alone have, or a singular term that refers to no one but me. To identify the proposition I came to believe, the advocate of the doctrine of propositions must identify this missing conceptual ingredient.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"belief state","aliases":["belief states"],"definition":"We use sentences with indexicals or relativized propositions to individuate belief states, for the purposes of classifying believers in ways useful for explanation and prediction. That is, belief states individuated in this way enter into our common sense theory about human behavior and more sophisticated theories emerging from it.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"limited accessibility","aliases":["inaccessible","accessibility","limited"],"definition":"there is a class of propositions which can only be expressed in special circumstances. In particular, only I could express the proposition I expressed when I said \"I am making a mess.\" Others can see, perhaps by analogy with their own case, that there is a proposition that I express, but it is in a sense inaccessible to them.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"I once followed a trail of sugar on a supermarket floor, pushing my cart down the aisle on one side of a tall counter and back the aisle on the other, seeking the shopper with the torn sack to tell him he was making a mess. With each trip around the counter, the trail became thicker. But I seemed unable to catch up. Finally it dawned on me. I was the shopper I was trying to catch.","phenomenon":"essential indexical","near":["immunity to error through misidentification","Kripke's puzzle about belief","self-deception"],"avoid":["de se belief","self-locating belief","self-locating knowledge","self-locating (de se) uncertainty","irreducibly de se ignorance","de re belief","de re (not de se) belief about oneself","believing of one person that he is two","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","Frege's puzzle about identity statements","Frege's puzzle about co-referring names","substitution failure in belief contexts","referential opacity (substitution failure)","same content, different character","misidentification","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","indexical (demonstrative) reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Imagine two lost campers who trust the same guidebook but disagree about where they are. If we were to try to characterize the beliefs of these campers without the use of indexicals, it would seem impossible to bring out this disagreement. If, for example, we characterized their beliefs by the set of \"eternal sentences,\" drawn from the guidebook they would mark \"true\", there is no reason to suppose that the sets would differ.","phenomenon":"self-locating belief","near":["faultless disagreement","verbal dispute","temporal self-locating ignorance"],"avoid":["essential indexical","de se belief","locating belief","self-locating knowledge","self-locating (de se) uncertainty","irreducibly de se ignorance","decision under self-locating uncertainty","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","loss of self-locating information"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Suppose there were mirrors at either end of the counter so that as I pushed my cart down the aisle in pursuit I saw myself in the mirror. I take what I see to be the reflection of the messy shopper going up the aisle on the other side, not realizing that what I am really seeing is a reflection of a reflection of myself. I point and say, truly, \"I believe that he is making a mess.\"","phenomenon":"de re (not de se) belief about oneself","near":["de se belief","merely de dicto (notional) belief","reference failure","Kripke's puzzle about belief"],"avoid":["essential indexical","de re belief","perceptual de re belief","believing of one person that he is two","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","Frege's puzzle about identity statements","Frege's puzzle about co-referring names","same content, different character","misidentification","direct reference of demonstratives","indexical (demonstrative) reference","immunity to error through misidentification","irreducibly de se ignorance","referential opacity (substitution failure)","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Of course if the change of context is not noted, the adjustment of belief states will not occur, and a wholesale change from believing truly to believing falsely may occur. This is what happened to Rip Van Winkle. He awakes in the same belief states he fell asleep in twenty years earlier, unadjusted to the dramatic change in context, and so with a whole new set of beliefs, such as that he is a young man, mostly false.","phenomenon":"cognitive dynamics: losing track of time","near":["Sleeping Beauty problem","problem of temporary intrinsics","Kripke's puzzle about belief"],"avoid":["self-locating belief","essential indexical","slow switching","same character, different content","same content, different character","de se belief","false de se belief","temporal self-locating ignorance","self-locating knowledge","self-locating (de se) uncertainty","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","loss of self-locating information"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2214792"},{"id":"burge1979-individualism-mental","title":"Individualism and the Mental","authors":["Tyler Burge"],"year":"1979","area":"language","topic":"externalism & mental content","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Social context infects even the distinctively mental features of mentalistic attributions. No man's intentional mental phenomena are insular.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"to a fair degree, mentalistic attribution rests not on the subject's having mastered the contents of the attribution … but on his having a certain responsibility to communal conventions","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"If an individual's physical, behavioral and phenomenal history is held fixed and only his community's use of a word he partly misunderstands is varied, what he believes changes; so mental content depends partly on social environment.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Ordinary practice correctly ascribes thoughts whose contents the thinker only partly understands, since speakers answer to communal standards; this tells against Cartesian transparency of content and against functionalist accounts confined to the individual's inputs, outputs and inner states.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"individualistic","aliases":["individualism","individualistically"],"definition":"philosophical treatments that seek to see a person's intentional mental phenomena ultimately and purely in terms of what happens to the person, what occurs within him, and how he responds to his physical environment, without any essential reference to the social context","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"oblique occurrence","aliases":["oblique","obliquely","oblique occurrences"],"definition":"When an expression like 'water' functions in a content clause so that it is not freely exchangeable with all extensionally equivalent expressions, we shall say that it has oblique occurrence.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"partial understanding","aliases":["partially understands","partially","partial"],"definition":"One may pick a case in which the subject only partially understands an expression. He may apply it firmly and correctly in a range of cases, but be unclear or agnostic about certain of its applications or implications which, in fact, are fully established in common practice.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"metalinguistic reinterpretation","aliases":["metalinguistic","reinterpreted","reinterpretation"],"definition":"to count the error of the subject as purely metalinguistic. Thus the patient's apparent belief that he had arthritis in the thigh would be reinterpreted as a belief that 'arthritis' applied to something (or some disease) in his thigh.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"The patient reports to his doctor his fear that his arthritis has now lodged in his thigh. The doctor replies by telling him that this cannot be so, since arthritis is specifically an inflammation of joints. … We are to conceive of a situation in which the patient proceeds from birth through the same course of physical events that he actually does … in our imagined case, physicians, lexicographers, and informed laymen apply 'arthritis' not only to arthritis but to various other rheumatoid ailments.","phenomenon":"social externalism (anti-individualism)","aliases":["social"],"near":["physical-environment externalism (Twin Earth)","indexical (demonstrative) reference","inverted qualia"],"avoid":["deferential use of a name","reference surviving a false associated theory","stability of reference across theory change","argument from error against descriptivism"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"When Alfred refers to an apple, saying to himself \"That is wholesome,\" what he refers to depends not just on the content of what he says or thinks, but on what apple is before him. Without altering the meaning of Alfred's utterance … we could conceive an exchange of the actual apple for another one that is indistinguishable to Alfred. We would thereby conceive him as referring to something different and even as saying something with a different truth value.","phenomenon":"indexical (demonstrative) reference","aliases":["indexicality","indexical","indexically"],"near":["social externalism (anti-individualism)","referential opacity","merely de dicto (notional) belief"],"avoid":["indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","direct reference of demonstratives","same character, different content","perceptual de re belief","de re belief"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"If Archie says, 'Lead the way and we will precede', we routinely reinterpret the words in describing his expectations.","phenomenon":"malapropism","aliases":["malapropisms"],"near":["spoonerism","verbal irony","regional dialect usage"],"avoid":["speaker meaning independent of sentence meaning","principle of charity","catachresis"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1111/j.1475-4975.1979.tb00374.x"},{"id":"davidson1967-logical-form-action","title":"The Logical Form of Action Sentences","authors":["Donald Davidson"],"year":"1967","area":"language","topic":"tense, events & logical form","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The basic idea is that verbs of action—verbs that say 'what someone did'—should be construed as containing a place, for singular terms or variables, that they do not appear to.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The problem [of action verbs' variable polyadicity] is solved in the natural way, by introducing events as entities about which an indefinite number of things can be said.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Action verbs carry a hidden event place: 'Shem kicked Shaun' is ∃x Kicked(Shem, Shaun, x), and prepositional modifiers become conjoined predicates of x, so ∃x(Flew(I, my spaceship, x) ∧ To(the Morning Star, x)) entails ∃x Flew(I, my spaceship, x).","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Unlike analyses via 'x brings it about that p' or an operator on whole sentences, quantifying over events keeps action sentences extensional: co-referring terms substitute, prepositional modifiers detach, and a Tarski-style truth theory can handle them.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"variable polyadicity","definition":"Kenny's name for the apparent capacity of action verbs to take an open-ended number of argument places ('Jones buttered the toast', '… in the bathroom', '… with a knife'); so analysed, their entailments are lost. The problem afflicts all event verbs, not just action verbs.","exact":false},{"id":"t2","term":"event-place","aliases":["event-places"],"definition":"Part of what we must learn when we learn the meaning of any predicate is how many places it has, and what sorts of entities the variables that hold these places range over. Some predicates have an event-place, some do not.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"logical form","definition":"[Limits on logical form] are set if our interest is in giving a coherent and constructive account of meaning: we must uncover enough structure to make it possible to state, for an arbitrary sentence, how its meaning depends on that structure, and we must not attribute more structure than such a theory of meaning can accommodate.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"intentionally","aliases":["intentional"],"definition":"intentional actions are not a class of actions, or … doing something intentionally is not a manner of doing it. To say someone did something intentionally is to describe the action in a way that bears a special relation to the beliefs and attitudes of the agent","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"(2) Jones buttered the toast in the bathroom with a knife at midnight. … If we go on to analyse 'Jones buttered the toast' as containing a two-place predicate, 'Jones buttered the toast in the bathroom' as containing a three-place predicate, and so forth, we obliterate the logical relations between these sentences, namely that (2) entails the others.","phenomenon":"adverb-dropping entailment (modifier drop)","near":["non-intersective (attributive) adjective modification","substitutivity of co-referring singular terms","causative/inchoative alternation"],"avoid":["logical dependence among atomic statements"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Susan says, 'I crossed the Channel in fifteen hours.' 'Good grief, that was slow.' … Now Susan adds, 'But I swam.' 'Good grief, that was fast.' We do not withdraw the claim that it was a slow crossing; this is consistent with its being a fast swimming.","phenomenon":"non-intersective (attributive) adjective modification","near":["intersective adjective modification","privative adjective modification","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)"],"avoid":["intensional (non-extensional) adjective"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"'I didn't know it was loaded' belongs to one standard pattern of excuse. I do not deny that I pointed the gun and pulled the trigger, nor that I shot the victim. My ignorance explains how it happened that I pointed the gun and pulled the trigger intentionally, but did not shoot the victim intentionally.","phenomenon":"one action under different descriptions","near":["deviant causal chain","weakness of will (akrasia)","doctrine of double effect"],"avoid":["excusing condition","referential opacity","intensionality of rationalizations"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Let 'x consists in the fact that S' be a predicate of events that allows substituting co-referring singular terms and logically equivalent sentences. S is logically equivalent to '{y: y=y ∧ S} = {y: y=y}', and for any R materially equivalent to S, '{y: y=y ∧ S}' and '{y: y=y ∧ R}' co-refer; so whatever consists in the fact that S consists in the fact that R, and all events are identical.","phenomenon":"slingshot argument","near":["Frege's puzzle about informative identity","Mates's puzzle about synonym substitution","Russell's paradox"],"exact":false},{"id":"e5","text":"'Jones did it with a knife.' 'Please tell me more about it.' The 'it' here doesn't refer to Jones or the knife, but to what Jones did—or so it seems.","phenomenon":"event anaphora","near":["donkey anaphora","propositional anaphora","bridging anaphora"],"avoid":["cross-sentential anaphora"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/jj.13027259.6"},{"id":"davidson1973-radical-interpretation","title":"Radical Interpretation","authors":["Donald Davidson"],"year":"1973","area":"language","topic":"interpretation & convention","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"what Tarski assumed outright for each T-sentence can be indirectly elicited by a holistic constraint. If that constraint is adequate, each T-sentence will in fact yield interpretations.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"interpreting an agent's intentions, his beliefs and his words are parts of a single project, no part of which can be assumed to be complete before the rest is.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Because belief and meaning are interdependent, the interpreter holds belief constant by charity—treating speakers' held-true sentences as mostly true by his own lights—and solves for meaning, fitting first-order logic first, then indexical sentences, then the rest.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A truth theory serves for interpretation if it entails, for each sentence s, 's is true ↔ p'—p merely true iff s is, not a translation—and its T-sentences jointly best fit evidence about which sentences speakers hold true.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"radical interpretation","definition":"The task, implicit in all understanding of another's speech, of saying what one could know that would suffice to understand a speaker's utterances, and what evidence—available without prior knowledge of the language and statable without notions like meaning or synonymy—could support it.","exact":false},{"id":"t3","term":"holding a sentence true","aliases":["holding true","holds true","held true","hold true"],"definition":"A belief, but a single attitude applicable to all sentences, so needing no finely discriminated attributions; an interpreter can identify it before he can interpret, since he may know that a speaker intends to express a truth without knowing what truth.","exact":false},{"id":"t4","term":"principle of charity","aliases":["charity"],"definition":"Solving the interdependence of belief and meaning by holding belief constant as far as possible while solving for meaning: assigning truth conditions that make speakers right when plausibly possible, by our own lights—applied across the board, not only to the sentential connectives as in Quine.","exact":false,"avoid":["davidson1967-truth-meaning/t4","davidson1974-very-idea-conceptual/t3"]}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Kurt belongs to the German speech community, and Kurt holds true \"Es regnet\" on Saturday at noon, and it is raining near Kurt on Saturday at noon. The interpreter counts this as evidence that \"Es regnet\" is true-in-German when spoken by x at t if and only if it is raining near x at t—though not conclusive evidence, since Kurt, or anyone else, may be wrong about whether it is raining near him.","phenomenon":"principle of charity","near":["indeterminacy of translation","non-natural meaning","semantic deference"],"avoid":["radical interpretation","Quinean radical translation","Tarskian T-sentence","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)"],"exact":false},{"id":"e2","text":"the T-sentence does fix the truth value relative to certain conditions, but it does not say the object language sentence is true because the conditions hold. Yet if truth value were all that mattered, the T-sentence for \"Snow is white\" could as well say that it is true if and only if grass is green or 2 + 2 = 4 as say that it is true if and only if snow is white.","phenomenon":"Foster's extensionality problem for T-theories","near":["slingshot argument","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","Mates's puzzle about synonym substitution"],"avoid":["Tarskian T-sentence","instance of the T-schema","indeterminacy of interpretation","indeterminacy of translation","coextensive but non-synonymous predicates"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Even when all the evidence about which sentences Kurt and other German speakers hold true is in, acceptable theories of truth for German will probably not all be identical: there will remain trade-offs between the beliefs we attribute to a speaker and the meanings we assign his words. Yet any theory that passes the tests will serve for understanding what he says.","phenomenon":"indeterminacy of interpretation","near":["Foster's extensionality problem for T-theories","conceptual-scheme relativism","rule-following paradox"],"avoid":["indeterminacy of translation","indeterminacy of compromise","indeterminacy (inscrutability) of reference","indeterminacy of reference","underdetermination of theory by evidence"],"exact":false}],"doi":"10.1111/j.1746-8361.1973.tb00623.x"},{"id":"davidson1974-very-idea-conceptual","title":"On the Very Idea of a Conceptual Scheme","authors":["Donald Davidson"],"year":"1974","area":"language","topic":"interpretation & convention","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[The] dualism of scheme and content … cannot be made intelligible and defensible. It is itself a dogma of empiricism, the third dogma.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Given the underlying methodology of interpretation, we could not be in a position to judge that others had concepts or beliefs radically different from our own.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Empiricism's third dogma—the dualism of an organizing scheme and an uninterpreted content waiting to be organized—is unintelligible; so we can say neither that schemes differ nor that all mankind shares one, and objective truth survives.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A different scheme would have to be largely true but untranslatable, yet truth cannot be understood apart from translation (Convention T); and because interpretation must be charitable, even partial untranslatability cannot reveal radically different concepts.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"conceptual scheme","aliases":["conceptual schemes","scheme"],"definition":"We may identify conceptual schemes with languages, then, or better, allowing for the possibility that more than one language may express the same scheme, sets of intertranslatable languages.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"scheme–content dualism","aliases":["dualism of scheme and content","dualism of conceptual scheme and empirical content","dualism"],"definition":"The new dualism is the foundation of an empiricism shorn of the untenable dogmas of the analytic-synthetic distinction and reductionism … this second dualism of scheme and content, of organizing system and something waiting to be organized … is itself a dogma of empiricism, the third dogma.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"charity","aliases":["Charity","principle of charity"],"definition":"charity is not an option, but a condition of having a workable theory … if we want to understand others, we must count them right in most matters.","exact":true,"avoid":["davidson1967-truth-meaning/t4","davidson1973-radical-interpretation/t4"]},{"id":"t5","term":"incommensurable","aliases":["Incommensurable","incommensurability"],"definition":"\"Incommensurable\" is, of course, Kuhn and Feyerabend's word for \"not intertranslatable.\"","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"If you see a ketch sailing by and your companion says, \"Look at that handsome yawl,\" … One natural possibility is that your friend has mistaken a ketch for a yawl, and has formed a false belief. But if his vision is good and his line of sight favorable it is even more plausible that he does not use the word \"yawl\" quite as you do, and has made no mistake at all.","phenomenon":"principle of charity","near":["social externalism (anti-individualism)","division of linguistic labor","conversational implicature"],"avoid":["malapropism","indeterminacy of translation","referential use of a definite description"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"some language, say Saturnian, may be translatable into English, and some further language, like Plutonian, may be translatable into Saturnian, while Plutonian is not translatable into English. Enough translatable differences may add up to an untranslatable one.","phenomenon":"intransitivity of translatability","near":["indeterminacy of translation","indeterminacy of reference","radical interpretation"],"avoid":["sorites paradox","incommensurability","conceptual-scheme relativism","failure of transitivity (hypothetical syllogism)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"language first of all is a classification and arrangement of the stream of sensory experience which results in a certain world-order … language does in a cruder but also in a broader and more versatile way the same thing that science does","phenomenon":"scheme–content dualism","near":["analytic–synthetic distinction","radical reductionism","indeterminacy of translation"],"avoid":["linguistic relativity","Sapir–Whorf hypothesis","conceptual relativism","conceptual-scheme relativism","incommensurability","underdetermination of theory by evidence"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"The sentence \"My skin is warm\" is true if and only if my skin is warm. Here there is no reference to a fact, a world, an experience, or a piece of evidence.","phenomenon":"Tarskian T-sentence","near":["verification condition","assertibility condition","liar sentence"],"avoid":["Convention T","disquotational schema","deflationism about truth","correspondence to the facts","analytic truth","instance of the T-schema","T-sentence"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/3129898"},{"id":"lewis1970-general-semantics","title":"General Semantics","authors":["David Lewis"],"year":"1970","area":"language","topic":"quantification & formal semantics","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"It is natural … to identify meanings with semantically interpreted phrase markers minus their terminal nodes: finite ordered trees having at each node a category and an appropriate intension.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Semantics with no treatment of truth conditions is not semantics.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Sentence, name and common-noun intensions are functions from indices (world, time, place, speaker, audience, indicated objects, previous discourse, assignment) to extensions; a (c/c₁…cₙ)-intension φ₀ maps c₁-,…,cₙ-intensions φ₁…φₙ to the compound's c-intension φ₀(φ₁…φₙ).","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"If every language of interest has a categorially based transformational grammar whose base structure alone fixes meaning, one simple format serves a great variety of possible languages: categorial base syntax paired with function-application semantics, transformations being semantically idle.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"Markerese","aliases":["Semantic Markerese","markers"],"definition":"Semantic markers are symbols: items in the vocabulary of an artificial language we may call Semantic Markerese. … we can know the Markerese translation of an English sentence without knowing the first thing about the meaning of the English sentence: namely, the conditions under which it would be true.","exact":true,"avoid":["katz1963-structure-semantic-theory/t3"]},{"id":"t2","term":"index","aliases":["indices"],"definition":"We may take indices as n-tuples (finite sequences) of the various items other than meaning that may enter into determining extensions. … an index is tentatively any octuple of which the first coordinate is a possible world, … and the [eighth] coordinate is an infinite sequence of things.","exact":true,"avoid":["lewis1980-index-context-content/t2"]},{"id":"t3","term":"compositional intension","aliases":["compositional intensions","intension","intensions"],"definition":"an appropriate intension for a (c/c1...cn) … is any n-place function from c1-intensions, ..., and cn-intensions to c-intensions. … We will call these intensions for derived categories compositional intensions.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"binder","aliases":["binding"],"definition":"a separate constituent called a binder: a certain sort of (S/N)/S that takes a sentence and makes an extensional verb phrase by binding a variable at all its free occurrences (if any) in the sentence.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"method of paraphrased performatives","aliases":["paraphrases","performatives"],"definition":"I propose that [non-declaratives like 'Be late!'] ought to be treated as paraphrases of the corresponding performatives [like 'I command you to be late'], having the same base structure, meaning, intension, and truth-value at an index or on an occasion.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"delineation coordinate","definition":"Let the indices contain a delineation coordinate: a positive real number, regarded as the boundary temperature between cool and warm things. … More generally, the delineation coordinate must be a sequence of boundary-specifying numbers.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"What is the set of things to which 'alleged' applies? An alleged Communist is not something which is, on the one hand, an alleged thing and, on the other hand, a Communist. … at certain indices 'Communist' and 'Maoist' have the same extension but 'alleged Communist' and 'alleged Maoist' do not","phenomenon":"intensional (non-extensional) adjective","near":["intersective adjective modification","subsective adjective","privative adjective modification"],"avoid":["non-intersective (attributive) adjective modification","coextensive but non-synonymous predicates","referential opacity (substitution failure)","referential opacity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"[Doubling object-takers] would give us one meaning for 'Every boy loves some girl': the weaker meaning, on which the sentence can be true even if each boy loves a different girl. But the sentence is ambiguous; where shall we get a stronger meaning, on which the sentence is true only if a certain girl - Zuleika, perhaps - is loved by all boys?","phenomenon":"quantifier scope ambiguity","aliases":["ambiguous"],"near":["de re / de dicto ambiguity","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","collective vs distributive reading"],"avoid":["inverse scope reading"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"'Lothario seeks a girl', in the sense in which it can be paraphrased as 'Lothario seeks a certain particular girl', can have the base structure … but what about the sense in which any old girl would do?","phenomenon":"de re / de dicto ambiguity","near":["de se belief","free-choice indefinite","intentional identity"],"avoid":["quantifier scope ambiguity","specific vs non-specific indefinite","intensional transitive verb","referential opacity (substitution failure)","referential opacity","de dicto vs de re modality","non-specific reading of an intensional transitive verb","merely de dicto (notional) belief","failure of existential generalization","existential statement carrying a speaker's reference","de dicto vs de re reading","quantifying into a modal context"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Consider the sentence 'This is older than this'. I might say it pointing at a 1962 Volkswagen when I say the first 'this' and at a 1963 Volkswagen when I say the second 'this'. The sentence should be true on such an occasion; but how can it be?","phenomenon":"repeated demonstrative with distinct demonstrata","near":["anaphoric (coreferential) pronoun","deferred ostension","shifted indexical (monster)"],"avoid":["indexical (demonstrative) reference","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","direct reference of demonstratives","same character, different content","reference shift within a conversation"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"Consider the sentence 'The door is open'. This does not mean that the one and only door that now exists is open; nor does it mean that the one and only door near the place of utterance, or pointed at, or mentioned in previous discourse, is open. Rather it means that the one and only door among the objects that are somehow prominent on the occasion is open.","phenomenon":"incomplete definite description resolved by salience","aliases":["prominent"],"near":["anaphoric definite description","deictic use of a definite description","generic definite description"],"avoid":["attributive use of a definite description","referential use of a definite description","quantifier domain restriction","semantic underdetermination (completion)","free pragmatic enrichment","saturation of a linguistically mandated slot"],"exact":true},{"id":"e6","text":"Pretend first that the only vagueness is the vagueness of 'cool' and 'warm' … sentences with vague constituents are not necessarily vague: 'This is cool or warm, but not both' is true at all delineations, on an occasion on which there is a unique indicated object, even if the indicated object is lukewarm.","phenomenon":"penumbral connection","near":["sorites paradox","higher-order vagueness","context-sensitivity of gradable adjectives"],"avoid":["vague predicate","excluded middle without bivalence","logical dependence among atomic statements"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00413598"},{"id":"stalnaker1974-pragmatic-presuppositions","title":"Pragmatic Presuppositions","authors":["Robert Stalnaker"],"year":"1974","area":"language","topic":"presupposition & projection","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"constraints on presuppositions are constraints on the contexts in which statements can be made, and not constraints on the truth-conditions of propositions expressed in making the statements","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Presupposing is … not a mental attitude like believing, but is rather a linguistic disposition—a disposition to behave in one's use of language as if one had certain beliefs","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"To a first approximation, a speaker presupposes P when she takes P for granted, takes her addressee to do so too, and takes the addressee to recognize this; presuppositions belong to speakers, not to sentences or propositions.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Many presupposition facts follow from conversational strategy, not word meanings: supposing or asking whether something holds signals it isn't presupposed (so 'discover' loses its presupposition in Karttunen's examples), and asserted content joins the background (yielding his rule for 'and').","formal":false}],"pick":0,"avoid":["stalnaker1973-presuppositions"],"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"pragmatic presupposition","aliases":["speaker presupposition"],"definition":"A proposition P is a pragmatic presupposition of a speaker in a given context just in case the speaker assumes or believes that P, assumes or believes that his addressee assumes or believes that P, and assumes or believes that his addressee recognizes that he is making these assumptions","exact":true,"avoid":["stalnaker1973-presuppositions/t1"]},{"id":"t2","term":"semantic presupposition","aliases":["presupposes"],"definition":"a proposition that P presupposes that Q if and only if Q must be true in order that P have a truth-value at all","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"common background","aliases":["background"],"definition":"a background of beliefs or assumptions which are shared by the speaker and his audience, and which are recognized by them to be so shared","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e2","text":"I am asked by someone who I have just met, \"Are you going to lunch?\" I reply, \"No, I've got to pick up my sister.\" Here I seem to presuppose that I have a sister, even though I do not assume that the addressee knows this. Yet the statement is clearly acceptable","phenomenon":"presupposition accommodation","near":["presupposition failure","factive presupposition","presupposition filtering"],"avoid":["existential presupposition","informative presupposition","defective context","particularized conversational implicature","conversational implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Compare \"If I regret later that I have not told the truth, I will confess it to everyone\" with the same sentence using \"realize\" or \"discover\" in place of \"regret\". With \"regret\" the speaker clearly presupposes that he has not told the truth; with \"realize\" or \"discover\" he clearly does not.","phenomenon":"presupposition cancellation with semi-factives","near":["presupposition failure","presupposition plugging by a verb of saying","presupposition filtering"],"avoid":["factive presupposition","local accommodation","presupposition projection"],"exact":false},{"id":"e4","text":"So if I say \"Harry is married and Harry's wife is a great cook,\" I assert, and do not presuppose, that Harry is married. But the second conjunct, stated alone (Harry's wife is a great cook), would require the presupposition that Harry is married.","phenomenon":"presupposition filtering","aliases":["presupposition"],"near":["presupposition accommodation","presupposition failure","temporal implicature of 'and'"],"avoid":["presupposition projection","anaphoric presupposition","existential presupposition"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"stalnaker1968-theory-conditionals","title":"A Theory of Conditionals","authors":["Robert Stalnaker"],"year":"1968","area":"language","topic":"conditionals","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"First, add the antecedent (hypothetically) to your stock of beliefs; second, make whatever adjustments are required to maintain consistency …; finally, consider whether or not the consequent is then true.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"A > B is true in α if B is true in f(A, α); A > B is false in α if B is false in f(A, α).","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"'If A, then B' is true just in case B is true in the one A-world that a selection function picks out as differing minimally from the actual world (the actual world itself, if A is true).","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"The conditional connective lies between material and strict implication but, unlike both, invalidates transitivity, strengthening of the antecedent and contraposition, while ¬(A > B) is equivalent to A > ¬B whenever A is possible.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"selection function","aliases":["s-function"],"definition":"a selection function, f, which takes a proposition and a possible world as arguments and a possible world as its value. [It picks], for each antecedent A, a particular possible world in which A is true.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"absurd world","definition":"[the one] in which contradictions and all their consequences are true. It is an isolated element under R … The purpose of λ is to allow for an interpretation of “If A, then B” in the case where A is impossible","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"pragmatic ambiguity","aliases":["pragmatic","pragmatically","ambiguous","ambiguity"],"definition":"A sentence is ambiguous if there is more than one proposition which it may properly be interpreted to express. … pragmatic (if the interpretation depends directly on the context of use). … Any sentence involving pronouns, tensed verbs, articles or quantifiers is pragmatically ambiguous.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"pragmatic problem of counterfactuals","aliases":["problem","selection function"],"definition":"The questions, “On what basis do we select a selection function from among the acceptable ones?” and “What are the criteria for ordering possible worlds?” are reformulations of the pragmatic problem of counterfactuals, which is a problem in the application of conditional logic.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Premisses. “If J. Edgar Hoover were today a communist, then he would be a traitor.” “If J. Edgar Hoover had been born a Russian, then he would today be a communist.” Conclusion. “If J. Edgar Hoover had been born a Russian, he would be a traitor.” It seems reasonable to affirm these premisses and deny the conclusion.","phenomenon":"failure of transitivity (hypothetical syllogism)","near":["failure of antecedent strengthening","failure of contraposition","conditional excluded middle"],"avoid":["subjunctive (counterfactual) conditional"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Consider “If this match were struck, it would light,” and “If this match had been soaked in water overnight and it were struck, it would light.”","phenomenon":"failure of antecedent strengthening","near":["failure of modus tollens","failure of contraposition","conditional excluded middle"],"avoid":["failure of transitivity (hypothetical syllogism)","failure of transitivity","Sobel sequence","subjunctive (counterfactual) conditional"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"“If the U.S. halts the bombing, then North Vietnam will not agree to negotiate.” A person would believe that this statement is true if he thought that the North Vietnamese were determined to press for a complete withdrawal of U.S. troops. But he would surely deny the contrapositive, “If North Vietnam agrees to negotiate, then the U.S. will not have halted the bombing.”","phenomenon":"failure of contraposition","aliases":["contrapositive"],"near":["failure of antecedent strengthening","failure of transitivity (hypothetical syllogism)","failure of modus tollens"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"To deny “If Kennedy were alive today, we wouldn’t be in this Vietnam mess,” we say, “If Kennedy were alive today, we would so be in this Vietnam mess.”","phenomenon":"conditional excluded middle","near":["would/might duality of counterfactuals","failure of contraposition","failure of antecedent strengthening"],"avoid":["semifactual","conditional negation","subjunctive (counterfactual) conditional"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"The statement is, “If the Chinese enter the Vietnam conflict, the United States will use nuclear weapons.” … the following piece of reasoning is an obvious non sequitur: “I firmly believe that the Chinese will stay out of the conflict; therefore I believe that the statement is true.”","phenomenon":"paradox of material implication","near":["denying the antecedent","affirming the consequent","failure of contraposition"],"avoid":["explosion (ex falso quodlibet)"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/978-94-009-9117-0_2"},{"id":"kamp1981-theory-truth-semantic","title":"A Theory of Truth and Semantic Representation","authors":["Hans Kamp"],"year":"1981","area":"language","topic":"dynamic semantics & anaphora","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Indefinite descriptions are … referential terms, not existential quantifiers.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"A discourse D is true in M, given a DRS K of D iff there is a proper embedding into M of the principal DR of K.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Representations of a discourse (plausibly hearers' mental ones)—partial models built by rules from syntax, in which indefinites introduce discourse referents and pronouns pick up available ones—fix truth conditions: the discourse is true iff its representation embeds properly into the world-model.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"'If A then B' is true in M iff every proper embedding f of A's DR m into M extends to a proper embedding g ⊇ f of m′ (m plus B's content); hence antecedent indefinites get universal force.","formal":true}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"discourse representation (DR)","aliases":["discourse representation","discourse representations","representation","representations","DR","DR's","DRs"],"definition":"The representations postulated here are … similar in structure to the models familiar from model-theoretic semantics. In fact, formally they are nothing other than partial models, typically with small finite domains.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"Discourse Representation Structure (DRS)","aliases":["Discourse Representation Structure","DRS","DRSs","DRS's"],"definition":"A partially ordered family of DR's rather than a single DR: a principal DR for the whole text, plus pairs of subordinate DR's representing the parts of each conditional or universal sentence, which cannot be decomposed inside the DR that contains it.","exact":false},{"id":"t3","term":"proper embedding","aliases":["proper embeddings"],"definition":"Compatibility of M with m … can be defined as the existence of a proper embedding of m into M, where a proper embedding is a map from the universe of m into that of M which, roughly speaking, preserves all the [conditions] which m specifies of the elements of its domain.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"accessibility (availability)","aliases":["accessibility","availability","accessible","available"],"definition":"the discourse referents available to a given pronoun must all occur in the same, or else in some superordinate, DR.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"(1) If Pedro owns a donkey he beats it (2) Every farmer who owns a donkey beats it. … How does an expression of a type which standardly (or so it always seemed) conveys existence manage to express universality in a sentence such as (1)?","phenomenon":"donkey anaphora","aliases":["donkey"],"near":["cross-sentential anaphora","Bach–Peters sentence","quantifier scope ambiguity"],"avoid":["E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora","donkey sentence","unselective binding","unselective quantification","quantificational variability effect","if-clause restricting a quantificational adverb","bound-variable anaphora","bound-variable pronoun","bound-variable reading of a pronoun"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"(8) Pedro owns a donkey. He beats it.","phenomenon":"cross-sentential anaphora","near":["deictic use of a pronoun","telescoping","modal subordination"],"avoid":["donkey anaphora","E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora","discourse anaphora","bound-variable anaphora","bound-variable pronoun","bound-variable reading of a pronoun","cross-sentential (discourse) anaphora"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"In (17) “If Pedro owns every donkey then he beats it.” ‘it’ cannot have ‘every donkey’ for its antecedent. … in (19) “If Pedro likes every woman who owns a donkey he feeds it” ‘it’ cannot be co-referential with ‘a donkey’, whereas such a link does seem possible in (20) “If Pedro likes a woman who owns a donkey he feeds it.”","phenomenon":"accessibility constraint on anaphora","near":["weak crossover","cataphora (backward anaphora)","Bach–Peters sentence"],"avoid":["donkey anaphora","E-type pronoun","scope island","inaccessible discourse referent (scope-limited lifespan)","quantifier scope ambiguity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"(25) If a woman loves him Pedro courts her. … a DRS which links him with Pedro and her with a woman","phenomenon":"cataphora (backward anaphora)","aliases":["cataphora","cataphoric","backward anaphora"],"near":["weak crossover","deictic use of a pronoun","disjoint reference (Principle B)"],"avoid":["donkey anaphora","E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora","accessibility constraint on anaphora","inaccessible discourse referent (scope-limited lifespan)","Bach–Peters sentence","bound-variable pronoun"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"heim1983-file-change-semantics","title":"File Change Semantics and the Familiarity Theory of Definiteness","authors":["Irene Heim"],"year":"1983","area":"language","topic":"dynamic semantics & anaphora","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"For every indefinite, start a new card. For every definite, update an old card.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"logical forms themselves are not assigned truth conditions, only files are. Only in an indirect way, i.e. via the files they affect, will logical forms be associated with truth conditions.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Indefinites are not quantifiers but open formulas whose variable stays free, which is why they can antecede pronouns beyond their scope; their existential force comes instead from what it takes for the resulting file to be true.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"For atomic p, F + p is defined only if every definite NPᵢ in p has i ∈ Dom(F) and every indefinite NPᵢ has i ∉ Dom(F); a file F is true iff Sat(F) ≠ ∅.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"file change potential","definition":"Each logical form is assigned a “file change potential”, i.e. a function from files into files. … this function will determine how you get from the file that obtains prior to the utterance to the file that comes to obtain as a result of the utterance.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"Novelty/Familiarity Condition","definition":"Let F be a file, p an atomic proposition. Then p is appropriate with respect to F only if, for every noun phrase NPi with index i that p contains: if NPi is definite, then i ∈ Dom(F), and if NPi is indefinite, then i ∉ Dom(F).","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"file","definition":"to understand an utterance is to keep a file which, at every time in the course of the utterance, contains the information that has so far been conveyed by the utterance.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"file card","aliases":["file cards"],"definition":"I would like to suggest that Karttunen's talk about “discourse referents” be rephrased by substituting “file card” for “discourse referent”: once we realize that discourse referents are essentially like file cards, their identity criteria and their relation to referents come to look much less mysterious.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"truth of a file","definition":"To establish the truth of a file, we have to, so to speak, line up the sequence of cards in the file with a sequence of actual individuals, such that each individual fits the description on the corresponding card.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Every time a soldier accompanies us he shoots. … The problem is that the “he” in [this sentence] fails to refer, and that deixis without reference is a contradiction in terms","phenomenon":"donkey anaphora","near":["cross-sentential anaphora","deictic (referential) pronoun","cataphora (backward anaphora)"],"avoid":["E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora","donkey sentence","unselective binding","bound-variable reading of a pronoun","bound-variable pronoun","quantificational variability effect"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"A soldier will accompany us. He will shoot.","phenomenon":"cross-sentential anaphora","near":["donkey anaphora","telescoping","cataphora (backward anaphora)"],"avoid":["discourse anaphora","E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora","cross-sentential (discourse) anaphora","bound-variable pronoun"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"(a) Everybody found a cat and kept it. (b) It ran away. The relevant facts here are that the “it” in (5a), but not the “it” in (5b), can be interpreted as anaphoric to “a cat”, (the intended reading being one with “everybody” taking wider scope than “a cat”).","phenomenon":"accessibility constraint on anaphora","aliases":["anaphoric"],"near":["weak crossover","telescoping","disjoint reference (Principle B)"],"avoid":["donkey anaphora","E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora","discourse anaphora","cross-sentential anaphora","quantifier scope ambiguity","bound-variable pronoun"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Every cat ate its food. … [It] has a reading where “its” … doesn't refer to any particular cat.","phenomenon":"bound-variable pronoun","near":["donkey anaphora","E-type pronoun","cross-sentential anaphora"],"avoid":["bound-variable reading of a pronoun","bound-variable anaphora"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1515/9783110852820.164"},{"id":"groenendijk1991-dynamic-predicate-logic","title":"Dynamic Predicate Logic","authors":["Jeroen Groenendijk","Martin Stokhof"],"year":"1991","area":"language","topic":"dynamic semantics & anaphora","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"A distinctive feature of [an input-output semantics for first-order formulas] is that it allows for existential quantifiers to bind variables which are outside their syntactic scope.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[Interpreting formulas as relations between assignments] allows us to translate an indefinite term uniformly as an existentially quantified phrase in situ … without any need of re-analysis.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Formulas denote relations between assignments: conjunction is relational composition, ⟦∃xφ⟧ = {⟨g,h⟩ | ∃k: k[x]g & ⟨k,h⟩∈⟦φ⟧}, and φ→ψ tests that every output of φ is an input for ψ; so ∃xPx∧Qx ≃ ∃x[Px∧Qx] and ∃xPx→Qx ≃ ∀x[Px→Qx].","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Keeping the ordinary syntax of first-order logic but taking meanings to be relations between variable assignments yields a compositional, representation-free account of discourse anaphora, empirically equivalent to Kamp's DRT, on which truth conditions do not exhaust meaning.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"internally dynamic","definition":"Because of its power to pass on variable bindings from its left conjunct to the right one, we call conjunction an internally dynamic connective.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"externally static","definition":"the universal quantifier shares with implication the characteristic of being externally static. Neither a universal quantifier itself, nor any existential quantifier inside its scope can bind variables outside the scope of that universal quantifier.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"test","aliases":["tests"],"definition":"various kinds of [formulas] have the characteristic that they do not pass on bindings created by expressions which occur in them. They function as a kind of ‘test’ in this sense that they examine whether an input assignment meets a certain condition, return it as output if it does, and reject it otherwise.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"dynamic entailment","aliases":["entails","entailment"],"definition":"We say that φ entails ψ if every successful execution of φ guarantees a succesful execution of ψ. Or, to put it slightly differently, φ entails ψ iff every assignment that is a possible output of φ is a possible input for ψ.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"mentalist","definition":"Thus, a mentalist we call someone who claims that a level of representation is necessary, not someone who merely claims that it exists.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"If a farmer owns a donkey, he beats it … Every farmer who owns a donkey, beats it","phenomenon":"donkey anaphora","aliases":["donkey"],"near":["cross-sentential anaphora","paycheck pronoun","telescoping"],"avoid":["E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora","donkey sentence","strong (universal) reading of donkey sentences","unselective binding","quantificational variability effect","if-clause restricting a quantificational adverb","bound-variable pronoun"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"A man walks in the park. He whistles","phenomenon":"cross-sentential anaphora","near":["donkey anaphora","telescoping","modal subordination"],"avoid":["discourse anaphora","E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora","bound-variable pronoun"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"(25) I dropped ten marbles and found all of them, except for one. It is probably under the sofa. (26) I dropped ten marbles and found only nine of them. It is probably under the sofa. There is a marked contrast between these two sequences of sentences. The first one is all right, and the pronoun it refers to the missing marble. The second sequence, however, is out.","phenomenon":"formal link (overt antecedent) requirement on anaphora","near":["donkey anaphora","scalar implicature","modal subordination"],"avoid":["cross-sentential anaphora","cross-sentential (discourse) anaphora","discourse anaphora","E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora","bridging","bridging inference","anaphoric presupposition","coextensive but non-synonymous predicates"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"If a client turns up, you treat him politely. You offer him a cup of coffee and ask him to wait","phenomenon":"modal subordination","near":["telescoping","bathroom sentence","paycheck pronoun"],"avoid":["cross-sentential anaphora","donkey anaphora","discourse anaphora","presupposition accommodation","if-clause restricting a modal","if-clause restricting a quantificational adverb","intermediate accommodation"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"Every player chooses a pawn. He puts it on square one.","phenomenon":"telescoping","near":["donkey anaphora","bathroom sentence","paycheck pronoun"],"avoid":["cross-sentential anaphora","discourse anaphora","quantificational subordination","quantificational subordination (telescoping)","bound-variable pronoun"],"exact":true},{"id":"e6","text":"Either there is no bathroom here, or it is in a funny place. In any case, it is not on the first floor","phenomenon":"bathroom sentence","aliases":["bathroom"],"near":["donkey anaphora","telescoping","modal subordination"],"avoid":["cross-sentential anaphora","discourse anaphora","anaphora under double negation","double negation anaphora","presupposition filtering","symmetric presupposition filtering"],"exact":true},{"id":"e7","text":"If I’ve got a quarter in my pocket, I’ll put [it] in the parking meter … one who utters [this sentence], probably does not intend to spend all the quarters in his pocket on the parking meter.","phenomenon":"weak (existential) reading of donkey sentences","near":["strong (universal) reading of donkey sentences","proportion problem","quantificational variability effect"],"avoid":["donkey anaphora","donkey sentence","unselective binding"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00628304"},{"id":"barwise1981-generalized-quantifiers-natural","title":"Generalized Quantifiers and Natural Language","authors":["Jon Barwise","Robin Cooper"],"year":"1981","area":"language","topic":"quantification & formal semantics","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"“most” must be treated as a determiner, not as a quantifier. It is the NP “most people” that is the quantifier.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"It is a universal semantic feature of determiners that they assign to any set A a quantifier (i.e. family of sets) that lives on A.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Noun phrases, proper names included, denote families of sets, and determiners map noun denotations to such families; properties such as monotonicity and the weak/strong split then motivate proposed empirical universals of human language.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"‘Det N VP’ is true iff ⟦VP⟧ ∈ ⟦Det⟧(⟦N⟧), where NPs denote families of sets — ⟦every⟧(A) = {X⊆E : A⊆X}, ⟦[Harry]NP⟧ = {X⊆E : h∈X} — and each D(A) lives on A: X∈D(A) ↔ X∩A ∈ D(A).","formal":true}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"lives on","aliases":["live on","living on"],"definition":"a quantifier Q lives on a set A ⊆ E if Q is a set of subsets of E with the property that, for any X ⊆ E, X ∈ Q iff (X ∩ A) ∈ Q.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"sieve","aliases":["sieves","sifting","proper quantifier denotation","proper quantifier"],"definition":"We think of a quantifier Q … as separating (“sifting”) the VP denotations into those that do and those that do not combine with it to make a true sentence … We call Q a proper quantifier denotation or sieve if … Q is a non-empty proper subset of Pow(E).","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"strong determiner","aliases":["positive strong","negative strong","strong","weak","determiner"],"definition":"A determiner D is positive strong (or negative strong, resp.) if for every model … and every A ⊆ E, if the quantifier ‖D‖(A) is defined then A ∈ ‖D‖(A). (Or A ∉ ‖D‖(A), resp.). If D is not (positive or negative) strong then D is weak.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"witness set","aliases":["witness sets","witness"],"definition":"A witness set for a quantifier D(A) living on A is any subset w of A such that w ∈ D(A). … A witness set for ‖most women‖ is any set of women which contains most women.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"fixed context assumption","definition":"The simplifying stipulation, made throughout, that a rich background held constant settles the precise meaning of basic expressions — even vague determiners like ‘most’, ‘many’ and ‘few’ — so finessing their vagueness.","exact":false}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"More than half of John's arrows hit the target. More than half the people voted for Carter. Most of John's arrows hit the target. Most people voted for Carter.","phenomenon":"proportional quantifier (not first-order definable)","near":["cardinal (intersective) quantifier","generic generalization","branching (Henkin) quantifier"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Many men run ⇔ Many men are men who run … Few women sneeze ⇔ Few women are women who sneeze; John loves Mary ⇔ John is John and loves Mary. … The peculiarity of the sentences on the right hand side of the biconditionals is presumably due to the fact that they are obviously redundant.","phenomenon":"conservativity (the ‘lives on’ property)","near":["symmetry (the intersection condition)","upward-entailing (monotone increasing) quantifier","persistence"],"avoid":["pleonasm (redundancy)","redundant assertion of presupposed content","violation of the non-redundancy (informativity) constraint on assertion"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Take VP1 to be entered the race early and VP2 to be entered the race. The following are valid: If some Republican / every linguist / John / most peanut farmers / many men entered the race early, then [the same NP] entered the race. Notice that the reverse implications do not hold, since there clearly could be people who entered the race but did not enter early.","phenomenon":"upward-entailing (monotone increasing) quantifier","near":["downward-entailing (monotone decreasing) quantifier","persistence (monotonicity in the noun argument)","conservativity (the ‘lives on’ property)"],"avoid":["adverb-dropping entailment (modifier drop)","upward monotonicity (monotone increasing NP)"],"exact":false},{"id":"e4","text":"given the utterance (21) we could naturally assume the truth of (22). … (21) No boy at the party kissed Mary. … (22) There were boys at the party. (23) No boy at the party kissed Mary since there weren't any boys at the party. … the explicit denial in (24c) sounds quite odd. … c. ?Every man at the party kissed Mary, but only because there weren't any men at the party.","phenomenon":"existential import of quantified noun phrases","near":["scalar implicature","quantifier domain restriction","negative polarity licensing"],"avoid":["existential presupposition","existence presupposition","presupposition failure","presupposition accommodation","presupposition surviving negation and questions","presupposition projection through holes","conversational implicature","generalized conversational implicature","explicit cancellation of an implicature"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00350139"},{"id":"kratzer1977-must-can-must","title":"What ‘Must’ and ‘Can’ Must and Can Mean","authors":["Angelika Kratzer"],"year":"1977","area":"language","topic":"modality","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"relative modal phrases like ‘must in view of’ and ‘can in view of’ should be considered as the foundation of the modals ‘must’ and ‘can’, respectively.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"We have found a method to clean up an inconsistent set of propositions while still staying as close as possible to all that these propositions tell us.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Modal verbs are not ambiguous among deontic, epistemic, dispositional and preferential senses: each has one invariant relational meaning, taking an 'in view of' argument (explicit or supplied by context) plus a proposition.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"With f(w) a possibly inconsistent set of propositions and X its consistent subsets: □p holds at w iff ∀A∈X ∃B∈X (A⊆B ∧ ∩B⊆p); ◇p iff ∃A∈X ∀B∈X (A⊆B → ∩B∩p≠∅).","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"relative modal phrase","aliases":["relative"],"definition":"… not an absolute ‘must’ but a relative ‘must in view of’. This relative modal phrase ‘must in view of’ has two arguments: … ‘what is known’ or ‘what is good for us’ etc. and a sentence.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"meaning proper","definition":"It is the task of semantics to describe all those features of the meaning of utterances of linguistic expressions which stay invariable in whatever context these expressions may be used. This invariable element we may call the meaning proper of a linguistic expression.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"You must and you can store must in a can. … The must which you can store in a can has nothing to do with necessity, and the can you can store your must in has nothing to do with possibility.","phenomenon":"lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","near":["polysemy","structural (syntactic) ambiguity","context-dependence of modal flavour"],"avoid":["homonymy (lexical ambiguity)","pun (deliberate ambiguity)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"“I must sneeze.” “Don’t be silly. … Everyone knows how to prevent sneezing. You feel that something fuzzy is going on in your nose. You feel it a good time in advance. And you can suppress it. That’s all.” “But once I have missed the right moment, I cannot help sneezing any more. It just comes out. It is too late to suppress it. I simply must sneeze.”","phenomenon":"dispositional (circumstantial) modality","near":["deontic modality","epistemic modality","bouletic modality"],"avoid":["natural (causal) impossibility"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Suppose a judge asks himself whether a murderer could have acted otherwise then he eventually did. … Professor Schielrecht’s answer was: Given the whole situation of the crime, which includes of course all the dispositions of the murderer, this man could not have acted otherwise than as he did. … He misunderstood the judge: what the judge probably meant was: Given such and such aspects of the situation, could the murderer have acted otherwise than he eventually did?","phenomenon":"context-supplied conversational background (modal base)","near":["modal scope ambiguity","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","Frankfurt-style case (counterfactual intervener)"],"avoid":["saturation of a linguistically mandated slot","semantic underdetermination (completion)","quantifier domain restriction"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"In Wellington a judgement was handed down which provided that deer are not personally responsible for damage they inflict on young trees. In Auckland a judgement was handed down which provided that deer are personally responsible … Although no New Zealand judge has ever doubted that murder is a crime, our semantic analysis forces us to suppose nevertheless that murder must be a crime and not a crime in view of the New Zealand judgements.","phenomenon":"explosion (ex falso quodlibet)","near":["Ross's paradox","contrary-to-duty obligation","preface paradox"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00353453"},{"id":"kripke1977-speakers-reference-semantic","title":"Speaker’s Reference and Semantic Reference","authors":["Saul Kripke"],"year":"1977","area":"language","topic":"descriptions & definiteness","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the problems Donnellan handles by semantic ambiguity should instead be treated by a general theory of speech acts.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the referential-attributive distinction is simply a special case of a general distinction, applicable to proper names as well as to definite descriptions","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Donnellan's referential uses don't refute Russell: the same phenomenon would arise among speakers of a language stipulated to have Russellian truth conditions, and economy favors explaining it pragmatically over positing a semantic ambiguity.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"What a speaker refers to, fixed by a specific intention on an occasion, can differ from a designator's conventional referent, fixed by a general intention; referential uses of descriptions, like mistaken uses of names, are cases where they may diverge.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"speaker's referent","aliases":["speaker's referent","speaker’s referent","speaker","referent"],"definition":"we may tentatively define the speaker's referent of a designator to be that object which the speaker wishes to talk about, on a given occasion, and believes fulfills the conditions for being the semantic referent of the designator.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"semantic referent","aliases":["semantic reference","referent"],"definition":"If a speaker has a designator in his idiolect, certain conventions of his idiolect (given various facts about the world) determine the referent in the idiolect: that I call the semantic referent of the designator.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"simple case","aliases":["simple","simply","simple use"],"definition":"[the speaker's] specific intention is simply to refer to the semantic referent: that is, his specific intention is simply his general semantic intention.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"complex case","aliases":["complex","complex use"],"definition":"[The speaker] has a specific intention, which is distinct from his general intention, but which he believes, as a matter of fact, to determine the same object as the one determined by his general intention. (For example, he wishes to refer to the man \"over there\" but believes that he is Jones.)","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"weak Russell language","aliases":["Russell","language"],"definition":"a language similar to English except that the truth conditions of sentences with definite descriptions are stipulated to coincide with Russell's … the semantic referent of a definite description is the unique object that satisfies the description, if any; otherwise there is no semantic referent.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"D-languages","aliases":["D-language"],"definition":"In the D-languages the apparent ambiguity between referential and attributive definite descriptions is explicitly built into the semantics of the language and affects truth conditions.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Two people see Smith in the distance and mistake him for Jones. They have a brief colloquy: \"What is Jones doing?\" \"Raking the leaves.\" \"Jones,\" in the common language of both, is a name of Jones; it never names Smith. Yet, in some sense, on this occasion, clearly both participants in the dialogue have referred to Smith, and the second participant has said something true about the man he referred to if and only if Smith was raking the leaves.","phenomenon":"speaker's reference diverging from a name's semantic reference","near":["referential use of a definite description","reference change of a proper name","reference failure"],"avoid":["misidentification","reference shift within a conversation","speaker meaning independent of sentence meaning","conflation (believing of two persons that they are one)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Someone sees a woman with a man. Taking the man to be her husband, and observing his attitude towards her, he says, \"Her husband is kind to her,\" and someone else may nod, \"Yes, he seems to be.\" Suppose the man in question is not her husband. Suppose he is her lover, to whom she has been driven precisely by her husband's cruelty.","phenomenon":"referential use of a definite description","near":["attributive use of a definite description","presupposition failure","narrow-scope (de dicto) reading of a description"],"avoid":["misidentification","reference shift within a conversation","speaker meaning independent of sentence meaning"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"(2) The number of planets might have been necessarily even. In a natural use, (2) can be interpreted as true: for example, there might have been exactly eight planets, in which case the number of planets would have been even, and hence necessarily even.","phenomenon":"intermediate scope reading of a description","near":["wide-scope (de re) reading","narrow-scope (de dicto) reading","rigid reading of a definite description"],"avoid":["quantifier scope ambiguity","de dicto vs de re reading","de re / de dicto ambiguity","de dicto vs de re modality","quantifying into a modal context"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"\"Exactly one person (or: some person or other) is drinking champagne in that corner, and I hear he is romantically linked with Jane Smith.\" The circumlocution, in English, expresses the delicacy of the topic, but the speaker's reference (in quite an ordinary sense) may well be clear, even if he in fact is drinking sparkling water.","phenomenon":"existential statement carrying a speaker's reference","aliases":["speaker's reference","speaker’s reference"],"near":["referential use of a definite description","attributive use of a definite description","quantifier domain restriction"],"avoid":["cross-sentential anaphora","cross-sentential (discourse) anaphora","discourse anaphora","E-type pronoun","uniqueness implication of E-type pronouns","specific indefinite","specific use of an indefinite description","speaker meaning independent of sentence meaning","conversational implicature","particularized conversational implicature"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1111/j.1475-4975.1977.tb00045.x"},{"id":"kripke1979-puzzle-belief","title":"A Puzzle about Belief","authors":["Saul Kripke"],"year":"1979","area":"language","topic":"attitude reports & de se","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The absurdities that disquotation plus substitutivity would generate are exactly paralleled by absurdities generated by disquotation plus translation, or even ‘disquotation alone’ (or: disquotation plus homophonic translation).","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Does Pierre, or does he not, believe that London is pretty? … our normal criteria for the attribution of belief lead, when applied to this question, to paradoxes and contradictions.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Everyday principles of belief ascription—disquotation (sincere reflective assent to 'p' shows belief that p) and truth-preserving translation—yield absurd or contradictory verdicts about someone unaware that two names (or two uses of one name) co-refer, without any substitutivity principle.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Cases like Cicero/Tully do not show that coreferential names fail to be intersubstitutable in belief contexts: the same absurdities arise without substitutivity, so such cases strain belief ascription, perhaps to breaking point, and support no Fregean conclusion.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"disquotational principle","aliases":["disquotational","disquotation"],"definition":"where ‘p’ is to be replaced, inside and outside all quotation marks, by any appropriate standard English sentence: “If a normal English speaker, on reflection, sincerely assents to ‘p’, then he believes that p.”","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"strengthened disquotational principle","aliases":["disquotational","biconditional","strengthened"],"definition":"A normal English speaker who is not reticent will be disposed to sincere reflective assent to ‘p’ if and only if he believes that p.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"principle of translation","aliases":["translation"],"definition":"if a sentence of one language expresses a truth in that language, then any translation of it into any other language also expresses a truth (in that other language).","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"Shakespearean","aliases":["Shakespearean"],"definition":"Said of a context in which codesignative proper names are interchangeable salva veritate; weaker than referential transparency in Quine's sense, which also requires coreferential definite descriptions to be interchangeable — modal contexts, for instance, have the property while being referentially opaque.","exact":false}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose Pierre is a normal French speaker who lives in France and speaks not a word of English … he says, in French, “Londres est jolie.” … Later, Pierre … moves to England, in fact to London itself, though to an unattractive part of the city … So he is inclined to assent to the English sentence: (5) London is not pretty. … he does not for a moment withdraw his assent from the French sentence, “Londres est jolie”","phenomenon":"Kripke's puzzle about belief","near":["substitution failure in belief contexts","indeterminacy of translation","Mates's puzzle about synonym substitution"],"avoid":["Frege's puzzle about co-referring names","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","believing of one person that he is two"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Peter … may learn the name ‘Paderewski’ with an identification of the person named as a famous pianist. Naturally, having learned this, Peter will assent to “Paderewski had musical talent,” … Later, in a different circle, Peter learns of someone called ‘Paderewski’ who was a Polish nationalist leader and prime minister. … He concludes that probably two people … were both named ‘Paderewski’. Using ‘Paderewski’ as a name for the statesman, Peter assents to, “Paderewski had no musical talent.”","phenomenon":"Paderewski case (puzzle without translation)","aliases":["Paderewski"],"near":["substitution failure in belief contexts","Mates's puzzle about synonym substitution","reference change of a proper name"],"avoid":["Kripke's puzzle about belief","believing of one person that he is two","Frege's puzzle about informative identity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Tom, a normal speaker of the language, may sincerely assent to “Tully denounced Catiline,” but not to “Cicero denounced Catiline.” He may even deny the latter. And his denial is compatible with his status as a normal English speaker … (without knowing that ‘Cicero’ and ‘Tully’ name the same person). Given this, it seems obvious that Tom believes that: Tully denounced Catiline, but that he does not believe … that: Cicero denounced Catiline.","phenomenon":"substitution failure in belief contexts","near":["substitution failure in modal contexts","Mates's puzzle about synonym substitution","essential indexical"],"avoid":["Kripke's puzzle about belief","Frege's puzzle about identity statements","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","Frege's puzzle about co-referring names","referential opacity","referential opacity (substitution failure)","believing of one person that he is two"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"there are no counterfactual situations in which Hesperus and Phosphorus would have been different. Admittedly, the truth of ‘Hesperus is Phosphorus’ was not known a priori, and may even have been widely disbelieved before appropriate empirical evidence came in.","phenomenon":"necessary a posteriori truth","aliases":["truth"],"near":["contingent a priori truth","analytic truth","substitution failure in modal contexts"],"avoid":["Frege's puzzle about identity statements","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","Frege's puzzle about co-referring names","modal argument against descriptivism"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/978-1-4020-4104-4_13"},{"id":"evans1973-causal-theory-names","title":"The Causal Theory of Names","authors":["Gareth Evans"],"year":"1973","area":"language","topic":"reference & names","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I think we can say that in general a speaker intends to refer to the item that is the dominant source of his associated body of information.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the important causal relation lies between [the referent's] states and doings and the speaker's body of information—not between the item's being dubbed with a name and the speaker's contemporary use","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Names can change their bearers, as 'Madagascar' did, which a pure dubbing-and-transmission chain cannot allow; for non-deferential uses, a name's referent is the dominant causal source of its users' associated information, not whatever best fits that information.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"'NN' names x if, in some community C, it is common knowledge that members use 'NN' to refer to x, success in reference being intended to rely on this, not on x's satisfying a predicate embedded in 'NN'.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"source (of a belief)","aliases":["source"],"definition":"X is the source of the belief S expresses by uttering 'Fa' if there was an episode which caused S's belief in which X and S were causally related in a type of situation apt for producing knowledge that something F-s","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"dominant source","aliases":["dominance","dominant","dominantly"],"definition":"Dominance is not simply a function of amount of information … each man's life presents a skeleton and the dominant source may be the man who contributed to covering most of it rather than the man who contributed most of the covering.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"deferential use","aliases":["deferentially","deferential"],"definition":"sometimes we use [expressions] with the over-riding intention to conform to the use made of them by some other person or persons. In that case I shall say that we use the expression deferentially (with respect to that other person or group of persons).","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"mouthpiece syndrome","aliases":["mouthpiece"],"definition":"what I call the 'mouthpiece syndrome' by which we attach sense and reference to a man's remarks only because we hear someone else speaking through him; as we might with a messenger, carrying a message about matters of which he was entirely ignorant.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"what the speaker denotes","aliases":["denotes"],"definition":"what the name denotes upon a particular occasion of its use when this is understood as being partly determinative of what the speaker strictly and literally said. I shall use the faintly barbarous coinage: what the speaker denotes (upon an occasion) for this notion.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"In the case of 'Madagascar' a hearsay report of Malay or Arab sailors misunderstood by Marco Polo . . . has had the effect of transferring a corrupt form of the name of a portion of the African mainland to the great African Island.","phenomenon":"reference change of a proper name","near":["reference failure","reference borrowing","descriptive name"],"avoid":["speaker's reference diverging from a name's semantic reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Archaeologists might find a tomb in the desert and claim falsely that it is the burial place of some little known character in the Bible. They could discover a great deal about the man in the tomb so that he and not the character in the Bible was the dominant source of their information. But, given the nature and point of their enterprise, … they denote [the biblical character], and say false things about him.","phenomenon":"deferential use of a name","aliases":["deferentially","deferential"],"near":["reference change of a proper name","reference fixed by dominant source","mouthpiece case"],"avoid":["speaker's reference diverging from a name's semantic reference","reference surviving a false associated theory","stability of reference across theory change","causal-historical chain of reference","causal-historical determination of reference","causal theory of reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"I may form the belief about the wife of some colleague that she has nice legs upon the basis of seeing someone else—but the girl I saw is the source.","phenomenon":"misidentification","near":["deferential use of a name","hallucination","reference change of a proper name"],"avoid":["conflation (believing of two persons that they are one)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"we may deduce that there is a tallest man in the world and deduce that he is over 6 feet tall. No man is the source of this information; a name introduced in relation to it might function very much as the unamended Description Theory suggested.","phenomenon":"descriptive name","aliases":["Description Theory"],"near":["empty name","reference borrowing","referential use of a definite description"],"avoid":["contingent a priori truth","attributive use of a proper name","attributive use of a definite description","descriptive name (name introduced by description)","reference fixing by description","singular thought without acquaintance","denotation without reference","merely de dicto (notional) belief"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1093/aristoteliansupp/47.1.187"},{"id":"lewis1979-attitudes-de-dicto","title":"Attitudes De Dicto and De Se","authors":["David Lewis"],"year":"1979","area":"language","topic":"attitude reports & de se","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Some belief and some knowledge cannot be understood as propositional, but can be understood as self-ascription of properties.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"To believe a proposition is to self-ascribe the corresponding property … the property that belongs to all and only the inhabitants of a certain region of logical space.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Take propositions as sets of worlds and properties as sets of possible individuals: believing P is self-ascribing {x : x's world ∈ P}, and some beliefs self-ascribe properties that correspond to no proposition.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Two gods who know every proposition true at their world, yet not which of the two each is, show that some knowledge is not propositional; so properties, not sets of worlds, should be the uniform objects of attitudes.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"belief de se","aliases":["de se","knowledge de se"],"definition":"Self-ascription of properties might suitably be called belief or knowledge de se. My thesis is that the de se subsumes the de dicto, but not vice versa.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"self-locating belief","aliases":["self-locating"],"definition":"I say that all belief is \"self-locating belief.\" Belief de dicto is self-locating belief with respect to logical space; belief irreducibly de se is self-locating belief at least partly with respect to ordinary time and space, or with respect to the population.","exact":true,"avoid":["perry1979-problem-essential-indexical/t2","lewis1979-attitudes-de-dicto/t1"]},{"id":"t3","term":"relation of acquaintance","aliases":["acquaintance","related"],"definition":"I and the one of whom I have beliefs de re are so related that there is an extensive causal dependence of my states upon his; and this causal dependence is of a sort apt for the reliable transmission of information.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"belief de re","aliases":["de re","beliefs de re"],"definition":"A subject ascribes property X to individual Y if and only if the subject ascribes X to Y under some description Z such that either (1) Z captures the essence of Y, or (2) Z is a relation of acquaintance that the subject bears to Y.","exact":true,"avoid":["lewis1979-attitudes-de-dicto/t3"]},{"id":"t5","term":"property","aliases":["properties"],"definition":"The word \"property\" also is used in many senses. I mean a set: the set of exactly those possible beings, actual or not, that have the property in question.","exact":true,"avoid":["lewis1983-new-work-theory/t8"]}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"[The two gods] inhabit a certain possible world, and they know exactly which world it is. Therefore they know every proposition that is true at their world. Insofar as knowledge is a propositional attitude, they are omniscient. Still I can imagine them to suffer ignorance: neither one knows which of the two he is. … Neither one knows whether he lives on the tallest mountain or on the coldest mountain; nor whether he throws manna or thunderbolts.","phenomenon":"irreducibly de se ignorance","aliases":["ignorance"],"near":["temporal self-locating ignorance","logical omniscience problem","Mary's room (knowledge argument)"],"avoid":["self-locating belief","self-locating knowledge","essential indexical","de se belief","de se knowledge","self-locating (de se) uncertainty","locating belief"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"[Heimson] believes he is Hume. Hume believed that too. Hume was right. If Hume believed he was Hume by believing a proposition, that proposition was true. Heimson believes just what Hume did. But Hume and Heimson are worldmates. Any proposition true for Hume is likewise true for Heimson. So Heimson, like Hume, believes he is Hume by believing a true proposition. So he's right. But he's not right. He's wrong, because he believes he's Hume and he isn't.","phenomenon":"false de se belief","near":["Kripke's puzzle about belief","de re (not de se) belief about oneself","Moore's paradox"],"avoid":["de se belief","self-locating belief","essential indexical","same character, different content","misidentification","de re belief","locating belief"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Consider the insomniac. Tonight, as most nights, he lies awake for hours. … He does not keep track of the time. So all through the night he wonders what time it is. To wonder is to lack knowledge, but what relevant knowledge does he lack? Not propositional knowledge; he knows, well enough, what sort of world is his. And not self-ascription of properties to his continuant self; he knows, well enough, what locus through space and time he occupies …","phenomenon":"temporal self-locating ignorance","near":["problem of temporary intrinsics","Mary's room (knowledge argument)","logical omniscience problem"],"avoid":["irreducibly de se ignorance","self-locating belief","self-locating knowledge","essential indexical","de se belief","cognitive dynamics: losing track of time","self-locating (de se) uncertainty","loss of self-locating information","locating belief"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"I watch myself in reflecting glass, unaware that I am watching myself. I ascribe to myself, under the description \"the one I am watching,\" the property of wearing pants that are on fire. … But I do not self-ascribe the property of wearing pants that are on fire. Very soon I will, but not yet.","phenomenon":"de re (not de se) belief about oneself","near":["de se belief","merely de dicto (notional) belief","self-deception"],"avoid":["essential indexical","Frege's puzzle","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","misidentification","de re belief","perceptual de re belief","immunity to error through misidentification","believing of one person that he is two","same content, different character","indexical (demonstrative) reference","self-locating belief","self-locating knowledge"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"[Lingens] has narrowed the possibilities down to two. Perhaps he is in aisle five, floor six, of Main Library, Stanford, in which case the way out is to go downstairs. Or perhaps he is on one of the lower floors in the stacks of Widener, in which case the thing to do is to go up. The books tell him that there are amnesiacs lost in both places, and he has figured out that he is one of the two.","phenomenon":"decision under self-locating uncertainty","near":["Newcomb's problem","Buridan's ass","Ellsberg paradox"],"avoid":["self-locating belief","self-locating knowledge","irreducibly de se ignorance","essential indexical","de se belief","self-locating (de se) uncertainty","locating belief"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2184843"},{"id":"perry1977-frege-demonstratives","title":"Frege on Demonstratives","authors":["John Perry"],"year":"1977","area":"language","topic":"indexicals & demonstratives","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Though senses considered as roles, and thoughts considered as information, cannot be identified, each does its job in a way that meshes with the other.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"To have a thought we need an object and an incomplete sense. The demonstrative in context gives us the one, the rest of the sentence the other.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Indexicals force a split between a sentence's sense and the thought it expresses: context supplies only an object, not a completing sense, and no associated or incommunicable sense can fill the gap.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"An indexical sentence's sense is a role: S(d) uttered in context c is true iff the value of d in c falls under the concept S( ) refers to; the thought is ⟨value, incomplete sense⟩.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"avoid":["perry1979-problem-essential-indexical"],"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"role","aliases":["roles"],"definition":"When we understand a word like “today,” what we seem to know is a rule taking us from an occasion of utterance to a certain object. “Today” takes us to the very day of utterance … I shall call this the role of the demonstrative.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"informationally equivalent","aliases":["informational equivalent","informational equivalents","informationally equivalent thoughts"],"definition":"just those thoughts obtainable by completing the sense of “Russia and Canada quarreled” with a sense completer that determines, as reference, August 1, 1976. I shall call thoughts related in this manner informationally equivalent.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"irrelevancy of belief","definition":"[The sense] I associate with my use of a demonstrative does not determine the thought expressed by a sentence containing that demonstrative.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"nonnecessity of belief","definition":"I can express a thought with “Today is sunny and bright”—that is, say something for which the question of truth arises—whether or not I associate any correct sense at all with “today.” I may have no idea at all what day it is …","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"nonsufficiency of belief","aliases":["sufficient"],"definition":"So with “now,” as with “I,” it is not sufficient to grasp the thought expressed with a demonstrative, to grasp an informational equivalent with a complete sense.","exact":true,"avoid":["perry1977-frege-demonstratives/t4"]}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose I am viewing the harbor from downtown Oakland; the bow and stern of the aircraft carrier Enterprise are visible, though its middle is obscured by a large building. The name “Enterprise” is clearly visible on the bow, so when I tell a visitor, “This is the Enterprise,” pointing towards the bow, this is readily accepted. When I say, pointing to the stern clearly several city blocks from the bow, “That is the Enterprise,” however, she refuses to believe me.","phenomenon":"Frege's puzzle about informative identity","near":["referential use of a definite description","reference failure","de re / de dicto ambiguity"],"avoid":["cognitive significance","same content, different character","informative identity statement","Frege's puzzle about identity statements","Frege's puzzle about co-referring names","believing of one person that he is two","misidentification","perceptual de re belief","substitution failure in belief contexts","referential opacity (substitution failure)","referential opacity","indexical (demonstrative) reference","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Let us imagine David Hume, alone in his study, on a particular afternoon in 1775, thinking to himself, “I wrote the Treatise.” … Now suppose Heimson is a bit crazy and thinks himself to be David Hume. Alone in his study, he says to himself, “I wrote the Treatise.” However much his inner life may, at that moment, resemble Hume’s on that afternoon in 1775, the fact remains: Hume was right, Heimson is wrong.","phenomenon":"same character, different content","near":["same content, different character","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","reference failure"],"avoid":["same role, different thought","de se belief","self-locating belief","essential indexical","false de se belief","misidentification","conflation (believing of two persons that they are one)","self-locating knowledge","irreducibly de se ignorance","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","indexical (demonstrative) reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"An amnesiac, Rudolf Lingens, is lost in the Stanford library. He reads … a biography of himself, and a detailed account of the library in which he is lost. He believes any Fregean thought you think might help him. He still will not know who he is and where he is … until that moment when he is ready to say, This place is aisle five, floor six, of Main Library, Stanford. I am Rudolf Lingens.","phenomenon":"self-locating belief","near":["Mary's room (knowledge argument)","Gettier case","brain-in-a-vat scenario"],"avoid":["locating belief","de se belief","de se knowledge","essential indexical","irreducibly de se ignorance","self-locating (de se) uncertainty","loss of self-locating information","decision under self-locating uncertainty","de re (not de se) belief about oneself","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","Frege's puzzle about identity statements","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","indexical (demonstrative) reference","self-locating knowledge"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"When you and I entertain the sense of “A bear is about to attack me,” we behave similarly. We both roll up in a ball and try to be as still as possible. … When you and I both apprehend the thought that I am about to be attacked by a bear, we behave differently. I roll up in a ball, you run to get help.","phenomenon":"essential indexical","near":["immunity to error through misidentification","Moore's paradox","weakness of will (akrasia)"],"avoid":["de se belief","self-locating belief","self-locating knowledge","locating belief","cognitive significance","same character, different content","same content, different character","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","indexical (demonstrative) reference"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2184564"},{"id":"grice1956-defense-dogma","title":"In Defense of a Dogma","authors":["H. P. Grice","P. F. Strawson"],"year":"1956","area":"language","topic":"analyticity & holism","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"if we are to give up the notion of sentence-synonymy as senseless, we must give up the notion of sentence-significance (of a sentence having meaning) as senseless too.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"If we can make sense of [conceptual revision], then we can perfectly well preserve [the analytic-synthetic distinction], while conceding to Quine the revisability-in-principle of everything we say.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"That 'analytic' and its kin lack non-circular strict definitions cannot show the distinction illusory: agreed application to an open class of cases creates a presumption that it exists, and informal explanations (not believing vs. not understanding) escape the circle.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Universal revisability and confirmation holism are compatible with the analytic–synthetic distinction: giving up a sentence by admitting falsity differs from giving it up through a shift in its words' sense, and confirmational synonymy can be relativized to background assumptions.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"family circle","aliases":["circle","family","family-circle"],"definition":"There is a certain circle or family of expressions, of which \"analytic\" is one, such that if any one member of the circle could be taken to be satisfactorily understood or explained, then other members of the circle could be verbally, and hence satisfactorily, explained in terms of it.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"satisfactory explanation","definition":"Quine requires of a satisfactory explanation of an expression that it should take the form of a pretty strict definition but should not make use of any member of a group of interdefinable terms to which the expression belongs.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"sentence-synonymy","aliases":["synonymous"],"definition":"Two sentences are synonymous if and only if any true answer to the question \"What does it mean?\" asked of one of them, is a true answer to the same question, asked of the other.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"conceptual revision","aliases":["concept","concepts"],"definition":"that kind of giving up which involves changing or dropping a concept or set of concepts. … the case where [a form of words held true comes to be held false] at least partly as a result of a shift in the sense of the words.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"philosopher's paradox","aliases":["philosopher"],"definition":"Instead of examining the actual use that we make of the notion of meaning the same, the philosopher measures it by some perhaps inappropriate standard (in this case some standard of clarifiability), and because it falls short of this standard … denies its reality, declares it illusory.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"(1) \"My neighbor's three-year-old child understands Russell's Theory of Types,\" … (4) \"I don't believe you—the thing's impossible.\" But if the child were then produced, and did (as one knows he would not) expound the theory correctly, answer questions on it, criticize it, and so on, one would in the end be forced to acknowledge that the claim was literally true and that the child was a prodigy.","phenomenon":"natural (causal) impossibility","near":["logical impossibility","metaphysical impossibility","category mistake"],"avoid":["circumstantial (root) modality","dispositional (circumstantial) modality"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"(1') \"My neighbor's three-year-old child is an adult.\" … (3') \"No, I mean what I say,\" … (5') \"No, he's not a freak, he's just an adult.\" At this stage—or possibly if we are patient, a little later—we shall be inclined to say that we just don't understand what Y is saying, and to suspect that he just does not know the meaning of some of the words he is using.","phenomenon":"logical impossibility","near":["natural (causal) impossibility","hyperbole (overstatement)","presupposition failure"],"avoid":["semantic anomaly"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"I do not know whether the statement \"Everything green is extended\" is analytic. … The indecision over \"analytic\" (and equally, in this case, the indecision over \"true\") arises, of course, from a further indecision: viz., that which we feel when confronted with such questions as \"Should we count a point of green light as extended or not?\"","phenomenon":"open texture (indeterminate word boundaries)","near":["indeterminacy of translation","conceptual revision","confirmation holism"],"avoid":["vague predicate"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2182828"},{"id":"carnap1952-meaning-postulates","title":"Meaning Postulates","authors":["Rudolf Carnap"],"year":"1952","area":"language","topic":"analyticity & holism","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Si is L-true in L' [(L plus postulates P)] = Df Si is L-implied by P in L. L-truth in L' is then the explicatum for analyticity.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[System builders] are free to choose their postulates, guided not by their beliefs concerning facts of the world but by their intentions with respect to the meanings","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Where P conjoins stipulated sentences constraining the meanings of the descriptive constants of a language L, a sentence S is analytic (L-true with respect to P) iff P L-implies S, i.e. iff P → S is L-true.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Against Quine's skepticism, analyticity can be explicated for constructed languages: stipulations such as 'no bachelor is married' exclude impossible state-descriptions, and confirmation in the system adopting them equals confirmation in the original system with them added to the evidence.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"meaning postulate","aliases":["meaning postulates","postulate","postulates"],"definition":"A sentence the system's author lays down by decision rather than knowledge, without giving rules of designation, stating as much about the intended meanings of descriptive constants (e.g. that two properties are incompatible) as analyticity requires.","exact":false},{"id":"t2","term":"L-true with respect to P","definition":"Suppose that certain meaning postulates have been accepted for the system L. Let P be their conjunction. Then the concept of analyticity … can now be explicated. … A statement Si in L is L-true with respect to P = Df Si is L-implied by P","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"state-description","aliases":["state-descriptions","state description","state descriptions"],"definition":"A state-description is a conjunction containing for every atomic statement either it or its negation but not both, and no other statements.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"'If Jack is a bachelor, then he is not married' … it is sufficient to understand the statement in order to establish its truth; knowledge of (extra-linguistic) facts is not involved. … [Here the] meanings of some descriptive words are involved, viz., those of 'bachelor' and 'married.'","phenomenon":"non-logical analytic truth","near":["logical truth","synthetic a priori truth","contingent empirical truth"],"avoid":["material inference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"let 'W' be a primitive predicate designating the relation Warmer. Then 'W' is transitive, irreflexive, and hence asymmetric in virtue of its meaning. Therefore the statements 'Wab · Wbc · ~Wac,' 'Wab · Wba,' and 'Waa' are false due to their meanings. The same holds for state-descriptions which contain one of these statements as subconjunctions; hence they do not represent possible cases.","phenomenon":"logical dependence among atomic statements","near":["logical independence of atomic statements","logical dependence among one-place predicates","synonymy of primitive predicates"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Suppose [a system's author] wishes the predicates 'Bl' and 'R' to correspond to the words 'black' and 'raven.' … There is no point for him to make an elaborate study … of common usage, in order to find out whether 'raven' always or mostly entails 'black.' It is rather his task to make up his mind whether he wishes the predicates 'R' and 'Bl' of his system to be used in such a way that the first logically entails the second.","phenomenon":"conventionalism about analyticity","near":["confirmation holism","a posteriori necessity","semantic externalism"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf02350366"},{"id":"dummett1959-truth","title":"Truth","authors":["Michael Dummett"],"year":"1959","area":"language","topic":"theory of meaning & truth conditions","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"We are entitled to say that a statement P must be either true or false … only when P is … an effectively decidable statement.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The sense of a sentence is determined wholly by knowing the case in which it has a designated value and the cases in which it has an undesignated one.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Like winning a game, truth has a point not captured by classifying sentences as true or false: we aim at true assertions. Truth-conditions explain meaning only given an account of assertion, which the redundancy theory cannot supply.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Intuitionism extended to ordinary statements: sense is given by when a statement may be asserted, built from when its constituents may be. Excluded middle then loses its status as a logical law for undecidable statements, without any third truth-value.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"realism","aliases":["realist","realists"],"definition":"Realism consists in the belief that for any statement there must be something in virtue of which either it or its negation is true","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"effectively decidable statement","aliases":["effectively decidable","decidable"],"definition":"P is a statement of such a kind that we could in a finite time bring ourselves into a position in which we were justified either in asserting or in denying P","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"designated value","aliases":["designated","undesignated","designated values","undesignated values"],"definition":"A statement has a designated value in a case if someone who asserts it is not taken as excluding that case, an undesignated one if he is; knowing which cases are which wholly determines its sense.","exact":false},{"id":"t4","term":"redundancy theory","aliases":["redundancy theory of truth"],"definition":"The theory that 'It is true that p if and only if p' and 'It is false that p if and only if not p' give the whole meaning of 'true' and 'false'.","exact":false}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"let us envisage a dispute over the logical validity of the statement ‘Either Jones was brave or he was not’. A imagines Jones to be a man, now dead, who never encountered danger in his life. B retorts that it could still be true that Jones was brave, namely if it is true that if Jones had encountered danger, he would have acted bravely.","phenomenon":"undecidable concealed conditional","near":["undecidable statement of unlimited generality","truth-value gap from reference failure","future contingent"],"avoid":["failure of conditional excluded middle","counterexample to Conditional Excluded Middle","conditional excluded middle","dispositional (circumstantial) modality","subjunctive (counterfactual) conditional","vague predicate"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Consider the statement ‘A city will never be built on this spot’. Even if we have an oracle which can answer every question of the kind, ‘Will there be a city here in 1990?’, ‘In 2100?’, etc., we might never be in a position either to declare the statement true or to declare it false.","phenomenon":"undecidable statement of unlimited generality","near":["undecidable concealed conditional","truth-value gap from reference failure","borderline case of a vague predicate"],"avoid":["future contingent"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"If P turns out true, then ‘If P, then Q’ is said to be true or false according as Q is true or false, while if P turns out false we say that nothing was said true or false.","phenomenon":"conditional assertion","near":["material conditional","strict conditional","subjunctive (counterfactual) conditional"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Let us compare truth and falsity with the winning and losing of a board game. … It is part of the concept of winning a game that a player plays to win … We can imagine a variant of chess in which it is the object of each player to be checkmated, and this would be an entirely different game; but the formal description we imagined would coincide with the formal description of chess.","phenomenon":"truth as the aim of assertion","near":["knowledge norm of assertion","correspondence theory of truth","redundancy theory of truth"],"avoid":["convention of truthfulness","convention of truthfulness and trust","meaning as use","the Frege point (force–content distinction)","institutional fact"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"searle1958-proper-names","title":"Proper Names","authors":["John Searle"],"year":"1958","area":"language","topic":"reference & names","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"referring uses of \"Aristotle\" presuppose the existence of an object of whom a sufficient but so far unspecified number of [the descriptive statements its users accept] are true","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the looseness of the criteria for proper names is a necessary condition for isolating the referring function from the describing function of language","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Referring with a name presupposes, without asserting, that its bearer satisfies a sufficient but unspecified number of the identifying descriptions speakers associate with it; which ones, and how many, is not settled in advance.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Because each name carries a loose cluster of descriptive presuppositions, 'Aristotle never existed' asserts that enough of them are false, and 'Tully = Cicero' is analytic when both names share their presuppositions but can be synthetic when they differ.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"proper name","aliases":["proper names","proper name"],"definition":"[proper names] enable us to refer publicly to objects without being forced to raise issues and come to agreement on what descriptive characteristics exactly constitute the identity of the object. They function not as descriptions, but as pegs on which to hang descriptions.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"conventional presupposition (of a name)","aliases":["presuppose","presupposed","presupposition","presuppositions","conventional presuppositions"],"definition":"To use a proper name referringly is to presuppose the truth of certain uniquely referring descriptive statements, but it is not ordinarily to assert these statements or even to indicate which exactly are presupposed.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"degenerate proper name","aliases":["degenerate proper names","degenerate"],"definition":"For [degenerate proper names like \"The Bank of England\"], it seems the sense is given as straightforwardly as in a definite description; the presuppositions, as it were, rise to the surface.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"(a) \"Tully = Tully\" is analytic. But is (b) \"Tully = Cicero\" synthetic? If so, then each name must have a different sense, which seems at first sight most implausible, for we do not ordinarily think of proper names as having a sense at all in the way that predicates do; we do not, e.g. give definitions of proper names. But of course (b) gives us information not conveyed by (a).","phenomenon":"Frege's puzzle about informative identity","aliases":["information"],"near":["Kripke's puzzle about belief","paradox of analysis","problem of negative existentials"],"avoid":["Frege's puzzle","Frege's puzzle about co-referring names","informative identity statement","cognitive significance","necessary a posteriori truth","Frege's puzzle about identity statements","substitution failure in belief contexts","referential opacity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose, for example, that we teach the name 'Aristotle' by explaining that it refers to a Greek philosopher born in Stagira, and suppose that our student continues to use the name correctly, that he gathers more information about Aristotle, and so on. Let us suppose it is discovered later on that Aristotle was not born in Stagira at all, but in Thebes. We will not now say that the meaning of the name has changed, or that Aristotle did not really exist at all.","phenomenon":"argument from error against descriptivism","near":["reference change of a proper name","modal argument against descriptivism","argument from ignorance against descriptivism"],"avoid":["reference surviving a false associated theory","stability of reference across theory change","causal-historical determination of reference","causal-historical chain of reference","causal theory of reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"if anyone did say that Aristotle did not exist he might mean much more than simply that the name does not denote anyone. If, for example, we challenged his statement by pointing out that a man named \"Aristotle\" lived in Hoboken in 1903, he would not regard this as a relevant countercharge. We say of Cerberus and Zeus that neither of them ever existed, without meaning that no object ever bore these names","phenomenon":"problem of negative existentials","aliases":["exist","existed","existence"],"near":["truth-value gap from reference failure","de re / de dicto ambiguity","referential use of a definite description"],"avoid":["negative existential statements","negative existentials","empty name","predication of non-existent objects","Plato's beard","reference failure","presupposition failure","absence of the referent"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"suppose we agree to drop \"Aristotle\" and use, say, \"the teacher of Alexander\", then it is a necessary truth that the man referred to is Alexander's teacher—but it is a contingent fact that Aristotle ever went into pedagogy","phenomenon":"modal argument against descriptivism","near":["argument from error against descriptivism","epistemic argument against descriptivism","Frege's puzzle about informative identity"],"avoid":["substitution failure in modal contexts","referential opacity","referential opacity (substitution failure)","de re / de dicto ambiguity","de dicto vs de re reading","de dicto vs de re modality","rigid designation"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1093/mind/lxvii.266.166"},{"id":"strawson1964-intention-convention-speech","title":"Intention and Convention in Speech Acts","authors":["P. F. Strawson"],"year":"1964","area":"language","topic":"speech acts & assertion","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Some illocutionary acts are conventional; others are not (except in so far as they are locutionary acts).","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the understanding of the force of an utterance in all cases involves recognizing … an audience-directed intention and recognizing it as wholly overt, as intended to be recognized.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"S non-naturally means something by x only if S intends (i₁) to produce response r in A, (i₂) that A recognize i₁, (i₃) that this recognition be (part of) A's reason for r, and (i₄) that A recognize i₂.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Illocutionary force is conventional only where an utterance conforms to procedural conventions beyond those fixing meaning (verdicts, umpiring, redoubling); warnings, entreaties and objections need no such convention, resting instead on overt, avowable, audience-directed intentions.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"uptake","definition":"The audience's understanding of an utterance's meaning and force, tentatively analyzed as the audience's taking the speaker to have the complex intention that it recognize (and recognize as intended to be recognized) his intention to produce a certain response.","exact":false},{"id":"t2","term":"essential avowability","aliases":["essential"],"definition":"An essential feature of the intentions which make up the illocutionary complex is their overtness. They have, one might say, essential avowability.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"essentially conventional illocutionary act","aliases":["essentially","convention"],"definition":"marrying, redoubling, giving out, pronouncing sentence, bringing in a verdict. Such acts could have no existence outside the rule- or convention-governed practices and procedures of which they essentially form parts.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"explicit performative formula","aliases":["first-person performative verb","explicit"],"definition":"the first-person performative verb … serves not exactly to ascribe an intention to the speaker but rather, in Austin's phrase, to make explicit the type of communication intention with which the speaker speaks, the type of force which the utterance has.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Surely there may be cases in which to utter the words \"The ice over there is very thin\" to a skater is to issue a warning (is to say something with the force of a warning)","phenomenon":"non-conventional (communicative) illocutionary act","near":["conventional (institutional) illocutionary act","perlocutionary act","explicit performative"],"avoid":["non-natural meaning","indirect speech act","conversational implicature","particularized conversational implicature","primary (implicit) performative","implicature from observing the maxim of Relation","R-based implicature","illocutionary vs. perlocutionary distinction"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"[S] arranges convincing-looking \"evidence\" that p … knowing that A is watching him at work, but knowing also that A does not know that S knows that A is watching him at work. He realizes that A will not take the arranged \"evidence\" as genuine or natural evidence that p, but realizes, and indeed intends, that A will take his arranging of it as grounds for thinking that he, S, intends to induce in A the belief that p.","phenomenon":"sneaky intention (counterexample to Gricean meaning)","near":["non-natural meaning","deliberately and openly letting someone know","natural meaning"],"avoid":["covert intention to induce belief","misleading without lying"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"the fact that the word \"guilty\" is pronounced by the foreman of the jury in court at the proper moment constitutes his utterance as the act of bringing in a verdict … if the appropriate umpire pronounces a batsman \"out,\" he thereby performs the act of giving the man out, which no player or spectator shouting \"Out!\" can do.","phenomenon":"conventional (institutional) illocutionary act","near":["non-conventional (communicative) illocutionary act","perlocutionary act","indirect speech act"],"avoid":["verdictive illocution","representative declaration","declaration","performative utterance","institutional fact","essentially conventional illocutionary act","self-fulfilling (self-verifying) performatives"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"A player might let slip the word \"redouble\" without meaning to redouble; but if the circumstances are appropriate and the play strict, then he has redoubled (or he may be held to have redoubled).","phenomenon":"unintentional illocutionary act (forms taking charge)","near":["misfire (void performative act)","abuse (insincere performative)","parasitic (non-serious) use of language"],"avoid":["conventional (institutional) illocutionary act","essentially conventional illocutionary act","performative utterance","explicit performative","declaration"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2183301"},{"id":"lewis1973-languages-language","title":"Languages and Language","authors":["David Lewis"],"year":"1973/1975","area":"language","topic":"interpretation & convention","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"a language ℒ is used by a population P if and only if there prevails in P a convention of truthfulness and trust in ℒ","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[T]he primary sort of conventional coordination in language use [is] that between the action of the truthful speaker and the responsive believing of his trusting hearer.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Languages are functions from strings to sets of possible worlds (σ is true in ℒ at w iff w ∈ ℒ(σ)); a population uses ℒ iff it has a convention of truthfulness and trust in ℒ.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"A population's conventions of truthfulness and trust fix which language—whole-sentence truth conditions—it uses, but cannot favor one grammar generating that language over another; so sub-sentential meaning is only as objective as our standards for a best grammar.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"convention","aliases":["conventions"],"definition":"Conventions are regularities in action, or in action and belief, which are arbitrary but perpetuate themselves because they serve some sort of common interest.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"truthfulness in a language","aliases":["truthful","truthfulness"],"definition":"To be truthful in ℒ is to act in a certain way: to try never to utter any sentences of ℒ that are not true in ℒ. Thus it is to avoid uttering any sentence of ℒ unless one believes it to be true in ℒ.","exact":false},{"id":"t3","term":"trust in a language","aliases":["trusting","trust"],"definition":"To be trusting in ℒ is to form beliefs … to impute truthfulness in ℒ to others, and thus to tend to respond to another's utterance of any sentence of ℒ by coming to believe that the uttered sentence is true in ℒ.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"a language","aliases":["languages"],"definition":"Something which assigns meanings to certain strings of types of sounds or of marks. It could therefore be a function, a set of ordered pairs of strings and meanings.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"language","definition":"A social phenomenon which is part of the natural history of human beings; a sphere of human action, wherein people utter strings of vocal sounds, or inscribe strings of marks, and wherein people respond by thought or action to the sounds or marks …","exact":true},{"id":"t7","term":"language-cluster","aliases":["cluster","clusters","language-clusters"],"definition":"some cluster of similar languages: languages with more or less the same sentences, and more or less the same truth-values for the sentences in worlds close to our actual world, but with increasing divergence in truth-values as we go to increasingly remote, bizarre worlds.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"What is the meaning of the sentence \"Mik Karthee was wise\" in the language of our 137th-century descendants, if all we can find in any of their minds is the inadequate dictionary entry: \"Mik Karthee: controversial American politician of the early atomic age\"? It depends, we might think, partly on which man stands at the beginning of the long causal chain ending in that inadequate dictionary entry.","phenomenon":"causal-historical chain of reference","aliases":["causal chain","causal"],"near":["reference fixing by description","referential use of a definite description","indeterminacy of reference"],"avoid":["causal theory of reference","causal theory of names","argument from ignorance against descriptivism","causal-historical determination of reference","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"A man isolated all his life from others might begin - through genius or a miracle - to use language, say to keep a diary.","phenomenon":"accidentally private language","aliases":["language"],"near":["necessarily private language","language of thought (Mentalese)","radical interpretation"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"If I utter a sentence σ of our language ℒ, you … will conclude that I take σ to be true in ℒ. … But you will draw other conclusions as well, based on your legitimate assumption that it is for some good reason that I chose to utter σ rather than remain silent, and rather than utter any of the other sentences of ℒ that I also take to be true in ℒ.","phenomenon":"conversational implicature","aliases":["conversational"],"near":["conventional implicature","presupposition accommodation","free pragmatic enrichment"],"avoid":["misleading without lying","implicature from observing the maxim of Relation","scalar implicature","R-based implicature","generalized conversational implicature","particularized conversational implicature"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"grice1969-utterers-meaning-intentions","title":"Utterer’s Meaning and Intentions","authors":["H. P. Grice"],"year":"1969","area":"language","topic":"speaker meaning & intention","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"why not make the direct intended effect not that A should think that p, but that A should think that U thinks that p?","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"eliminate potential counterexamples not by requiring U to have certain additional (\"backward-looking\") intentions, but rather by requiring U not to have a certain sort of intention or complex of intentions.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"U meant something by x ↔ ∃A∃r: U intended A to produce r, recognize this intention, and produce r on that basis; ¬∃ inference-element E: U intends A to rely on E yet think U intends A not to.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Since examinees and reminders can mean that p without trying to make anyone believe p, the directly intended response is the hearer's thinking the speaker believes p; imperatives must further aim to make the hearer form an intention.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"utterer's occasion-meaning","definition":"The fourth and last type [of meaning-specification] to be considered involves, instead, the use of indirect speech. … To have meant that he would then be dead, U would have to have committed himself to its being the case that he would then be dead","exact":true,"avoid":["grice1957-meaning/t2"]},{"id":"t2","term":"M-intending","aliases":["M-intended","M-intend"],"definition":"abbreviate \"U utters x intending A: (1) to produce r (2) to think U intends A to produce r (3) to think U intends the fulfillment of (1) to be based on the fulfillment of (2)\" to \"U utters x M-intending that A produce r.\"","exact":true,"avoid":["grice1957-meaning/t2"]},{"id":"t3","term":"anti-deception clause","aliases":["anti-deception","prohibitive clause"],"definition":"there is no inference-element E such that U uttered x intending both (1') that A's determination of r should rely on E and (2') that A should think U to intend that (1') be false.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"activated belief","definition":"\"activated belief\" (that A should be in a state of believing that p and having it in mind that p). … So one who reminds intends the same final response as one who informs, but is intending to remedy a different deficiency.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"purely exhibitive","aliases":["exhibitive"],"definition":"\"purely exhibitive\" utterances (utterances by which the utterer U intends to impart a belief that he [U] has a certain propositional attitude)","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"protreptic","definition":"utterances which are not only exhibitive but also what I might call \"protreptic\" (that is, utterances by which U intends, via imparting the belief that he [U] has a certain propositional attitude, to induce a corresponding attitude in the hearer)","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"A man is playing bridge against his boss. He wants … his boss to know that he wants him to win … when he gets a good hand, he smiles in a certain way; the smile is very like, but not quite like, a spontaneous smile of pleasure. He intends his boss to detect the difference and to argue … \"he hoped I would learn that he has a good hand by taking his smile as a spontaneous give-away.\"","phenomenon":"sneaky intention (counterexample to Gricean meaning)","near":["non-natural meaning","conversational implicature","bluffing"],"avoid":["covert intention to induce belief","deliberately and openly letting someone know","misleading without lying"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Consider the following sentence (S): \"If I shall then be helping the grass to grow, I shall have no time for reading.\" … a particular utterer (U) of S … meant by the words, \"I shall be helping the grass to grow\" … \"I shall then be dead.\" … The words … neither mean nor mean here \"I shall be dead.\" … it would not be true to say that he meant by these words that he would then be dead.","phenomenon":"utterance-type occasion-meaning","near":["applied timeless meaning","utterer's occasion-meaning","timeless meaning"],"avoid":["non-natural meaning","metaphor","dead metaphor","speaker meaning independent of sentence meaning","conversational implicature","particularized conversational implicature","metonymy","substitution view of metaphor"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"The proprietor of a shop … is standing in his doorway in Port Said, sees a British visitor, and in dulcet tones and with an alluring smile says to him the Arabic for \"You pig of an Englishman.\" I should be quite inclined to say that he had meant that the visitor was to come in … I would not … say that he had meant by the words which he uttered that the visitor was to come in","phenomenon":"speaker meaning independent of sentence meaning","near":["verbal irony","conversational implicature","malapropism"],"avoid":["non-natural meaning","non-conventional (communicative) illocutionary act","indirect speech act","indirect request","autonomy of linguistic meaning"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Q: \"When was the Battle of Waterloo\"? A: \"1815\" (\"1816\") Here the examinee meant that the Battle of Waterloo was fought in 1815 (1816) but hardly M-intended to induce a belief to that effect in his examiner. The examiner's beliefs (whatever they may be) are naturally to be thought of by the examinee as independent of candidates' answers.","phenomenon":"purely exhibitive utterance","near":["protreptic utterance","reminding","conversational implicature"],"avoid":["non-natural meaning","violation of the non-redundancy (informativity) constraint on assertion","autonomy of linguistic meaning"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2184179"},{"id":"fine1975-vagueness-truth-logic","title":"Vagueness, Truth and Logic","authors":["Kit Fine"],"year":"1975","area":"language","topic":"vagueness & gradability","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the super-truth theory, that a vague sentence is true if it is true for all admissible and complete specifications.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"an adequate account of penumbral connection appears to require that the logic [for vague sentences] be classical.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Truth is super-truth: t ⊨ A iff u ⊨ A for every complete admissible u ≥ t (u extending t), A is true simpliciter iff true at the base point, and validity and consequence stay exactly classical.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Three-valued truth-functional semantics cannot respect penumbral connections ('the blob is pink and red' is false though both conjuncts are indefinite), so a vague language should be evaluated across its admissible precisifications, securing excluded middle despite truth-value gaps.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"avoid":["vanfraassen1966-singular-terms-truth"],"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"penumbral connection","aliases":["penumbral connection","penumbral truths","penumbral","penumbra"],"definition":"Let us refer to the possibility that logical relations hold among indefinite sentences as penumbral connection … If language is like a tree, then penumbral connection is the seed from which the tree grows.","exact":false},{"id":"t2","term":"super-truth","aliases":["super-truth","supertruth"],"definition":"A sentence is true simpliciter if and only if it is true at the appropriate specification-point, i.e. at all complete and admissible specifications. Truth is super-truth, truth from above.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"intensional vagueness","aliases":["intensionally vague","intensional vagueness"],"definition":"A predicate F is extensionally vague if it has borderline cases, intensionally vague if it could have borderline cases. Thus 'bald' is extensionally vague, I presume, and remains intensionally vague in a world of hairy or hairless men.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"truth-value shift","aliases":["truth-value shift","shifts","shift"],"definition":"[One cause of the temptation to call the second sorites premiss true is] what one might call truth-value shift. This also lies behind LEM. Thus A ∨ ¬A holds in virtue of a truth that shifts from disjunct to disjunct for different complete specifications","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"higher-order vagueness","aliases":["higher-order vagueness","higher-order","higher order"],"definition":"The vague may itself be vague, or vaguely vague, and so on. … Thus higher-order vagueness can be expressed in the material mode, with the help of the definitely-operator, or in the formal mode, with the help of the truth-predicate.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose that a certain blob is on the border of pink and red and let P be the sentence 'the blob is pink' and R the sentence 'the blob is red'. Then the conjunction P & R is false since the predicates 'is pink' and 'is red' are contraries. But on the maximizing account the conjunction P & R is indefinite since both of the conjuncts P and R are indefinite.","phenomenon":"penumbral connection","near":["higher-order vagueness","sorites paradox","truth-value glut"],"avoid":["open texture (indeterminate word boundaries)","logical dependence among atomic statements"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"let J be the ambiguous sentence 'John went to the bank'; let J₁ and J₂ be its disambiguations, viz. 'John went to the money bank' and 'John went to the river bank'; and suppose that John is after fish rather than money. Then the disjunction J ∨ ¬J is true, for its disambiguations, J₁ ∨ ¬J₁ and J₂ ∨ ¬J₂, are true. However, neither disjunct is true, for each disjunct has a false disambiguation.","phenomenon":"excluded middle without bivalence","near":["truth-value glut","presupposition failure","failure of non-contradiction"],"avoid":["truth-value shift","truth-value gap","truth-value gap from reference failure","supervaluation","lexical ambiguity","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","homonymy (lexical ambiguity)","ambiguity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Suppose that the meaning of the natural number predicate nice₁ is given by the following clauses: (a) n is nice₁ if n > 15; (b) n is not nice₁ if n < 13.","phenomenon":"vague predicate","aliases":["predicate"],"near":["ambiguous predicate","highly general predicate","undecidable predicate"],"avoid":["open texture (indeterminate word boundaries)","open texture","semantic underdetermination (completion)","semantic underdetermination","borderline case","truth-value gap"],"exact":false},{"id":"e5","text":"suppose that James has a few fewer hairs on his head than his friend Herbert. Then he may well be a borderline case of a borderline case or a borderline case of a borderline case of a borderline case of a bald man.","phenomenon":"higher-order vagueness","near":["sorites paradox","multidimensional vagueness","ontic vagueness"],"avoid":["open texture (indeterminate word boundaries)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e6","text":"Suppose I press my hand against my eyes and 'see stars'. Then LEM should hold for the sentence S = 'I see many stars', if it is taken as a vague description of a precise experience. However, LEM should fail for S if it is taken as a precise description of an intrinsically vague experience.","phenomenon":"ontic vagueness","aliases":["intrinsically vague"],"near":["higher-order vagueness","vagueness as ignorance","penumbral connection"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00485047"},{"id":"langton1993-speech-acts-unspeakable","title":"Speech Acts and Unspeakable Acts","authors":["Rae Langton"],"year":"1993","area":"language","topic":"speech acts & assertion","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Some speech acts are unspeakable for women in some contexts: although the appropriate words can be uttered, those utterances fail to count as the actions they were intended to be.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The claims that pornography subordinates and silences women make perfect sense … Moreover, if pornographic speech is indeed authoritative, the claims may well be true.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Understood in terms of its illocutionary force, pornography can subordinate women, ranking them as sex objects and legitimating sexual violence, and can silence them by disabling illocutions like refusal and protest, provided pornographers' speech is authoritative.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Silencing need not stop anyone uttering words: authoritative speech such as pornography can set the felicity conditions for others' speech acts, so that a woman's 'no' fails to secure uptake and does not count as refusal.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"illocutionary disablement","aliases":["illocutionary","disablement","disabled"],"definition":"silencing that happens when one speaks, one utters words, and fails not simply to achieve the effect one aims at, but fails to perform the very action one intends. … although the appropriate words are uttered, with the appropriate intention, the speaker fails to perform the intended illocutionary act.","exact":true,"avoid":["austin1961-performative-utterances/t3"]},{"id":"t2","term":"perlocutionary frustration","aliases":["frustrated","frustration"],"definition":"where a speaker says words, succeeds in performing the intended illocution, but fails to achieve the intended effect","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"authoritative illocutions","aliases":["authority","authoritative illocution"],"definition":"actions whose felicity conditions require that the speaker occupy a position of authority in a relevant domain","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"illocutionary act of subordination","aliases":["subordinate","subordinates","subordinating"],"definition":"speech can subordinate when it has a certain verdictive and exercitive force: when it unfairly ranks members of a social group as inferior, when it legitimates discriminatory behavior towards them, and when it unjustly deprives them of some important powers.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"exercitive","aliases":["exercitives"],"definition":"illocutions that confer powers and rights on people, or deprive people of powers and rights. Actions of ordering, permitting, prohibiting, authorizing, enacting law, and dismissing an employee are illocutions of this kind","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Consider this utterance: \"Blacks are not permitted to vote.\" Imagine that it is uttered by a legislator in Pretoria in the context of enacting legislation that underpins apartheid. It is a locutionary act: by \"Blacks\" it refers to blacks. It is a perlocutionary act: it will have the effect, among others, that blacks stay away from polling booths. But it is, first and foremost, an illocutionary act: it makes it the case that blacks are not permitted to vote.","phenomenon":"subordinating speech act","near":["depiction of subordination","perlocutionary frustration","commissive illocutionary act"],"avoid":["exercitive","exercitive speech act","authoritative illocution","verdictive illocution","declaration","representative declaration","conventional (institutional) illocutionary act","performative utterance","explicit performative","institutional fact","directive speech act","perlocutionary act","illocutionary vs. perlocutionary distinction","illocutionary silencing","illocutionary disablement","self-fulfilling (self-verifying) performatives","one action under different descriptions"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"An umpire calls \"Fault\" at a tennis match. He expresses his opinion. He describes the world as he sees it. But he does much more than that: he gives his verdict. A bystander says \"Fault.\" He expresses his opinion. He describes the world as he sees it. What he says has just the same content as what the umpire says: they perform the same locutionary act. But the bystander's utterance makes no difference to the score. The umpire's does.","phenomenon":"verdictive illocution","aliases":["verdict"],"near":["commissive illocutionary act","behabitive illocutionary act","abuse (insincere performative)"],"avoid":["representative declaration","declaration","conventional (institutional) illocutionary act","institutional fact","performative utterance","explicit performative","exercitive","authoritative illocution","expositive illocutionary act","representative (assertive) speech act","constative utterance","the Frege point (force–content distinction)","preparatory condition (speaker–hearer status)","self-fulfilling (self-verifying) performatives"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Sometimes \"no,\" when spoken by a woman, does not count as the act of refusal. The hearer fails to recognize the utterance as a refusal; uptake is not secured. In saying \"no\" she may well intend to refuse. By saying \"no\" she intends to prevent sex, but she is far from doing as she intends.","phenomenon":"illocutionary silencing","near":["perlocutionary frustration","simple (locutionary) silencing","abuse (insincere performative)"],"avoid":["misfire (void performative act)","illocutionary misfire","illocutionary disablement","misunderstanding (failure of uptake)","failure of uptake","illocutionary vs. perlocutionary distinction"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Uptake is secured. In saying \"no,\" she really does refuse. By saying \"no,\" she intends to prevent her hearer from continuing his advances. But the hearer goes ahead and forces sex on the woman. She prohibits, but he fails to obey. She fails to achieve the goal of her refusal.","phenomenon":"perlocutionary frustration","near":["illocutionary silencing","simple (locutionary) silencing","misfire (void performative act)"],"avoid":["illocutionary vs. perlocutionary distinction"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"Ordeal is an act of protest, a resounding denunciation of the industry in which Marchiano says she was forced to perform. … Why then is Ordeal in a mail-order catalog for adult reading? … here, in this context, for these intended hearers, the uptake secured is bound to be that of pornography. Marchiano says the words appropriate for an act of protest. … She intends to protest. But her speech misfires.","phenomenon":"illocutionary silencing","near":["perlocutionary frustration","simple (locutionary) silencing","abuse (insincere performative)"],"avoid":["misfire (void performative act)","illocutionary misfire","illocutionary disablement","misunderstanding (failure of uptake)","failure of uptake","depiction of subordination","parasitic (non-serious) use of language"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"williamson1996-knowing-asserting","title":"Knowing and Asserting","authors":["Timothy Williamson"],"year":"1996","area":"language","topic":"speech acts & assertion","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The rule of assertion is the knowledge rule; one must not assert that P unless one knows that P.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"One can think of the knowledge rule as giving the condition on which a speaker has the authority to make an assertion.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Assertion is individuated by a single constitutive rule, must(assert p → Kp), the conditional inside the scope of 'must' but outside that of 'assert'; since Kp → p, the truth norm is a mere corollary.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Lottery cases show no probability short of 1 warrants flat-out assertion; 'How do you know?' challenges and Moorean 'P and I do not know that P' confirm a knowledge requirement; belief-based rules cannot explain why false assertions are faulty.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"knowledge rule","aliases":["knowledge rules"],"definition":"One must: assert that P only if one knows that P.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"constitutive rule","aliases":["Constitutive rules","constitutive rules","constitutive"],"definition":"Constitutive rules are not conventions. … a rule will count as constitutive of an act only if it is essential to that act: necessarily, the rule governs every performance of the act.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"warrant","aliases":["Warrant","warrants","warranted"],"definition":"'Warrant' is used here as a term of art, for that evidential condition (if any) which plays the role of the condition that C(P) in the correct simple account of assertion.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"RBK rule","aliases":["RBK"],"definition":"One must: assert that P only if one rationally believes that one knows that P.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"factiveness","aliases":["factive"],"definition":"A way of having warrant to assert that P is factive just in case a necessary condition of having warrant to assert that P in that way is that P.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose that you have bought a ticket in a very large lottery. Only one ticket wins. Although the draw has been held, the result has not yet been announced. In fact, your ticket did not win, but I have no inside information to that effect. On the merely probabilistic grounds that your ticket was only one of very many, I assert to you flat-out, \"Your ticket did not win,\" … Intuitively, my grounds are quite inadequate for that outright unqualified assertion","phenomenon":"lottery case","aliases":["lottery"],"near":["preface paradox","Moore's paradox","fake barn case"],"avoid":["lottery proposition","lottery paradox","lottery problem","Gettier case"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Something is wrong with any assertion of the form \"P and I do not know that P,\" even though such assertions would often be true if made.","phenomenon":"Moore's paradox","near":["epistemic contradiction","preface paradox","concessive knowledge attribution"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"It looks exactly as it would if there were snow outside, but in fact that white stuff is not snow but foam put there by a film crew of whose existence I have no idea. I do not know that there is snow outside, because there is no snow outside, but it is quite reasonable for me to believe … that I know that there is … Surely it is then reasonable for me to assert that there is snow outside.","phenomenon":"blameless (excusable) norm violation","near":["Gettier case","selfless assertion","lottery case"],"avoid":["excusing condition","the justification–truth distinction"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"When I have a genuine proof, expert mathematicians may tell me falsely that it contains a fallacy. … I may recall other occasions on which what I believed for broadly similar reasons to be a proof really did turn out to be fallacious. In such cases, it would be unreasonable for me to assert that P, for it is unreasonable for me to believe that I have warrant to assert that P.","phenomenon":"higher-order evidence","near":["Gettier case","blameless (excusable) norm violation","selfless assertion"],"avoid":["undercutting (undermining) defeater","dogmatism paradox","peer disagreement","defeat by misleading higher-order evidence"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2998423"},{"id":"karttunen1973-presuppositions-compound-sentences","title":"Presuppositions of Compound Sentences","authors":["Lauri Karttunen"],"year":"1973","area":"language","topic":"presupposition & projection","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"an adequate solution to the projection problem makes it necessary to distinguish between three different types of complementizable predicates, which I will informally call \"plugs\", \"holes\", and \"filters\".","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The most important aspect of [my] filtering conditions is that the cancelling of presuppositions depends on the semantic relation between the sentences involved, not on their actual truth values.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A complex sentence's presuppositions cannot be computed from its clauses alone: whether a connective cancels a clause's presupposition depends on which background assumptions are in play, so presupposition must be relativized to a linguistic context.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Relative to assumed facts X, 'if A then B', 'A and B' and 'A or B' inherit every presupposition of A, and a presupposition C of B unless X ∪ {A} ⊨ C (for 'or', X ∪ {¬A} ⊨ C).","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"plug","aliases":["plugs"],"definition":"predicates which block off all the presuppositions of the complement sentence; … verbs that are commonly called \"verbs of saying\" or \"performatives\", such as say, mention, tell, ask, promise, warn, request, order, accuse","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"hole","aliases":["holes"],"definition":"predicates which let all the presuppositions of the complement sentence become presuppositions of the matrix sentence","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"filter","aliases":["filters"],"definition":"predicates which, under certain conditions, cancel some of the presuppositions of the complement","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"filtering conditions","aliases":["filtering condition"],"definition":"Assuming that B presupposes C (relative to X), a sentence of the form \"If A then B\" or \"A and B\" need not have this presupposition (relative to X) provided that C is entailed by the set which results from adding A to X.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"If Jack has children, then all of Jack's children are bald. [Here] the consequent clause presupposes that Jack has children. Nevertheless, the conditional as a whole has no such presupposition.","phenomenon":"presupposition filtering","aliases":["presupposition"],"near":["presupposition accommodation","presupposition failure","presupposition plugging by a verb of saying"],"avoid":["filtering relative to background assumptions","anaphoric presupposition"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Bill ordered Fred to stop beating Zelda. … assume that Bill mistakenly believed that Fred was beating Zelda and ordered him to stop. In reporting on what Bill did, the speaker does not have to commit himself to the false belief which led Bill to issue his order.","phenomenon":"presupposition plugging by a verb of saying","near":["presupposition filtering","presupposition projection through holes","presupposition accommodation"],"avoid":["presupposition failure"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"\"Cecilia knew that it surprised Mary that Fred hesitated to stop beating his wife.\" From this one can conclude that Fred has a wife, that he has been beating her, that he hesitated to stop, and that this surprised Mary, but nothing about whether he stopped.","phenomenon":"presupposition projection through holes","near":["presupposition plugging by a verb of saying","presupposition filtering","presupposition accommodation"],"avoid":["factive presupposition"],"exact":false},{"id":"e4","text":"Suppose that our speaker, Fred, believes that Mormons are required to wear a special kind of undergarment, called \"holy underwear\". … Furthermore, Fred has some reason to believe that a certain girl, Geraldine, is a Mormon. … One evening, Fred manages to peer into Geraldine's bedroom and sees her in an ordinary bra and a pair of panties. Being somewhat surprised Fred utters … Either Geraldine is not a Mormon or she has given up wearing her holy underwear.","phenomenon":"filtering relative to background assumptions","near":["presupposition projection through holes","presupposition failure","presupposition-cancelling external negation"],"avoid":["presupposition filtering"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"I ask you to stop beating Zelda. In order to count as a sincere utterance, [this] requires that it be part of the linguistic context that the addressee has been beating Zelda.","phenomenon":"leaky plug","near":["presupposition plugging by a verb of saying","presupposition filtering","presupposition accommodation"],"avoid":["presupposition projection through holes","explicit performative","performative utterance"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"heim1983-projection-problem-presuppositions","title":"On the Projection Problem for Presuppositions","authors":["Irene Heim"],"year":"1983","area":"language","topic":"presupposition & projection","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"we have reduced two seemingly independent semantic properties, the content and the heritage property, to just one, the [context change potential].","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"admittance conditions are conditions on the definedness of the [context change potential], i.e. that c+S is defined iff c admits S.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Projection needs no separate lexical stipulation: a connective's context change potential, motivated by truth conditions alone, fixes each constituent's local context, and a context admits a complex sentence just in case each constituent's presupposition is entailed by its local context.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"c + If A, B = c \\ (c+A \\ c+A+B), with c+S defined iff c admits S; hence c admits 'If A, B' only if c admits A and c+A admits B.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"context change potential","aliases":["CCP","CCPs","context change","context"],"definition":"What are CCPs? Intuitively, they are instructions specifying certain operations of context change. The CCP of \"It is raining\", for instance, is the instruction to conjoin the current context with the proposition that it is raining.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"heritage property","definition":"[Lexical information that] … concerns the item's permeability for the presuppositions of its arguments. E.g. … \"if\" lets through the full presupposition of its left argument, as well as as much of the presupposition of its right argument as doesn't follow from the left argument.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"context","aliases":["contexts"],"definition":"Technically, files and, I suggest, contexts can be identified, with properties of sequences of individuals, i.e. with sets of pairs (g, w), where g is a sequence of individuals … and w is a world.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"global accommodation","aliases":["global"],"definition":"The \"global\" option: Amend c to c&p and, instead of c + Not S, calculate c&p + Not S. … [This] is more like pretending that c&p obtained instead of c all along (hence the word \"global\").","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"local accommodation","aliases":["local"],"definition":"The \"local\" option: Amend c to c&p so that you can calculate c&p + S instead of c + S. … [This] is rather like adjusting the context only for the immediate purpose of evaluating the constituent sentence S (hence \"local\").","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"If the king has a son, the king's son is bald.","phenomenon":"presupposition filtering","near":["local accommodation","presupposition failure","proviso problem"],"avoid":["presupposition projection","local satisfaction of presuppositions","anaphoric presupposition"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"If John has children, then Mary will not like his twins. … [This] is slightly strange, at least out of context. It somehow suggests that it is a matter of course that someone with children will have twins among them.","phenomenon":"conditional presupposition","near":["presupposition cancellation","local accommodation","presupposition failure"],"avoid":["the proviso problem","proviso problem","global accommodation","presupposition filtering","presupposition projection"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"The king of France didn't come. … uttered in a context which is compatible with France having no king. … the speaker continues [it] with \"because France doesn't have a king\".","phenomenon":"local accommodation","near":["global accommodation","presupposition filtering","presupposition projection through holes"],"avoid":["presupposition cancellation","metalinguistic negation","presupposition-denying (external) negation","presupposition failure","reference failure"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"A fat man was pushing his bicycle. [Karttunen and Peters's] rules assign to [it] a presupposition that they admit is too weak: that some fat man had a bicycle. On the other hand, a universal presupposition that every fat man had a bicycle would be too strong. What one would like to predict is, vaguely speaking, a presupposition to the effect that the same fat man that verifies the content of [the sentence] had a bicycle.","phenomenon":"binding problem for presuppositions","aliases":["presupposition"],"near":["donkey anaphora","universal presupposition projection","presupposition filtering"],"avoid":["the binding problem"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"karttunen1974-presupposition-linguistic-context","title":"Presupposition and Linguistic Context","authors":["Lauri Karttunen"],"year":"1974","area":"language","topic":"presupposition & projection","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Do not ask what the [presuppositions] of a complex sentence are, ask what it takes to satisfy them.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Context X satisfies-the-presuppositions-of S just in case the presuppositions of each of the constituent sentences in S are satisfied by the corresponding local context.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"A theory of presupposition should constrain successive contexts, not assign presuppositions to compound sentences: a context admits a sentence when each clause's presuppositions are satisfied by its local context, and hearers tacitly extend contexts that fall short.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A context X admits a simple sentence iff X entails its basic presuppositions; X admits 'If A then B' (or 'A and B') iff X admits A and X ∪ {A} admits B.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"local context","aliases":["context"],"definition":"In compound sentences, the initial context is incremented in a left-to-right fashion giving for each constituent sentence a local context that must satisfy its presuppositions.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"admittance","aliases":["satisfies-the-presuppositions-of","satisfaction","admits"],"definition":"Context X satisfies-the-presuppositions-of A just in case X entails all of the basic presuppositions of A … For compound sentences we define satisfaction recursively by associating each part of the sentence with a different context.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"context","definition":"a set of logical forms that describe the set of background assumptions, that is, whatever the speaker chooses to regard as being shared by him and his intended audience","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"fully explicit discourse","definition":"In a fully explicit discourse, the presuppositions of the next sentence uttered are satisfied by the current context.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"(a) If Dean told the truth, Nixon is guilty too. (b) If Haldeman is guilty, Nixon is guilty too. … the consequent clause \"Nixon is guilty too\" is interpreted in the sense in which it presupposes the guilt of someone else. The question is: does the compound sentence as a whole carry that presupposition? In the case of [(a)], the answer seems to be definitely yes, in the case … of [(b)] definitely no","phenomenon":"presupposition filtering","aliases":["presupposition"],"near":["presupposition accommodation","presupposition failure","presupposition plugging by a verb of saying"],"avoid":["presupposition projection","local satisfaction of presuppositions","presupposition cancellation","incremental (left-to-right) presupposition filtering","conditional presupposition","the proviso problem","proviso problem","anaphoric presupposition"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"We regret that children cannot accompany their parents to commencement exercises. … [It] presupposes that its complement is true. Yet the sentence could readily be used in a conversational context that does not satisfy this presupposition. Perhaps the whole point of uttering [it] is to let it be known that parents should not bring their kids along.","phenomenon":"presupposition accommodation","aliases":["presupposition"],"near":["presupposition failure","presupposition cancellation","presupposition filtering"],"avoid":["factive presupposition","informative presupposition","global accommodation","indirect speech act","indirect request","defective context","conversational implicature","particularized conversational implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Ziegler announced that Nixon will stop protecting his aides. [This sentence] can be spoken felicitously, perhaps even truly, no matter what the facts are understood to be or whether anyone is supposed to hold a set of beliefs that satisfy the presuppositions of the complement.","phenomenon":"presupposition plugging by a verb of saying","aliases":["presuppositions"],"near":["presupposition projection under attitude verbs","presupposition projection through holes","presupposition filtering"],"avoid":["presupposition plug","presupposition plugging","leaky plug"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"John fears that Nixon will stop protecting his aides. To satisfy the presuppositions of [this sentence], a context must ascribe to John a set of beliefs that satisfy-the-presuppositions-of \"Nixon will stop protecting his aides\".","phenomenon":"presupposition projection under attitude verbs","aliases":["presuppositions"],"near":["presupposition plugging by attitude verbs","presupposition projection through holes","presupposition plugging by a verb of saying"],"avoid":["presupposition projection","the proviso problem","proviso problem","projection of a change-of-state presupposition","presupposition accommodation","local accommodation"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1515/thli.1974.1.1-3.181"},{"id":"lewis1976-probabilities-conditionals-conditional","title":"Probabilities of Conditionals and Conditional Probabilities","authors":["David Lewis"],"year":"1976","area":"language","topic":"conditionals","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"if a class of probability functions is closed under conditionalizing, then there can be no probability conditional for that class unless the class consists entirely of trivial probability functions.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The quest for a probability conditional is futile, and we must admit that assertability does not go by absolute probability in the case of indicative conditionals.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"If P(A→C) = P(C|A) for all P in a class closed under conditionalizing, then whenever P(A∧C), P(A∧¬C) > 0: P(A→C|C) = 1 and P(A→C|¬C) = 0, so by cases P(C|A) = P(C), absurd unless P is trivial.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Indicative conditionals are assertable to the degree P(C|A), but that cannot be their probability of truth; better to treat them as material conditionals whose probability, minus a Gricean penalty for mostly vacuous truth, equals P(C|A).","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"probability conditional","aliases":["probability"],"definition":"→ is interpreted in such a way that, for some particular probability function P, and for any sentences A and C, … P(A → C) = P(C/A), if P(A) is positive","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"trivial language","aliases":["language"],"definition":"If an interpreted language cannot provide three possible but pairwise incompatible sentences, then we may justly call it a trivial language.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"imaging","aliases":["image"],"definition":"the image on A of a probability function is formed by shifting the original probability of each world W over to W_A, the closest A-world to W. Probability is moved around but not created or destroyed","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"probability-revision conditional","aliases":["revision","probability"],"definition":"the connective → is interpreted in such a way that … P(A → C) = P_A(C), if A is possible, where P_A is (in some sense) the minimal revision of P that raises the probability of A to 1","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"We are gathering mushrooms; I say to you \"You won't eat that one and live.\" A dirty trick: I thought that one was safe and especially delicious, I wanted it myself, so I hoped to dissuade you from taking it … I thought it highly probable that my trick would work, that you would not eat the mushroom, and therefore that I would turn out to have told the truth.","phenomenon":"misleading without lying","near":["lying","bullshit (indifference to truth)","Moore's paradox"],"avoid":["conversational implicature","particularized conversational implicature","generalized conversational implicature","covert intention to induce belief"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"I'll probably flunk, and it doesn't matter whether I study; I'll flunk if I do and I'll flunk if I don't.","phenomenon":"non-interference conditional","near":["biscuit conditional","subjunctive (counterfactual) conditional","Dutchman conditional"],"avoid":["concessive conditional","even-if conditional","lazy argument for fatalism","idle argument"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2184045"},{"id":"kaplan1968-quantifying","title":"Quantifying In","authors":["David Kaplan"],"year":"1968","area":"language","topic":"attitude reports & de se","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"α represents x to Ralph … if and only if (i) α denotes x, (ii) α is a name of x for Ralph, and (iii) α is (sufficiently) vivid.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"According to my analysis, Ralph must have quite a solid conception of x before we can say that Ralph believes x to be a spy.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Relational belief, primitive for Quine, is defined from dyadic belief in sentences: Ralph Bel('x is a spy', y) iff ∃α[R(α, y, Ralph) ∧ Ralph B ⌜α is a spy⌝], α denoting y, being of y for Ralph, and vivid.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Against Quine, expressions in opaque contexts are treated Frege's way, as denoting intermediate entities; de re necessity and belief then need privileged names: standard names (numerals) for abstract objects, vivid causally grounded names for perceptible individuals.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"intermediate occurrence","aliases":["intermediate occurrences","intermediate contexts","intermediate","occurrence"],"definition":"[Uses of 'nine' in quotation, modal and belief contexts] diverge from the paradigm of vulgar occurrence (they fail the substitution test, the existential generalization test …), but they are not … clearly orthographic accidents either: for in them, the meaning of 'nine' seems, somehow, relevant.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"standard name","aliases":["standard names","standard"],"definition":"A standard name is one whose denotation is fixed on logical, or perhaps I should say linguistic, grounds alone. Numerals and quotation names are prominent among the standard names.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"descriptive content","definition":"Returning now to names, it is their descriptive content that determines what if anything they denote. Thus, denotation is the analogue for names to resemblance for pictures.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"genetic character","definition":"The genetic character of a name in a given person's usage will account for how he acquired the name … It is the genetic character of the name that determines what if anything it is a name of","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"vivid name","aliases":["vivid names","vividness","vivid"],"definition":"the conglomeration of images, names, and partial descriptions which Ralph employs to bring x before his mind … when suitably arranged and regimented, is what I call a vivid name. … it depends only on Ralph's current mental state, and ignores all links whether by resemblence or genesis with the actual world.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"someone other than Ortcutt, say Wyman, is introduced to Ralph as Ortcutt. Suppose that the introduction took place with intent to deceive and that Fred, who made the introduction, acquired the name 'Ortcutt' as a name of Ortcutt. Clearly we should count 'Ortcutt' as a name of Wyman for Ralph, but also, through Fred, as a name of Ortcutt.","phenomenon":"conflation (believing of two persons that they are one)","near":["believing of one person that he is two","empty name","referential use of a definite description"],"avoid":["misidentification","speaker's reference diverging from a name's semantic reference","reference change of a proper name","deferential use of a name","causal-historical chain of reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Believing that spies differ widely in height, Ralph believes that one among them is shortest. Thus, … Ralph [believes] 'the shortest spy is a spy'. Supposing that there is in fact one shortest spy … we would not expect the interest of [the F.B.I.] to be piqued by Ralph's conviction that no two spies share a size.","phenomenon":"merely de dicto (notional) belief","near":["de re belief","de se belief","failure of substitutivity"],"avoid":["exportation from de dicto to de re","attributive use of a definite description","de dicto vs de re reading","de re / de dicto ambiguity","denotation without reference","singular thought without acquaintance","quantifier scope ambiguity","de re ascription of a de dicto attitude","failure of existential generalization","vividness requirement on de re belief"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"if we were to drop clause (iii), and allow any name which both denotes x and is a name of x to represent x to Holmes, then after Holmes observed the victim, 'the murderer' would represent the murderer to him. … There is someone whom Holmes believes to be the murderer. But this last should presage an arrest and not the mere certification of homicide.","phenomenon":"vividness requirement on de re belief","near":["genetic (causal) requirement on de re belief","denotation requirement on de re belief","standard-name requirement on de re necessity"],"avoid":["exportation from de dicto to de re","attributive use of a definite description","singular thought without acquaintance","denotation without reference","de dicto vs de re reading","de re / de dicto ambiguity","merely de dicto (notional) belief","de re ascription of a de dicto attitude","failure of existential generalization"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"I am unwilling to adopt any theory of proper names which … permits me to perform a dubbing in absentia, as by solemnly declaring \"I hereby dub the first child to be born in the twenty-second century 'Newman 1'\", and thus grant myself standing to have beliefs about that as yet unborn child.","phenomenon":"descriptive name","near":["standard name","vivid name","referential use of a definite description"],"avoid":["singular thought without acquaintance","reference fixing by description","denotation without reference","singular (Russellian) proposition","attributive use of a proper name","descriptive name (name introduced by description)","contingent a priori truth"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"had a photograph of Julius Caesar been xeroxed, and the xerox copy televised to a monastery, where it was copied by a monk, and so was reproduced down through the ages, I would call the resulting copy, no matter how distorted, no matter who, if anyone, it resembled, a picture of Julius Caesar.","phenomenon":"causal-historical chain of reference","near":["resemblance-based (descriptivist) reference","vividness of a representation","standard name"],"avoid":["causal theory of reference","causal-historical determination of reference","argument from error against descriptivism","reference surviving a false associated theory","stability of reference across theory change","accidental satisfier of a name's descriptions"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00568057"},{"id":"burge1977-belief-de-re","title":"Belief De Re","authors":["Tyler Burge"],"year":"1977","area":"language","topic":"attitude reports & de se","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"A de re belief is a belief whose correct ascription places the believer in an appropriate nonconceptual, contextual relation to objects the belief is about.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"de re belief is in important ways more fundamental than the de dicto variety; and this can be seen if one attends to its role in basic cognitive activities.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Formally, de dicto belief relates a believer to what a closed sentence expresses, Bd(Ortcutt, ⌜∃x Spy(x)⌝); de re belief relates a believer to an open sentence's content and a res: ∃x Br(Ortcutt, ⟨x⟩, ⌜Spy(y)⌝).","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Believing something of an object requires a nonconceptual, contextual relation to it, paradigmatically perception; such belief cannot be reduced to fully conceptualized belief and is more basic, being needed for understanding language and for empirical knowledge.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"de dicto belief","aliases":["de dicto","dictum"],"definition":"From a semantical viewpoint, a de dicto belief is a belief in which the believer is related only to a completely expressed proposition (dictum). The epistemic analogue is a belief that is fully conceptualized.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"de re locution","aliases":["de re locutions","de re"],"definition":"De re locutions are about predication broadly conceived. They describe a relation between open sentences (or what they express) and objects.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"nonconceptual","aliases":["non-conceptual"],"definition":"The term 'nonconceptual' does not imply that no concepts or other mental notions enter into a full statement of the relation. … The crucial point is that the relation not be merely that of the concepts' being concepts of the object—concepts that denote or apply to it.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"sense₃","aliases":["sense","senses","sense3"],"definition":"The third of the three functions Frege's notion of sense fills: providing the entities that expressions denote in oblique contexts, roughly the dicta attributed in ordinary belief talk; not in general identifiable with mode of presentation, mode of designation, or linguistic meaning.","exact":false},{"id":"t5","term":"de re belief","aliases":["de re","de re beliefs"],"definition":"A de re belief is a belief whose correct ascription places the believer in an appropriate nonconceptual, contextual relation to objects the belief is about.","exact":true,"avoid":["burge1977-belief-de-re/t2","burge1977-belief-de-re/t3"]}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"On seeing a man coming from a distance in a swirling fog, we may plausibly be said to believe … of him that he is wearing a red cap. But we do not see the man well enough to describe or image him in such a way as to individuate him fully.","phenomenon":"perceptual de re belief","near":["merely de dicto (notional) belief","attributive use of a definite description","de se belief"],"avoid":["de re belief","singular (Russellian) proposition","indexical (demonstrative) reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"If Alfie says, \"The most powerful man on earth in 1970 (whoever he is) is a crook,\" not having the slightest idea who the most powerful man is, a friend of the potentate may say to him, \"Alfie believes that you are a crook.\"","phenomenon":"de re ascription of a de dicto attitude","near":["genuine (en rapport) de re belief","referential use of a definite description","de se belief"],"avoid":["exportation","exportation from de dicto to de re","pronoun of laziness","attributive use of a definite description","denotation without reference","singular thought without acquaintance","de dicto vs de re reading","de re / de dicto ambiguity","E-type pronoun","cross-sentential anaphora","law of exportation"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"We would find boring a claim of the form \"this = this\", where 'this' is twice used to refer to some object under the same circumstances … But we might be surprised by a claim of the form \"this = this\", where the first 'this' is used with a nod toward a picture of the Hope diamond, and the second with a gesture to a dirty stone.","phenomenon":"Frege's puzzle about informative identity","near":["essential indexical","rigid designation","pronoun of laziness"],"avoid":["Frege's puzzle","Frege's puzzle about identity statements","Frege's puzzle about co-referring names","same content, different character","repeated demonstrative with distinct demonstrata","indexical (demonstrative) reference","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"if we say that Alfie believes … (i) that 2 squared is 4, we may refuse to say that Alfie believes (ii) that the only even prime squared is 4. … Alfie may not realize that 2 is the only even prime number. Even if he does, we would say that his believing (i) is one thing, and his believing (ii) another.","phenomenon":"substitution failure in belief contexts","near":["de re belief","exportation","substitution failure in modal contexts"],"avoid":["referential opacity","Frege's puzzle about identity statements","Frege's puzzle about co-referring names","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","de dicto vs de re reading","de re / de dicto ambiguity","merely de dicto (notional) belief","de dicto belief","referential opacity (substitution failure)"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2025871"},{"id":"kratzer1981-notional-category-modality","title":"The Notional Category of Modality","authors":["Angelika Kratzer"],"year":"1981","area":"language","topic":"modality","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The full range of possible modal meanings expressed in natural languages can … be characterized by conditions on three parameters: modal base, ordering source, and modal force.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Simplifying slightly, a proposition is a necessity just in case it is true in all accessible worlds that come closest to the ideal determined by the ordering source.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Every modal is interpreted relative to two context-supplied conversational backgrounds: a modal base, which fixes the accessible worlds, and an ordering source, which ranks them by some norm or ideal; modal force then quantifies over the best-ranked worlds.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Facts and norms must be kept apart: ranking the worlds compatible with the facts by an ideal is what yields graded possibility ('a good possibility', 'more likely'), correct predictions for practical inference, and non-vacuous deontic conditionals.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"modal base","aliases":["modal bases"],"definition":"The modal base determines the set of accessible worlds, and the ordering source induces an ordering on it.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"ordering source","aliases":["ordering sources"],"definition":"ordering sources are used as domain restrictions for the set of accessible worlds: not all, but only the “closest” accessible worlds matter for what is possible or necessary.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"conversational background","aliases":["conversational backgrounds"],"definition":"I take conversational backgrounds to be functions mapping possible worlds to premise sets—that is, sets of propositions.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"realistic conversational background","aliases":["realistic conversational backgrounds","realistic"],"definition":"A realistic conversational background is a function f such that for any world w, w ∈ ∩f(w). That is, f assigns to every possible world a set of propositions that are true in it.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"totally realistic conversational background","aliases":["totally realistic conversational backgrounds","totally realistic"],"definition":"A totally realistic conversational background is a function f such that for any w ∈ W, ∩f(w) = {w}. That is, f assigns to any world a set of propositions that characterizes it uniquely.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"stereotypical conversational background","aliases":["stereotypical conversational backgrounds","stereotypical"],"definition":"A stereotypical conversational background is a function f such that for any world w, f(w) represents what is normal in w according to some suitable normalcy standard for w.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Much-Girgl has been murdered on his way home. There is an ongoing investigation. Conclusions … are being drawn from the available evidence, and utterances of the following sentences might have occurred: … Kastenjakl may be the murderer. … Gauzner-Michl must be the murderer. In uttering [the first], a police inspector may have claimed that given the available evidence, it is possible that Kastenjakl committed the murder.","phenomenon":"epistemic modality","near":["deontic modality","circumstantial (root) modality","teleological modality"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose I am traveling in an exotic country and discover that soil and climate are very much like that in my own country, where plum trees prosper everywhere. In such a situation, [‘Plum trees can grow in this area’] … would probably be true. But [‘It may be that plum trees grow in this area’] might very well be false, given that that country has had no contacts whatsoever with Western civilization and the vegetation is altogether different from ours.","phenomenon":"circumstantial (root) modality","aliases":["circumstantial"],"near":["deontic modality","bouletic modality","teleological modality"],"avoid":["root modality","circumstantial modality","dynamic modality","dispositional (circumstantial) modality","metaphysical modality","natural (causal) impossibility"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Suppose two burglars are trying to enter a farmhouse and whisper to each other: … Jetzt dürfen wir keinen Lärm machen. Now may we no noise make. It is not that they are not allowed to make a noise. They can't make a noise in view of their goal to burgle the farmers without getting caught.","phenomenon":"teleological modality","aliases":["goal"],"near":["deontic modality","epistemic modality","ability (dynamic) modality"],"avoid":["instrumental (means-end) reasoning","anankastic modality","priority modality","bouletic modality"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Imagine that tomorrow is the coronation of the King and I say: … Morgen darf es nicht regnen. Tomorrow may it not rain. What I say here is roughly that according to what we all want, it shouldn't rain tomorrow.","phenomenon":"bouletic modality","near":["deontic modality","epistemic modality","ability (dynamic) modality"],"avoid":["buletic modality","volitional modality","teleological modality"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"(59) Every person must be treated justly. (60) If someone was treated unjustly, the injustice must be amended for. (61) If someone was treated unjustly, the injustice must be rewarded. Analysed as modalized material implications, necessarily (α → β), over the morally accessible worlds: if (59) is true there is no injustice in any morally accessible world, so (60) and (61) both come out vacuously true.","phenomenon":"contrary-to-duty obligation","near":["Ross's paradox","free choice permission","the miners puzzle"],"avoid":["paradox of material implication","wide-scope reading of a modal conditional","contrary-to-duty paradox","Chisholm's paradox","Good Samaritan paradox","deontic conditional","conditional obligation"],"exact":false}],"doi":null},{"id":"derrida1972-signature-event-context","title":"Signature Event Context","authors":["Jacques Derrida"],"year":"1972","area":"language","topic":"speech acts & assertion","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Every sign, linguistic or nonlinguistic … can be cited … thereby it can break with every given context, and engender infinitely new contexts in an absolutely nonsaturable fashion.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[The] iterability of the mark is not an accident or an anomaly, but is that … without which a mark could no longer even have a so-called \"normal\" functioning.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Because every mark must be iterable—repeatable and citable in the absence of its sender, addressee, referent and intended meaning—no context is ever fully determinable or saturated, and conscious intention can never wholly govern meaning.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Speech-act theory cannot quarantine 'non-serious' citation (actors, poems, soliloquy) as parasitic: even a successful performative works only by repeating a coded, iterable formula, so a general citationality is a condition of ordinary utterance, not a deviation from it.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"iterability","definition":"[The] unity of the signifying form is constituted only by its iterability, by the possibility of being repeated in the absence not only of its referent … but of a determined signified or current intention of signification, as of every present intention of communication.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"dissemination","definition":"The semantic horizon which habitually governs the notion of communication is exceeded or punctured by the intervention of writing, that is of a dissemination which cannot be reduced to a polysemia.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"deconstruction","definition":"Deconstruction cannot limit itself or proceed immediately to a neutralization: it must, by means of a double gesture, a double science, a double writing, practice an overturning of the classical opposition and a general displacement of the system.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"signature","definition":"In order to function, that is, in order to be legible, a signature must have a repeatable, iterable, imitable form; it must be able to detach itself from the present and singular intention of its production.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"If I say, while looking out the window, \"The sky is blue,\" the statement will be intelligible (let us provisionally say, if you will, communicable), even if the interlocutor does not see the sky; even if I do not see it myself, if I see it poorly, if I am mistaken, or if I wish to trick my interlocutor.","phenomenon":"absence of the referent","near":["absence of the signified","agrammaticality (Sinnlosigkeit)","absence of the addressee"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"a performative utterance will, for example, be in a peculiar way hollow or void if said by an actor on the stage, or if introduced in a poem, or spoken in soliloquy.","phenomenon":"parasitic (non-serious) use of language","near":["misfire (void performative act)","abuse (insincere performative)","indirect speech act"],"avoid":["etiolated (non-serious) use of language","non-serious (etiolated) utterance"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Since \"green is or\" or \"abracadabra\" do not constitute their context in themselves, nothing prevents their functioning in another context as signifying marks … Not only in the contingent case in which, by means of the translation of German into French \"le vert est ou\" might be endowed with grammaticality, ou (oder, or) becoming when heard où (where, the mark of place) … But even \"green is or\" still signifies an example of agrammaticality.","phenomenon":"citational grafting (citationality)","near":["countersense (Widersinnigkeit)","category mistake","polysemy"],"avoid":["homonymy (lexical ambiguity)","lexical ambiguity","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","pun (deliberate ambiguity)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Let us imagine a writing with a code idiomatic enough to have been founded and known, as a secret cipher, only by two \"subjects.\" Can it still be said that upon the death of the addressee, that is, of the two partners, the mark left by one of them is still a writing?","phenomenon":"iterability of the mark","near":["absence of the referent","parasitic (non-serious) use of language","polysemy"],"avoid":["accidentally private language"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"lewis1984-putnams-paradox","title":"Putnam’s Paradox","authors":["David Lewis"],"year":"1984","area":"language","topic":"reference & names","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Global descriptivism stands refuted. It may be part of the truth about reference, but it cannot be the whole story. There must be some additional constraint on reference","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"When we limit ourselves to the eligible interpretations, … there is no longer any guarantee that (almost) any world can satisfy (almost) any theory.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"The model-theoretic argument refutes only global descriptivism: since almost any world satisfies almost any consistent theory, something beyond fit with total theory must fix reference, namely the eligibility of natural, joint-carving referents, traded off against making total theory true.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Against the objection that any proposed constraint on reference is 'just more theory', intended interpretations must conform to the constraint itself, not merely satisfy our theory of it; reference is not settled by our own stipulations.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"global descriptivism","definition":"We can introduce terms in little families. … How about the biggest family of all—the entire vocabulary of the language? … The intended interpretation will be the one, if such there be, that makes the term-introducing theory come true. … this time, the term-introducing theory is total theory!","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"causal descriptivism","aliases":["descriptivism","causal"],"definition":"If we subject a causal theory of reference (or a more modest causal constraint) to the 'just more theory' treatment, we get what I call causal descriptivism. That is: descriptivism, global or local, in which the descriptions are largely couched in causal terms.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"eligibility","aliases":["eligible"],"definition":"Only an elite minority are carved at the joints … Only these elite things and classes are eligible to serve as referents. … Merrill makes eligibility an all-or-nothing matter; I would prefer to make it a matter of degree.","exact":true,"avoid":["lewis1983-new-work-theory/t6","lewis1983-new-work-theory/t1"]},{"id":"t4","term":"'just more theory' treatment","aliases":["just more theory"],"definition":"It is still trivially easy for a world to make total theory come true … Constraint C is to be imposed by accepting C-theory, according to Putnam. But C-theory is just more theory, more grist for the mill; and more theory will go the way of all theory.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"'Jack the Ripper did this, that, and the other' says the detective; his point is in part to hypothesise that there is someone who did this, that, and the other, and in part to stipulate that the one who did, if such there be, is to become the referent of 'Jack the Ripper'.","phenomenon":"reference fixing by description","aliases":["referent"],"near":["causal-historical chain of reference","referential use of a definite description","ostensive baptism"],"avoid":["Ramsey-style simultaneous implicit definition","descriptive name","descriptive name (name introduced by description)","attributive use of a definite description","contingent a priori truth","singular thought without acquaintance","attributive use of a proper name","denotation without reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose that our detective hypothesised that the murders were the joint work of a couple: Jack the Ripper and Jill the Slasher, as he chose to call them. 'Jack did this,' he says, 'Jill did that and the other, and Jack and Jill are related thus'. Then, if the world provides suitable candidates, 'Jack' and 'Jill' gain referents together.","phenomenon":"Ramsey-style simultaneous implicit definition","near":["partial reference","causal-historical chain of reference","ostensive baptism"],"avoid":["reference fixing by description","descriptive name","descriptive name (name introduced by description)","attributive use of a proper name"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"The mereological sum of the coffee in my cup, the ink in this sentence, a nearby sparrow, and my left shoe is a miscellaneous mess of an object, yet its boundaries are by no means unrelated to the joints in nature. It is an eligible referent, but less eligible than some others. … the metal things are less of an elite, eligible class than the silver things, and the green things are worse, and the grue things are worse still","phenomenon":"degrees of naturalness","aliases":["eligible"],"near":["perfectly natural property","Goodman's new riddle of induction","borderline vagueness"],"avoid":["reference magnetism","eligibility of referents"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"If the brain says (in his inner speech) 'I am in Vienna', we might carelessly suppose that he means what we do and is therefore mistaken. (The brain and his vat are not in Vienna.) For he cannot think of Vienna, for he cannot refer to it, for ex hypothesi he is causally isolated from it.","phenomenon":"causal theory of reference","aliases":["causally"],"near":["description theory of reference","narrow content","division of linguistic labor"],"avoid":["semantic externalism","semantic externalism about natural-kind terms","social externalism (anti-individualism)","causal-historical determination of reference","causal-historical chain of reference","reference failure","brain in a vat","radical skeptical deception"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1080/00048408412340013"},{"id":"putnam1980-models-reality","title":"Models and Reality","authors":["Hilary Putnam"],"year":"1980","area":"language","topic":"reference & names","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The metaphysical realist's claim that even the ideal theory T₁ might be false … seems to collapse into unintelligibility.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Models are not lost noumenal waifs looking for someone to name them; they are constructions within our theory itself, and they have names from birth.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"By completeness, any consistent ideal theory T₁ has a model that can be made standard on the observables; it meets every operational and theoretical constraint, so unless something else singles out intended models, T₁ is true in all of them.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Skolem-style indeterminacy of reference dissolves once understanding a language is taken to be mastery of verification procedures rather than grasp of truth conditions: use then fixes reference, so permuted models are trivially unintended.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"metaphysical realism","aliases":["metaphysical realist","metaphysical"],"definition":"the hard-core metaphysical realist—wishes it to be the case that truth and rational acceptability should be independent notions. … holds that our intentions single out \"the\" model, and that our beliefs are then either true or false in \"the\" model whether we can find out their truth values or not.","exact":true,"avoid":["dummett1959-truth/t1"]},{"id":"t2","term":"operational constraints","aliases":["operational","operational constraint"],"definition":"Let MAG be a countable set of physical magnitudes which includes all magnitudes that sentient beings in this physical universe can actually measure … Let OP be the \"correct\" assignment of values … Then all the information \"operational constraints\" might give us … is coded into OP.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"nonrealist semantics","aliases":["non-realist semantics","nonrealist","semantic","semantics"],"definition":"a semantic theory which holds that a language is completely understood when a verification procedure is suitably mastered, and not when truth conditions (in the classical sense) are learned.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"a model in which, as it might be, the set of cats and the set of dogs are permuted (i.e., 'cat' is assigned the set of dogs as its extension, and 'dog' is assigned the set of cats) … [while] corresponding adjustments in the extensions of all the other predicates make it end up that the operational and theoretical constraints of total science or total belief are all \"preserved\".","phenomenon":"indeterminacy of reference","near":["inverted spectrum","semantic externalism about natural-kind terms","Goodman's new riddle of induction"],"avoid":["indeterminacy (inscrutability) of reference","inscrutability of reference","indeterminacy of interpretation","indeterminacy of translation","ontological relativity","permutation argument","model-theoretic argument against realism","Putnam's paradox","rule-following paradox"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"So your formalized set theory says that a certain set (call it \"S\") is nondenumerable. So S must be nondenumerable in all models of your set theory. … But this is impossible! … no theory can have only nondenumerable models; if a theory has a nondenumerable model, it must have denumerably infinite ones as well.","phenomenon":"Skolem's paradox","aliases":["Skolem"],"near":["Russell's paradox","Cantor's paradox","Banach–Tarski paradox"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"if \"mentalese\" is to be a vehicle for describing the external world, then the various predicate letters must have extensions which are sets of external things … But if … the brain's program for using \"mentalese\" comprises its entire \"understanding\" of \"mentalese\"—where the program for using \"mentalese\", like any program, refers only to what is inside the computer—then how do extensions ever come into the picture at all?","phenomenon":"symbol grounding problem","near":["frame problem","homunculus regress","binding problem"],"avoid":["indeterminacy (inscrutability) of reference","indeterminacy of reference","indeterminacy of interpretation","Chinese room"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"If \"refers\" can be defined in terms of some causal predicate or predicates in the metalanguage of our theory, then … unless the word 'causes' (or whatever the causal predicate or predicates may be) is already glued to one definite relation with metaphysical glue, this does not fix a determinate extension for 'refers' at all.","phenomenon":"\"just more theory\" objection","near":["qua problem","reference change of a proper name","argument from ignorance and error"],"avoid":["indeterminacy (inscrutability) of reference","indeterminacy of reference","indeterminacy of interpretation","indeterminacy of translation","causal theory of reference","causal-historical chain of reference","causal-historical determination of reference"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2273415"},{"id":"searle1975-indirect-speech-acts","title":"Indirect Speech Acts","authors":["John Searle"],"year":"1975","area":"language","topic":"speech acts & assertion","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"In indirect speech acts the speaker communicates to the hearer more than he actually says by way of relying on their mutually shared background information, both linguistic and nonlinguistic","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"there can be conventions of usage that are not meaning conventions. … can you, could you, I want you to, and numerous other forms are conventional ways of making requests","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"When 'Can you pass the salt?' is used to request, the literal question is still asked; the hearer infers the request from speech-act felicity conditions, conversational cooperation and shared background, without positing ambiguity or conversational postulates.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"One can request indirectly by stating or asking whether the hearer can do the act, will do it, or has reason to; but one's wanting it can only be stated, and the hearer's wanting to do it only asked.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"indirect speech act","aliases":["indirectly"],"definition":"cases in which one illocutionary act is performed indirectly by way of performing another.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"primary illocutionary act","aliases":["illocutionary act"],"definition":"the primary illocutionary act performed in Y's utterance is the rejection of the proposal made by X … the secondary illocutionary act is literal; the primary illocutionary act is not literal","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"maxim of idiomaticity","aliases":["idiomatically","unidiomatically"],"definition":"In general, if one speaks unidiomatically, hearers assume that there must be a special reason for it, and in consequence, various assumptions of normal speech are suspended. … Speak idiomatically unless there is some special reason not to.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Student X: Let's go to the movies tonight. … Student Y: I have to study for an exam.","phenomenon":"indirect speech act","near":["indirect request","explicit performative","scalar implicature"],"avoid":["conversational implicature","particularized conversational implicature","implicature from observing the maxim of Relation","flouting the maxim of Relation","indirect rejection","R-based implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"At the dinner table, X says to Y, Can you pass the salt?","phenomenon":"indirect request","near":["explicit performative","rhetorical question","conventional implicature"],"avoid":["indirect speech act","conventional indirect request","whimperative","speech act idiom","conversational implicature","generalized conversational implicature","directive speech act","R-based implicature","polar (yes/no) question"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Can I help you? I can do that for you. I could get it for you. Could I be of assistance? … Would you like some help? Do you want me to go now, Sally?","phenomenon":"indirect offer","near":["indirect request","explicit performative promise","request for permission"],"avoid":["indirect speech act","conversational implicature","commissive speech act","commissive illocutionary act","indirect commissive","indirect promise","R-based implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"I'd like you to do this for me, Bill, but I am not asking you to do it or requesting that you do it or ordering you to do it or telling you to do it.","phenomenon":"cancellation of an indirect directive","near":["Moore's paradox","performative contradiction","presupposition cancellation"],"avoid":["cancellation of a conversational implicature","cancellability of implicature","implicature cancellation","explicit cancellation of an implicature","cancelability of an implicature"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1163/9789004368811_004"},{"id":"karttunen1977-syntax-semantics-questions","title":"Syntax and Semantics of Questions","authors":["Lauri Karttunen"],"year":"1977","area":"language","topic":"questions & focus","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I choose to make questions denote the set of propositions expressed by their true answers instead of the set of propositions expressed by their possible answers.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The meaning assigned to WH-phrases such as who and which man is the same as the meaning of the existentially quantified noun phrases someone and a man.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"All indirect questions — yes/no, alternative, single or multiple wh — denote the set of true propositions jointly forming a complete answer, not all possible answers, which explains why 'tell who', unlike 'tell that', is veridical.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A proto-question ?φ translates as λp[ˇp ∧ p = ˆφ′]; wh-phrases translate like existential NPs (who = λP∃x P(x)), so quantifying one in gives ⟦who walks⟧ = λp∃x[ˇp ∧ p = ˆwalk′(x)], the true propositions 'x walks'.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"proto-question","aliases":["proto-questions"],"definition":"the indirect questions generated by [prefixing a declarative sentence with ‘?’] (let us call them proto-questions) are not proper expressions of English. They are just embryonic structures which exist in order to be developed into genuine indirect questions","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"WH-Quantification Rule","aliases":["WH-Quantification","WHQ"],"definition":"forms wh-questions by inserting a WH-phrase into a proto-question or a wh-question that contains an occurrence of the corresponding unbound pronoun (that is heₙ, himₙ, or hisₙ). The pronoun is either replaced in its original location by the incoming WH-phrase or deleted in case the WH-phrase is preposed.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"WH-phrase","aliases":["WH-phrases","interrogative noun phrases","interrogative noun phrase","WH"],"definition":"The syntactic category of interrogative noun phrases, let us call it ‘WH’, is defined as t//IV. … WH-phrases come to have the same type of denotation as ordinary noun phrases (Montague’s T-phrases). Furthermore, for semantic reasons, we make WH-phrases equivalent to existentially quantified noun phrases.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"pragmatically self-verifying","aliases":["self-verifying"],"definition":"On any occasion where [Is it raining?] is uttered, [‘I ask you (to tell me) whether it is raining’] expresses a true proposition. … [Is it raining?] is, so to speak, pragmatically self-verifying. Whenever it is uttered, it is true.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"a person who asks (iii) Who, for instance, came to the party last night? may be perfectly satisfied with an answer that lists some but not all of the people who came to the party.","phenomenon":"mention-some reading","near":["exhaustive (mention-all) reading","pair-list reading","echo question"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"John remembers who came although he doesn’t remember that Mary came. … sentences of the above sort are generally felt to be contradictory","phenomenon":"exhaustivity of embedded wh-questions","near":["mention-some reading","existential presupposition of a question","pair-list reading"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"questions of the sort in (53) are ambiguous; they can be answered in two ways. (53) Who remembers where Mary keeps which book? The two kinds of admissible answers are exhibited in (54). (54) (a) Bill remembers where Mary keeps which book. (b) Joe remembers where Mary keeps Aspects and Max remembers where Mary keeps Syntactic Structures.","phenomenon":"Baker ambiguity (scope of in-situ wh-phrase)","aliases":["ambiguous"],"near":["superiority effect","wh-island violation","mention-some reading"],"avoid":["quantifier scope ambiguity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"What grade does every student deserve? … Under the second interpretation, [the question] is not one but several requests for information at once. It can be paraphrased roughly as ‘For every student y, I ask you (to tell me) what grade y deserves.’ [This interpretation] requires multiple answers; for example, ‘Mary deserves an A, Bill deserves a B, . . .’ and so on for each of the students.","phenomenon":"pair-list reading","near":["mention-some reading","functional reading","echo question"],"avoid":["quantifier scope ambiguity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"(83) (a) Tell me who killed whom. (b) *Tell me whom who killed. (c) Tell me who went where. (d) *Tell me where who went. (e) Tell me what you bought where. (f) Tell me where you bought what.","phenomenon":"superiority effect","near":["wh-island violation","weak crossover","Complex NP Constraint violation"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00351935"},{"id":"black1954-metaphor","title":"Metaphor","authors":["Max Black"],"year":"1954–55","area":"language","topic":"metaphor & figurative language","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The metaphor works by applying to the principal subject a system of “associated implications” characteristic of the subsidiary subject.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Metaphorical statement is not a substitute for a formal comparison or any other kind of literal statement, but has its own distinctive capacities and achievements.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Philosophically important metaphors are not decorative substitutes for literal paraphrases or condensed similes: they filter our view of the principal subject through the system of commonplaces associated with the subsidiary subject.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Interaction metaphors cannot be replaced by literal paraphrase without loss of cognitive content: the paraphrase says too much, with the wrong emphasis, and fails to give the same insight; some metaphors create the similarity rather than record it.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"interaction view","aliases":["interaction-metaphors","interaction"],"definition":"A metaphorical statement has two distinct subjects—a “principal” subject and a “subsidiary” one. … The metaphor selects, emphasizes, suppresses, and … organizes features of the principal subject by implying statements about it that normally apply to the subsidiary subject.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"substitution view","aliases":["substitution"],"definition":"Any view which holds that a metaphorical expression is used in place of some equivalent literal expression, I shall call a substitution view of metaphor.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"comparison view","aliases":["comparison"],"definition":"If a writer holds that a metaphor consists in the presentation of the underlying analogy or similarity, he will be taking what I shall call a comparison view of metaphor. … This is a view of metaphor as a condensed or elliptical simile.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"system of associated commonplaces","aliases":["system of commonplaces associated","associated commonplaces","commonplaces"],"definition":"Imagine some layman required to say, without taking special thought, those things he held to be true about wolves; the set of statements resulting would approximate to what I am here calling the system of commonplaces associated with the word “wolf”.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"focus","aliases":["focal"],"definition":"the focus of a metaphor, the word or expression having a distinctively metaphorical use within a literal frame","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"catachresis","definition":"the use of a word in some new sense in order to remedy a gap in the vocabulary. Catachresis is the putting of new senses into old words.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"If the man is a wolf, he preys upon other animals, is fierce, hungry, engaged in constant struggle, a scavenger, and so on. … A suitable hearer will be led by the wolf-system of implications to construct a corresponding system of implications about the principal subject. … Any human traits that can without undue strain be talked about in “wolf-language” will be rendered prominent, and any that cannot will be pushed into the background.","phenomenon":"interaction view of metaphor","aliases":["interaction"],"near":["comparison view of metaphor","substitution view of metaphor","catachresis"],"avoid":["interaction metaphor","metaphor as filter"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Mathematicians spoke of the “leg” of an angle because there was no brief literal expression for a bounding line; we say “cherry lips”, because there is no form of words half as convenient for saying quickly what the lips are like.","phenomenon":"catachresis","near":["metonymy","hyperbole (overstatement)","verbal irony"],"avoid":["dead metaphor","lexical gap filling","lexical ambiguity","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","homonymy (lexical ambiguity)","polysemy"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"When Schopenhauer called a geometrical proof a mousetrap, he was, according to such a view, saying (though not explicitly): “A geometrical proof is like a mousetrap, since both offer a delusive reward, entice their victims by degrees, lead to disagreeable surprise, etc.”","phenomenon":"comparison view of metaphor","aliases":["comparison"],"near":["interaction view of metaphor","catachresis","metonymy"],"avoid":["substitution view of metaphor","elliptical simile","metaphor as elliptical simile","elliptical simile theory","simile"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Thus in the somewhat unfortunate example, “Richard is a lion”, which modern writers have discussed with boring insistence, the literal meaning is taken to be the same as that of the sentence, “Richard is brave”.","phenomenon":"substitution view of metaphor","aliases":["substitution"],"near":["comparison view of metaphor","interaction view of metaphor","catachresis"],"avoid":["metaphor as decoration"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"davidson1978-metaphors-mean","title":"What Metaphors Mean","authors":["Donald Davidson"],"year":"1978","area":"language","topic":"metaphor & figurative language","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"metaphors mean what the words, in their most literal interpretation, mean, and nothing more.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"We must give up the idea that a metaphor carries a message, that it has a content or meaning (except, of course, its literal meaning).","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A metaphorical sentence has only its ordinary literal meaning and literal truth value, usually a patent falsehood; metaphor belongs to use, not meaning, and carries no second figurative meaning or special cognitive content.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Metaphor works not by encoding a message but by prompting us to see one thing as another; since what it makes us notice is endless and largely non-propositional, trying to state its 'content' in literal words is misguided.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t5","term":"simile","aliases":["similes"],"definition":"The most obvious semantic difference between simile and metaphor is that all similes are true and most metaphors are false. The earth is like a floor, the Assyrian did come down like a wolf on the fold, because everything is like everything.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"elliptical simile theory","aliases":["elliptical simile","simile"],"definition":"the common theory that a metaphor is an elliptical simile. This theory makes no distinction in … meaning between a metaphor and some related simile and does not provide any ground for speaking of figurative, metaphorical, or special meanings.","exact":true,"avoid":["black1954-metaphor/t3"]},{"id":"t3","term":"paraphrase","aliases":["paraphrasing"],"definition":"what we attempt in \"paraphrasing\" a metaphor cannot be to give its meaning, for that lies on the surface; rather we attempt to evoke what the metaphor brings to our attention.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"metaphorical truth","definition":"This is not to deny that there is such a thing as metaphorical truth, only to deny it of sentences. … there is no reason, I suppose, not to say these visions, thoughts, and feelings inspired by the metaphor, are true or false.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"When Shakespeare's Cressida is welcomed bawdily into the Grecian camp, Nestor says, \"Our general doth salute you with a kiss.\" Here we are to take \"general\" two ways: once as applying to Agamemnon, who is the general; and once, since she is kissing everyone, as applying to no one in particular, but everyone in general.","phenomenon":"pun (deliberate ambiguity)","near":["metaphor","dead metaphor","simile"],"avoid":["lexical ambiguity","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","homonymy (lexical ambiguity)","polysemy","syllepsis","zeugma"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Once upon a time, I suppose, rivers and bottles did not, as they do now, literally have mouths. … when \"mouth\" applied only metaphorically to bottles, the application made the hearer notice a likeness between animal and bottle openings. … Once one has the present use of the word, with literal application to bottles, there is nothing left to notice.","phenomenon":"dead metaphor","aliases":["metaphorically"],"near":["pun (deliberate ambiguity)","synecdoche","metonymy"],"avoid":["catachresis","lexical ambiguity","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","homonymy (lexical ambiguity)","polysemy"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"if I show you Wittgenstein's duck-rabbit, and I say, \"It's a duck,\" then with luck you see it as a duck; if I say, \"It's a rabbit,\" you see it as a rabbit. But no proposition expresses what I have led you to see.","phenomenon":"seeing-as (aspect perception)","aliases":["see it as"],"near":["seeing-that (propositional perception)","aspect blindness","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1086/447971"},{"id":"chomsky1959-review-b-f","title":"A Review of B. F. Skinner’s Verbal Behavior","authors":["Noam Chomsky"],"year":"1959","area":"language","topic":"theory of meaning & truth conditions","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"elimination of the independent contribution of the speaker and learner … can be achieved only at the cost of eliminating all significance from the descriptive system [of reinforcement theory]","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The fact that all normal children acquire essentially comparable grammars of great complexity with remarkable rapidity suggests that human beings are somehow specially designed to do this","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Behaviorist notions of stimulus, response, control and reinforcement, taken literally, cover almost no verbal behavior; taken metaphorically, they merely rename everyday mentalistic talk (\"X wants Y\") while adding no objectivity or explanatory content.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Adults constantly understand novel sentences, and children rapidly acquire complex grammars without needing meticulous reinforcement; so explaining verbal behavior requires first characterizing the internalized grammar, and explaining acquisition requires studying the learner's built-in contribution.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"mand","definition":"a verbal operant in … which the response is reinforced by a characteristic consequence and is therefore under the functional control of relevant conditions of deprivation or aversive stimulation","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"tact","definition":"a verbal operant in which a response of given form is evoked (or at least strengthened) by a particular object or event or property of an object or event","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"reinforcement","aliases":["reinforced"],"definition":"The phrase “X is reinforced by Y (stimulus, state of affairs, event, etc.)” is being used as a cover term for “X wants Y,” “X likes Y,” “X wishes that Y were the case,” etc. Invoking the term reinforcement has no explanatory force","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"grammar","definition":"It is reasonable to regard the grammar of a language L ideally as a mechanism that provides an enumeration of the sentences of L in something like the way in which a deductive theory gives an enumeration of a set of theorems.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"A typical example of stimulus control for Skinner would be the response … to a painting with the response Dutch. … Suppose instead of saying Dutch we had said Clashes with the wallpaper, I thought you liked abstract work, Never saw it before, Tilted, Hanging too low, Beautiful, Hideous, Remember our camping trip last summer? … Skinner could only say that each of these responses is under the control of some other stimulus property of the physical object.","phenomenon":"stimulus-freedom of language use","near":["stimulus generalization","metaphorical extension","poverty of the stimulus"],"avoid":["creative aspect of language use","productivity of language","language entry transition"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Every time an adult reads a newspaper, he undoubtedly comes upon countless new sentences which are not at all similar, in a simple, physical sense, to any that he has heard before, and which he will recognize as sentences and understand; he will also be able to detect slight distortions or misprints.","phenomenon":"productivity of language","near":["stimulus generalization","systematicity","displacement (talk of the remote or absent)"],"avoid":["creative aspect of language use","linguistic creativity","poverty of the stimulus"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"The “classical” experiment of Blodgett indicated that rats who had explored a maze without reward showed a marked drop in number of errors (as compared to a control group which had not explored the maze) upon introduction of a food reward, indicating that the rat had learned the structure of the maze without reduction of the hunger drive.","phenomenon":"latent learning","aliases":["learned"],"near":["imprinting","secondary reinforcement","insight learning"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Please pass the salt is a request (but not a question), whether or not the listener happens to be motivated to fulfill it … A response does not cease to be a command if it is not followed; nor does a question become a command if the speaker answers it because of an implied or imagined threat. Not all advice is good advice, and a response does not cease to be advice if it is not followed.","phenomenon":"illocutionary vs. perlocutionary distinction","near":["indirect speech act","explicit performative","conversational implicature"],"avoid":["perlocutionary frustration","non-conventional (communicative) illocutionary act"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/411334"},{"id":"geach1965-assertion","title":"Assertion","authors":["Peter Geach"],"year":"1965","area":"language","topic":"speech acts & assertion","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"A proposition may occur in discourse now asserted, now unasserted, and yet be recognizably the same proposition.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[In cases like 'bad', 'true' and 'I know',] the very same sentence can occur in an \"if\" clause; and to such occurrences the anti-descriptive theories will not apply.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A proposition keeps the same content whether or not it is asserted; so analyses of 'bad', 'true' or 'I know' as non-descriptive acts (condemning, conceding, warranting) fail, since those sentences also occur unasserted in conditionals and modus ponens.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Apart from double-barreled idioms like 'the fact that p', no ordinary expression—'it is true that', 'exists', the copula, the predicate—carries assertoric force, since each also occurs unasserted; hence Frege's special, sui generis assertion sign.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"Frege point","aliases":["Frege"],"definition":"A thought may have just the same content whether you assent to its truth or not; a proposition may occur in discourse now asserted, now unasserted, and yet be recognizably the same proposition.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"proposition","aliases":["propositions"],"definition":"a form of words in which something is propounded, put forward for consideration; it is surely clear that what is then put forward neither is ipso facto asserted nor gets altered in content by being asserted","exact":true,"avoid":["geach1965-assertion/t1"]},{"id":"t3","term":"double-barreled assertion","aliases":["double-barreled","assertoric"],"definition":"\"A has pointed out that p\" is exponible as the double-barreled assertion of \"A has maintained that p\" and of \"p\" itself. … [Such sentences] will certainly retain part of their assertoric force when put, for example, into an \"if\" clause","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"rejection","aliases":["rejected","reject","rejecting"],"definition":"logic … demands the use of a negation sign which is not polarly opposed to the assertion sign and does not express rejection of what is negated; and when a proposition is rejected, we may equally well conceive this as asserting the negation of a proposition.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"The theory that to call a kind of act \"bad\" is not to describe but to condemn it is open to similar objections. … If doing a thing is bad, getting your little brother to do it is bad. Tormenting the cat is bad. Ergo, getting your little brother to torment the cat is bad. The whole nerve of the reasoning is that \"bad\" should mean exactly the same at all four occurrences … But in the major premise the speaker … is certainly not uttering acts of condemnation","phenomenon":"Frege–Geach problem","near":["open question argument","is–ought gap","Moore's paradox"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"If I ask, \"Is Jim aware of the fact that his wife is deceiving him?\" I am not just asking a question; I am asserting that Jim's wife is deceiving him. The question as I pose it cannot be properly answered \"Yes\" or \"No\" by someone who does not accept this assertion","phenomenon":"factive presupposition","near":["conversational implicature","referential opacity","Moore's paradox"],"avoid":["projection of a factive presupposition","presupposition projection through holes","informative presupposition","conventional implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"[Ryle argues against the two-premise account of modus ponens] that if we needed to supply \"if p, then q\" as a premise for the inference of \"q\" from \"p,\" then by parity of reasoning we should need to supply \"if both p and if p then q, then q\" as a premise for the inference of \"q\" from \"p\" and \"if p then q\"","phenomenon":"Lewis Carroll's regress","near":["epistemic regress problem","rule-following paradox","paradox of material implication"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2183123"},{"id":"stanley2000-context-logical-form","title":"Context and Logical Form","authors":["Jason Stanley"],"year":"2000","area":"language","topic":"context & common ground","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I defend the thesis that all effects of extra-linguistic context on the truth-conditions of an assertion are traceable to elements in the actual syntactic structure of the sentence uttered.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"for each alleged example of an unarticulated constituent, there is an unpronounced pronominal element in the logical form of the sentence uttered, whose value is the alleged unarticulated constituent.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Apart from disambiguation, context affects what an assertion says only by assigning values to elements actually present in its syntactic structure (indexicals, demonstratives, pronouns, covert variables); there are no convincing cases of unarticulated constituents or non-sentential assertions.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Binding argument: 'Every time John lights a cigarette, it rains' can mean ∀t(light(j,t) → rain(t, g(t))), where g(t) is where John lights up at t; bound readings need a variable, so the location is no unarticulated constituent.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"avoid":["stanley2000-quantifier-domain-restriction"],"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"unarticulated constituent","aliases":["unarticulated constituents","constituent","constituents"],"definition":"x is an unarticulated constituent of an utterance u iff (1) x is an element supplied by context to the truth-conditions of u, and (2) x is not the semantic value of any constituent of the logical form of the sentence uttered.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"non-sentential assertion","aliases":["non-sentential assertions","non-sentential"],"definition":"utterances of expressions that do not appear to have sentential structure, yet appear to express full-blown propositions","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"Binding Assumption","aliases":["BA","binding"],"definition":"If α and β are within the same clause, and α semantically binds β, then α either is, or introduces, a variable-binding operator which is co-indexed with … a variable which is either identical to, or is a constituent of, β.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"narrow indexical","aliases":["indexical","indexicals"],"definition":"a primitive lexical item whose content varies from context to context, and which is resistant to binding by a variable-binding operator with scope over it.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"grammatical role of context","aliases":["context"],"definition":"One role context plays is in helping us to decide which logical form is the one that has been uttered. That is, we draw upon extra-linguistic context to help us decide what to interpret.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Every time John lights a cigarette, it rains. … One natural interpretation of [this sentence] is: For every time t at which John lights a cigarette, it rains at t at the location in which John lights a cigarette at t.","phenomenon":"bound implicit location argument","aliases":["location"],"near":["quantifier domain restriction","strict/sloppy ellipsis ambiguity","weak crossover effect"],"avoid":["bound-variable use of tense","temporal anaphora","saturation of a linguistically mandated slot","free pragmatic enrichment","semantic underdetermination (completion)","bound-variable pronoun","bound-variable reading of a pronoun"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Most species have members that are small. … Most species S have members that are small for S.","phenomenon":"comparison class bound by a quantifier","near":["quantifier domain restriction","sorites paradox","inverse scope reading"],"avoid":["vague predicate","non-intersective (attributive) adjective modification","context-dependent standards of precision","saturation of a linguistically mandated slot","free pragmatic enrichment","bound-variable pronoun","bound-variable reading of a pronoun"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"John is too old. Jill is too. … Suppose that John, a forty-two year old professional swimmer and Jill, a twenty three year old professional gymnast, have decided to wed. Shocked at their age difference, I ask Bill how John and Jill can relate to one another, to which he replies by uttering [it]. Relative to this context, [it] can express the proposition that John is too old for his sport, and Jill is too old for hers.","phenomenon":"sloppy identity reading","near":["strict identity reading","E-type pronoun","antecedent-contained deletion"],"avoid":["bound-variable pronoun","bound-variable reading of a pronoun"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Suppose that a group of friends, including John and Bill, has gone bungee jumping. Every member of the group is watching Bill, who is the first to muster the courage to bungee jump. As Bill is standing eight stories above the water on the platform of a crane, ready to plummet into the water below, Sarah, aware of John's terror of heights, turns to one of the other friends and utters [“John won't.”], shaking her head.","phenomenon":"VP ellipsis with a contextually salient antecedent","near":["non-sentential assertion","null-subject (pro-drop) sentence","sloppy identity reading"],"avoid":["formal link (overt antecedent) requirement on anaphora","strong contextual felicity constraint","manifest event"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1023/a:1005599312747"},{"id":"stanley2000-quantifier-domain-restriction","title":"On Quantifier Domain Restriction","authors":["Jason Stanley","Zoltán Gendler Szabó"],"year":"2000","area":"language","topic":"quantification & formal semantics","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the phenomenon of quantified contexts shows that quantifier expressions contain variables which can be bound, and whose purpose is to supply the domains for quantifier expressions.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[A sentence such as ‘Every bottle is empty’] can communicate a proposition concerning a restricted domain of bottles, because, relative to certain contexts, it expresses such a proposition.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Domain variables attach to common nouns, not determiners: ⟦⟨man, f(i)⟩⟧c = ⟦man⟧ ∩ {x : x ∈ c(f)(c(i))}, where context assigns f a function from objects to sets and i an object.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Every common noun carries a covert, context-valued variable in logical form, so ‘Every bottle is empty’ can say, not merely convey, that every bottle just bought is empty; that higher operators can bind this variable defeats pragmatic accounts.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"quantified contexts","aliases":["quantified context"],"definition":"cases involving sentences containing multiple [determiner phrases] whose intuitive readings are only possible to capture by assuming that an index representing the [domain] of the second [one] is bound by the first [one].","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"descriptive problem","aliases":["descriptive problem of context dependence","problem"],"definition":"The descriptive problem of context dependence for an expression e relative to a context c is the problem of deriving the interpretation of e relative to c, given a prior characterization of what features of the context c have a bearing on the interpretation.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"foundational problem","aliases":["foundational problem of context dependence"],"definition":"the foundational problem of context dependence for an expression e relative to a context c is specifying what it is about the context in virtue of which certain entities … play the role they do in the interpretation of an occurrence of e.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"pragmatic approach","aliases":["pragmatic","approach"],"definition":"an approach is pragmatic just in case the quantifier domain restriction determined by contextual features does not affect what is said, but rather only what is communicated.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"semantic role of context","aliases":["context"],"definition":"the role context plays in supplying additional values to what is said by an occurrence of a grammatical sentence, values that are not determined just from the linguistic meaning of the sentence uttered","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose Lisa went to the store to buy some bottles to give to Max, who wanted to fill them with his home-made beer. Max asks whether the bottles Lisa bought need to be emptied first. In response, Lisa utters (1): (1) Every bottle is empty. In this situation, we can plausibly assume that by uttering (1) Lisa conveyed to Max the proposition that every bottle she just bought is empty.","phenomenon":"quantifier domain restriction","near":["incomplete definite description resolved by salience","domain widening","comparison class of a gradable adjective"],"avoid":["saturation of a linguistically mandated slot","free pragmatic enrichment","conversational impliciture (expansion)","semantic underdetermination (completion)","contextual quantifier domain restriction"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"(24) In most of John’s classes, he fails exactly three Frenchmen. (25) In every room in John’s house, every bottle is in the corner. (26) Whatever John does, most of the class falls asleep. … the proposition intuitively expressed by an utterance of (25) is the proposition that in every room x in John’s house, every bottle in x is in the corner.","phenomenon":"quantifier domain bound by a higher quantifier","aliases":["quantified contexts","quantified context"],"near":["bound-variable pronoun","donkey anaphora","inverse scope reading"],"avoid":["contextual quantifier domain restriction","quantifier domain restriction","bound implicit location argument"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"(32) Every sailor waved to every sailor. This sentence can express the proposition that every sailor on the ship waved to every sailor on the shore","phenomenon":"different domains for quantifiers in one clause","near":["reciprocal reading","inverse scope reading","collective reading"],"avoid":["contextual quantifier domain restriction","quantifier domain restriction","free pragmatic enrichment","saturation of a linguistically mandated slot"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Imagine a child who is crying because of a minor cut. Her mother attempts to calm her by uttering: (22) You are not going to die. Intuitively, the proposition communicated in this case is something like that expressed by an occurrence of ‘You are not going to die from that cut’.","phenomenon":"free pragmatic enrichment","near":["scalar implicature","presupposition accommodation","conventional implicature"],"avoid":["conversational implicature","free enrichment","pragmatic enrichment","particularized conversational implicature","generalized conversational implicature","implicature from observing the maxim of Relation","unarticulated constituent","impliciture","conversational impliciture (expansion)","R-based implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e6","text":"the sentence uttered is ‘Every bottle which is F is empty’, where F abbreviates the unpronounced predicate. … It seems natural then to assume that F is simply the predicate ‘was just bought by me’. … Why could F not be the predicate ‘was recently purchased by me’, or ‘is one of those things that I bought at the store’? … F could equally be something like ‘is one of those things you are looking for’.","phenomenon":"underdetermination problem for ellipsis accounts","near":["VP ellipsis with a contextually salient antecedent","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","sloppy identity reading"],"avoid":["semantic underdetermination (completion)","coextensive but non-synonymous predicates","quantifier domain restriction","saturation of a linguistically mandated slot","meaning-intention problem","indeterminacy of translation","indeterminacy of interpretation","indeterminacy of reference"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1111/1468-0017.00130"},{"id":"partee1973-structural-analogies-tenses","title":"Some Structural Analogies between Tenses and Pronouns in English","authors":["Barbara Partee"],"year":"1973","area":"language","topic":"tense, events & logical form","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the tenses have a range of uses which parallels that of the pronouns, including a contrast between deictic (demonstrative) and anaphoric use","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"tense operators are appropriate for tense and aspect notions like future, progressive, and perfect, whereas variables are appropriate representations for the functions of the two tense morphemes Past and Present.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Tense morphemes are time variables: 'If Susan comes in, John will leave immediately' is ∃t φ(t) → ψ(Imm(Fut)(ιt φ(t))) (φ: Susan come in; ψ: John leave), reckoning the future from the if-clause time, which tense operators alone cannot do.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Past and Present have deictic, nonspecific, anaphoric and bound-variable uses mirroring the personal pronouns', with time adverbials playing the role of full noun phrases; so tenses, unlike modals and aspect, should be variables, not sentence operators.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"deictic Past tense","aliases":["deictic","deictic Past","deictic Past tense"],"definition":"[It refers to] a particular time—not a particular instant, most likely, but a definite interval whose identity is generally clear from the extralinguistic context … In the case of deictic Past tense there is no analog to the pointing gesture that is often used with deictic pronouns.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"nonspecific deictic use","aliases":["nonspecific","deictic","deictics"],"definition":"[Such uses] are not picking out particular referents …; but they are certainly not generic or anaphoric either. 'They' … seems to be referring to whoever it is that's supposed to install the telephone, and Past … seems to refer to whenever it was that John went to school.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"time adverbials","aliases":["time adverbial","time adverbials","adverbials"],"definition":"When a pronoun is not used deictically, it is used in connection with some full term phrase … What, if anything, plays the role of such term phrases in the tense system? The answer seems to be time adverbials.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"pro-form","aliases":["pro-form","pro-forms"],"definition":"Past seems to be the pro-form for all the auxiliaries that contain a morphological Past, such as would have, even when the Past tense does not represent a past time. Correspondingly, Present seems to be the pro-form for all the auxiliaries containing a morphological Present.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"I didn't turn off the stove. When uttered, for instance, halfway down the turnpike, such a sentence clearly does not mean either that there exists some time in the past at which I did not turn off the stove or that there exists no time in the past at which I turned off the stove.","phenomenon":"deictic (referential) use of tense","near":["nonspecific deictic use of tense","bound-variable use of tense","sequence of tense"],"avoid":["indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","indexical (demonstrative) reference","direct reference of demonstratives","quantifier domain restriction","incomplete definite description resolved by salience","referential use of a definite description","saturation of a linguistically mandated slot","free pragmatic enrichment","conversational impliciture (expansion)","semantic underdetermination (completion)","temporal anaphora","existential statement carrying a speaker's reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Sam took the car yesterday and Sheila took it today. … there are comparable uses of tenses, where the time is specified in one clause and the tense of a subsequent clause refers to the same time. … Sheila had a party last Friday and Sam got drunk.","phenomenon":"temporal anaphora","near":["deictic (referential) use of tense","nonspecific deictic use of tense","sequence of tense"],"avoid":["discourse anaphora","cross-sentential anaphora","cross-sentential (discourse) anaphora","event anaphora","bound-variable pronoun"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"When you eat Chinese food, you're always hungry an hour later. … John never answers when I call his home. … Most of the time, if I write John a letter, he answers within a week. … Richard always gave assignments that were due the next day.","phenomenon":"bound-variable use of tense","near":["deictic (referential) use of tense","nonspecific deictic use of tense","fake past tense (in counterfactuals)"],"avoid":["unselective binding","unselective quantification","quantificational variability effect","if-clause restricting a quantificational adverb","bound-variable pronoun","bound-variable reading of a pronoun","bound-variable anaphora","donkey anaphora","quantifier domain restriction","temporal anaphora"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2025024"},{"id":"roberts1996-information-structure-discourse","title":"Information Structure in Discourse: Towards an Integrated Formal Theory of Pragmatics","authors":["Craige Roberts"],"year":"1996","area":"language","topic":"questions & focus","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"A requirement of Relevance is satisfied by an utterance (whether an assertion, a question or a suggestion) iff it addresses the question under discussion.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the prosodic focus on an utterance gives rise to the presupposition that the utterance, whether assertion or question, is congruent to the question under discussion.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Questions denote alternative sets; accepted questions are pushed onto a QUD stack and popped once answered or unanswerable; a move m is Relevant iff it partially answers last(QUD(m)) (assertions) or is part of a strategy to answer it (questions).","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Because prosodic focus presupposes congruence with the question under discussion and every move must be Relevant to that question, the domain of ‘only’ (association with focus) falls out as a conversational implicature, with no focus-sensitive lexical semantics or structured meanings.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"question under discussion","aliases":["questions under discussion","question"],"definition":"When we accept a question, we add it to the top of the stack. … When a question is answered or determined to be practically unanswerable, it is popped off the stack … the question on top of the stack is the (immediate) question under discussion.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"strategy of inquiry","aliases":["strategies of inquiry"],"definition":"Strategies of inquiry are sequences of setup moves, or questions, designed to (at least partially) satisfy the aims of the game while obeying the game’s constraints. … a strategy of inquiry will have a hierarchical structure, a set of questions partially ordered by entailment.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"Relevance","aliases":["Relevant"],"definition":"A move m is Relevant to the question under discussion q, i.e., to last(QUD(m)), iff m either introduces a partial answer to q (m is an assertion) or is part of a strategy to answer q (m is a question).","exact":true,"avoid":["simons2010-projects-why/t3"]},{"id":"t4","term":"congruence","aliases":["congruent"],"definition":"Move β is congruent to a question ?(α) iff its focal alternatives ‖β‖ are the q-alternatives determined by ?(α), i.e., iff ‖β‖ = q-alt(α).","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"Big Question","aliases":["question"],"definition":"Stalnaker’s goal of discourse can itself be viewed as a question, the Big Question, What is the way things are?, whose corresponding set of alternatives is the set of all singleton sets of worlds in the context set at a given point in discourse.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"proffered","definition":"I use the term proffered as a cover for what is asserted in an assertion and for the nonpresupposed content of questions and commands.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Who did Mary invite for dinner? … Mary only invited [ Lyn ]F for dinner.","phenomenon":"association with focus","near":["contrastive topic","metalinguistic negation","quantifier scope ambiguity"],"avoid":["quantifier domain restriction","conversational implicature","implicature from observing the maxim of Relation","presupposition accommodation","question–answer congruence","implicature from contrastive stress","particularized conversational implicature","contrastive focus","R-based implicature","free pragmatic enrichment"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"[ When are you going to China ]F? … Well, I’m going to [ China ]B in [ April ]A.","phenomenon":"contrastive topic","near":["association with focus","alternative question","verum focus"],"avoid":["presupposition accommodation","conversational implicature","informative presupposition","implicature from contrastive stress","question–answer congruence","contrastive focus","particularized conversational implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Consider the gossip’s use of factive verbs to introduce scandalous information with impunity — Isn’t it shocking that Mary has run away from home?","phenomenon":"presupposition accommodation","near":["conversational implicature","presupposition cancellation","presupposition filtering"],"avoid":["factive presupposition","presupposition projection through holes","presupposition hole","informative presupposition","projection of a factive presupposition","presupposition surviving negation and questions","presupposition projection through negation and modals","global accommodation"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Do you want coffee or tea?, pronounced [ Do you want [ coffee ]F ] [ or [ tea ]F ] ?, with a rising H–H% closing the phrase on ‘coffee’ and a final falling L–L% on ‘tea’ — the hearer is to say which of the two drinks she wants.","phenomenon":"alternative question","near":["polar (yes/no) question","echo question","contrastive topic"],"avoid":["lexical ambiguity","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","homonymy (lexical ambiguity)","contrastive focus","symmetric contrastive focus","indirect offer","indirect speech act"],"exact":false}],"doi":"10.3765/sp.5.6"},{"id":"rooth1992-theory-focus-interpretation","title":"A Theory of Focus Interpretation","authors":["Mats Rooth"],"year":"1992","area":"language","topic":"questions & focus","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The key to a uniform interpretation for focus is an interpretation principle which introduces a variable, thought of as a contrasting element or set of contrasting elements.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[My] theory is unitary in that focus has a uniform semantic import, a presupposition defined in terms of focus and ordinary semantic values.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Intonational prominence has one uniform semantic import: it introduces a free variable, presupposed to be a subset or member of the alternatives it evokes, whose antecedent—a question, the domain of 'only', a scale, a contrasting phrase—is found anaphorically.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A purely presuppositional operator ~, adjoined in LF, interprets focus: α ~ Γ presupposes Γ ⊆ ⟦α⟧ᶠ, ⟦α⟧ᵒ ∈ Γ and ∃β ∈ Γ (β ≠ ⟦α⟧ᵒ); α ~ γ presupposes γ ∈ ⟦α⟧ᶠ, γ ≠ ⟦α⟧ᵒ.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"focus semantic value","aliases":["focus semantic values","semantic value"],"definition":"At an intuitive level, we think of the focus semantic value of a sentence as a set of alternatives from which the ordinary semantic value is drawn, or a set of propositions which potentially contrast with the ordinary semantic value.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"Focus Interpretation Principle","aliases":["FIP"],"definition":"Adjoin an operator ~ v to a phrase α in LF, where v is a variable with either the same type as α (individual case), or the type of a set of objects with the same type as α (set case).","exact":true,"avoid":["rooth1992-theory-focus-interpretation/t3"]},{"id":"t3","term":"focus interpretation operator","aliases":["focus"],"definition":"φ ~ Γ presupposes that Γ is a subset of the focus semantic value for φ and contains both the ordinary semantic value of φ and an element distinct from the ordinary semantic value of φ","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"free parameter hypothesis","aliases":["free parameter theory"],"definition":"association with focus arises when focus has the opportunity of restricting a free variable contributed by some lexical or constructional meaning.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"weak theory","aliases":["weak theory of alternative semantics","weak theory of focus"],"definition":"[A theory] which merely supplies focus-sensitive semantic objects and leaves it to particular lexical items or semantic interpretive rules to say how focus is used semantically.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Mary introduced Bill and Tom to Sue, and there were no other introductions. As a description of this situation, (3a) is false and (3b) is true. … (3) a. Mary only introduced BILL to Sue. b. Mary only introduced Bill to SUE.","phenomenon":"association with focus","near":["scalar implicature","quantifier scope ambiguity","contrastive topic"],"avoid":["quantifier domain restriction"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"An AMERICAN farmer was talking to a CANADIAN farmer . . .","phenomenon":"symmetric contrastive focus","near":["association with focus","question–answer congruence","split-antecedent anaphora"],"avoid":["implicature from contrastive stress","contrastive focus","anaphoric presupposition"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"My roommates Steve and Paul and I took a quiz in our self-paced calculus class, which was graded right away by the TA. Afterwards, George asked me how it went. My answer was: (16) Well, I PASSED. With this focus marking … the answer tends to suggest that I did no better than passing, that I did not, for instance, ace the exam.","phenomenon":"scalar implicature","aliases":["implicature","implicates"],"near":["conventional implicature","flouting a maxim","presupposition accommodation"],"avoid":["conversational implicature","generalized conversational implicature","particularized conversational implicature","implicature from contrastive stress","Horn scale (scale of predicate strength)","quantity implicature","Q-based implicature","question–answer congruence"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"(Aa) might be an answer to (Qa), but probably not to (Qb). Conversely, (Ab) might be an answer to (Qb), but probably not to (Qa). (23) Qa. Who cut Bill down to size? Qb. Who did Mary cut down to size? … Aa. MARY cut Bill down to size. Ab. Mary cut BILL down to size.","phenomenon":"question–answer congruence","aliases":["answer"],"near":["contrastive topic","association with focus","scalar implicature"],"avoid":["contrastive focus"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"(48) a. she beats ME more often than Sue (= than she beats Sue) b. SHE beats me more often than Sue (= than Sue beats me)","phenomenon":"bare remnant ellipsis","near":["VP ellipsis","sluicing","sloppy identity reading"],"avoid":["quantifier scope ambiguity","symmetric contrastive focus","contrastive focus","association with focus","bare argument ellipsis","comparative ellipsis"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf02342617"},{"id":"hamblin1973-questions-montague-english","title":"Questions in Montague English","authors":["C. L. Hamblin"],"year":"1973","area":"language","topic":"questions & focus","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Pragmatically speaking a question sets up a choice-situation between a set of propositions, namely, those propositions that count as answers to it.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"a unified set of rules can be constructed quite simply if indicatives are regarded as equivalent to one-alternative or Hobson's-choice interrogatives.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A question denotes the set of propositions that would answer it, true and false alike, and a statement is the limiting case of a question with exactly one answer, so one compositional semantics covers both.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Expressions denote sets of denotations, combined pointwise: for name a and verb b, ⟦a b⟧ = {f(x) : f ∈ ⟦b⟧, x ∈ ⟦a⟧}. Indicatives denote singletons, e.g. ⟦Mary⟧ = {m}; a question denotes its possible answers.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"denotation-set","aliases":["denotation-sets"],"definition":"What every expression, indicative or interrogative, is assigned in place of a single semantic value: a set of such values — a unit set for an indicative ('Mary' stands for {Mary}), and for a question the set of propositions that count as its answers.","exact":false},{"id":"t2","term":"joint image","aliases":["images"],"definition":"Formally a‴b = Df{c : (∃d∈a, e∈b)(c = d(e))}. We might call a‴b the joint image of b by a: it is the union of the set of images d″b of b by members d of a.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"Hobson's-choice interrogative","aliases":["Hobson's-choice interrogatives","Hobson's choice","one-alternative interrogative","one-alternative interrogatives"],"definition":"A question whose denotation-set has exactly one member, so that it offers only one possible answer; indicatives are treated as such, which lets one unified set of rules cover statements and questions.","exact":false},{"id":"t4","term":"answer","aliases":["answers","answer-relation"],"definition":"Semantically, a statement whose denotation, on a suitable reading, is one of the propositions in the question's denotation-set; there may also be syntactic requirements, e.g. that it be formed from the question by substituting indicative terms for the interrogative ones.","exact":false},{"id":"t5","term":"interrogative proper name","aliases":["interrogative proper names","interrogative proper noun","interrogative proper nouns","interrogative proper-nouns","proper names","proper nouns"],"definition":"although we are inclined to class 'who' and 'what' with proper names we cannot by any stretch regard them as denoting individuals. … they can be regarded as denoting sets of individuals, namely the set of humans and the set of non-humans respectively.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Prefixing 'is it the case that' to a formula such as 'John sleeps' yields an expression whose denotation-set has just two members: the proposition that John sleeps and the proposition that John sleeps not. So 'is it the case that' is given the denotation-set {I, the denotation of 'not'}, where I is the identity function.","phenomenon":"polar (yes/no) question","aliases":["yes-no","yes/no"],"near":["constituent (wh-) question","multiple wh-question","echo question"],"avoid":["alternative question"],"exact":false},{"id":"e3","text":"Questions such as 'John does what' or 'Rover is a what' keep the order of the corresponding statement. In many languages, for example all Melanesian ones, word-order is always that of the corresponding indicative and there is not even a distinct inflexion.","phenomenon":"wh-in-situ","aliases":["in situ"],"near":["wh-movement (wh-fronting)","subject-auxiliary inversion","pied-piping"],"avoid":["echo question"],"exact":false}],"doi":null},{"id":"katz1963-structure-semantic-theory","title":"The Structure of a Semantic Theory","authors":["Jerrold J. Katz","Jerry A. Fodor"],"year":"1963","area":"language","topic":"theory of meaning & truth conditions","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the semantic relations among the various senses of a lexical item and among the various senses of different lexical items are represented by formal relations between markers and distinguishers","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the doctrine that the meaning of a sentence is a function of the meanings of its parts. The system of projection rules is just this function.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"With k the number of readings the dictionary and projection rules assign to S: k = 0 → S is anomalous; k = 1 → unambiguous; k > 1 → k-ways ambiguous; S and P share a reading → paraphrases.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Explaining speakers' grasp of novel sentences requires a dictionary decomposing each lexical item's senses into semantic markers and distinguishers, plus projection rules that amalgamate senses bottom-up through the syntactic tree wherever selection restrictions are satisfied.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"projection problem","aliases":["project","problem"],"definition":"the speaker's knowledge of his language takes the form of rules which project the finite set of sentences he has fortuitously encountered to the infinite set of sentences of the language. … The problem of formulating these rules we shall refer to as the projection problem.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"projection rules","aliases":["projection rule","rules"],"definition":"rules for applying the information in the dictionary—rules which take account of semantic relations between morphemes and of the interaction between meaning and syntactic structure in determining the correct semantic interpretation for any of the infinitely many sentences which the grammar generates.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"semantic marker","aliases":["semantic markers","semantic"],"definition":"Semantic markers are the elements in terms of which semantic relations are expressed in a theory. … The semantic markers assigned to a lexical item … are intended to reflect whatever systematic semantic relations hold between that item and the rest of the vocabulary","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"distinguisher","aliases":["distinguishers"],"definition":"the distinguishers assigned to a lexical item are intended to reflect what is idiosyncratic about its meaning. … a change in a distinguisher merely alters the relation between one item and its synonyms.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"amalgamation","aliases":["Amalgamation"],"definition":"Amalgamation is the joining of elements from different sets of paths under a given grammatical marker if these elements satisfy the appropriate selection restrictions represented by the material in angles.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"semantic anomaly","aliases":["Semantic anomaly"],"definition":"Semantic anomaly can then be construed as the limiting case of selection: the case where there is a lexical item in a sentence whose paths are ALL excluded by selections due to other material in the sentence.","exact":true},{"id":"t7","term":"selection restriction","aliases":["selection restrictions"],"definition":"left and right angles enclosing a function of syntactic or semantic markers. Such configurations of symbols will be affixed to the terminal element of a path … and will be construed, relative to the projection rules, as providing a necessary and sufficient condition for a semantically acceptable combination.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose S is the sentence The bill is large. Speakers of English will agree that this sentence is ambiguous, i.e. that it has at least two readings. According to one it means that some document demanding a sum of money to discharge a debt exceeds in size most such documents; according to the other it means that the beak of a certain bird exceeds in bulk those of most similar birds.","phenomenon":"lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","aliases":["ambiguous"],"near":["structural (syntactic) ambiguity","vagueness","semantic anomaly"],"avoid":["homonymy (lexical ambiguity)","lexical ambiguity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Now let S be the sentence The paint is silent. English speakers will at once recognize that this sentence is anomalous in some way. For example, they will distinguish it from such sentences as The paint is wet and The paint is yellow by applying to it such epithets as 'odd', 'peculiar', 'paradoxical', and 'bizarre'.","phenomenon":"semantic anomaly","aliases":["anomalous"],"near":["ungrammaticality","contradiction (analytic falsehood)","pleonasm (redundancy)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Consider (1) Our store sells alligator shoes and (2) Our store sells horse shoes. In normal settings … occurrences of (1) will be taken on the reading 'our store sells shoes made from alligator skins' while (2) will be taken on the reading 'our store sells shoes for horses'. Notice, however, that (1) is open to the reading 'our store sells shoes for alligators' and (2) is open to the reading 'our store sells shoes made from the skin of horses'.","phenomenon":"disambiguation by world knowledge","near":["disambiguation by selection restrictions","disambiguation by grammatical category","conversational implicature"],"avoid":["saturation of a linguistically mandated slot","semantic underdetermination (completion)","free pragmatic enrichment"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"The limiting case, where the addition to the compound of semantic material from the modifier is zero, is of considerable theoretical significance. The compound unmarried bachelor is a case in point. … in forming the compound unmarried bachelor all the semantic information in the path of the modifier unmarried will be deleted so that the derived path for unmarried bachelor will contain no more than the semantic material which comes from the path for bachelor.","phenomenon":"pleonasm (redundancy)","near":["contradiction (analytic falsehood)","semantic anomaly","oxymoron"],"avoid":["non-logical analytic truth","violation of the non-redundancy (informativity) constraint on assertion"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/411200"},{"id":"yalcin2007-epistemic-modals","title":"Epistemic Modals","authors":["Seth Yalcin"],"year":"2007","area":"language","topic":"modality","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Epistemic possibility modals simply effect existential quantification over the set of worlds provided by the information parameter. No covert material is assumed, and no accessibility relation is appealed to.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Like Moore-paradoxical sentences, epistemic contradictions are not assertable; but unlike Moore-paradoxical sentences, they are also not supposable, not entertainable as true.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"⟦◇φ⟧(c,s,w) = 1 iff ∃w′ ∈ s: ⟦φ⟧(c,s,w′) = 1, s being an information state (set of worlds) in the index; 'suppose' and 'if'-clauses shift s, and no state accepts ¬φ ∧ ◇φ, so such conjunctions cannot be supposed.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"'Might φ' describes no one's evidence: it quantifies over an information state that is a parameter of the index, not worlds accessible from the world of evaluation; saying it proposes that the common ground leave φ open.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"epistemic contradiction","aliases":["epistemic contradictions"],"definition":"A conjunction of the form (φ ∧ ◇¬φ) or (¬φ ∧ ◇φ), with ◇ a 'might'/'possibly' modal — e.g. 'It is raining and it might not be raining': unassertable and, unlike a Moore-paradoxical sentence, not even coherently supposable.","exact":false},{"id":"t2","term":"domain semantics","aliases":["domain"],"definition":"In addition to a world parameter, let our index include also an information parameter s. This coordinate will range over bodies of information … Our plan is to use this new s parameter to supply the domain of quantification for epistemic modal clauses.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"informational consequence","aliases":["consequence","information"],"definition":"The notion of consequence I have in mind preserves, not truth, but a different property of sentences in context—one they have in relation to a state of information. … Now we can define a notion of consequence according to which consequence preserves acceptance.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"conversational tone","definition":"An attitude is the conversational tone of a group of interlocutors just in case it is common knowledge in the group that everyone is to strike this attitude towards the propositions which are common ground.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"credal expressivism","aliases":["expresses"],"definition":"in saying that it is probably raining, one thereby expresses one's credence in the proposition that it is raining—where this is not the same as saying that one's credence in the proposition is thus and so.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"(7) # Suppose it is raining and it might not be raining (8) # Suppose it is not raining and it might be raining … The resulting imperatival sentences are not acceptable. Indeed they are not even obviously intelligible. … (13) # If it is raining and it might not be raining, then (still) it is raining —strikes us as unintelligible rather than trivially true","phenomenon":"epistemic contradiction","aliases":["epistemic contradictions"],"near":["Moore's paradox","Fitch's paradox of knowability","Frege–Geach problem"],"avoid":["epistemic modality"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Suppose my guard dog Fido hears a noise downstairs and goes to check it out. You ask me why Fido suddenly left the room. I say: (20) Fido thinks there might be an intruder downstairs That is good English. What does it mean? Does it mean, as a relational semantics requires, that Fido believes that it is compatible with what Fido believes that there is an intruder downstairs? That is not plausible.","phenomenon":"epistemic modal embedded under an attitude verb","near":["de re attitude report","circumstantial (root) modality","factive attitude ascription"],"avoid":["epistemic modality"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"(21) Cheerios may reduce the risk of heart disease (22) Late Antarctic spring might be caused by ozone depletion We tend to hear these sentences as (not just making certain possibilities explicit but) serving to communicate real information. Indeed, they strike us as the result of some actual research.","phenomenon":"objective (expert-relative) epistemic modality","near":["subjective epistemic modality","deontic modality","metaphysical (alethic) possibility"],"avoid":["epistemic modality","circumstantial (root) modality","dispositional (circumstantial) modality","context-supplied conversational background (modal base)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"Either the butler or the gardener did it. Therefore: (C1) If the butler did not do it, the gardener did. (C2) If the butler did not do it, the gardener must have. (C1) and (C2) sound semantically equivalent.","phenomenon":"if-clause restricting a modal","near":["modal subordination","conditional perfection","if-clause restricting a quantificational adverb"],"avoid":["quantifier domain restriction","restrictor analysis of conditionals","or-to-if inference","epistemic modality"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1093/mind/fzm983"},{"id":"searle1976-classification-illocutionary-acts","title":"A Classification of Illocutionary Acts","authors":["John Searle"],"year":"1976","area":"language","topic":"speech acts & assertion","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"What I propose to do is take illocutionary point, and its corollaries, direction of fit and expressed sincerity conditions, as the basis for constructing a classification.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"There are not, as Wittgenstein (on one possible interpretation) and many others have claimed, an infinite or indefinite number of language games or uses of language.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Against Austin's unprincipled, verb-based scheme, there are just five basic kinds of speech act — representatives, directives, commissives, expressives, declarations — distinguished chiefly by their point or purpose, with direction of fit and expressed psychological state as corollaries.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Representatives have word-to-world fit; directives and commissives world-to-word, one trying to get the hearer to act, the other committing the speaker; expressives no direction of fit, their content being presupposed; declarations both, successful performance itself creating the fit.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"illocutionary point","aliases":["point","illocutionary","illocution"],"definition":"The point or purpose of a type of illocution I shall call its illocutionary point. Illocutionary point is part of but not the same as illocutionary force. … the illocutionary point of request is the same as that of commands","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"direction of fit","definition":"Some illocutions have as part of their illocutionary point to get the words (more strictly—their propositional content) to match the world, others to get the world to match the words. … I propose to call this difference a difference in direction of fit.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"representatives","aliases":["representative"],"definition":"The point or purpose of the members of the representative class is to commit the speaker (in varying degrees) to something's being the case, to the truth of the expressed proposition.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"directives","definition":"The illocutionary point of these consists in the fact that they are attempts … by the speaker to get the hearer to do something. … The propositional content is always that the hearer H does some future action A.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"commissives","definition":"those illocutionary acts whose point is to commit the speaker (again in varying degrees) to some future course of action. … The direction of fit is world-to-words and the sincerity condition is Intention.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"expressives","aliases":["expressive","express"],"definition":"The illocutionary point of this class is to express the psychological state specified in the sincerity condition about a state of affairs specified in the propositional content. … in expressives there is no direction of fit.","exact":true},{"id":"t7","term":"declarations","aliases":["declaration"],"definition":"It is the defining characteristic of this class that the successful performance of one of its members brings about the correspondence between the propositional content and reality … if I successfully perform the act of appointing you chairman, then you are chairman","exact":true,"avoid":["strawson1964-intention-convention-speech/t3","langton1993-speech-acts-unspeakable/t5"]},{"id":"t8","term":"representative declarations","aliases":["representative","declarations"],"definition":"Some institutions require representative claims to be issued with the force of declarations in order that the argument over the truth of the claim can come to an end somewhere","exact":true,"avoid":["strawson1964-intention-convention-speech/t3"]}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose a man goes to the supermarket with a shopping list … he is followed by a detective who writes down everything he takes. As they emerge from the store both shopper and detective will have identical lists. … If the detective gets home and suddenly realizes that the man bought pork chops instead of bacon, he can simply erase the word 'bacon' and write 'pork chops'. But if the shopper gets home … he cannot correct the mistake by erasing 'bacon' from the list","phenomenon":"direction of fit","near":["sincerity condition","propositional content condition","degree of strength of illocutionary point"],"avoid":["the Frege point (force–content distinction)","force/content distinction","illocutionary force vs propositional content"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"when I apologize for having stepped on your toe, it is not my purpose either to claim that your toe was stepped on or to get it stepped on. … One cannot say: *I apologize that I stepped on your toe; rather the correct English is, I apologize for stepping on your toe.","phenomenon":"expressive speech act","near":["representative (assertive) speech act","directive speech act","declaration"],"avoid":["performative utterance","explicit performative utterance","explicit performative","self-fulfilling (self-verifying) performatives","factive presupposition"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Both, the judge and the umpire, make factual claims; 'you are out', 'you are guilty'. Such claims are clearly assessable in the dimension of word-world fit. Was he really tagged off base? Did he really commit the crime? … If the umpire calls you out (and is upheld on appeal), then for baseball purposes you are out regardless of the facts in the case, and if the judge declares you guilty (on appeal), then for legal purposes you are guilty.","phenomenon":"representative declaration","aliases":["declares"],"near":["misfire (void performative act)","commissive speech act","expressive speech act"],"avoid":["declaration","verdictive illocution","verdictive","conventional (institutional) illocutionary act","institutional fact","representative (assertive) speech act","performative utterance","explicit performative","self-fulfilling (self-verifying) performatives"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"'I resign', 'You're fired', 'I excommunicate you', 'I christen this ship, the battleship Missouri', 'I appoint you chairman'","phenomenon":"declaration","near":["verdictive illocution","commissive speech act","directive speech act"],"avoid":["performative utterance","explicit performative utterance","explicit performative","self-fulfilling (self-verifying) performatives","conventional (institutional) illocutionary act","essentially conventional illocutionary act","institutional fact","exercitive"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"If the general asks the private to clean up the room, that is in all likelihood a command or an order. If the private asks the general to clean up the room, that is likely to be a suggestion or proposal or request but not an order or command.","phenomenon":"preparatory condition (speaker–hearer status)","near":["sincerity condition","propositional content condition","essential condition"],"avoid":["indirect speech act","indirect request","authoritative illocution","conventional (institutional) illocutionary act"],"exact":true},{"id":"e6","text":"I can insist that we go to the movies or I can suggest that we go to the movies; but I can also insist that the answer is found on p. 16 or I can suggest that it is found on p. 16. The first pair are directives, the second, representatives.","phenomenon":"degree of strength of illocutionary point","near":["style of performance of an illocution","relation to the rest of the discourse","preparatory condition (speaker–hearer status)"],"avoid":["hedging","hedge","Horn scale (scale of predicate strength)","direction of fit"],"exact":true},{"id":"e7","text":"One may announce orders, promises and reports, but announcing is not on all fours with ordering, promising and reporting. … An announcement is never just an announcement. It must also be a statement, order, etc.","phenomenon":"style of performance of an illocution","near":["degree of strength of illocutionary point","relation to the rest of the discourse","perlocutionary act"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1017/s0047404500006837"},{"id":"austin1961-performative-utterances","title":"Performative Utterances","authors":["J. L. Austin"],"year":"1961","area":"language","topic":"speech acts & assertion","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"when I say 'I do' (sc. take this woman to be my lawful wedded wife), I am not reporting on a marriage, I am indulging in it","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"What we need besides the old doctrine about meanings is a new doctrine about all the possible forces of utterances","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Some utterances that look like first-person statements, such as 'I bet' or 'I name this ship', are neither true nor false but perform acts, and they go wrong as infelicities: misfires that void the act, or abuses such as insincerity.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"The doing/stating contrast breaks down: statements suffer the same infelicities (Moore's paradox as avowed insincerity, reference failure as voidness), so stating is itself a speech act, while warnings and verdicts are assessed against the facts.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"performative utterance","aliases":["performative","performatives","performative utterances","utterance"],"definition":"a kind of utterance which looks like a statement … which is not nonsensical, and yet is not true or false. … if a person makes an utterance of this sort we should say that he is doing something rather than merely saying something.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"infelicity","aliases":["infelicities","infelicitous"],"definition":"The various ways in which a performative utterance may be unsatisfactory we call, for the sake of a name, the infelicities; and an infelicity arises—that is to say, the utterance is unhappy—if certain rules, transparently simple rules, are broken.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"misfire","aliases":["misfires"],"definition":"the convention invoked must exist and be accepted … the circumstances … must be appropriate for its invocation. If this is not observed, then the act that we purport to perform would not come off—it will be, one might say, a misfire.","exact":true,"avoid":["langton1993-speech-acts-unspeakable/t1"]},{"id":"t4","term":"abuse","aliases":["abused","abuses"],"definition":"if you use one of these formulae when you do not have the requisite thoughts or feelings or intentions then there is an abuse of the procedure, there is insincerity. … We should not say that I didn't in fact promise, but rather that I did promise but promised insincerely","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"explicit performative utterance","aliases":["explicit performative","explicit performative utterances","utterance","explicit"],"definition":"any utterance that was reduced to one or the other of our standard forms we could call an explicit performative utterance … In issuing an explicit performative utterance we are not stating what act it is, we are showing or making explicit what act it is.","exact":true,"avoid":["strawson1964-intention-convention-speech/t4"]},{"id":"t6","term":"force","aliases":["forces"],"definition":"besides the question … as to what a certain utterance means, there is a further question distinct from this as to what was the force, as we may call it, of the utterance … whether as uttered at a certain time it was an order, an entreaty or whatnot.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"suppose that in the course of a marriage ceremony I say, as people will, 'I do'—(sc. take this woman to be my lawful wedded wife). Or again, suppose that I tread on your toe and say 'I apologize'. Or again, suppose that I have the bottle of champagne in my hand and say 'I name this ship the Queen Elizabeth'. Or suppose I say 'I bet you sixpence it will rain tomorrow'.","phenomenon":"performative utterance","aliases":["performative"],"near":["constative utterance","perlocutionary act","indirect speech act"],"avoid":["explicit performative","self-fulfilling (self-verifying) performatives","conventional (institutional) illocutionary act","declaration","institutional fact"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose that you are just about to name the ship, you have been appointed to name it, and you are just about to bang the bottle against the stem; but at that very moment some low type comes up, snatches the bottle out of your hand, breaks it on the stem, shouts out 'I name this ship the Generalissimo Stalin', and then for good measure kicks away the chocks. … We agree that the ship certainly isn't now named the Generalissimo Stalin","phenomenon":"misfire (void performative act)","aliases":["misfire"],"near":["abuse (insincere performative)","breach of commitment","misunderstanding (failure of uptake)"],"avoid":["performative utterance","explicit performative","conventional (institutional) illocutionary act"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"If I say 'I congratulate you' when I'm not pleased or when I don't believe that the credit was yours, then there is insincerity. Likewise if I say I promise to do something, without having the least intention of doing it or without believing it feasible.","phenomenon":"abuse (insincere performative)","aliases":["insincerely","insincere","insincerity","abuse"],"near":["misfire (void performative act)","breach of commitment","parasitic (non-serious) use of language"],"avoid":["performative utterance","explicit performative"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"there is something very odd about saying something like this: 'The cat is on the mat but I don't believe it is'. Now this is an outrageous thing to say, but it is not self-contradictory. There is no reason why the cat shouldn't be on the mat without my believing that it is.","phenomenon":"Moore's paradox","aliases":["Moore"],"near":["self-contradiction","epistemic contradiction","preface paradox"],"avoid":["abuse (insincere performative)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"somebody says 'All John's children are bald', when as a matter of fact—he doesn't say so—John has no children. Now those who study statements have worried about this; ought they to say that the statement 'All John's children are bald' is meaningless in this case?","phenomenon":"presupposition failure","aliases":["presupposition"],"near":["Moore's paradox","category mistake","presupposition accommodation"],"avoid":["reference failure","truth-value gap from reference failure","existential import of quantified noun phrases","misfire (void performative act)"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"carlson1977-unified-analysis-english","title":"A Unified Analysis of the English Bare Plural","authors":["Gregory N. Carlson"],"year":"1977","area":"language","topic":"generics & kinds","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"A brief analysis is sketched in which bare plurals are treated in all instances as proper names of kinds of things.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The indefinite plural reading arises whenever it is a dog-stage that something is predicated of. A dog-stage … is whatever realizes the kind dogs at a time and a place.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Determinerless plurals are neither the plural of 'a' nor ambiguous: they never take wide scope or transparent readings, can scope below what 'a' allows, and a generic use can antecede an existential one, or vice versa.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"'dogs' ↦ λP P{d}, d the kind; a 'property' applies to d directly ('Dogs are intelligent' = I(d)), but a 'state' or progressive applies to a stage: 'Dogs are running' = ∃x[R(x,d) ∧ run′(x)], R = 'realizes'.","formal":true}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"stage","aliases":["stages","Jake-stages"],"definition":"Suppose we take an individual, Jake, and look at him as being composed of a set of Jake-stages, or temporally-bounded portions of Jake's existence. … stages are conceived of as being much more closely related to events than to objects.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"kind","aliases":["kinds"],"definition":"kinds are to be construed as individuals. Of course, these individuals are a little different from more normal individuals in that kinds can be here and there, whereas normal individuals are generally confined to one location … at a given time.","exact":true,"avoid":["putnam1970-semantics-possible/t1"]},{"id":"t3","term":"differentiated scope","aliases":["differentiated","scope"],"definition":"the scope possibilities of 'a' and [the null determiner] are differentiated, and it is not the case that one exhibits simply a subset of the readings allowed by the other. … Under certain circumstances [an NP with the null determiner] can have narrower scope than the indefinite singular possibly can.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"generic sentence","aliases":["generic"],"definition":"We might try, then, to define a 'generic sentence' as any sentence that attributes a property to the individual that serves as the subject of the sentence, and not to one of that individual's realizations.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Minnie wishes to talk with a young psychiatrist. On one reading, there is some particular young psychiatrist that Minnie has in mind, and she wishes to speak with him. … On the other reading … Minnie's desires are fulfilled by talking to anyone, so long as that person is a young psychiatrist. … Minnie wishes to talk with young psychiatrists. However, [this] does not have both of these readings; the [first] reading … is absent.","phenomenon":"obligatory narrow scope of bare plurals","near":["exceptional wide scope of indefinites","scope island effect","inverse scope reading"],"avoid":["de re / de dicto ambiguity","de dicto vs de re reading","quantifier scope ambiguity","merely de dicto (notional) belief","referential opacity","non-specific reading of an intensional transitive verb","de dicto vs de re modality"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Mick traps lemmings even though he knows full well that they are protected by law. … Lemmings are protected by law, but Mick goes ahead and traps them anyway.","phenomenon":"anaphora across generic and existential uses","near":["zeugma (crossed readings of an ambiguous word)","bound-variable pronoun","cataphora (backward anaphora)"],"avoid":["cross-sentential (discourse) anaphora","cross-sentential anaphora","discourse anaphora"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"(126) a. Soldiers were available. b. Dentists were drunk. c. Frogs are awake. (127) a. Soldiers are brave. b. Dentists were tall. c. Frogs are clever. In (126), the only possible interpretation of the subject is the indefinite plural, whereas in (127) only the generic reading is possible.","phenomenon":"stage-level vs. individual-level predicates","near":["intersective vs. subsective adjectives","telic vs. atelic predicates","collective vs. distributive predicates"],"avoid":["characterizing generic"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"[In the following,] it is clear that a universal quantifier or the quantifier 'most' would simply be inappropriate. … a. Horses are widespread. b. Horses are extinct. c. Horses are indigenous to eastern Chile.","phenomenon":"kind-level predication","near":["characterizing generic","stage-level predication","universal quantification over members"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00353456"},{"id":"brandom1983-asserting","title":"Asserting","authors":["Robert Brandom"],"year":"1983","area":"language","topic":"speech acts & assertion","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"In asserting a claim one not only authorizes further assertions, but commits oneself to vindicate the original claim, showing that one is entitled to make it.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The meanings do not determine the appropriate inferences, but what inferences are socially appropriate determines the meanings of the sentences involved in those inferences.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Making a claim institutes a social status with two dimensions: it licenses others to rely on and re-assert it, deferring challenges back to the speaker, and commits the speaker to justify it if challenged.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Taking another's claim as justified is recognizing its authority to license further claims; taking it as true is being prepared to assert it and its consequences oneself. The difference is perspectival: others' legitimate commitments versus one's own.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"inference license","aliases":["inference licenses","assertion license"],"definition":"asserting is issuing an inference license. Since inferring is drawing a conclusion, such an inference license amounts to a warrant for further assertions, specifically assertions of those sentences which can appropriately be inferred from the sentence originally asserted.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"bare assertion","aliases":["bare assertions"],"definition":"bare assertions, which involve no offer to defend a claim, operating solely as licenses authorizing inferences to further claims.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"deferral","aliases":["deferring","deferrals","defer"],"definition":"An assertion in force licenses others to re-assert the original claim (and to assert its immediate consequences) deferring to the author of the original assertion the justificatory responsibility which would otherwise thereby be undertaken.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"extended repertoire","aliases":["repertoire"],"definition":"there is also an extended repertoire consisting of all those sentences others are prepared to take that individual as committed to. In general the speaker need not be aware of all that he or she has become socially committed to in virtue of the commitments explicitly undertaken.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"The sentence 'Saturn is larger than Mars' has the logical form of a predication. In the conditional 'If Saturn is larger than Mars, then it is larger than Mercury' … though the same predication occurs, it is not asserted that Saturn is larger than Mars.","phenomenon":"the Frege point (force–content distinction)","near":["Frege's puzzle about informative identity","presupposition projection","use–mention distinction"],"avoid":["Frege–Geach problem"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"a parrot trained reliably to say \"It's getting warmer\" only as the temperature … climbs past 80 degrees never succeeds in asserting that it is getting warmer. However well the bird's responsive dispositions to produce this report match our own, it is incompetent as an assertor. The parrot cannot tell justifications of his remark from arguments against it, and cannot discriminate the further assertions which would thereby be licensed from those incompatible with what it has said.","phenomenon":"reliable differential responsiveness without conceptual understanding","aliases":["reliably","responsive dispositions"],"near":["bare assertion","insincere assertion","deferential use of a term"],"avoid":["language entry transition","pattern-governed behavior","natural meaning"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"I may be willing to accept as justified your claim that there is a candle in front of you, given your information. Yet if I accept, as you do not, that there is a mirror in front of you, I will not be prepared to endorse your claim as true, that is to make inferences from it myself.","phenomenon":"the justification–truth distinction","aliases":["justified","true"],"near":["Gettier case","bare assertion","Moore's paradox"],"avoid":["blameless (excusable) norm violation","defective context"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"in Football a referee confers the social status \"Offsides\" on a performance by responding to it in a special way. That special response gets its significance in turn from its being appropriately responded to on the part of other officials by their imposition of a 5-yard penalty.","phenomenon":"institutional fact","near":["brute fact","natural kind","regulative rule"],"avoid":["verdictive illocution","conventional (institutional) illocutionary act","declaration","representative declaration","performative utterance","explicit performative","self-verifying explicit performatives","self-fulfilling (self-verifying) performatives"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2215086"},{"id":"grice1978-further-notes-logic","title":"Further Notes on Logic and Conversation","authors":["H. P. Grice"],"year":"1978","area":"language","topic":"implicature","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I would like to propose for acceptance a principle which I might call Modified Occam's Razor, ‘Senses are not to be multiplied beyond necessity.’","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"given a truth-functional ‘or’ it is predictable (assuming conversational principles) that people would use ‘A or B’ to imply the existence of non-truth-functional grounds.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A word should not be credited with an extra sense when the extra content is predictable from the maxims: 'or' is simply truth-functional, and its suggestion that the speaker has non-truth-functional grounds is implicated via Quantity and Quality.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Stress, irony and the endorsing force of 'true' carry no conventional meaning: stress implicates via relevance extended to how things are said; irony is pretence expressing hostility; 'It is true that p' implicates someone said, or might say, p.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"Modified Occam's Razor","aliases":["Occam's Razor","Occam"],"definition":"Senses are not to be multiplied beyond necessity. … One might think, for example, of not allowing the supposition that a word has a further (and derivative) sense unless the supposition that there is such a sense does some work","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"cancelability","aliases":["cancelable"],"definition":"a putative conversational implicature that p is explicitly cancelable if … it is admissible to add ‘but not p’, or ‘I do not mean to imply that p’, and it is contextually cancelable if one can find situations in which the utterance of the form of words would simply not carry the implicature.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"nondetachability","aliases":["nondetachable"],"definition":"The implicature is nondetachable insofar as it is not possible to find another way of saying the same thing (or approximately the same thing) which simply lacks the implicature.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"irony","aliases":["ironically","ironical","ironic"],"definition":"irony is intimately connected with the expression of a feeling, attitude, or evaluation. I cannot say something ironically unless what I say is intended to reflect a hostile or derogatory judgment … To be ironical is, among other things, to pretend","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose that it is generally known that New York and Boston were blacked out last night, and A asks B whether C saw a particular TV program last night. It will be conversationally unobjectionable for B, who knows that C was in New York, to reply, “No, he was in a blacked-out city.”","phenomenon":"interaction of maxims (Relation over Quantity)","near":["clash between maxims (Quantity versus Quality)","flouting the maxim of Quantity","conventional implicature"],"avoid":["implicature from observing the maxim of Relation","particularized conversational implicature","conversational implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose that two people are considering the purchase of a tie which both of them know to be medium green; they look at it in different lights, and say such things as “It is a light green now” … Strictly (perhaps) it would be correct for them to say “It looks light green now” … but it would be unnecessary to put in such qualificatory words, since both know (and know that the other knows) that there is no question of a real change of color.","phenomenon":"loose (relaxed) use of an expression","near":["contextual cancellation of an implicature","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","verbal irony"],"avoid":["context-dependent standards of precision"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"I can say to my children at some stage in a treasure hunt, “The prize is either in the garden or in the attic. I know that because I know where I put it, but I'm not going to tell you.”","phenomenon":"explicit cancellation of an implicature","near":["loose (relaxed) use of an expression","presupposition accommodation","conventional implicature"],"avoid":["ignorance implicature of a disjunction","conversational implicature","scalar implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"One would normally implicate that there was a failure, or some chance of failure, or that someone thinks or thought there to be some chance of failure, if one said ‘A tried to do x’; this implicature would also be carried if one said ‘A attempted to do x’, ‘A endeavored to do x’, or ‘A set himself to do x’.","phenomenon":"nondetachability of an implicature","aliases":["implicate","implicature"],"near":["cancelability of an implicature","conventional implicature","loose (relaxed) use of an expression"],"avoid":["generalized conversational implicature","conversational implicature","scalar implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"Without a preceding statement to the effect that Jones paid the bill, B says “JONES didn't pay the bill; SMITH did.” … the remark is not prompted by a previous remark (it is volunteered)","phenomenon":"implicature from contrastive stress","near":["scalar implicature","conventional implicature","presupposition triggered by a cleft"],"avoid":["association with focus","contrastive focus","symmetric contrastive focus","particularized conversational implicature","conversational implicature","presupposition accommodation"],"exact":true},{"id":"e6","text":"“The policeman's statement was true” … the utterer may not … know what that statement was; he may think that the policeman's statement was true because policemen always speak the truth … When to my surprise I learn that the policeman actually said, “Monkeys can talk,” I say (perhaps), “Well, I was wrong,” not “I withdraw that” … I never was committed to it.","phenomenon":"blind truth ascription","near":["Liar paradox","Moore's paradox","redundancy (Ramsey) use of 'true'"],"avoid":["convention of truthfulness","convention of truthfulness and trust","truth as the aim of assertion"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1163/9789004368873_006"},{"id":"evans1977-pronouns","title":"Pronouns, Quantifiers, and Relative Clauses (I)","authors":["Gareth Evans"],"year":"1977","area":"language","topic":"dynamic semantics & anaphora","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"some pronouns with quantifier antecedents are quite unlike bound variables; … they may be assigned a reference and their immediate sentential contexts can be evaluated independently for truth and falsehood.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"In the sentence ‘John owns a donkey and beats it’, … ‘it’ has the function of designating the object (if any) that verifies the antecedent clause.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Pronouns can simply co-refer with singular antecedents, Fregean quantifier clauses then handling bound pronouns for free; but pronouns not governed by their quantifier antecedents are singular terms whose reference is fixed by a description drawn from the antecedent clause.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"In antecedent sentence A(Qᵢ[δ: B(WH)]) (main clause A, common noun δ, restricting relative clause B if any), the E-type pronoun #κᵢ denotes x iff x is the unique object satisfying A(_), δ and B(_).","formal":true}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"E-type pronoun","aliases":["E-type pronouns","E-type","pronouns","pronoun"],"definition":"there are pronouns with quantifier antecedents that function in a quite different way. Such pronouns typically stand in a different grammatical relation to their antecedents, and, in contrast with bound pronouns, must be assigned a reference, so that their most immediate sentential contexts can always be assigned a truth value.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"co-referential treatment","definition":"a simple principle according to which a pronoun refers to whatever its singular antecedent refers to. Such an account is entirely adequate … in the sense that no further explanation of the functioning of pronouns with quantifier antecedents is called for.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"in construction with","definition":"Roughly, and not inflexibly, the scope of a quantifier is naturally interpreted as constituting the smallest sentence which includes all the constituents which are in construction with it. (Klima defines the notion ‘in construction with’ so that a constituent is in construction with another iff the former is dominated by the first branching node which dominates the latter.)","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"affirmative embedding","aliases":["affirmatively embedded"],"definition":"A sentence σ is affirmatively embedded in Σ relative to σ′ iff σ is true on every valuation on which σ′ and Σ are true and Σ would be false were σ′ false; so if σ′'s truth value matters, an E-type pronoun in σ′ looking back to σ's quantifier has a referent.","exact":false}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"John owns some sheep and Harry vaccinates them. … Upon the most natural interpretation, the sentence would not be true unless Harry vaccinates all the sheep which John owns.","phenomenon":"E-type pronoun","near":["bound-variable pronoun","paycheck pronoun","referential use of an indefinite"],"avoid":["E-type anaphora","cross-sentential anaphora","discourse anaphora","donkey anaphora","reference fixing by description"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Most men who own a car wash it on Sundays … If the sentence is to express the intended restriction upon the major quantifier … it would appear that the second quantifier must be given a scope which does not extend beyond the relative clause","phenomenon":"donkey anaphora","near":["bound-variable pronoun","quantifier scope ambiguity","cross-sentential anaphora"],"avoid":["E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora","donkey sentence","unselective binding","proportional quantifier (not first-order definable)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"What could explain our unwillingness to express the thought: “Some finger is such that it is Socrates’ and it hurts Socrates” by uttering “Socrates has a finger and it hurts Socrates”? What can explain our reluctance to report the existence of at least one woman doctor in Manchester by uttering “There is a doctor in Manchester and she is a woman”?","phenomenon":"uniqueness implication of E-type pronouns","near":["existence presupposition","pronoun of laziness","referential (specific) indefinite"],"avoid":["E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora","uniqueness presupposition","maximality","failure of uniqueness"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"‘*Either John owns a car or he drives it on Sunday’ … in view of the well-formedness of sentences like ‘Either John does not own a donkey or he keeps it very quiet’ … it is too stringent to insist that the sentence containing the quantifier antecedent should be embedded in the whole sentence in such a way that the truth of the whole sentence should require its truth.","phenomenon":"bathroom sentence","near":["modal subordination","telescoping","Bach–Peters sentence"],"avoid":["E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora","donkey anaphora","presupposition filtering","double negation anaphora","anaphora under double negation","symmetric presupposition filtering"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1080/00455091.1977.10717030"},{"id":"donnellan1970-proper-names-identifying","title":"Proper Names and Identifying Descriptions","authors":["Keith Donnellan"],"year":"1970","area":"language","topic":"reference & names","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"It seems clear to me that in some way the referent must be historically, or, we might say, causally connected to the speech act.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"my view is that what we should substitute for the question, 'What is the referent?' is 'What would the speaker be attributing [the] predicate to on this occasion?'","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A name can refer to something its users' associated descriptions fail to fit, and need not refer to what uniquely fits them; a single set of descriptions can even go with two different referents.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Instead of 'What fits the speaker's descriptions?', ask 'What is the speaker attributing the predicate to on this occasion?'; descriptions are evidence, not criteria, and the referent must be historically or causally connected to the utterance.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"avoid":["donnellan1974-speaking-nothing"],"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"principle of identifying descriptions","definition":"A two-stage thesis: a proper name's users must be able to supply a set of answers to 'To whom does the name refer?' (excluding ones like 'the entity I referred to'), and its referent, if any, is whatever uniquely fits a sufficient number of them.","exact":false,"avoid":["searle1958-proper-names/t2"]},{"id":"t2","term":"question-begging","definition":"Descriptions that fit the referent simply in virtue of the fact that the speaker did, in fact, refer to it or had it in mind as the object he meant to refer to are question-begging in answer to the question, 'who (or what) did you refer to?'","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"parasitic use of a name","aliases":["parasitic"],"definition":"our use of proper names for persons in history (and also those we are not personally acquainted with) is parasitic on uses of the names by other people - in conversation, written records, etc.","exact":true,"avoid":["evans1973-causal-theory-names/t3"]}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"a subject is seated before a screen … On the screen are painted two squares of identical size and color, one directly above the other. … he will call the top square 'alpha' and the bottom square 'beta'. … unknown to the subject, he has been fitted with spectacles that invert his visual field. Thus, the square he sees as apparently on top is really on the bottom and vice versa. … He might report, 'Alpha is now a different color'.","phenomenon":"argument from error against descriptivism","near":["modal argument against descriptivism","inverted spectrum","argument from ignorance against descriptivism"],"avoid":["referential use of a definite description","denotation without reference","descriptive name (name introduced by description)","descriptive name","speaker's reference diverging from a name's semantic reference","misidentification","reference surviving a false associated theory","stability of reference across theory change","causal theory of reference","causal-historical chain of reference","causal-historical determination of reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose a child is gotten up from sleep at a party and introduced to someone as 'Tom', who then says a few words to the child. Later the child says to his parents, \"Tom is a nice man\". The only thing he can say about 'Tom' is that Tom was at a party. Moreover, he is unable to recognize anyone as 'Tom' on subsequent occasions. His parents give lots of parties and they have numerous friends named 'Tom'.","phenomenon":"argument from ignorance against descriptivism","near":["argument from error against descriptivism","reference failure","epistemic argument against descriptivism"],"avoid":["contextual disambiguation of a shared name","causal theory of reference","causal-historical chain of reference","causal-historical determination of reference","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","disambiguation by world knowledge"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"A student meets a man he takes to be the famous philosopher, J. L. Aston-Martin. … In fact, however, although the student never suspects it, the man at the party is not the famous philosopher … He might begin by saying, \"Last night I met J. L. Aston-Martin and talked to him for almost an hour\". … \" … and then Robinson tripped over Aston-Martin's feet and fell flat on his face\"","phenomenon":"reference shift within a conversation","near":["Paderewski case (puzzle without translation)","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","argument from ignorance against descriptivism"],"avoid":["reference change of a proper name","misidentification","speaker's reference diverging from a name's semantic reference","referential use of a definite description","denotation without reference","argument from error against descriptivism","contextual disambiguation of a shared name","deferential use of a name","causal theory of reference","causal-historical chain of reference","causal-historical determination of reference","conflation (believing of two persons that they are one)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Suppose … that fortuitously [Aristotle's and Herodotus's] descriptions fitted uniquely someone they had never heard about and who was not referred to by any authors known to us. Such a person … is not 'our' Thales. Or … suppose no one to have held the ridiculous doctrine that all is water, but that Aristotle and Herodotus were referring to a real person … a well-digger with a reputation for saying wise things","phenomenon":"causal-historical chain of reference","near":["reference failure","reference change of a proper name","descriptive name"],"avoid":["causal-historical determination of reference","causal theory of reference","argument from error against descriptivism","reference surviving a false associated theory","stability of reference across theory change","accidental satisfier of a name's descriptions","deferential use of a name","denotation without reference"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00484804"},{"id":"donnellan1974-speaking-nothing","title":"Speaking of Nothing","authors":["Keith Donnellan"],"year":"1974","area":"language","topic":"reference & names","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"If N is a proper name … then ⌜N does not exist⌝ is true if and only if the history of [its uses] ends in a block.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"any theory that rejects the principle of identifying descriptions for predicative statements must also reject it for existence statements.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A true negative existential like 'Santa Claus does not exist' is true not because nothing satisfies the associated descriptions but because the historical explanation of the name's uses ends in a block, such as a fiction told as fact.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Negative existentials using different names, such as 'Santa Claus does not exist' and 'Père Noël n'existe pas', express the same proposition when the blocks in the names' histories are themselves historically connected, not through any shared referent.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"block","definition":"When the historical explanation of the use of a name (with the intention to refer) ends … with events that preclude any referent being identified, I will call it a \"block\" in the history.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"historical explanation theory","aliases":["historically correct explanation"],"definition":"when a speaker uses a name intending to refer to an individual and predicate something of it, successful reference will occur when there is an individual that enters into the historically correct explanation of who it is that the speaker intended to predicate something of.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"discourse about fiction","aliases":["discourse","fictional"],"definition":"Under \"discourse about fiction\" I mean to include those occasions on which it is a presupposition of the discourse that fictional, mythological, legendary (and so forth) persons, places, or things are under discussion.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"discourse about actuality","definition":"Discourse about actuality carries the presupposition that the speaker is talking about people, places, or things that occur in the history of our world.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose a child who believed in Santa Claus now learns the truth … he has learned that when in the past he believed something … which he would have expressed by saying, \"Santa Claus comes tonight,\" … the historical explanation of this belief does not involve any individual who could count as the referent of \"Santa Claus\"; rather it ends in a story given to him by his parents, a story told to him as factual.","phenomenon":"block in the chain of reference","aliases":["referent"],"near":["reference change of a proper name","discourse about fiction","argument from error against descriptivism"],"avoid":["empty name","negative existential statements","problem of negative existentials","predication of non-existent objects","reference failure","presupposition failure","absence of the referent","causal-historical chain of reference","causal-historical determination of reference","causal theory of reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"The Horn Papers … purported to contain the diary of one Jacob Horn and … would, if genuine, have shed light on the colonial history of Washington County, Pennsylvania. Many people believed them to be genuine, but … it seems likely that they are not and that Jacob Horn did not exist. There must have been many believers, however, who made statements using the name \"Jacob Horn\" with the intention of predicating various properties of a historical figure.","phenomenon":"empty name","near":["discourse about fiction","reference change of a proper name","argument from error against descriptivism"],"avoid":["block in the chain of reference","negative existential statements","problem of negative existentials","predication of non-existent objects","reference failure","presupposition failure","absence of the referent","truth-value gap from reference failure"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Suppose, for example, that contrary to what we adults believe we know, there is, in fact, a man with a long white beard and a belly like a bowl full of jelly who comes down chimneys on Christmas night to leave gifts … We must, of course, imagine that it is absolutely fortuitous that our descriptions of Santa Claus happen to fit so accurately this jolly creature. In that case I do not think that he is Santa Claus.","phenomenon":"accidental satisfier of a name's descriptions","aliases":["descriptions","fortuitous"],"near":["descriptive name","reference change of a proper name","referential use of a definite description"],"avoid":["argument from error against descriptivism","empty name","block in the chain of reference","reference failure","denotation without reference","causal-historical chain of reference","causal-historical determination of reference","causal theory of reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"Suppose someone says, \"Socrates was snub-nosed,\" and we ask to whom he is referring. … the speaker may believe that Socrates—that is, the person he refers to—was a philosopher who invented the Socratic method. But it is clearly imaginable that our omniscient observer sees that while the author of the dialogues did intend one of the characters to be taken as a portrayal of a real person, he modestly attributed to him a method that was his own brain child.","phenomenon":"argument from error against descriptivism","near":["reference change of a proper name","argument from ignorance against descriptivism","referential use of a definite description"],"avoid":["causal-historical chain of reference","causal-historical determination of reference","reference surviving a false associated theory","stability of reference across theory change","causal theory of reference"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2183871"},{"id":"putnam1970-semantics-possible","title":"Is Semantics Possible?","authors":["Hilary Putnam"],"year":"1970","area":"language","topic":"theory of meaning & truth conditions","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The problem in semantic theory is to get away from the picture of the meaning of a word as … a list of concepts; not to formalize that misguided picture.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Meaning indeed determines extension; but only because extension (fixed by some test or other) is, in some cases, \"part of the meaning\".","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Semantic theory's real task is empirical: to explain how conveying a small set of 'core facts' about a word (for natural-kind words, a stereotype plus some way of fixing the extension) lets a learner acquire its normal use.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Natural-kind words have no analytic definitions—abnormal members, false theories and changing characteristics defeat each attempted one—so the traditional conjunction-of-properties theory, and Katz's semantic markers that merely formalize it, falsify how such words work.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"natural kind","aliases":["natural kinds"],"definition":"classes of things that we regard as of explanatory importance; classes whose normal distinguishing characteristics are \"held together\" or even explained by deep-lying mechanisms.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"core facts","aliases":["core fact"],"definition":"certain core facts such that (1) one cannot convey the normal use of the word … without conveying those core facts, and (2) in the case of many words and many speakers, conveying those core facts is sufficient to convey at least an approximation to the normal use.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"stereotype","aliases":["stereotypical","stereotypes"],"definition":"an oversimplified theory which describes … a normal member of the natural kind. It is not necessary that we believe this theory … But it is necessary that we be aware that this theory is associated with the word","exact":true,"avoid":["putnam1975-meaning-meaning/t2"]},{"id":"t4","term":"one-criterion word","aliases":["one-criterion words","one criterion"],"definition":"words which possess an analytical necessary and sufficient condition … e.g., \"man who has never been married\", in the case of \"bachelor\"","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"About all I know about aluminum is that it is a light metal, … and that it doesn't appear to rust … every one of these characteristics may also fit molybdenum. … a colony of English-speaking Earthlings is leaving in a spaceship for a distant planet. … no one remembers the atomic weight … of aluminum, nor … of molybdenum. … they guess which is which, and they guess wrong. Henceforth, they use \"aluminum\" as the name for molybdenum","phenomenon":"stereotype underdetermining extension","near":["abnormal members of a natural kind","borderline case of vagueness","meaning change through stereotype change"],"avoid":["semantic externalism about natural-kind terms","natural-kind externalism","division of linguistic labor","social externalism (anti-individualism)","misidentification","reference change of a proper name","accidental satisfier of a name's descriptions","same character, different content"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"A green lemon is still a lemon—even if, owing to some abnormality, it never turns yellow. A three-legged tiger is still a tiger. Gold in the gaseous state is still gold.","phenomenon":"abnormal members of a natural kind","aliases":["abnormality"],"near":["borderline case of vagueness","meaning change through stereotype change","stereotype underdetermining extension"],"avoid":["argument from error against descriptivism"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Even if cats turn out to be robots remotely controlled from Mars we will still call them \"cats\"; even if it turns out that the stripes on tigers are painted on to deceive us, we will still call them \"tigers\"","phenomenon":"stability of reference across theory change","near":["abnormal members of a natural kind","cluster concept (family resemblance)","division of linguistic labor"],"avoid":["argument from error against descriptivism","semantic externalism about natural-kind terms","causal theory of reference","causal-historical determination of reference","causal-historical chain of reference","open texture (indeterminate word boundaries)","reference surviving a false associated theory"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"But, of course, if lemons really did turn blue (and stayed that way) then in time \"lemon\" would come to have a meaning with the following representation: lemon: natural kind word associated characteristics : blue peel, tart taste, etc.","phenomenon":"meaning change through stereotype change","near":["reference change through misidentification","abnormal members of a natural kind","stereotype underdetermining extension"],"avoid":["stability of reference across theory change"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1111/j.1467-9973.1970.tb00602.x"},{"id":"quine1956-quantifiers-propositional-attitudes","title":"Quantifiers and Propositional Attitudes","authors":["W. V. O. Quine"],"year":"1956","area":"language","topic":"attitude reports & de se","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"we can clap on a hard and fast rule against quantifying into propositional-attitude idioms; but we give it the form now of a rule against quantifying into names of intensions.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The notation of intensions, of degree one and higher, is in effect a device for inking in a boundary between referential and non-referential occurrences of terms.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"'There is someone whom Ralph believes to be a spy' becomes ∃x[Ralph believes ẑ(z is a spy) of x]: x fills the referential object place of an irreducibly triadic believer–object–attribute relation, never a place inside an opaque 'that'-clause.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Since attitude contexts are referentially opaque, quantifying into them is prima facie meaningless, yet relational belief is indispensable; the remedy marks referential versus non-referential positions explicitly—via attributes, or, dodging intensions, via quoted sentences relativized to a language.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"relational sense","aliases":["relational senses","relational"],"definition":"The force of [(∃x)(x is a lion . Ernest is hunting x)] is rather that there is some individual lion (or several) which Ernest is hunting; stray circus property, for example.","exact":true,"avoid":["burge1977-belief-de-re/t2","burge1977-belief-de-re/t5"]},{"id":"t2","term":"notional sense","aliases":["notional senses","notional"],"definition":"'Ernest is hunting lions' and 'I want a sloop' in their notional senses may be rendered rather thus: Ernest strives that (∃x)(x is a lion . Ernest finds x), I wish that (∃x)(x is a sloop . I have x).","exact":true,"avoid":["burge1977-belief-de-re/t1"]},{"id":"t3","term":"exportation","aliases":["exportation"],"definition":"The kind of exportation which leads from ['Ralph believes that Ortcutt is a spy'] to ['Ralph believes ẑ(z is a spy) of Ortcutt'] should doubtless be viewed in general as implicative.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"triadic belief","aliases":["triadic","triadic relation of belief","triadic relation"],"definition":"we may recognize also a triadic relation of belief among a believer, an object, and an attribute … to be viewed not as dyadic belief between Ralph and the proposition that Ortcutt has ẑ(z is a spy), but rather as an irreducibly triadic relation among the three things Ralph, ẑ(z is a spy), and Ortcutt.","exact":true,"avoid":["burge1977-belief-de-re/t2"]},{"id":"t5","term":"believes-true","aliases":["believes-true"],"definition":"Instead of speaking of intensions we can speak of sentences, naming these by quotation. … 'w believes-true S' is explicitly definable in [intensional] terms as 'w believes the proposition meant by S.'","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"There is a certain man in a brown hat whom Ralph has glimpsed several times under questionable circumstances on which we need not enter here; suffice it to say that Ralph suspects he is a spy. Also there is a gray-haired man, vaguely known to Ralph as rather a pillar of the community, whom Ralph is not aware of having seen except once at the beach. Now Ralph does not know it, but the men are one and the same. … Ralph is ready enough to say, in all sincerity, 'Bernard J. Ortcutt is no spy.'","phenomenon":"believing of one person that he is two","near":["conflation (believing of two persons that they are one)","merely de dicto (notional) belief","referential use of a definite description"],"avoid":["substitution failure in belief contexts","referential opacity (substitution failure)","referential opacity","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","Frege's puzzle about identity statements","Kripke's puzzle about belief","Paderewski case (puzzle without translation)","perceptual de re belief","de re belief","misidentification","failure of existential generalization"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Both may perhaps be ambiguously phrased as 'Ralph believes that someone is a spy,' but they may be unambiguously phrased respectively as 'There is someone whom Ralph believes to be a spy' and 'Ralph believes there are spies.' The difference is vast; indeed, if Ralph is like most of us, [the second] is true and [the first] false.","phenomenon":"de re / de dicto ambiguity","aliases":["ambiguously","unambiguously"],"near":["de dicto vs de re modality","collective/distributive ambiguity","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)"],"avoid":["merely de dicto (notional) belief","de re belief","de dicto vs de re reading","quantifier scope ambiguity","specific/non-specific indefinite","referential/attributive ambiguity","failure of existential generalization"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"[Consider] 'I want a sloop.' The version: (∃x)(x is a sloop . I want x) is suitable insofar only as there may be said to be a certain sloop that I want. If what I seek is mere relief from slooplessness, then [this version] conveys the wrong idea.","phenomenon":"non-specific reading of an intensional transitive verb","near":["generic reading of an indefinite","referential use of a definite description","reference failure of an empty name"],"avoid":["merely de dicto (notional) belief","de re / de dicto ambiguity","de dicto vs de re reading","quantifier scope ambiguity","intensional transitive verb","failure of existential generalization"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Thus 'Someone believes he is Napoleon' must be rendered: (∃w)[w believes ŷ(y = Napoleon) of w].","phenomenon":"de se belief","near":["pronoun of laziness","donkey anaphora","believing of one person that he is two"],"avoid":["essential indexical","self-locating belief","de re (not de se) belief about oneself","false de se belief","bound-variable pronoun","bound-variable reading of a pronoun","de re belief"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2022451"},{"id":"quine1953-reference-modality","title":"Reference and Modality","authors":["W. V. O. Quine"],"year":"1953","area":"language","topic":"modality","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Being necessarily or possibly thus and so is in general not a trait of the object concerned, but depends on the manner of referring to the object.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the way to do quantified modal logic, if at all, is to accept Aristotelian essentialism. To defend Aristotelian essentialism, however, is not part of my plan.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Since □(9 > 7) holds but □(the number of planets > 7) fails, though 9 = the number of planets, ∃x □(x > 7) lacks clear sense: necessity attaches to modes of specification, not objects.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Restricting variables to intensional objects cannot rescue quantified modal logic; only Aristotelian essentialism—objects having some traits necessarily, however specified—could, and since essentialism is unreasonable, so much the worse for quantified modal logic.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"referentially opaque","aliases":["referential opacity","opacity","opaque"],"definition":"referential opacity can obstruct substitutivity of identity. We now see that it also can interrupt quantification: quantifiers outside a referentially opaque construction need have no bearing on variables inside it.","exact":true,"avoid":["kaplan1968-quantifying/t1","burge1979-individualism-mental/t2","kripke1979-puzzle-belief/t4"]},{"id":"t2","term":"purely referential","aliases":["purely referential occurrence"],"definition":"Failure of substitutivity reveals merely that the occurrence to be supplanted is not purely referential, that is, that the statement depends not only on the object but on the form of the name.","exact":true,"avoid":["burge1979-individualism-mental/t2","kaplan1968-quantifying/t1"]},{"id":"t3","term":"Aristotelian essentialism","aliases":["essentialism","essentialist"],"definition":"An object, of itself and by whatever name or none, must be seen as having some of its traits necessarily and others contingently, despite the fact that the latter traits follow just as analytically from some ways of specifying the object as the former traits do from other ways of specifying it.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"strict modality","aliases":["strict modalities","strict necessity"],"definition":"a statement of the form ‘Necessarily ...’ is true if and only if the component statement which ‘necessarily’ governs is analytic, and a statement of the form ‘Possibly ...’ is false if and only if the negation of the component statement which ‘possibly’ governs is analytic.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Philip is unaware that Tully denounced Catiline, … Philip believes that Tegucigalpa is in Nicaragua. Substitution on the basis of [‘Cicero = Tully’] transforms [the first] into the statement: … Philip is unaware that Cicero denounced Catiline, no doubt false. Substitution on the basis of the true identity: [‘Tegucigalpa = capital of Honduras’] transforms the truth [the second] likewise into the falsehood: … Philip believes that the capital of Honduras is in Nicaragua.","phenomenon":"substitution failure in belief contexts","aliases":["Substitution"],"near":["substitution failure in modal contexts","substitution failure in quotation contexts","Mates's puzzle about synonym substitution"],"avoid":["referential opacity","referential opacity (substitution failure)","Frege's puzzle about co-referring names","de re / de dicto ambiguity","de dicto vs de re reading","merely de dicto (notional) belief","believing of one person that he is two","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","Frege's puzzle about identity statements"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"these statements would be regarded as true: (15) 9 is necessarily greater than 7, … and these as false: (18) The number of planets is necessarily greater than 7, … substitution on the basis of the true identities: (24) The number of planets = 9, … turns the truths [like (15)] into the falsehoods [like (18)].","phenomenon":"substitution failure in modal contexts","aliases":["substitution"],"near":["substitution failure in belief contexts","substitution failure in quotation contexts","quantifying into a modal context"],"avoid":["referential opacity","referential opacity (substitution failure)","de re / de dicto ambiguity","de dicto vs de re reading","quantifier scope ambiguity","modal argument against descriptivism"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"in a game of a type admitting of no tie it is necessary that some one of the players will win, but there is no one player of whom it may be said to be necessary that he win.","phenomenon":"de dicto vs de re modality","near":["necessity of identity","Barcan formula","contingent a priori truth"],"avoid":["de dicto vs de re reading","de re / de dicto ambiguity","quantifier scope ambiguity","modal scope ambiguity","exportation from de dicto to de re","quantifying into a modal context","referential opacity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"(30) (∃x)(x is necessarily greater than 7) … What is this number which, according to (30), is necessarily greater than 7? According to [‘9 is necessarily greater than 7’], from which (30) was inferred, it was 9, that is, the number of planets; but to suppose this would conflict with the fact that [‘The number of planets is necessarily greater than 7’] is false.","phenomenon":"quantifying into a modal context","near":["quantifying into a belief context","Barcan formula","necessity of identity"],"avoid":["failure of existential generalization","substitution failure in modal contexts","referential opacity","referential opacity (substitution failure)","de dicto vs de re modality","de re / de dicto ambiguity","de dicto vs de re reading","exportation from de dicto to de re","quantifier scope ambiguity"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"sellars1953-inference-meaning","title":"Inference and Meaning","authors":["Wilfrid Sellars"],"year":"1953","area":"language","topic":"theory of meaning & truth conditions","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Material rules are as essential to meaning (and hence to language and thought) as formal rules, contributing the architectural detail of its structure within the flying buttresses of logical form.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"material transformation rules determine the descriptive meaning of the expressions of a language within the framework established by its logical transformation rules","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Subjunctive conditionals like 'if there were lightning, there would be thunder' cannot be obtained by logic alone from merely factual generalizations, so the material rules they express have underived authority and are indispensable to descriptive language.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Rules of inference are norms for asserting, not structural relations between expressions; modal statements convey, without asserting, the speaker's conformity to such norms; and since rules fix all conceptual content, the a priori is pragmatic and partly synthetic.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"material rule of inference","aliases":["inferences"],"definition":"rules authorizing inferences which, to be logically valid would have to have as an additional premise a sentence formulating a law of nature","exact":true,"avoid":["sellars1954-reflections-language-games/t5","prior1960-runabout-inference-ticket/t1"]},{"id":"t2","term":"derivable (B from A)","aliases":["derivable"],"definition":"it is permissible to assert B, given that one has asserted A, whereas it is not permissible to assert not-B, given that one has asserted A","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"conveying","aliases":["conveys","convey","conveyed"],"definition":"There is the sense in which my early morning utterance, 'The sky is clear', conveys meteorological information; and there is the sense in which it conveys information about my state of mind. Let us use the term 'asserts' for the first sense of 'conveys', and 'conveys' for the second.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"modal sentences","aliases":["language of modalities","modalities","modal","necessary"],"definition":"sentences involving the phrase 'logically necessary' convey the same information … as syntactical rules to the effect that we may do thus and so, and ought not do this and that … The language of modalities is interpreted as a 'transposed' language of norms.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Metaphysicus rehearsed for their benefit the argument 'I am releasing a piece of chalk, therefore of necessity it will fall', adding by way of commentary, 'Surely that was a reasonable argument. It is not, however, formally valid, so the necessity in question cannot be logical necessity.'","phenomenon":"material inference","near":["formally valid inference","abductive inference","inductive generalization"],"avoid":["enthymeme","natural (causal) impossibility"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"'If I had released this piece of chalk, it would have fallen', and 'If there were to be a flash of lightning, there would be thunder'","phenomenon":"subjunctive (counterfactual) conditional","near":["material conditional","indicative conditional","logically true conditional"],"avoid":["non-counterfactual subjunctive conditional"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"It is as though one offered the following 'definition' as a formulation of a basic rule governing the activities of policemen: 'X is arrestable Df X has broken a law'.","phenomenon":"naturalistic fallacy","near":["circular definition","operational definition (operationalism)","use–mention confusion"],"avoid":["institutional fact"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1093/mind/lxii.247.313"},{"id":"sellars1954-reflections-language-games","title":"Some Reflections on Language Games","authors":["Wilfrid Sellars"],"year":"1954","area":"language","topic":"rule-following & normativity","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"an organism might come … to move from position to position in a system of moves and positions … \"because of the system\" without having to obey rules","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"to say that it is a law of nature that all A is B is, in effect, to say that we may infer 'x is B' from 'x is A'","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Learning a first language cannot be learning to obey rules, which would presuppose a metalanguage and a vicious regress; it is acquiring pattern-governed behavior, moves made because of a system without envisaging or intending it.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Mere conformity versus obedience to rules is a false dichotomy: as natural selection explains why each step of a bee's dance occurs because of the whole dance, conditioning can make acts occur because of a system the agent never envisages.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"pattern governed behavior","aliases":["pattern governed behavior","pattern governed","pattern-governed behavior","pattern-governed"],"definition":"the moves in a language game as pattern governed behavior are exactly the moves which, if the game were played in a rule obeying manner, would be made in the course of obeying formation and transformation rules formulated in a metalanguage game.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"language entry transition","aliases":["language entry transitions","entry transitions","entry transition","transitions","transition"],"definition":"those learned transitions (S-R connections) in which one comes to occupy a position in the game (R is a position in the game functioning as such) but the terminus a quo of the transition is not (S is not a position in the game functioning as such)","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"language departure transition","aliases":["language departure transitions","departure transitions","departure transition","transitions","transition"],"definition":"these learned transitions (S-R connections) in which from occupying a position in the game (S is a position in the game functioning as such) we come to behave in a way which is not a position in the game (R is not a position in the game functioning as such)","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"material move","aliases":["material moves"],"definition":"our understanding of the laws of nature resides in what we have called the material moves (inferences) of our language, that is to say, those moves whereby we go from one sentence to another which is not a logically analytic consequence of it.","exact":true,"avoid":["sellars1953-inference-meaning/t1"]}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"What would it mean to say of a bee returning from a clover field that its turnings and wigglings occur because they are part of a complex dance. Would this commit us to the idea that the bee envisages the dance and acts as it does by virtue of intending to realize the dance? If we reject this idea, must we refuse to say that the dance pattern as a whole is involved in the occurrence of each wiggle and turn? Clearly not.","phenomenon":"pattern-governed behavior","aliases":["pattern"],"near":["rule-obeying behavior","merely accidental conformity to a rule","intentional (purposive) action"],"avoid":["reliable differential responsiveness without conceptual understanding"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"When Jacques' retina is stimulated by light coming from an orange pencil, he says 'ce crayon est orange'","phenomenon":"language entry transition","near":["language departure transition","intra-linguistic move (inference)","auxiliary (free) position"],"avoid":["pattern-governed behavior","reliable differential responsiveness without conceptual understanding","perceptual de re belief","indexical (demonstrative) reference","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","causal theory of reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"When Jacques says to himself 'Je dois lever la main' he raises his hand.","phenomenon":"language departure transition","near":["language entry transition","intra-linguistic move (inference)","observation report"],"avoid":["pattern-governed behavior"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Certainly there seems to be something like a contradiction, or, in any event, some kind of logical absurdity, in saying 'Jones ought to do A in C but it is not the case that people (or chess players) generally ought to do A in C'.","phenomenon":"universalizability of ought-judgments","near":["Moore's paradox","ought implies can","is–ought gap"],"avoid":["logical impossibility"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"Thus, \"piece\" words might be syntactically related to expressions mentioning various shapes of wood in New York, and to expressions mentioning different makes of cars in Texas—pawns being Fords, the king a Cadillac, squares counties—and yet the game be \"the same.\"","phenomenon":"multiple realizability of a functional role","near":["family resemblance","indeterminacy of translation","use–mention distinction"],"avoid":["autonomy of linguistic meaning","meaning as use","institutional fact"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1086/287344"},{"id":"mcdowell1977-sense-reference-proper","title":"On the Sense and Reference of a Proper Name","authors":["John McDowell"],"year":"1977","area":"language","topic":"reference & names","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"we can claim that a clause which does no more than state—in a suitable way—what the reference of an expression is may nevertheless give—or as good as give—that expression's sense.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"knowledge that 'Hesperus' stands for Hesperus would suffice, in the context of suitable other knowledge not directly involving the name, for understanding utterances containing 'Hesperus'","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A name's sense is given by an axiom that simply states its bearer using the name itself (“'Hesperus' stands for Hesperus”); understanding needs no identifying description or way of recognizing the bearer, and a bearerless name has no sense.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Co-referring names differ in sense just in that a theory making speakers intelligible must use each name on the right-hand side of its own axiom; demanding richer, recognitional senses rests on the psychologistic idea that implicit theory-knowledge guides speech.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"austere conception","aliases":["austere","austerity"],"definition":"Treating a name in a theory of sense by an axiom that just states its bearer using that very name (“'Hesperus' stands for Hesperus”), knowledge of which, in context, suffices for understanding it; no way of recognizing or identifying the bearer is specified.","exact":false},{"id":"t2","term":"rich conception","aliases":["richer conception","richer","rich","richness"],"definition":"the sense of a name is a criterion for, or way of, recognizing or identifying an object as its bearer","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"modesty (of a theory of meaning)","aliases":["modesty","modest","immodest","immodesty"],"definition":"the notion of a theory which … refuses to make itself intelligible except to someone who already understands the expressions it deals with (or others with the same sense)","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"extrinsic characterization","aliases":["extrinsic","extrinsically"],"definition":"When we mention an object in describing a thought we are giving only an extrinsic characterization of the thought (since the mention of the object takes us outside the subject's mind)","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose a smoothly functioning hypothesis has it that the mountain which some of the interpreter's subjects call 'Afla' … is also called, by some of them, 'Ateb'. Suppose competence with both names coexists, in at least some cases, with ignorance that there is only one mountain involved.","phenomenon":"Frege's puzzle about informative identity","near":["empty name","one name with two bearers","reference change of a proper name"],"avoid":["Frege's puzzle about identity statements","substitution failure in belief contexts","referential opacity (substitution failure)","referential opacity","Kripke's puzzle about belief","radical interpretation","Frege's puzzle about co-referring names","believing of one person that he is two","necessary a posteriori truth"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose an interpreter finds an expression—say 'Mumbo-Jumbo'—which functions, syntactically, like other expressions which he can construe as names, but for which he can find no bearer, and reasonably believes there is no bearer.","phenomenon":"empty name","near":["descriptive name","reference change of a proper name","Frege's puzzle about informative identity"],"avoid":["reference failure","absence of the referent","radical interpretation","block in the chain of reference","truth-value gap from reference failure","presupposition failure"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"a theory which—to illustrate with a simple case—might … generate instructions on this pattern: 'First find the object (if any) which is thus and so. Then apply such and such tests to it. If the outcome is thus and so, adopt a preparedness to volunteer the sentence or utter 'Yes' on hearing it.'","phenomenon":"full-blooded (immodest) theory of meaning","near":["modest theory of meaning","homophonic truth theory","causal theory of reference"],"avoid":["language entry transition"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"From 'He is expressing a belief that Hesperus is visible above the elm tree' (an opaque attribution) one can … move to the transparent 'He is expressing a belief, concerning Hesperus, that it is visible above the elm tree'.","phenomenon":"exportation from de dicto to de re","aliases":["opaque","transparent"],"near":["failure of substitutivity","de se belief","referential use of a definite description"],"avoid":["de re ascription of a de dicto attitude","referentially transparent context","de re / de dicto ambiguity","de dicto vs de re reading","law of exportation","referential opacity"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1093/mind/lxxxvi.342.159"},{"id":"vanfraassen1966-singular-terms-truth","title":"Singular Terms, Truth-Value Gaps, and Free Logic","authors":["Bas C. van Fraassen"],"year":"1966","area":"language","topic":"reference & names","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"We have seen so far that the interpretations involving truth-value gaps do not lead to very unusual logical systems. Their unorthodoxy is mainly one of semantics.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"… what the classical valuations over a model have in common we can take as correctly reflecting truth and falsity in the model.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Non-referring names may leave sentences neither true nor false without making logic exotic: arbitrary classical valuations and their common verdicts (supervaluations) yield the same logical truths; excluded middle survives though bivalence fails.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"s(A) = T (F) iff v(A) = T (F) for every classical valuation v over the model; each v settles atomic sentences with empty names arbitrarily, so for empty b, s(Fb ∨ ¬Fb) = T but s(Fb) is undefined.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"supervaluation","aliases":["supervaluations"],"definition":"A supervaluation over a model is a function that assigns T (F) exactly to those statements assigned T (F) by all the classical valuations over that model.","exact":true,"avoid":["fine1975-vagueness-truth-logic/t2"]},{"id":"t2","term":"classical valuation","aliases":["classical valuations"],"definition":"A classical valuation over a model is a function v that assigns T or F to each statement … We have not put any conditions on v(A) when A is an atomic statement containing some nonreferring name—except that v(A) is either T or F.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"argument-completeness","aliases":["argument-complete"],"definition":"classical propositional logic validates just the right arguments. … This property of propositional logic we may call its \"argument-completeness\" with respect to the present view.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"law of bivalence","aliases":["bivalence"],"definition":"the logical law of the excluded middle and the semantic law of bivalence. The first says that any proposition of the form P ∨ ¬P is logically true. The second says that every proposition is either true or false","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Pegasus has a white hind leg. … the fact that Pegasus does not exist may be taken to mean that the question whether he has a white hind leg does not arise … I cannot find a single plausible reason to call it true or to call it false.","phenomenon":"truth-value gap from reference failure","near":["borderline case of vagueness","future contingent","category mistake"],"avoid":["presupposition failure","reference failure","empty name","absence of the referent","predication of non-existent objects","incompleteness of fiction"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Fa ⊃ (∃x)Fx … If we added to [Quine's ML] some rule to replace variables by names, then [this] would become a theorem. But since [it] is not logically true once we take into account the possibility that the name a does not refer, we shall of course not add such a rule.","phenomenon":"failure of existential generalization","near":["failure of substitutivity of identicals","failure of excluded middle","failure of the reflexivity of identity"],"avoid":["failure of universal instantiation","empty name","reference failure"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"A language has one predicate F and two names: a refers, b does not, and everything in the domain is F. One classical valuation makes Fb true, the other false; the supervaluation, recording only what both agree on, gives Fb and ¬Fb no truth-value, yet assigns T to Fb ∨ ¬Fb.","phenomenon":"excluded middle without bivalence","near":["truth-value glut","strong Kleene three-valued disjunction","failure of non-contradiction"],"avoid":["truth-value gap","truth-value gap from reference failure","empty name","reference failure","penumbral connection"],"exact":false},{"id":"e4","text":"using the quantifiers, we can state in the language itself that a given name does not refer. Hence … If A(c) is atomic, then both 'A(c), hence (∃x)(x = c)' and '¬A(c), hence (∃x)(x = c)' are S-valid. Of course, the corresponding conditionals 'A(c) ⊃ (∃x)(x = c)' and '¬A(c) ⊃ (∃x)(x = c)' are not SL-true.","phenomenon":"failure of the deduction theorem","near":["failure of excluded middle","nonmonotonic consequence","failure of transitivity of consequence"],"avoid":["failure of contraposition","empty name","reference failure","failure of conditional proof","failure of argument by cases","failure of reasoning by cases"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2024549"},{"id":"davidson1965-theories-meaning-learnable","title":"Theories of Meaning and Learnable Languages","authors":["Donald Davidson"],"year":"1965","area":"language","topic":"theory of meaning & truth conditions","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"a learnable language has a finite number of semantical primitives.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"we must be able to specify, in a way that depends effectively and solely on formal considerations, what every sentence means.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Because finite speakers come to understand indefinitely many sentences, an adequate semantics for a natural language must make each sentence's meaning depend on finitely many features: a language with infinitely many semantical primitives could never be mastered.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Analysing quotations as unstructured names, that-clauses as unitary predicates (Scheffler), belief sentences as unanalysed predications (Quine), or giving each iteration of 'believes' new sense-expressions (Church) leaves infinitely many semantical primitives — a language no finite speaker could master.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"semantical primitive","aliases":["semantical primitives"],"definition":"Let us call an expression a semantical primitive provided the rules which give the meaning for the sentences in which it does not appear do not suffice to determine the meaning of the sentences in which it does appear.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"theory of meaning","aliases":["theories of meaning"],"definition":"it must be possible to give a constructive account of the meaning of the sentences in the language. Such an account I call a theory of meaning for the language","exact":true,"avoid":["davidson1967-truth-meaning/t1"]},{"id":"t3","term":"building-block theory","definition":"First we learn a few names and predicates that apply to medium-sized lovable or edible physical objects … the learning takes place through a conditioning process involving ostension. Next come complex predicates and singular terms for objects not necessarily yet observed … Then come theoretical terms","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Quotation-mark names may be treated like single words of a language … The single constituents of these names—the quotation marks and the expressions standing between them—fulfil the same function as the letters and complexes of successive letters in single words. Hence they can possess no independent meaning. Every quotation-mark name is then a constant individual name of a definite expression . . . and in fact a name of the same nature as the proper name of a man","phenomenon":"proper name theory of quotation","aliases":["proper name","quotation-mark","quotations","quotation"],"near":["spelling theory of quotation","demonstrative theory of quotation","mixed quotation"],"avoid":["referential opacity","substitution failure in quotation contexts"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"[A]lthough quotations are ‘logically unstructured’ (190), and of course expressions have non-referential occurrences inside quotation marks, still the latter feature is ‘dispelled by an easy change in notation’ (144) which leaves us with the logically structured devices of spelling and concatenation. … the truth conditions for sentences containing quotations can be equated with the truth conditions for the sentences got from them by substituting for the quotations their definitional equivalents in the idiom of spelling.","phenomenon":"spelling theory of quotation","aliases":["spelling","quotations","quotation"],"near":["proper name theory of quotation","demonstrative theory of quotation","mixed quotation"],"avoid":["referential opacity","substitution failure in quotation contexts"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Scheffler suggests we analyse ‘Tonkin said that snow is white’ as ‘Tonkin spoke a that-snow-is-white utterance’. … the expression ‘that-snow-is-white’ is to be treated as a unitary predicate (of utterances or inscriptions).","phenomenon":"inscriptional approach to indirect discourse","aliases":["Scheffler","inscriptions"],"near":["paratactic analysis of indirect discourse","propositional analysis of that-clauses","Fregean reference shift in oblique contexts"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"in a logically more transparent language, different words would be used to refer to sense and to denotation … But now it is only a few steps to an infinite primitive vocabulary. After the first appearances of verbs like ‘believes’ we introduce new expressions for senses. … A new vocabulary is again needed, along the same lines, each time we iterate ‘believes’; and there is no limit to the number of possible iterations.","phenomenon":"Fregean hierarchy of indirect senses","aliases":["sense","senses"],"near":["Tarskian hierarchy of metalanguages","ramified hierarchy of types","Bradley's regress"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"davidson1979-moods-performances","title":"Moods and Performances","authors":["Donald Davidson"],"year":"1979","area":"language","topic":"speech acts & assertion","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Mood-setters function semantically as sentences, utterances of which are true or false according as the utterance of the indicative core does or does not have the specified illocutionary force.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"mood is not a conventional sign of assertion or command because nothing is, or could be, a conventional sign of assertion or command.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Uttering a non-indicative sentence decomposes into two speech acts: uttering its indicative core, with ordinary truth conditions, and uttering a marker, semantically a sentence, true iff that core utterance has the specified force; the pair lacks a truth value.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"No linguistic marker could by convention make an utterance an assertion or command, since jokers and actors would exploit any such marker; mood's conventional tie to force is only that the concept of force is part of mood's meaning.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"mood-setter","aliases":["mood-setters","mood setter"],"definition":"We may think of non-indicative sentences, then, as indicative sentences plus an expression that syntactically represents the appropriate transformation; call this expression the mood-setter.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"autonomy of linguistic meaning","aliases":["autonomy of meaning","autonomy","linguistic"],"definition":"Once a feature of language has been given conventional expression, it can be used to serve many extra-linguistic ends; symbolic representation necessarily breaks any close tie with extra-linguistic purpose.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"indicative core","aliases":["indicative cores"],"definition":"The declarative sentence of which a non-declarative is a transformation ('You will put on your hat' for 'Put on your hat'): syntactically what the moods share, its truth conditions being their common semantic element; the mood-setter is true iff its utterance has the specified force.","exact":false}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"we can ask a question with an imperative or indicative (\"Tell me who won the third race,\" \"I'd like to know your telephone number\"), or issue a command with an indicative (\"In this house we remove our shoes before entering\").","phenomenon":"indirect speech act","near":["explicit performative","rhetorical question","parasitic (non-serious) use of language"],"avoid":["indirect request","indirect directive","indirect question","directive speech act","R-based implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Whatever is conventional about assertion can be put into words … Let us suppose … that Frege's assertion sign is not just the formal equivalent of the indicative mood, but a more complete expression of the conventional element in assertion. It is easy to see that merely speaking a sentence in the strengthened mood cannot be counted on to result in an assertion; every joker, storyteller and actor will immediately take advantage of the strengthened mood to simulate assertion.","phenomenon":"autonomy of linguistic meaning","aliases":["autonomy"],"near":["Frege–Geach problem","conventional implicature","indirect speech act"],"avoid":["parasitic (non-serious) use of language","etiolated (non-serious) use of language","the Frege point (force–content distinction)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Austin held that performatives have no truth value on the ground that uttering a sentence like \"I order you to go\" is not typically to describe one's own speech act but rather to issue an order.","phenomenon":"explicit performative","aliases":["performatives","performative"],"near":["primary (implicit) performative","reported speech (indirect discourse)","perlocutionary act"],"avoid":["self-fulfilling (self-verifying) performatives","performative utterance","indirect speech act","constative","explicit performative utterance","self-verifying explicit performatives","directive speech act","declaration"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/978-1-4020-4104-4_2"},{"id":"davidson1968-saying-that","title":"On Saying That","authors":["Donald Davidson"],"year":"1968","area":"language","topic":"attitude reports & de se","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[Sentences in indirect discourse] consist of an expression referring to a speaker, the two-place predicate 'said', and a demonstrative referring to an utterance. Period.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"from a semantic point of view the content-sentence in indirect discourse is not contained in the sentence whose truth counts.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"'Galileo said that the earth moves' is two utterances, 'Galileo said that. The earth moves.', where the first is true iff ∃x(Galileo uttered x ∧ x and the demonstrated utterance make Galileo and me samesayers).","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Apparent substitution failures in indirect discourse are explained without intensional entities: the reported sentence is semantically independent of the attribution, its words keep their ordinary reference, and changing it changes which utterance 'that' refers to.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"semantic innocence","aliases":["pre-Fregean semantic innocence"],"definition":"If we could recover our pre-Fregean semantic innocence, I think it would seem to us plainly incredible that the words \"The earth moves\", uttered after the words \"Galileo said that\", mean anything different, or refer to anything else, than is their wont when they come in other environments.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"samesaying","aliases":["samesayers","samesayer"],"definition":"Galileo utters his words \"Eppur si muove\", I utter my words, \"The earth moves.\" There is no problem yet in recognizing that we are samesayers; an utterance of mine matches an utterance of his in purport.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"content-sentence","aliases":["content-sentences"],"definition":"intuition suggests, and theory demands, that we discover semantically significant structure in the 'content-sentences' of indirect discourse (as I shall call sentences following 'said that').","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"performative","aliases":["performatives","performances"],"definition":"what an utterance of \"Galileo said that\" does is announce a further utterance. … we do the same with words like \"This is a joke\", \"This is an order\", \"He commanded that\", \"Now hear this\". Such expressions might be called performatives, for they are used to usher in performances on the part of the speaker.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Let someone say (and now discourse is direct), \"There's a hippopotamus in the refrigerator\"; am I necessarily right in reporting him as having said that there is a hippopotamus in the refrigerator? Perhaps; but under questioning he goes on, \"It's roundish, has a wrinkled skin, does not mind being touched. It has a pleasant taste, at least the juice, and it costs a dime. I squeeze two or three for breakfast.\"","phenomenon":"indeterminacy of translation","near":["Mates's puzzle about synonym substitution","division of linguistic labor","Kripke's puzzle about belief"],"avoid":["principle of charity","indeterminacy of interpretation","radical interpretation","malapropism","indeterminacy (inscrutability) of reference","indeterminacy of reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"I need only produce the required utterance and replace 'y' by a reference to it. Here goes: The earth moves. (∃x) (Galileo's utterance x and my last utterance make us samesayers). Definitional abbreviation is all that is needed to bring this little skit down to: The earth moves. Galileo said that.","phenomenon":"paratactic analysis of indirect discourse","near":["quotational analysis of indirect discourse","Fregean indirect reference","inscriptional approach to indirect discourse"],"avoid":["indexical (demonstrative) reference","direct reference of demonstratives"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"if we can make trouble for the sentence \"Scott said that Venus is an inferior planet\" by substituting 'the Evening Star' for 'Venus', we can equally make trouble by substituting \"is identical with Venus or with Mercury\" for the coextensive \"is an inferior planet\".","phenomenon":"referential opacity","near":["Mates's puzzle about synonym substitution","quantifying into attitude contexts","slingshot argument"],"avoid":["coextensive but non-synonymous predicates","Frege's puzzle about co-referring names","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","Frege's puzzle about identity statements","substitution failure in quotation contexts","de dicto vs de re reading","de re / de dicto ambiguity","substitution failure in belief contexts"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"\"I assert that Entebbe is equatorial\". Of course by saying this I may not assert anything … But if my utterance of the performative is true, then I do say something in the assertive mode that has the content of my second utterance - I do, that is, assert that Entebbe is equatorial. If I do assert it, an element in my success is no doubt my utterance of the performative, which announces an assertion","phenomenon":"self-fulfilling (self-verifying) performatives","aliases":["performative","performatives"],"near":["Moore's paradox","self-falsifying utterance","free indirect discourse"],"avoid":["explicit performative","performative utterance","misfire (void performative act)","indirect speech act","the Frege point (force–content distinction)","declaration","representative declaration","autonomy of linguistic meaning"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00568054"},{"id":"lewis1980-index-context-content","title":"Index, Context, and Content","authors":["David Lewis"],"year":"1980","area":"language","topic":"indexicals & demonstratives","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Contexts and indices will not do each other's work. Therefore we need both. … We need the relation: sentence s is true at context c at index i.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The propositional contents of sentences do not obey the compositional principle, therefore they are not semantic values.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Whether sentences get context-relative functions from indices to truth values or one context-invariant function from context–index pairs is a distinction without a difference: each converts into the other, and appeals to content give no reason to prefer the first.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Semantic values fix 'true at context c at index i'; s is true at c iff true at c at i_c, c's own index; s's content at c is {w : s true at c at i_c[world:=w]}.","formal":true}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"context","definition":"A context is a location - time, place, and possible world - where a sentence is said. It has countless features, determined by the character of the location.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"index","definition":"packages of features of context so combined that they can vary independently. An index is an n-tuple of features of context of various sorts … We impose no requirement that the coordinates of an index should all be features of any one context.","exact":true,"avoid":["lewis1970-general-semantics/t2","kaplan1989-demonstratives/t4"]},{"id":"t3","term":"semantic value","aliases":["semantic values"],"definition":"First, the semantic values of some expressions, the sentences, must enter somehow into determining whether truth-in-English would be achieved if the expression were uttered in a given context. Second, the semantic value of any expression is to be determined by the semantic values of the (immediate) constituents from which it is built, together with the way it is built from them.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"shiftiness","aliases":["shifted"],"definition":"Often the truth (-in-English) of a sentence in a context depends on the truth of some related sentence when some feature of the original context is shifted. 'There have been dogs.' is true now iff 'There are dogs.' is true at some time before now.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"schmentence","aliases":["schmentences"],"definition":"a compositional grammar in which it never happens that sentences are constituents of other sentences … we will need replacements for the sentences hitherto regarded as constituents. The stand-ins will have to be more or less sentence-like. … Let us call them schmentences instead.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"variable but simple","definition":"A value for a sentence is a function, perhaps partial, from indices to truth values. … However, a sentence may have different semantic values in different contexts, and the grammar must tell us how value depends on context.","exact":true,"avoid":["kaplan1989-demonstratives/t2"]},{"id":"t7","term":"constant but complicated","definition":"A value for a sentence is a function, perhaps partial, from combinations of a context and an index to truth values. … The semantic value of a sentence … does not vary from context to context. The grammar assigns it once and for all.","exact":true,"avoid":["kaplan1989-demonstratives/t1"]},{"id":"t8","term":"horizontal propositional content","definition":"The set of worlds w such that s is true at c at the index obtained by taking c's own index and shifting only its world coordinate to w; fixed by, but not identical with, a compositional semantic value.","exact":false,"avoid":["stalnaker1970-pragmatics/t2"]}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"under one disambiguation, 'If someone is speaking here then I exist.' is true at any context whatever. No shift from one context to another can make it false. But a time shift, holding other features fixed, can make it false; … Likewise a world shift can make it false; that is why 'Necessarily, if someone is speaking here then I must exist.' is false in the original context.","phenomenon":"contingent a priori truth","near":["necessary a posteriori truth","Moore's paradox","monster (context-shifting operator)"],"avoid":["indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","indexical (demonstrative) reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Consider the tribe of Liars - the ones in the riddles, the folk we fear to meet at forks in the road. Unlike common liars, the Liars have no wish to mislead. They convey information smoothly to each other … They are as truthful in their own way as we are in ours. But they are truthful in Liarese and we are truthful in English, and Liarese is a language like English but with all the truth values reversed.","phenomenon":"convention of truthfulness and trust","aliases":["truthful"],"near":["lying (assertion meant to deceive)","verbal irony","flouting the maxim of Quality"],"avoid":["convention of truthfulness"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"If the words are 'France is hexagonal.' of course the shape of France matters, but so do the aspects of previous discourse … Truth-in-English has been achieved if the last thing said before was 'Italy is sort of boot-shaped.' but not if the last thing said before was 'Shapes in geometry are ever so much simpler than shapes in geography'.","phenomenon":"context-dependent standards of precision","aliases":["standards of precision","precision"],"near":["sorites paradox","scalar implicature","faultless disagreement"],"avoid":["accommodating rise in standards of precision","loose (relaxed) use of an expression","vague predicate","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"we could speak a language in which 'As for you, I am hungry.' is true iff 'I am hungry.' is true when the role of speaker is shifted from me to you - in other words, iff you are hungry. We could - but we don't.","phenomenon":"monster (context-shifting operator)","near":["direct quotation (mention)","deferred reference","de se attitude report"],"avoid":["indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","indexical (demonstrative) reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"(1) I say 'I am hungry.'. You simultaneously say to me 'You are hungry.'. What is said is the same. … (3) I say on 6 June 1977 'Today is Monday.'. You say on 7 June 1977 'Yesterday was Monday.'. What is said is the same.","phenomenon":"same content, different character","near":["same character, different content","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","monster (context-shifting operator)"],"avoid":["indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","indexical (demonstrative) reference","cognitive dynamics: losing track of time"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/978-94-009-9012-8_6"},{"id":"lewis1974-radical-interpretation","title":"Radical Interpretation","authors":["David Lewis"],"year":"1974","area":"language","topic":"interpretation & convention","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"we are not really interested in a practical method of finding out anything; we are interested in the determination of the mental and semantic facts by the physical facts","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"something may count as a person's system of beliefs, desires, and meanings if and only if it … more or less conforms to the principles of [the common-sense theory]","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"The physical facts about a person fix that person's beliefs, desires and meanings, to whatever extent they are fixed, through common-sense constraints: charity, rationalization, truthfulness, generativity, manifestation, and a triangle principle.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Better to settle beliefs and desires first, by charity and by rationalizing raw behavior decision-theoretically, then assign truth conditions making the speaker's attitudes part of a convention of truthfulness, than to start, as Davidson does, from sentences held true.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"avoid":["davidson1973-radical-interpretation"],"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"problem of radical interpretation","aliases":["radical interpretation"],"definition":"my problem of radical interpretation is not any real-life task of finding out about Karl's beliefs, desires, and meanings. I am not really asking how we could determine these facts. Rather: how do [the physical facts] determine these facts?","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"Principle of Charity","aliases":["Charity","charity","charitable"],"definition":"Karl should be represented as believing what he ought to believe, and desiring what he ought to desire. And what is that? In our opinion, he ought to believe what we believe, or perhaps what we would have believed in his place","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"Rationalization Principle","aliases":["rationalization","rational"],"definition":"Karl should be represented as a rational agent; the beliefs and desires ascribed to him by Ao should be such as to provide good reasons for his behavior, as given in physical terms by P.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"Principle of Truthfulness","aliases":["truthfulness","Truthfulness"],"definition":"Some of the beliefs and desires ascribed to Karl by Ao … should constitute Karl's part of the pattern of attitudes that I have elsewhere … called a convention of truthfulness in Karl's language, under the truth conditions assigned by M to sentences thereof.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"minimal materialism","aliases":["materialism"],"definition":"I am presupposing that there cannot possibly be two Karls exactly alike with respect to P but differing somehow with respect to Ao, Ak, or M.","exact":true},{"id":"t7","term":"indeterminacy of compromise","aliases":["compromise","indeterminacy"],"definition":"a more general indeterminacy can arise because no solution fits all the constraints perfectly, and many different ways to strike a balance give many different compromise solutions.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Imagine that we have undertaken the task of coming to know Karl as a person. We would like to know what he believes, what he desires, what he means … Imagine also that we must start from scratch. At the outset we know nothing about Karl's beliefs, desires, and meanings. Whatever we may know about persons in general, our knowledge of Karl in particular is limited to our knowledge of him as a physical system.","phenomenon":"radical interpretation","near":["Quinean radical translation","Laplace's demon","Mary's room (knowledge argument)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"if M assigns to 'Ionlay!' the truth condition that a lion is present, then some of the beliefs and desires ascribed to Karl by Ao should be as follows: (1) a desire not to utter 'Ionlay!' unless a lion is present; (2) a belief that his partners have a like desire; (3) a belief that a lion is present, at times when he hears 'Ionlay!' uttered; (4) a belief that his partners respond in the same way to utterances of 'Ionlay!'","phenomenon":"convention of truthfulness and trust","near":["principle of charity","Cooperative Principle","Quinean stimulus meaning"],"avoid":["convention of truthfulness"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00484599"},{"id":"lewis1975-adverbs-quantification","title":"Adverbs of Quantification","authors":["David Lewis"],"year":"1975","area":"language","topic":"quantification & formal semantics","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the unselective ∀ and ∃ can show up as the adverbs always and sometimes. Likewise never, usually, often, and seldom can serve as the unselective analogs of the selective quantifiers…","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I conclude that the if of our restrictive if-clauses should not be regarded as a sentential connective. It has no meaning apart from the adverb it restricts.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Adverbs like 'always', 'usually' and 'seldom' cannot be mere quantifiers over times or events, since they also generalize over abstract objects like quadratic equations; they quantify over cases—assignments to all the variables they bind at once.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"‘Always/sometimes/never/usually/often/seldom if ψ, φ’ is true iff φ holds in all/some/no/most/many/few of the admissible cases satisfying ψ — a case being an assignment of values to the free variables, plus perhaps a time or event coordinate.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"unselective quantifier","aliases":["unselective quantifiers","unselective"],"definition":"It is an entirely routine matter to introduce unselective quantifiers ∀ and ∃ that bind all the variables in their scope indiscriminately.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"case","aliases":["cases"],"definition":"Our adverbs are quantifiers over cases; a case may be regarded as the 'tuple of its participants; and these participants are values of the variables that occur free in the open sentence modified by the adverb.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"restrictive term","aliases":["restrictive"],"definition":"[an] if-clause of the form if α is τ, where α is a variable and τ is an indefinite singular term formed from a common noun … by prefixing the indefinite article or some … we can delete the if-clause and place [τ] in apposition to an occurrence of the variable α","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"generality interpretation","aliases":["generality interpretation of free variables"],"definition":"Always may simply be omitted … Thus we reconstruct the so-called ‘generality interpretation’ of free variables: the variables are bound by the omitted always.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"adverb of quantification","aliases":["adverbs of quantification"],"definition":"What we can say, safely and with full generality, is that our adverbs of quantification are quantifiers over cases. What holds always, sometimes, never, usually, often, or seldom is what holds in, respectively, all, some, no, most, many, or few cases.","exact":true,"avoid":["lewis1975-adverbs-quantification/t1","lewis1975-adverbs-quantification/t2"]}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"A man who owns a donkey always beats it now and then means that every continuing relationship between a man and his donkey is punctuated by beatings","phenomenon":"donkey anaphora","aliases":["donkey"],"near":["paycheck pronoun","cataphora (backward anaphora)","cross-sentential anaphora"],"avoid":["donkey sentence","E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora","unselective binding","unselective quantification","quantificational variability effect","bound-variable anaphora","bound-variable pronoun","bound-variable reading of a pronoun","if-clause restricting a quantificational adverb"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"A quadratic equation never has more than two solutions … A quadratic equation usually has two different solutions mean, respectively, that no quadratic equation has more than two solutions and that most — more precisely, all but a set of measure zero under the natural measure on the set of triples of coefficients — have two different solutions.","phenomenon":"quantificational variability effect","near":["donkey anaphora","referential (specific) indefinite","kind-level predication"],"avoid":["unselective binding","unselective quantification","generic generalization","generic sentence","proportional quantifier (not first-order definable)","downward-entailing (monotone decreasing) quantifier"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Sometimes it happens that x sells stolen goods to y, who sells them to z, who sells them back to x … is true iff some triple of persons satisfies the open sentence after sometimes","phenomenon":"unselective binding","near":["selective quantification","substitutional quantification","branching (Henkin) quantifier"],"avoid":["unselective quantification","resumptive quantification","polyadic quantification"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Often if it is raining my roof leaks (in which the time-coordinate is all there is to the case)","phenomenon":"if-clause restricting a quantificational adverb","near":["material conditional","biscuit conditional","if-clause restricting a modal"],"avoid":["restrictor analysis of conditionals","restrictor if-clause","restrictive if-clause","quantifier domain restriction","unselective binding","unselective quantification","proportional quantifier (not first-order definable)"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1017/cbo9780511897696.003"},{"id":"lewis1978-truth-fiction","title":"Truth in Fiction","authors":["David Lewis"],"year":"1978","area":"language","topic":"conditionals","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"What is true in the Sherlock Holmes stories is what would be true if those stories were told as known fact rather than fiction.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The worlds we should consider [for what holds in a fiction], I suggest, are the worlds where the fiction is told, but as known fact rather than fiction.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"'In fiction f, φ' is non-vacuously true iff for each collective belief world w of f's originating community, some world where f is told as known fact and φ holds is closer to w than any such world where ¬φ.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Many descriptions of fictional characters are better read not at face value, Meinong-style, but as abbreviations of sentences prefixed by an intensional operator 'In fiction f'; mixing prefixed and unprefixed premisses explains why fictional truths seem cut off from consequences.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"ambiguity of prefixing","aliases":["ambiguous","ambiguity","prefix","prefixed","prefixing"],"definition":"Many things we might say about Holmes are potentially ambiguous. They may or may not be taken as abbreviations for sentences carrying the prefix \"In the Sherlock Holmes stories . . .\".","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"storytelling","aliases":["storyteller"],"definition":"Storytelling is pretence. The storyteller purports to be telling the truth about matters whereof he has knowledge. … Nevertheless he plays a false part, goes through a form of telling known fact when he is not doing so.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"overt belief","aliases":["overt"],"definition":"Call a belief overt in a community at a time iff more or less everyone shares it, more or less everyone thinks that more or less everyone else shares it, and so on.","exact":true,"avoid":["stalnaker2002-common-ground/t1"]},{"id":"t4","term":"collective belief worlds","aliases":["collective belief world","worlds"],"definition":"Then we can assign to the community a set of possible worlds, called the collective belief worlds of the community, comprising exactly those worlds where the overt beliefs all come true.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"impossible fiction","aliases":["impossible","fiction"],"definition":"Let us call a fiction impossible iff there is no world where it is told as known fact rather than fiction. … First, the plot might be impossible. Second, a possible plot might imply that there could be nobody in a position to know or tell of the events in question.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"We can truly say that Holmes lived at 221B Baker Street. I have been told that the only building at 221B Baker Street, then or now, was a bank. It does not follow, and certainly is not true, that Holmes lived in a bank.","phenomenon":"ambiguity of prefixing","near":["imaginative resistance","inter-fictional carry-over","impossible fiction"],"avoid":["fallacy of equivocation","referential opacity","substitution failure in modal contexts","predication of non-existent objects"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"In \"The Adventure of the Speckled Band\" Sherlock Holmes solves a murder mystery by showing that the victim has been killed by a Russell's viper that has climbed up a bell rope. What Holmes did not realize was that Russell's viper is not a constrictor. The snake is therefore incapable of concertina movement and could not have climbed the rope. Either the snake reached its victim some other way or the case remains open.","phenomenon":"Reality Principle","aliases":["realize"],"near":["Mutual Belief Principle","inter-fictional carry-over","intra-fictional carry-over"],"avoid":["natural (causal) impossibility"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"In the Threepenny Opera, the principal characters are a treacherous crew. They constantly betray one another, for gain or to escape danger. There is also a streetsinger. He shows up, sings the ballad of Mack the Knife, and goes about his business without betraying anyone. Is he also a treacherous fellow?","phenomenon":"intra-fictional carry-over","near":["inter-fictional carry-over","Mutual Belief Principle","Reality Principle"],"avoid":["incompleteness of fiction"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Suppose I write a story about the dragon Scrulch, a beautiful princess, a bold knight, and what not. It is a perfectly typical instance of its stylized genre, except that I never say that Scrulch breathes fire. Does he nevertheless breathe fire in my story?","phenomenon":"inter-fictional carry-over","near":["intra-fictional carry-over","Reality Principle","Mutual Belief Principle"],"avoid":["incompleteness of fiction"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"Is the world of Sherlock Holmes a world where Holmes has an even or an odd number of hairs on his head at the moment when he first meets Watson? What is Inspector Lestrade's blood type?","phenomenon":"incompleteness of fiction","near":["inconsistent fiction","intra-fictional carry-over","imaginative resistance"],"avoid":["incompleteness of fictional objects","failure of conditional excluded middle","excluded middle without bivalence"],"exact":true},{"id":"e6","text":"Conan Doyle contradicted himself from one story to another about the location of Watson's old war wound. Still, I do not want to say that just anything is true in the Holmes stories!","phenomenon":"inconsistent fiction","aliases":["contradicted"],"near":["pragmatic paradox","intra-fictional carry-over","imaginative resistance"],"avoid":["impossible fiction","incompleteness of fiction","explosion (ex falso quodlibet)","logical impossibility"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"stalnaker1973-presuppositions","title":"Presuppositions","authors":["Robert Stalnaker"],"year":"1973","area":"language","topic":"presupposition & projection","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"My point is that there need be no essential connection between presupposition requirements and truth value gaps.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the technical semantic relation of presupposition will be just one reason among others why a statement may require a presupposition in the pragmatic sense.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Linguists' sentence presuppositions are explicated pragmatically: a sentence requires P when using it would normally be inappropriate unless the speaker presupposes P. Truth-value gaps are just one possible source of such requirements, not their definition.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Since asserted content may thereafter be presupposed, the first conjunct joins the background before the second is asserted; so 'A and B' requires A's presuppositions plus only those of B not entailed by A, though 'and' is semantically symmetric.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"avoid":["stalnaker1974-pragmatic-presuppositions"],"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"pragmatic presupposition","aliases":["presupposes"],"definition":"A speaker presupposes that P at a given moment in a conversation just in case he is disposed to act, in his linguistic behavior, as if he takes the truth of P for granted, and as if he assumes that his audience recognizes that he is doing so.","exact":true,"avoid":["stalnaker1970-pragmatics/t3"]},{"id":"t2","term":"presupposition set","definition":"this set of possible worlds, which I will call the presupposition set, contains just the alternative possibilities which the speaker considers to be relevant to the purposes of the participants in the conversation","exact":true,"avoid":["stalnaker1978-assertion/t1"]},{"id":"t3","term":"presupposition requirement","aliases":["presupposition","requires a presupposition"],"definition":"a sentence has a presupposition in a derivative sense just in case the use of that sentence would for some reason normally be inappropriate unless the speaker presupposed a particular proposition. In such a case, I will say that a sentence requires a presupposition.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"projection problem","aliases":["problem","compositional problem"],"definition":"This is the problem of how the presuppositions required by a complex sentence relate to the presuppositions required by its component clauses. … from the perspective I am suggesting, the problem looks quite different; it concerns the way that pragmatic presuppositions, or background assumptions, change in the course of a conversation.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"someone asks of my daughter, 'how old is he?' I answer, 'she is ten months old'. Or, a says, of the new secretary, 'Jennifer is certainly an attractive woman', b replies, 'Yes, her husband thinks so too'.","phenomenon":"presupposition accommodation","near":["presupposition cancellation","presupposition filtering","factive presupposition"],"avoid":["informative presupposition","defective context","anaphoric presupposition","particularized conversational implicature","conversational implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"if 'Ted is the only man who could have won' presupposes that Ted could have won, then so does 'Ted is not the only man who could have won', as well as 'Ted might be the only man who could have won'.","phenomenon":"presupposition projection through holes","near":["presupposition filtering","presupposition cancellation","presupposition accommodation"],"avoid":["presupposition hole","presupposition projection from negation and questions","presupposition projection through negation and modals"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"'John has children and all of his children are asleep' does not require the presupposition that John has children, even though the second conjunct does require this presupposition.","phenomenon":"presupposition filtering","aliases":["presupposition"],"near":["presupposition accommodation","presupposition projection through holes","temporal implicature of 'and'"],"avoid":["presupposition cancellation","anaphoric presupposition"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"the oddity of sentences like 'John's aardvark is sleeping, and John has an aardvark'.","phenomenon":"violation of the non-redundancy (informativity) constraint on assertion","near":["contradictory presuppositions","Moore's paradox","presupposition failure"],"avoid":["pleonasm (redundancy)","anti-backgrounding of appositives"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00262951"},{"id":"stalnaker1970-pragmatics","title":"Pragmatics","authors":["Robert Stalnaker"],"year":"1970","area":"language","topic":"context & common ground","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"An interpreted sentence … corresponds to a function from contexts into propositions, and a proposition is a function from possible worlds into truth values.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The simpler account of pragmatics which merges possible worlds with contexts cannot account for Donnellan's distinction.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Context and possible world should not be merged into a single point of reference: the proposition expressed in context is needed as the common content of speech acts and attitudes, as when a question and its answer share it.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Donnellan's referential/attributive contrast is a pragmatic ambiguity: a description's denotation is fixed either by what the context presupposes, yielding a singular proposition, or within the proposition itself, varying from world to world.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"pragmatics","aliases":["Pragmatics"],"definition":"Syntax studies sentences, semantics studies propositions. Pragmatics is the study of linguistic acts and the contexts in which they are performed.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"proposition","aliases":["propositions"],"definition":"a proposition will be a way - any way - of dividing a set of possible states of the world into two parts: the ones that are ruled out by the truth of the proposition, and the ones that are not.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"pragmatic presupposition","aliases":["presupposition","presuppose","pragmatic"],"definition":"According to the pragmatic conception, presupposition is a propositional attitude, not a semantic relation. … To presuppose a proposition in the pragmatic sense is to take its truth for granted, and to assume that others involved in the context do the same.","exact":true,"avoid":["stalnaker1973-presuppositions/t1","stalnaker1974-pragmatic-presuppositions/t1"]},{"id":"t4","term":"pragmatic ambiguity","aliases":["pragmatically ambiguous"],"definition":"a sentence has the potential for pragmatic ambiguity if some rule involved in the interpretation of that sentence may be applied either to the context or to the possible world. … Applied to the possible world, the rule is incorporated into the proposition itself, contributing to the determination of a truth value.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Consider a statement 'everybody is having a good time'. … assume also that you are omniscient with respect to people having a good time … you may still be in doubt about the truth of the statement … you do not know what class of people it was made about. It is unlikely that the speaker meant everybody in the universe. He may have meant everybody at some party, or everyone listening to some philosophical lecture","phenomenon":"quantifier domain restriction","near":["incomplete definite description resolved by salience","scalar implicature","presupposition accommodation"],"avoid":["context-sensitivity","indexicality","contextual domain restriction","unarticulated constituent","free pragmatic enrichment","saturation of a linguistically mandated slot","semantic underdetermination (completion)","conversational impliciture (expansion)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"(4) The man in the purple turtleneck shirt might have been someone else. … If [it] means, roughly, that Daniels might have been someone else, it is false, perhaps contradictory. On the other hand, if it means that someone else might have been the one wearing the turtleneck shirt (perhaps he almost lent it to me), then it may be true.","phenomenon":"de re / de dicto ambiguity","near":["lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","presupposition failure","contingent a priori truth"],"avoid":["scope ambiguity","referential use of a definite description","attributive use of a definite description","referential/attributive distinction","pragmatic ambiguity","de dicto vs de re modality","quantifier scope ambiguity","modal scope ambiguity","quantifying into a modal context"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"there are cookies in the cupboard if you want some","phenomenon":"biscuit conditional","near":["subjunctive (counterfactual) conditional","conditional perfection","concessive (even-if) conditional"],"avoid":["relevance conditional","speech-act conditional","conditional assertion","indirect offer","indirect speech act","non-interference conditional"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"When you ask, \"Which one is Daniels?\" you are not referring to Daniels, since you do not presuppose of any one person that he is Daniels. When I answer \"Daniels is the bald one\" … I am telling you not that Daniels is bald, but that he is Daniels.","phenomenon":"attributive use of a proper name","near":["descriptive name","rigid designation","deferred reference"],"avoid":["referential use of a definite description","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","metalinguistic use of a name","informative identity statement","deferential use of a name","denotation without reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"If O'Leary says \"Are you going to the party?\" and you answer, \"Yes, I'm going\", your answer is appropriate because the proposition you affirm is the one expressed in his question. … A truth value, of course, is not enough to be the common content. If O'Leary asks \"Are you going to the party?\" it would be inappropriate for you to answer, \"Yes, snow is white.\"","phenomenon":"same content, different character","aliases":["content"],"near":["same character, different content","essential indexical","diagonalization"],"avoid":["the Frege point (force–content distinction)","question–answer congruence","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","indexical (demonstrative) reference"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00413603"},{"id":"stalnaker1975-indicative-conditionals","title":"Indicative Conditionals","authors":["Robert Stalnaker"],"year":"1975","area":"language","topic":"conditionals","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I will argue that, although the premiss of the direct argument [from disjunction to conditional] does not semantically entail its conclusion, the inference is nevertheless a reasonable inference.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the world selected must, if possible, be within the context set as well (where C is the context set, if i ∈ C, then f(A,i) ∈ C)","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"The inference from 'A or B' to 'if not-A, then B' is invalid yet reasonable: every context where the disjunction is appropriately asserted and accepted also entails the conditional, which explains the truth-functional analysis's appeal.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Indicative 'if A, B' is appropriate only in contexts compatible with A, so presupposed-false antecedents require the subjunctive, which conventionally signals that presuppositions are suspended and the selection function may reach outside the context set.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"reasonable inference","aliases":["reasonable","inference"],"definition":"an inference from a sequence of assertions or suppositions (the premisses) to an assertion or hypothetical assertion (the conclusion) is reasonable just in case, in every context in which the premisses could appropriately be asserted or supposed, it is impossible for anyone to accept the premisses without committing himself to the conclusion","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"direct argument","aliases":["argument"],"definition":"The argument we began with has the form A or B, therefore, if not-A, then B.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"pragmatic constraint on selection functions","aliases":["selected"],"definition":"if the conditional is being evaluated at a world in the context set, then the world selected must, if possible, be within the context set as well … In other words, all worlds within the context set are closer to each other than any worlds outside it.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"constraint on the appropriateness of disjunctive statements","aliases":["disjunctive","disjunct","appropriately"],"definition":"a disjunctive statement is appropriately made only in a context which allows either disjunct to be true without the other. That is, one may say A or B only in a situation in which both A and not-B and B and not-A are open possibilities.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Either the butler or the gardener did it. Therefore, if the butler didn't do it, the gardener did.","phenomenon":"or-to-if inference","near":["disjunctive syllogism","paradox of material implication","constructive dilemma"],"avoid":["if-clause restricting a modal"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Consider, for example, \"the butler did it; therefore, if he didn't, the gardener did.\" … this argument has no trace of intuitive plausibility. Or consider what may be inferred from the denial of a conditional. Surely I may deny that if the butler didn't do it, the gardener did without affirming the butler's guilt.","phenomenon":"paradox of material implication","near":["or-to-if inference","disjunctive syllogism","failure of contraposition"],"avoid":["explosion (ex falso quodlibet)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"If the butler had done it, we would have found just the clues which we in fact found. Here a conditional is presented as evidence for the truth of its antecedent.","phenomenon":"non-counterfactual subjunctive conditional","aliases":["conditional"],"near":["indicative conditional","counterpossible conditional","biscuit conditional"],"avoid":["subjunctive (counterfactual) conditional","counterfactual conditional","subjunctive conditional"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"The setting of the example is wartime Britain during an air raid. I reason as follows: \"Either I will be killed in this raid or I will not be killed. Suppose that I will. Then even if I take precautions I will be killed … But suppose I am not going to be killed. Then I won't be killed even if I neglect all precautions … Either way, any precautions I take will be either ineffective or unnecessary, and so pointless.\"","phenomenon":"lazy argument for fatalism","near":["Newcomb's problem","consequence argument","Diodorus's Master Argument"],"avoid":["logical fatalism","sea-battle argument","idle argument"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf02379021"},{"id":"stalnaker2002-common-ground","title":"Common Ground","authors":["Robert Stalnaker"],"year":"2002","area":"language","topic":"context & common ground","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"we might identify the common ground with common belief about what is accepted.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the logic of presupposition obeys positive introspection, but not negative introspection","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"The presumed shared information of a conversation is mutual belief about what every participant accepts for its purposes, acceptance being broader than belief; to presuppose something is to believe it has that status.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Accommodation can be ordinary belief change: an utterance is a manifest event, so it becomes common belief that the speaker presupposes φ; if the hearer then believes φ, φ becomes common belief: C(BₛCφ), Bₕφ ⊢ Cφ.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"common ground","definition":"It is common ground that φ in a group if all members accept (for the purpose of the conversation) that φ, and all believe that all accept that φ, and all believe that all believe that all accept that φ, etc.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"acceptance","aliases":["accept"],"definition":"a category of propositional attitudes and methodological stances toward a proposition, a category that includes belief, but also some attitudes (presumption, assumption, acceptance for the purposes of an argument or an inquiry) that contrast with belief … To accept a proposition is to treat it as true for some reason.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"manifest event","aliases":["event"],"definition":"The occurrence of a speech act is, in the normal case, a manifest event, by which I mean an event that, when it occurs, is mutually recognized to have occurred.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"accommodation","definition":"The phenomenon of accommodation, in general, is the process by which something becomes common ground in virtue of one party recognizing that the other takes it to be common ground.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"speaker presupposition","aliases":["speaker presuppositions","presuppositions"],"definition":"If the common ground of a conversation is identified (on our simplifying assumption) with the common belief of the participants, then the presuppositions of an individual speaker can be identified with what the speaker believes to be common belief. … the complex operator ‘BaC’ will be the operator for Alice’s speaker presuppositions.","exact":true,"avoid":["stalnaker1970-pragmatics/t3","stalnaker1973-presuppositions/t1","stalnaker1974-pragmatic-presuppositions/t1"]}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Alice says “I can't come to the meeting – I have to pick up my sister at the airport”. … suppose Bob had no idea, prior to the speech act, that Alice had a sister, and that Alice knew that Bob did not know about her sister.","phenomenon":"presupposition accommodation","near":["factive presupposition","presupposition cancellation","presupposition filtering"],"avoid":["informative presupposition","existential presupposition","accommodation","global accommodation","presupposition failure","presupposition projection","referential use of a definite description"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"two Californians are sitting in a room together in the evening, and suddenly there is an unexpected power outage, and all the lights go out. Assuming that the two are initially mutually aware of each other, when the event happens it will immediately be common belief that it has happened.","phenomenon":"manifest event","aliases":["event"],"near":["presupposition accommodation","defective context","informative presupposition"],"avoid":["common knowledge","common belief","mutual knowledge","public announcement"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Alice says to Bob, who is holding his baby daughter, “how old is he?” … Bob recognizes that Alice is taking something to be common belief that he knows to be false.","phenomenon":"defective context","near":["informative presupposition","presupposition projection","presupposition cancellation"],"avoid":["presupposition failure","false presupposition","presupposition accommodation","accommodation"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"to use a sentence like “tonight Sam is having dinner in New York too” appropriately, it is not enough to presuppose that someone other than Sam is having dinner in New York tonight. That would be common belief in any normal context, but the use of that sentence would be odd in any context in which there has been no prior discussion of others having dinner in New York","phenomenon":"anaphoric presupposition","near":["factive presupposition","bridging (associative) anaphora","scalar implicature"],"avoid":["presupposition failure","conventional implicature","additive presupposition","strong contextual felicity constraint","formal link (overt antecedent) requirement on anaphora","discourse anaphora","cross-sentential anaphora","cross-sentential (discourse) anaphora","hard presupposition trigger"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1023/a:1020867916902"},{"id":"bach1994-conversational-impliciture","title":"Conversational Impliciture","authors":["Kent Bach"],"year":"1994","area":"language","topic":"implicature","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I suggest that we simply recognize a distinct category, the implicit, between the explicit and the implicated.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Completion is the filling in of a propositional radical, and expansion is the fleshing out of the minimal proposition expressible by an utterance.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Grice's said/implicated distinction is not exhaustive: there is a middle ground of content implicit in what is said, built out of it by filling in or fleshing out, which is neither strictly said nor a separate implicature.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"What is said must correspond closely to the uttered sentence's syntactic constituents, so it may be a mere propositional radical; its completions and expansions belong neither to it nor to 'explicit content', contra relevance-theoretic explicature and Recanati's availability principle.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"conversational impliciture","aliases":["impliciture"],"definition":"the speaker is not being fully explicit. Rather, he intends the hearer to read something into the utterance, to regard it as if it contained certain conceptual material that is not in fact there. The result of completion and/or expansion is what I call conversational impliciture.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"completion","aliases":["complete"],"definition":"whenever an utterance, even after disambiguation and reference fixing, does not by virtue of linguistic meaning express a complete proposition. When a sentence is in this way semantically underdeterminate, understanding its utterance requires a process of completion to produce a full proposition.","exact":true,"avoid":["recanati1989-pragmatics-said/t3"]},{"id":"t3","term":"expansion","definition":"The process of expansion is … not mandated conceptually but merely pragmatically. … there is already a complete proposition …, albeit not the one that is being communicated by the speaker. The proposition being communicated is a conceptually enriched or elaborated version of the one explicitly expressed by the utterance itself.","exact":true,"avoid":["recanati1989-pragmatics-said/t4"]},{"id":"t4","term":"sentence nonliterality","aliases":["nonliterality","nonliterally"],"definition":"a common but not widely recognized kind of nonliterality, whereby a sentence is used nonliterally without any of its constituents being so used. … It is unlike metaphor, metonymy, and other sorts of constituent nonliterality, for it does not involve the figurative use of any particular word or phrase.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"propositional radical","definition":"What the conventional meaning of a semantically underdeterminate sentence determines, even after disambiguation and reference assignment: content short of a truth-evaluable whole, which yields a truth condition only once it is conceptually filled in (the sentence elaborated somehow).","exact":false}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"(1) Steel isn't strong enough. … With (1) we need to know strong enough for what. Notice that (1) does not express the weak proposition that steel isn't strong enough for something or other. … An utterance of (1) must be taken to mean that steel isn't strong enough in some contextually identifiable respect, e.g. for building a 500-storey building or to resist bending by Superman.","phenomenon":"semantic underdetermination (completion)","near":["free pragmatic enrichment","syntactic ellipsis","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)"],"avoid":["unarticulated constituent","conversational impliciture","propositional radical","constituent underdetermination","saturation of a linguistically mandated slot","saturation","explicature","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","conversational implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"You can use [‘I haven’t eaten breakfast’] to mean you haven’t eaten breakfast today or even [‘I haven’t eaten’] to mean that you haven’t eaten dinner today. Similarly, you might use [‘I have nothing to wear’] to mean that you have nothing appropriate to wear to a certain wedding, without having to include an extra phrase in your utterance.","phenomenon":"free pragmatic enrichment","near":["semantic underdetermination (completion)","conventional implicature","metonymy"],"avoid":["sentence nonliterality","free enrichment","pragmatic enrichment","unarticulated constituent","quantifier domain restriction","impliciture","particularized conversational implicature","generalized conversational implicature","conversational implicature","explicature","hyperbole (overstatement)","loose (relaxed) use of an expression","conversational impliciture (expansion)","deictic (referential) use of tense"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"(1) The ham sandwich is getting restless. … where 'the ham sandwich' is being used by a waiter to refer to a patron who ordered a ham sandwich. … how can the hearer go from the concept of ham sandwich to that of ham-sandwich-orderer without first entertaining the absurd minimal proposition?","phenomenon":"metonymy","aliases":["metonymic","metonymical"],"near":["metaphor","catachresis","semantic underdetermination (completion)"],"avoid":["referential use of a definite description","deferred reference","local expansion","conversational impliciture (expansion)","free pragmatic enrichment","speaker's reference","explicature","semantic anomaly"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"(20) I didn't trap two mongeese—I trapped two mongooses. (21) He's not an animal doctor—he's a veterinarian. (22) I was referring not to DonnELLan but to DONNellan. (23) I'm not his brother—he's my brother. (22), for example, would obviously not be used to mean 'I was referring not only to DonnELLan but to DONNellan'. What is going on in these utterances is that the speaker is objecting to one way of putting something and puts it another way.","phenomenon":"metalinguistic negation","near":["presupposition-denying (external) negation","scalar implicature","verbal irony"],"avoid":["contrastive focus","symmetric contrastive focus","use–mention distinction","local expansion","association with focus"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1111/j.1468-0017.1994.tb00220.x"},{"id":"recanati1989-pragmatics-said","title":"The Pragmatics of What Is Said","authors":["François Recanati"],"year":"1989","area":"language","topic":"implicature","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"In deciding whether a pragmatically determined aspect of utterance meaning is part of what is said, … we should always try to preserve our pre-theoretic intuitions on the matter.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Besides the conversational implicatures, which are external to (and combine with) what is said, there are other nonconventional, pragmatic aspects of utterance meaning, which are constitutive of what is said.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Whether a contextually determined aspect of an utterance's meaning belongs to the proposition expressed or is merely implicated must be settled in line with ordinary speakers' pre-theoretic intuitions, since the proposition expressed is consciously accessible to them.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"An implicature is defined by how it is recovered—inferred from an already enriched proposition—not by formal relations like entailment; and genuine implicatures, unlike contextually supplied constituents of the proposition expressed, cannot fall within the scope of negation or conditionals.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"Availability Principle","aliases":["Availability","available","availability"],"definition":"any decision concerning what is said and what is implicated must be consistent with our pre-theoretic intuitions on the matter","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"Minimalist Principle","aliases":["Minimalist","minimalist"],"definition":"A pragmatically determined aspect of meaning is part of what is said if and only if its determination is necessary for the utterance to express a complete proposition.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"saturation","aliases":["saturating","saturated"],"definition":"the meaning of the sentence sets up a slot (representable as a variable: 'He has bought the book that bears relation x to John') that must be contextually filled for the utterance to express a complete proposition. This type of enrichment I shall call 'saturation'","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"strengthening","aliases":["strengthened","strengthen"],"definition":"The input to this second type of enrichment is a complete proposition, and the output is a richer proposition, i.e. one that entails the input proposition. I shall refer to this type of enrichment as 'strengthening'.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"Scope Principle","aliases":["scope"],"definition":"A pragmatically determined aspect of meaning is part of what is said (and, therefore, not a conversational implicature) if—and, perhaps, only if—it falls within the scope of logical operators such as negation and conditionals.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"formal fallacy","aliases":["formal","fallacy"],"definition":"The mistake of classifying a communicated content (as a direct speech act, an explicature, or part of what is said) by some relation it happens to bear to the sentence or to what is said—congruence, development of logical form, entailment—rather than by how it is actually performed or recovered.","exact":false}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose the speaker utters 'John has three children', thereby communicating that John has exactly three children. It is customary to say that the proposition literally expressed by 'John has three children' is the proposition that John has at least three children, even if what the speaker means to communicate by this utterance is that John has exactly three children.","phenomenon":"scalar implicature","aliases":["scalar"],"near":["conventional implicature","existential presupposition","quantifier domain restriction"],"avoid":["conversational implicature","generalized conversational implicature","Q-based implicature","Q-implicature","quantity implicature","Horn scale (scale of predicate strength)","free pragmatic enrichment","pragmatic strengthening","explicature","conversational impliciture (expansion)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"once the identity of the speaker and the time of utterance has been fixed, ['I have had breakfast'] expresses a proposition, viz. the proposition that the speaker has had breakfast at least once before the time of utterance. This proposition, which would be true if the speaker had had breakfast twenty years earlier and never since, does not correspond to what the speaker means to say when he utters 'I have had breakfast'. What the speaker says goes beyond the minimal proposition expressible","phenomenon":"free pragmatic enrichment","aliases":["enrichment","enrich","enriched"],"near":["lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","presupposition accommodation","conventional implicature"],"avoid":["conversational implicature","strengthening","pragmatic strengthening","explicature","free enrichment","pragmatic enrichment","enrichment","conversational impliciture (expansion)","impliciture","unarticulated constituent","saturation of a linguistically mandated slot","quantifier domain restriction","semantic underdetermination (completion)","R-based implicature","deictic (referential) use of tense"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"[Consider] the utterance 'Everybody went to Paris'. Under ordinary circumstances, what a speaker would mean by this is not that everybody in the absolute sense … went to Paris, but that everybody in some (contextually identifiable) group went to Paris. Suppose, for example, it is established that what the speaker means is that every member of the Johnson and Johnson staff went to Paris.","phenomenon":"quantifier domain restriction","aliases":["domain","restriction","restricted"],"near":["incomplete definite description resolved by salience","scalar implicature","generic interpretation"],"avoid":["conversational impliciture (expansion)","impliciture","free pragmatic enrichment","saturation of a linguistically mandated slot","sentence nonliterality","standardized nonliterality","semantic underdetermination (completion)","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"To understand what is said by 'He has bought John's book', … one must also identify the relation that is supposed to hold between John and the book. … 'John's book' therefore means something like 'the book that bears relation x to John'.","phenomenon":"saturation of a linguistically mandated slot","aliases":["saturation","saturate","slot"],"near":["pragmatic strengthening","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","conversational implicature"],"avoid":["semantic underdetermination (completion)","completion","free pragmatic enrichment","reference assignment","contextual disambiguation of a shared name","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"If the old king has died of a heart attack and a republic has been declared, then Tom will be quite content. If a republic has been declared and the old king has died of a heart attack, then Tom will be quite content. … it is possible to assert [the first] and to deny [the second] in the same breath without contradiction.","phenomenon":"embedded implicature","aliases":["embedded","implicature","implicatures"],"near":["presupposition projection","conditional perfection","conventional implicature"],"avoid":["conversational implicature","generalized conversational implicature","intrusive construction","free pragmatic enrichment","pragmatic strengthening"],"exact":true},{"id":"e6","text":"A: Was there anybody rich at the party, who might be asked to pay for the damages? B: Jim is rich. A: Yes, but did he go to the party? B: I don't know, but I can tell you that if anybody was there, Jim was there. A: Somebody was there—this I know for sure (I saw John going there). So it looks as if the damages will be paid for, after all.","phenomenon":"particularized conversational implicature","aliases":["implicature","implicates","implicated"],"near":["explicature","conventional implicature","presupposition accommodation"],"avoid":["conversational implicature","implicature from observing the maxim of Relation","relevance implicature","existential statement carrying a speaker's reference"],"exact":false}],"doi":"10.1111/j.1468-0017.1989.tb00258.x"},{"id":"partee1986-noun-phrase-interpretation","title":"Noun Phrase Interpretation and Type-Shifting Principles","authors":["Barbara Partee"],"year":"1986","area":"language","topic":"quantification & formal semantics","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I will retain from Montague's approach the requirement of a systematic category-to-type correspondence, but allow each category to correspond to a family of types rather than just a single type.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"For an extensional sublanguage I propose basic NP types e (“referential”), (e, t) (“predicative”), and ((e, t), t) (“quantificational”).","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Montague's uniform generalized-quantifier treatment of noun phrases and the traditional split into referring, predicative and quantificational NPs are compatible: every NP has a quantifier meaning, whose model-theoretic properties largely predict which entity and predicate meanings it also has.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Mappings link the domains e, ⟨e,t⟩, ⟨⟨e,t⟩,t⟩: lift(j) = λP[P(j)], inverse lower; ident(j) = λx[x = j], inverse iota; BE(𝒫) = λx[{x} ∈ 𝒫], the unique Boolean homomorphism with BE(lift(j)) = ident(j); and BE(A(P)) = P.","formal":true}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"BE","definition":"What exactly does BE do? … in set-theoretical terms: λ𝒫[λx[{x} ∈ 𝒫]]. That is, it applies to a generalized quantifier, finds all the singletons therein, and collects their elements into a set. … a (potentially universal) operator that is always available to turn an ((e,t),t) meaning into an (e,t) meaning.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"lift","aliases":["Lift"],"definition":"Lift maps any entity a onto the principal ultrafilter generated by a; in IL terms, it maps (the denotation of) j onto (the denotation of) λP[P(j)]; in set-theoretic terms, it maps a onto λX[a ∈ X]. Lift is total and injective.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"ident","aliases":["Ident"],"definition":"Ident is the total, injective operation mapping any element onto its singleton set; in IL terms, it maps j onto λx[x = j].","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"attribute noun","aliases":["attribute nouns","attribute"],"definition":"Color is a common noun, its type (e,t); entities in its extension are … properties (blue, red, etc.). This is the semantic content of what I mean by “attribute noun”: these nouns express properties of properties.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"John/the man/a man walked in. He looked tired. … Every man/no man/more than one man walked in. *He looked tired.","phenomenon":"cross-sentential anaphora","near":["donkey anaphora","bound-variable pronoun","cataphora (backward anaphora)"],"avoid":["cross-sentential (discourse) anaphora","E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora","accessibility constraint on anaphora","inaccessible discourse referent (scope-limited lifespan)","discourse anaphora"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Mary considers John competent in semantics and an authority on unicorns. … Mary considers that an island / two islands / many islands / the prettiest island / the harbor / *every island / *most islands / *this island / *?Schiermonnikoog / Utopia.","phenomenon":"predicative noun phrase (⟨e,t⟩ reading)","near":["definiteness effect (existential there-sentences)","quantifier scope ambiguity","generic noun phrase"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"(14) This house has been every color. I believe this apparent counterexample and others like it can be explained in terms of the idiosyncratic and language-particular behavior of the head noun. … (16) (a) This shirt is blue. (b) Blue is a nice color. (c) This shirt is a nice color.","phenomenon":"predicative attribute NP (Williams puzzle)","near":["equative (identity) copular sentence","specificational copular sentence","generic sentence"],"avoid":["predicative noun phrase (⟨e,t⟩ reading)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"John would have to be “lifted” from j to λP[P(j)] to interpret the conjunction John and every woman.","phenomenon":"type lifting (e to ⟨⟨e,t⟩,t⟩)","aliases":["lifted"],"near":["type lowering","quantifier raising (QR)","nominalization of a predicate"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1515/9783112420027-006"},{"id":"sperber1978-irony-use-mention","title":"Irony and the Use-Mention Distinction","authors":["Dan Sperber","Deirdre Wilson"],"year":"1978/1981","area":"language","topic":"metaphor & figurative language","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"What we are claiming is that all standard cases of irony, and many that are nonstandard from the traditional point of view, involve (generally implicit) mention of a proposition.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[In irony, the] speaker mentions a proposition in such a way as to make clear that he rejects it as ludicrously false, inappropriate, or irrelevant.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Ironical utterances do not substitute a figurative or implicated opposite for what is said; they echo a real or imagined utterance or opinion and convey the speaker's dissociative, mocking attitude toward it.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Violating the maxim of truthfulness is neither necessary nor sufficient for irony: ironical questions and understatements need not be false, and many false utterances are not ironical; what irony requires is that some proposition be echoed.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"echoic mention","aliases":["mention","mentioned","echoic","echo","echoing"],"definition":"Mention of a proposition that someone has uttered, implied, or is taken to think (just now, long ago, or in anticipation), made not to inform anyone of its content but to convey the speaker's own attitude (doubtful, scornful, approving…) toward it.","exact":false},{"id":"t2","term":"parody","aliases":["parodist"],"definition":"Both irony and parody are types of mention: Irony involves mention of propositions; parody involves mention of linguistic expressions. In other words, parody is related to direct discourse as irony is to free indirect discourse.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"sarcasm","definition":"When the utterance or opinion echoed has no specific originator, there will be no victim; when there is a specific, recognizable originator, he will be the victim. Thus, when the speaker echoes himself, the irony will be self-directed; when he echoes his hearer, the result will be sarcasm.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"rhetorical-pragmatic theory","aliases":["rhetorical-pragmatic","rhetorical"],"definition":"a logical-pragmatic theory dealing with the interpretation of utterances as an inferential process must be supplemented by what could be called a 'rhetorical-pragmatic,' or 'rhetorical' theory dealing with evocation.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Consider the utterances … exchanged between two people caught in a downpour in circumstances that are otherwise normal: … It seems to be raining.","phenomenon":"meiosis (understatement)","near":["ironical antiphrasis (saying the opposite)","hyperbole (overstatement)","rhetorical question"],"avoid":["verbal irony","flouting the maxim of Relation","flouting the maxim of Quantity","conversational implicature","particularized conversational implicature","citational grafting (citationality)","parasitic (non-serious) use of language","ironical understatement (meiosis)","violation of the non-redundancy (informativity) constraint on assertion","R-based implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"'You take an eager interest in that gentleman's concerns', said Darcy in a less tranquil tone, and with a heightened colour. 'Who that knows what his misfortunes have been, can help feeling an interest in him?' 'His misfortunes!' repeated Darcy contemptuously, 'yes, his misfortunes have been great indeed.'","phenomenon":"echoic irony","aliases":["repeated"],"near":["dramatic irony","situational irony","Socratic irony (feigned ignorance)"],"avoid":["verbal irony","conversational implicature","particularized conversational implicature","citational grafting (citationality)","parasitic (non-serious) use of language"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"The master began to understand and to share the intense disgust which the archdeacon always expressed when Mrs Proudie's name was mentioned. What was he to do with such a woman as this?","phenomenon":"free indirect discourse","near":["direct quotation","indirect (reported) speech","free direct discourse"],"avoid":["citational grafting (citationality)","parasitic (non-serious) use of language","perspective shift of an expressive"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Suppose the following remark is made ironically to someone who dislikes classical music: … Of course all classical music sounds the same!","phenomenon":"sarcasm","near":["self-directed irony","dramatic irony","meiosis (understatement)"],"avoid":["verbal irony","conversational implicature","particularized conversational implicature","citational grafting (citationality)","parasitic (non-serious) use of language"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"horn1984-toward-new-taxonomy","title":"Toward a New Taxonomy for Pragmatic Inference: Q-Based and R-Based Implicature","authors":["Laurence R. Horn"],"year":"1984","area":"language","topic":"implicature","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I have tentatively boiled down the maxims (leaving aside Quality) to two fundamental principles, echoing the two functional economies of Zipf and Martinet.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[Diverse linguistic domains] are all motivated and governed by the same functional dynamic, the ongoing Zipfo-Gricean dialectic between the Q-based Sufficiency Principle and the R-based Principle of Least Effort.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Quality aside, Grice's maxims reduce to two opposed principles: a hearer-based one demanding sufficient information, which yields upper-bounding implicatures (some → not all), and a speaker-based least-effort one, which yields lower-bounding, enriching implicatures (can you pass the salt → request).","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Opposed pressures for sufficiency and economy reach an equilibrium: unmarked, simpler forms convey stereotypical situations, while a marked, more prolix alternative implicates a non-stereotypical one (kill vs cause to die); the pattern recurs across syntax, lexicon and language change.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"Q Principle","aliases":["principle"],"definition":"Hearer-based: MAKE YOUR CONTRIBUTION SUFFICIENT … SAY AS MUCH AS YOU CAN … Lower-bounding principle, inducing upper-bounding implicata","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"R Principle","aliases":["principle"],"definition":"Speaker-based: MAKE YOUR CONTRIBUTION NECESSARY … SAY NO MORE THAN YOU MUST … Upper-bounding principle, inducing lower-bounding implicata","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"division of pragmatic labor","definition":"The use of a marked (relatively complex and/or prolix) expression when a corresponding unmarked (simpler, less 'effortful') alternate expression is available tends to be interpreted as conveying a marked message (one which the unmarked alternative would not or could not have conveyed).","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"autohyponymy","aliases":["autohyponym","Autohyponymy"],"definition":"some words are hyponyms of themselves: dog and bitch are (sex-differentiated) co-hyponyms of dog, lion and lioness of lion, etc. In these cases, we can call the unmarked term (dog or lion) an 'autohyponym'. Autohyponymy thus represents privative polysemy or ambiguity within a single lexical item","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"If I tell you that some of my friends are Buddhists, I license you to draw the inference that not all my friends are Buddhists.","phenomenon":"scalar implicature","near":["R-based implicature","conventional implicature","presupposition"],"avoid":["Q-based implicature","Q-implicature","quantity implicature","generalized conversational implicature","conversational implicature","Horn scale (scale of predicate strength)","upper-bounding implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"my confiding to you that I broke a finger would normally be taken to refer to a finger of mine (given Atlas and Levinson's Conventions of Noncontroversiality) unless I knew you knew that I was an enforcer for the mob.","phenomenon":"R-based implicature","near":["Q-based implicature","conventional implicature","presupposition accommodation"],"avoid":["I-implicature","informativeness implicature","stereotype implicature","generalized conversational implicature","conversational implicature","conversational impliciture (expansion)","free pragmatic enrichment","pragmatic enrichment","implicature from observing the maxim of Relation","interaction of maxims (Relation over Quantity)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"He didn't eat 3 carrots—he ate 4 of them. … You didn't eat some of the cookies—you ate all of them. … It isn't possible she'll win—it's certain she will. … I'm not happy—I'm ecstatic. … It isn't warm—it's downright hot.","phenomenon":"metalinguistic negation","near":["neg-raising","litotes","descriptive (truth-functional) negation"],"avoid":["implicature cancellation","cancellation of an implicature","cancellability","scalar implicature","Horn scale (scale of predicate strength)","conversational implicature","corrective negation","explicit cancellation of an implicature","generalized conversational implicature","embedded implicature","symmetric contrastive focus"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Black Bart killed the sheriff. … Black Bart caused the sheriff to die. … [the latter] suggests that [the former] could not have been used appropriately, possibly because Bart caused the sheriff's gun to backfire by stuffing it with cotton, or arranged for scorpions to be placed in the room of the sheriff (who is known to have a weak heart)","phenomenon":"division of pragmatic labor","near":["conventional implicature","conditional perfection","neg-raising"],"avoid":["M-implicature","manner implicature","marked form, marked meaning","blocking","partial blocking","conversational implicature","scalar implicature","Q-based implicature","R-based implicature","generalized conversational implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"the existence of bitch 'female dog', in the absence of a sex-specific mate, leads the general term dog to develop a hyponymic sense designating the male of the species. … it is only when the sex of the beast is relevant that bitches do not count as dogs.","phenomenon":"Q-based lexical narrowing","near":["R-based lexical narrowing","lexical broadening","synonym differentiation (Bréal's Law of Differentiation)"],"avoid":["autohyponymy","Q-based narrowing","partial blocking","conversational implicature","scalar implicature","Q-based implicature","division of pragmatic labor","lexical ambiguity","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","homonymy (lexical ambiguity)","semantic narrowing","lexical narrowing","synecdoche","metonymy","generalized conversational implicature"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"horn1969-presuppositional-analysis-only","title":"A Presuppositional Analysis of Only and Even","authors":["Laurence R. Horn"],"year":"1969","area":"language","topic":"presupposition & projection","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"even (like also) asserts what only presupposes and presupposes the negation of what only asserts.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[Only Muriel voted for Hubert] presupposes that Muriel voted for Hubert and asserts that … No one other than Muriel voted for Hubert.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Term-scope only is a two-place predicate: Only(x=a, Fx) presupposes Fx and asserts ¬∃y(y≠x ∧ Fy); so 'Only Muriel voted for Hubert' presupposes that Muriel voted for Hubert and asserts that no one else did.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"An exclusive sentence does not mean the logicians' conjunction 'Muriel voted for Hubert and no one else did': its denials and questions leave the first conjunct intact, so that conjunct is presupposed and the second alone is asserted.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"presupposition","aliases":["presupposes","Presuppositions","presupposition"],"definition":"If (S→S') and (¬S→S'), then S presupposes S'. … Presuppositions are thereby distinguished from assertions by their invariance under both question and negation.","exact":true,"avoid":["stalnaker1974-pragmatic-presuppositions/t2"]},{"id":"t2","term":"even","aliases":["Even"],"definition":"Even(x=a, Fx) — P: (∃y)(y≠x ∧ Fy); A: Fx. 'Even Muriel voted for Hubert' presupposes that someone other than Muriel voted for Hubert and asserts that Muriel did; its negation denies only the assertion, keeping the presupposition.","exact":false},{"id":"t4","term":"scale of degree of strength","aliases":["scales of degree of strength","scales","scale"],"definition":"some set E of scales of degree of strength such that each member Eᵢ ∈ E is a two-place relation which partially orders a (semantic) class of predicates","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"It's not true that only Muriel voted for Hubert. b. Not only Muriel voted for Hubert. … Did only Muriel vote for Hubert? No,... Possible and impossible candidates for such a continuation include (9) a. Lyndon did too. b. Somebody else did as well, but I forget who. c. *She didn't. d. *The election never took place.","phenomenon":"presupposition projection through holes","near":["presupposition filtering","presupposition accommodation","negative polarity licensing"],"avoid":["presupposition projection from negation and questions","presupposition projection through negation and modals","presupposition surviving negation and questions","presupposition hole","presupposition projection","presupposition failure"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"John only likes rice (...he doesn't love it). b. John only dislikes rice (...he doesn't hate it). c. ?John only eats rice (...?). d. *John only loves rice, he doesn't like it. e. *John only hates rice, he doesn't dislike it.","phenomenon":"Horn scale (scale of predicate strength)","near":["contrary antonymy","vagueness","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)"],"avoid":["scalar implicature","association with focus"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Only a few, for example, behaves like an L-class quantifier (e.g. few) rather than an M-class quantifier (e.g. a few), as indicated by the direction of entailment in the sentences (48) a. Only a few girls are clever and only a few girls are seductive. --> b. Only a few girls are both clever and seductive.","phenomenon":"downward-entailing (monotone decreasing) quantifier","aliases":["quantifier"],"near":["upward-entailing (monotone increasing) quantifier","non-monotone quantifier","quantifier scope ambiguity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Given that Hubert is Muriel's husband, 'Only Muriel voted for her husband' is not a paraphrase of 'Only Muriel voted for Hubert': it can be taken (and the author can only take it) to mean 'Muriel was the only one who voted for her (own) husband.' Compare 'Only Lucifer pities himself' with 'Only Lucifer pities Lucifer'.","phenomenon":"bound-variable pronoun","near":["strict (coreferential) reading","donkey anaphora","E-type pronoun"],"avoid":["bound-variable reading of a pronoun","sloppy identity reading"],"exact":false}],"doi":null},{"id":"karttunen1979-conventional-implicature","title":"Conventional Implicature","authors":["Lauri Karttunen","Stanley Peters"],"year":"1979","area":"language","topic":"implicature","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"we do take issue with the view that all so called presuppositions are instances of the same phenomenon.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The main objective of our chapter is to show how model-theoretic methods of semantic interpretation can be extended to account for both truth-conditional and conventionally implicated meanings of sentences.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"What are called presuppositions are heterogeneous: some are conversational implicatures, but those of even, too, manage, certain factives and clefts are encoded and uncancelable, captured by giving each phrase of a Montague grammar a second, non-truth-conditional translation.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Each phrase translates as a pair ⟨αᵉ; αⁱ⟩ of extension and implicature expressions; functors' heritage expressions govern projection, and conditionals get (if φ then ψ)ⁱ = ¬K¬φᵉ ∧ φⁱ ∧ (φᵉ → ψⁱ).","formal":true}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"implicature expression","aliases":["expressions","implicates","implicatures"],"definition":"Each English phrase is associated with two expressions of intensional logic. … The second one, the implicature expression, signifies what the phrase conventionally implicates (if it is a sentence), what it contributes (if it is smaller than a sentence) to the conventional implicatures of sentences having it as a part.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"extension expression","aliases":["expressions","expression"],"definition":"Each English phrase is associated with two expressions of intensional logic. One of these, which we will call the extension expression, is identical to the single translation that Montague would provide. This expression stands for what logicians would call the denotation of the phrase, roughly the things of which the phrase is true.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"heritage function","aliases":["heritage expression"],"definition":"the heritage function, which assigns to every [predicate or operator phrase] a suitable heritage expression that determines what happens to the implicatures of its argument phrases","exact":true,"avoid":["heim1983-projection-problem-presuppositions/t2"]},{"id":"t4","term":"contradiction negation","aliases":["negation"],"definition":"ordinary negation pertains just to the proposition expressed by the corresponding affirmative sentence; it does not affect conventional implicatures. Contradiction negation, on the other hand, pertains to the total meaning of its target sentence, ignoring the distinction between truth conditions and conventional implicatures.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"common ground","definition":"a set of propositions that any participant is rationally justified in taking for granted, for example, by virtue of what has been said in the conversation up to that point, what all the participants are in a position to perceive as true, whatever else they mutually know, assume, and so on.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"If Shakespeare were the author of Macbeth, there would be proof in the Globe Theater's records for the year 1605. Certainly it is possible to indicate one's belief that Shakespeare did not write Macbeth by uttering this sentence in a context where the Globe Theater's records for the year 1605 have just been searched and found to lack any evidence of Shakespeare's authorship. … In a different sort of context, the sentence may indicate that its antecedent could well be true.","phenomenon":"particularized conversational implicature","near":["generalized conversational implicature","conventional implicature","semantic presupposition"],"avoid":["conversational implicature","contextual cancellation of an implicature","cancelability of an implicature","implicature from observing the maxim of Relation","subjunctive (counterfactual) conditional","non-counterfactual subjunctive conditional"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Even Bill likes Mary. … the word even in this sentence plays no role in determining its truth conditions … One is entitled to infer from [it] not just that Bill likes Mary but also … Other people besides Bill like Mary. … Of the people under consideration, Bill is the least likely to like Mary.","phenomenon":"conventional implicature","near":["particularized conversational implicature","generalized conversational implicature","truth-conditional entailment"],"avoid":["scalar implicature","association with focus","anaphoric presupposition","presupposition","existential presupposition"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"if I report John's speech act by saying [\"John criticized Harry for writing the letter\"], then in the absence of further qualification the principle of trust justifies the assumption on the part of my addressee that John's speech act was felicitous. And if it was felicitous, then its preparatory condition had to have been met … Thus my utterance of [the sentence] will usually convey that Harry did write the letter.","phenomenon":"generalized conversational implicature","near":["particularized conversational implicature","conventional implicature","scalar implicature"],"avoid":["conversational implicature","factive presupposition","presupposition","semantic presupposition","presupposition accommodation","convention of truthfulness and trust","convention of truthfulness","expressive speech act"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Someone managed to succeed George V on the throne of England. What the rules given here predict is (correctly) that this sentence is true iff someone succeeded George V to the throne and (incorrectly) that it conventionally implicates that it was difficult for someone to do that. … What our rules as stated lack is any way of linking the choice of a person who is implicated to have difficulty to the choice of a person who is asserted to have succeeded.","phenomenon":"binding problem for presuppositions","near":["presupposition filtering","donkey anaphora","local accommodation"],"avoid":["binding problem","the binding problem for presuppositions"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1163/9789004368880_002"},{"id":"potts2007-expressive-dimension","title":"The Expressive Dimension","authors":["Christopher Potts"],"year":"2007","area":"language","topic":"slurs, expressives & social language","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Expressives do not change the descriptive content at all. They function solely at the contextual level, by actively changing the context.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"An expressive index is a triple ⟨a I b⟩, where a and b are in the domain of entities and I ⊑ [-1, 1].","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Expressive content (damn, bastard, honorifics) is a meaning layer separate from descriptive content: nondisplaceable, judge-relative, descriptively ineffable, performatively immediate, and strengthened rather than made redundant by repetition; all six properties flow from one formal source.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Expressive morphemes denote functions into ε, the type of context-to-context functions: ⟦damn⟧(d)(c) = c′, where c′ records the judge's index ⟨cJ I d⟩ with I ⊑ [-1, 0], length ≤ .5, narrowing any earlier interval.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"expressive index","aliases":["expressive indices","expressive"],"definition":"An expressive index is a triple ⟨a I b⟩, where a and b are in the domain of entities and I ⊑ [-1, 1]. … We can read ⟨a I b⟩ as conveying that individual a is at expressive level I for individual b.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"expressive consistency","aliases":["expressive"],"definition":"A context c is admissible only if cε contains at most one expressive object ⟨a I b⟩ for every salient pair of entities a and b.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"nondisplaceability","aliases":["nondisplaceable"],"definition":"Expressives cannot (outside of direct quotation) be used to report on past events, attitudes, or emotions, nor can they express mere possibilities, conjectures, or suppositions. They always tell us something about the utterance situation itself.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"perspective dependence","aliases":["perspective"],"definition":"Expressive content is evaluated from a particular perspective. In general, the perspective is the speaker's, but there can be deviations if conditions are right.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"descriptive ineffability","aliases":["ineffability","ineffable","descriptive"],"definition":"Speakers are never fully satisfied when they paraphrase expressive content using descriptive, i.e., nonexpressive, terms.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"immediacy","aliases":["immediate"],"definition":"Like performatives, expressives achieve their intended act simply by being uttered; they do not offer content so much as inflict it.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Whenever I pour wine, the damn bottle drips. … One might expect the meaning of damn to vary as well … That is not what we intuit, though. Rather, we infer from the speaker's use of damn that he is in a heighted emotional state right this minute.","phenomenon":"nondisplaceability of expressives","aliases":["nondisplaceable","nondisplaceability"],"near":["quantificational variability effect","perspective shift of an expressive","local accommodation"],"avoid":["projection of conventional implicatures","conventional implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Kratzer argues that the epithet that bastard indicates a negative emotion of the speaker's father … rather than the speaker herself. … My father screamed that he would never allow me to marry that bastard Webster.","phenomenon":"perspective shift of an expressive","aliases":["perspective","judge"],"near":["nondisplaceability of expressives","speaker-oriented expressive content","ironic use of an epithet"],"avoid":["monster (context-shifting operator)","de re / de dicto ambiguity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"in the following group of sentences, we find a heightening of the emotional state of the judge (speaker) as we move down the list: … Damn, I left my keys in the car. … Damn, I left my damn keys in the car. … Damn, I left my damn keys in the damn car.","phenomenon":"repeatability of expressives","aliases":["repeatability","repeated","repetition"],"near":["contrastive focus reduplication","nondisplaceability of expressives","descriptive ineffability"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"A school superintendent, meaning to \"make his stand against racism clear\", says in a speech that n——s come in all colors, and that to him a n—— is someone who doesn't respect themselves or others. His intentions are good, but the community is outraged; the damage is done as soon as the word escapes his lips, and his attempt to redefine it is futile.","phenomenon":"immediacy of expressives","aliases":["immediacy","immediate"],"near":["slur reclamation (appropriation)","perspective shift of an expressive","nondisplaceability of expressives"],"avoid":["performative utterance","unintentional illocutionary act (forms taking charge)","perlocutionary frustration"],"exact":false},{"id":"e5","text":"I'm the only one around here who will admit that I could be wrong. … [I am the unique x such that x will admit that x could be wrong]","phenomenon":"fake indexical","aliases":["indexical"],"near":["essential indexical","monstrous indexical shift","donkey anaphora"],"avoid":["bound-variable pronoun","sloppy identity reading"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1515/tl.2007.011"},{"id":"simons2010-projects-why","title":"What Projects and Why","authors":["Mandy Simons","Judith Tonhauser","David Beaver","Craige Roberts"],"year":"2010","area":"language","topic":"presupposition & projection","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"All and only those implications of (embedded) sentences which are not-at-issue relative to the Question Under Discussion in the context have the potential to project.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"projection is a consequence of the scope of sentential operators such as negation, conditionals and modals typically being limited roughly to what is understood as the main point","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"p is at-issue relative to the QUD Q iff ?p = {p, ¬p} is relevant to Q: some answer to ?p contextually entails a partial or complete answer to Q. Implications can project iff not at-issue.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Common-ground and anaphoric explanations of projection fail for appositives, whose content is new and needs no antecedent; independent-dimension accounts fail for presuppositions, which interact with ordinary content. Unified alternative: operators ignore whatever is not the main point.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"projection","aliases":["projects"],"definition":"An implication projects if and only if it survives as an utterance implication when the expression that triggers the implication occurs under the syntactic scope of an entailment-cancelling operator.","exact":true,"avoid":["tonhauser2013-toward-taxonomy-projective/t3"]},{"id":"t2","term":"at-issueness (basic definition)","aliases":["at-issueness","at-issue"],"definition":"For any proposition p, let ?p denote the question whether p, i.e. the partition on the set of worlds with members p and ¬p. … A proposition p is at-issue relative to a question Q iff ?p is relevant to Q.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"Relevance to the QUD","aliases":["relevant"],"definition":"a. An assertion is relevant to a QUD iff it contextually entails a partial or complete answer to the QUD. b. A question is relevant to a QUD iff it has an answer which contextually entails a partial or complete answer to the QUD.","exact":true,"avoid":["roberts1996-information-structure-discourse/t3"]},{"id":"t4","term":"at-issueness (revised definition)","aliases":["at-issueness","at-issue"],"definition":"a. A proposition p is at-issue iff the speaker intends to address the QUD via ?p. b. An intention to address the QUD via ?p is felicitous only if: i. ?p is relevant to the QUD, and ii. the speaker can reasonably expect the addressee to recognize this intention.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Background scenario: a nutritionist has been visiting first grade classrooms to talk to the children about healthy eating Q: What most surprised you about the first graders? A: They didn’t know that you can eat raw vegetables.","phenomenon":"projection of a factive presupposition","near":["local accommodation","presupposition filtering","metalinguistic negation"],"avoid":["factive presupposition","presupposition projection through negation and modals","presupposition surviving negation and questions","presupposition projection through holes"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"(6) If Bill, a big drinker, is here, we’ll have fun. (7) If that son-of-a-bitch Bill left, he’d better not have taken the flower arrangement.","phenomenon":"projection of conventional implicatures","near":["presupposition filtering","local accommodation","projection of a factive presupposition"],"avoid":["conventional implicature","nondisplaceability of expressives","speaker-oriented expressive content","presupposition projection through holes","immediacy of expressives"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Bob and Carl are sledders. #Bob, a sledder, participated in the 2010 Winter Olympic Games.","phenomenon":"anti-backgrounding of appositives","near":["presupposition failure","informative presupposition","presupposition accommodation"],"avoid":["redundant assertion of presupposed content","novelty condition","pleonasm (redundancy)","violation of the non-redundancy (informativity) constraint on assertion","conventional implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Q3: Does France have a king? A3: Well, the king of France didn’t attend the opening of Parliament. … [A3] receives an interpretation … paraphrasable as “There was no king of France attending the opening of Parliament”.","phenomenon":"local accommodation","near":["global accommodation","presupposition filtering","presupposition projection through holes"],"avoid":["presupposition-denying (external) negation","metalinguistic negation","presupposition failure","presupposition cancellation","presupposition accommodation"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.3765/salt.v20i0.2584"},{"id":"tonhauser2013-toward-taxonomy-projective","title":"Toward a Taxonomy of Projective Content","authors":["Judith Tonhauser","David Beaver","Craige Roberts","Mandy Simons"],"year":"2013","area":"language","topic":"presupposition & projection","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Projective content is divided into four subclasses … distinguished by two properties … (i) being subject to a strong contextual felicity constraint, and (ii) giving rise to obligatory local effect.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The evidence we have presented confirms that projection does not, in fact, pick out the traditional class of presuppositions in English or Guaraní.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Implications m that survive embedding are cross-classified by strong contextual felicity (trigger acceptable only in contexts entailing or implying m) and obligatory local effect (m necessarily within an embedding belief predicate's scope): A (+,+), B (−,−), C (−,+), D (+,−).","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Triggers of many projective contents (factive complements, change-of-state prestates, appositives, expressives) are acceptable in contexts not already establishing them, so theories deriving projection from presuppositions' constraints on context, or from local satisfaction, cannot easily cover all projective content.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"strong contextual felicity constraint","aliases":["context"],"definition":"An m-positive context is an utterance context that entails or implies m. … If utterance of trigger t of projective content m is acceptable only in an m-positive context, then t imposes a strong contextual felicity constraint with respect to m.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"obligatory local effect","definition":"A projective content m with trigger t has obligatory local effect if and only if, when t is syntactically embedded in the complement of a belief-predicate B, m necessarily is part of the content that is targeted by, and within the scope of, B.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"projection","aliases":["projective"],"definition":"A content m of expression t is projective (i.e. has the property of projection) if and only if m is typically implied by utterances of atomic sentences S containing t and may also be implied by utterances of family-of-sentences variants of S.","exact":true,"avoid":["simons2010-projects-why/t1"]},{"id":"t4","term":"indirect implication judgment","aliases":["implication","indirect implication judgments"],"definition":"speakers are asked a question that is unrelated to the target implication, but the answer to which allows the fieldworker to determine whether the target implication arises from the utterance or not.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"class D","aliases":["class D projective content"],"definition":"an implication that is associated with a strong contextual felicity requirement, but that does not have obligatory local effect","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"There is a health program that gives medicine to everybody who has ever smoked or currently smokes. Maria is administering the program in a particular town … ‘Marko doesn’t smoke anymore.’ … ‘It’s possible that Marko doesn’t smoke anymore.’ … ‘If Marko doesn’t smoke anymore, he has lots of money.’ … ‘Does Marko not smoke anymore?’ … the consultants thought that Maria would administer the medicine to Marko as a consequence of each of the utterances","phenomenon":"projection of a change-of-state presupposition","near":["local accommodation","strong contextual felicity constraint","projection of a factive presupposition"],"avoid":["presupposition projection","presupposition projection through negation and modals","presupposition surviving negation and questions","presupposition projection through holes","presupposition hole","presupposition accommodation","global accommodation","informative presupposition","presupposition projection from negation and questions","defective context"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Malena is eating her lunch, a hamburger, on the bus going into town. A woman who she doesn’t know sits down next to her and says: … #‘Our bus driver is eating empanadas, too.’ … [When] Malena is eating empanadas, not a hamburger … [the same utterance] was judged acceptable by the consultants","phenomenon":"strong contextual felicity constraint","near":["informative presupposition","presupposition accommodation","obligatory local effect"],"avoid":["presupposition failure","class D projective content","class A projective content","anaphoric presupposition","existential presupposition","defective context"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Context: Laura, who doesn’t live with her parents, visits them and asks them to sit down with her because she has to tell them something: … ‘I’ve stopped doing drugs.’","phenomenon":"informative presupposition","near":["presupposition failure","strong contextual felicity constraint","local accommodation"],"avoid":["presupposition accommodation","global accommodation","defective context"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"#Jane believes that Bill has stopped smoking and that he has never been a smoker. … the conjoined clauses embedded under the belief-predicate attribute inconsistent beliefs to Jane: the first conjunct necessarily implies that Jane believes that Bill has been a smoker, which is contradicted by the second conjunct.","phenomenon":"obligatory local effect","near":["strong contextual felicity constraint","informative presupposition","global accommodation"],"avoid":["contradictory presuppositions","local accommodation","local satisfaction of presuppositions","presupposition plugging by attitude verbs","presupposition projection under attitude verbs"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"Barney and Fred are walking down the street. They haven’t been discussing cars. Barney does not point to or otherwise indicate any of the cars parked in the street. Barney says: #Wilma likes that car. … #If Wilma likes that car, she has good taste. … Barney points at a car and says: Pebbles thinks Wilma likes that car, but of course Pebbles has no idea that I’m pointing to it.","phenomenon":"class D projective content","near":["class A projective content","class B projective content","obligatory local effect"],"avoid":["strong contextual felicity constraint","presupposition failure","indexical (demonstrative) reference","direct reference of demonstratives","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","anaphoric presupposition","reference failure","presupposition projection through holes"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1353/lan.2013.0001"},{"id":"vandersandt1992-presupposition-projection-anaphora","title":"Presupposition Projection as Anaphora Resolution","authors":["Rob A. van der Sandt"],"year":"1992","area":"language","topic":"presupposition & projection","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[Presuppositions] only differ from pronouns or other kinds … in that they contain enough descriptive content to establish a reference marker in case discourse does not provide one.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"To say that a presupposition is projected … simply means that the lexical information contained in the 'presuppositional anaphor' has been accommodated at some level of discourse structure","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Unlike satisfaction theories, entailment by the local context does not suffice to neutralize a presupposition: only binding to a selected antecedent does; otherwise it is accommodated, preferably globally, predicting genuine ambiguities between presupposing and non-presupposing readings.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Each DRS is a triple ⟨U, Con, A⟩ whose A-structure holds unresolved anaphors; deepest first, each is bound to an accessible marker or else accommodated as high on its projection line as consistency, informativeness and free-variable constraints allow.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"A-structure","aliases":["A-structures"],"definition":"we will construe a DRS as consisting of three components, a universe of discourse markers, a set of conditions, and its so-called A-structure. The latter component is a set of DRSs. They collect the anaphoric elements of the sentence to be processed.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"projection line","aliases":["projection lines","projected"],"definition":"A projection line is one path through an accessibility tree from a sub-DRS to the root of the tree. It tells us which route an anaphor must take when it is projected to a higher position in a DRS.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"accommodation","aliases":["accommodated","Accommodation","accommodate"],"definition":"If no suitable antecedent for a presuppositional anaphor can be found, it will be accommodated. … Technically, accommodation consists in transferring the anaphoric marker plus its conditions to the level of accommodation, … thus adjusting the discourse structure by establishing an accessible antecedent after all.","exact":true,"avoid":["stalnaker2002-common-ground/t4"]},{"id":"t4","term":"cancellation","definition":"the notion of cancellation makes no sense anymore. … a misleading label which was introduced to cover those cases where a presupposition is … bound to … some antecedent, as well as those cases where a presupposition has been accommodated at some subordinate level","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e2","text":"[Karttunen & Peters'] two-dimensional semantics thus predicts that the indefinite (9a) presupposes the corresponding indefinite (9b): (9a) Someone managed to succeed George V on the throne of England. (9b) It took someone some effort to succeed George V on the throne of England. … we would want to identify the actual successor of George V with the person who is presupposed to have had difficulties doing so.","phenomenon":"binding problem for presuppositions","near":["proviso problem","presupposition cancellation","donkey anaphora"],"avoid":["conventional implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"(68) Either the king or the president of France opened the exhibition. … Assuming that countries cannot have both monarchs and presidents, (68) admits only one solution.","phenomenon":"local accommodation","near":["global accommodation","presupposition filtering","intermediate accommodation"],"avoid":["presupposition cancellation","contradictory presuppositions"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"(53) Every man loves his wife. … The current theory predicts that no sentence which contains an open phrase in which a variable is bound by an outside quantifier can ever have a presupposing reading. Instead (53) will come out as equivalent with (57) … (57) Every man who has a wife loves her.","phenomenon":"intermediate accommodation","near":["global accommodation","local accommodation","presupposition filtering"],"avoid":["binding problem for presuppositions","bound-variable pronoun","donkey anaphora","quantifier domain restriction","E-type pronoun"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"If a sentence is presupposing in a given context the discourse marker thus created should allow anaphoric take-up in subsequent sentences. … (31) If John has an oriental girlfriend, his girlfriend won't be happy. She has always been rather jealous.","phenomenon":"global accommodation","near":["local accommodation","modal subordination","presupposition filtering"],"avoid":["presupposition accommodation","presupposition projection","cross-sentential anaphora","informative presupposition","proviso problem","anaphoric presupposition"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1093/jos/9.4.333"},{"id":"schlenker2008-articulate-pragmatic-theory","title":"Be Articulate: A Pragmatic Theory of Presupposition Projection","authors":["Philippe Schlenker"],"year":"2008","area":"language","topic":"presupposition & projection","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"presupposition projection follows from the combination of a fully classical semantics with two … principles of manner","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"a presupposition is a distinguished part of a bivalent meaning, one that strives to be articulated … as a separate conjunct.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Dynamic semantics faces a dilemma: Stalnaker's pragmatic story fails to generalize beyond unembedded conjunctions, while Heim's context change potentials cannot explain why 'and' exists but a deviant 'and*' does not; bivalent semantics plus manner maxims can.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"In context set C, dd′ (d presupposed) is acceptable after initial string α iff, for every γ of d's type and every good final β, C ⊨ α(d∧γ)β ↔ αγβ; so 'p and qq′' requires C ⊨ p→q.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"Be Articulate","aliases":["Articulate","articulated"],"definition":"requires that whenever possible the distinguished status of a pre-condition should be made syntactically apparent, and thus that the meaning of an expression dd’ should be preferably expressed as (d and dd’)","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"Be Brief","aliases":["Brief"],"definition":"sometimes prohibits that a conjunction be uttered if its first conjunct is certain to be idle given the rest of the sentence","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"Incremental Transparency","aliases":["incrementally transparent","Transparency","transparent"],"definition":"a predicative or propositional occurrence of dd’ is acceptable in a sentence that begins with α dd’ if … for any expression γ of the same type as d and for any good final β, C |= α (d and γ) β ⇔ α γ β","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"Symmetric Transparency","aliases":["symmetrically transparent","Transparency","transparent","symmetric"],"definition":"a predicative or propositional occurrence of dd’ is (somewhat) acceptable in a sentence of the form α dd’ β if … for any expression γ of the same type as d, C |= α (d and γ) β ⇔ α γ β","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"good final","aliases":["good finals"],"definition":"the ‘good finals’ of an initial string s, which are all the strings s’ that can be added to s to make a syntactically acceptable sentence s s’","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"quasi-presupposition","aliases":["quasi-presuppositions"],"definition":"presupposition-like phenomena that are triggered at the compositional level and are weaker in force than standard presuppositions","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"(2) a. The king of Moldavia is powerful. b. Moldavia is a monarchy and the king of Moldavia is powerful. c. If Moldavia is a monarchy, the king of Moldavia is powerful. (2)a presupposes (incorrectly, as it happens) that Moldavia has a king, but the examples in (2)b-c require no such thing; they only presuppose that if Moldavia is a monarchy, it has a king","phenomenon":"presupposition filtering","near":["local accommodation","global accommodation","presupposition plugging"],"avoid":["presupposition failure","conditional presupposition","anaphoric presupposition","presupposition projection","incremental (left-to-right) presupposition filtering","filtering relative to background assumptions","local satisfaction of presuppositions","reference failure"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"The bathroom is well hidden or (else) this house has no bathroom. … If the bathroom is not hidden, this house has no bathroom. … each sentence can be read without presupposition, though this is easier in the ‘canonical’ than in the ‘reversed’ order","phenomenon":"symmetric presupposition filtering","aliases":["symmetric","presupposition"],"near":["incremental (left-to-right) presupposition filtering","global accommodation","proviso problem"],"avoid":["bathroom sentence","local accommodation","presupposition filtering"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"John doesn’t know that he has cancer because he isn’t even sick! is (more or less) acceptable to convey that it is not the case that John has cancer and that he believes that he has cancer.","phenomenon":"local accommodation","near":["global accommodation","intermediate accommodation","presupposition filtering"],"avoid":["metalinguistic negation","presupposition-denying (external) negation","presupposition cancellation","presupposition failure","presupposition-cancelling external negation","presupposition cancellation with semi-factives","factive presupposition"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"We can easily define a deviant conjunction and* which is equivalent to and in non-presuppositional cases, but which has a different projective behavior … Moldavia is a monarchy and* the king of Moldavia is powerful is predicted to have the presupposition that Moldavia is a monarchy. The obvious question is: why does natural language have and but not and*?","phenomenon":"explanatory problem for dynamic semantics","near":["proviso problem","binding problem for presuppositions","triggering problem"],"avoid":["presupposition projection"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"John believes Peter to know that Mary is competent. … [This] tends to presuppose that Mary is competent and John believes it, which is slightly stronger than either Heim 1983 or the Transparency would predict","phenomenon":"proviso problem","near":["presupposition plugging by attitude verbs","local accommodation","binding problem for presuppositions"],"avoid":["global accommodation","the proviso problem","presupposition projection under attitude verbs","factive presupposition"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1515/thli.2008.013"},{"id":"kripke1975-outline-theory-truth","title":"Outline of a Theory of Truth","authors":["Saul Kripke"],"year":"1975","area":"logic","topic":"truth & paradox","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"an adequate theory must allow our statements involving the notion of truth to be risky: they risk being paradoxical if the empirical facts are extremely (and unexpectedly) unfavorable.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[My] hierarchy [of languages] uses a single truth predicate, ever increasing with increasing levels until the level of the minimal fixed point is reached.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Interpret T(x) by a partial set (S₁,S₂); φ sends it to (the sentences then true, those then false). Kleene's strong rules make φ monotone, so iterating from (∅,∅), with unions at limits, reaches a least fixed point φ(S₁,S₂) = (S₁,S₂).","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Rather than Tarski's hierarchy of distinct subscripted truth predicates, a single truth predicate with gaps lets a statement seek its own level, which—like whether it is grounded or paradoxical—depends on empirical facts, not on its form.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"grounded","aliases":["ungrounded"],"definition":"Given a sentence A of ℒ, let us define A to be grounded if it has a truth value in the smallest fixed point ℒσ; otherwise, ungrounded.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"paradoxical","definition":"a sentence is paradoxical if it has no truth value in any fixed point. That is, a paradoxical sentence A is such that if φ((S₁,S₂)) = (S₁,S₂), then A is neither an element of S₁ nor an element of S₂.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"fixed point","definition":"Clearly, if T(x) is to be interpreted as truth for the very language ℒ containing T(x) itself, we must have S₁ = S₁′ and S₂ = S₂′. [This means that if A is any sentence, A satisfies (falsifies) T(x) iff A is true (false) by the evaluation rules.] A pair (S₁,S₂) that satisfies this condition is called a fixed point.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"level (of a sentence)","aliases":["level"],"definition":"If A is grounded, define the level of A to be the smallest ordinal α such that A has a truth value in ℒα.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"closing off","definition":"Take any fixed point ℒ(S₁,S₂). Modify the interpretation of T(x) so as to make it false of any sentence outside S₁. [We call this \"closing off\" T(x).]","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Consider the ordinary statement, made by Jones: (1) Most (i.e., a majority) of Nixon's assertions about Watergate are false. … Suppose, however, that Nixon's assertions about Watergate are evenly balanced between the true and the false, except for one problematic case, (2) Everything Jones says about Watergate is true. Suppose, in addition, that (1) is Jones's sole assertion about Watergate, or alternatively, that all his Watergate-related assertions except perhaps (1) are true.","phenomenon":"contingent Liar paradox","aliases":["paradox","paradoxical","Liar"],"near":["truth-teller sentence","Curry's paradox","Yablo's paradox"],"avoid":["Liar paradox","blind truth ascription","groundedness"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"(4) All of Nixon's utterances about Watergate are false. … Suppose Dean asserts (4), while Nixon in turn asserts (5) Everything Dean says about Watergate is false. Dean, in asserting the sweeping (4), wishes to include Nixon's assertion (5) within its scope … and Nixon, in asserting (5), wishes to do the same with Dean's (4). … Yet intuitively, we can often assign unambiguous truth values to (4) and (5).","phenomenon":"statements seeking their own levels","aliases":["seeking","level","levels"],"near":["truth-teller sentence","Yablo's paradox","systematic ambiguity of 'true' (implicit subscripts)"],"avoid":["blind truth ascription"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Some of the intuitive trouble with Liar sentences is shared with such sentences as (3) (3) is true. which, though not paradoxical, yield no determinate truth conditions.","phenomenon":"truth-teller sentence","aliases":["truth-teller"],"near":["Liar paradox","Curry's paradox","contingent Liar paradox"],"avoid":["groundedness"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Suppose we are explaining the word 'true' to someone who does not yet understand it. … (7) Some sentence printed in the New York Daily News, October 7, 1971, is true. … our subject, if he is willing to assert 'snow is white', will according to the rules be willing to assert '(6) is true'. But suppose that among the assertions printed in the New York Daily News, October 7, 1971, is (6) itself. … he will deduce (7) by existential generalization.","phenomenon":"groundedness","aliases":["grounded","groundedness"],"near":["ungrounded sentence","truth-teller sentence","contingent Liar paradox"],"avoid":["blind truth ascription","Tarskian T-sentence","metaphysical priority (grounding)"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2024634"},{"id":"sellars1956-empiricism-philosophy-mind","title":"Empiricism and the Philosophy of Mind","authors":["Wilfrid Sellars"],"year":"1956","area":"epistemology","topic":"perception & foundations","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"In characterizing an episode or a state as that of knowing, we are not giving an empirical description of [it]; we are placing it in the logical space of reasons","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[Empirical knowledge] is rational, not because it has a foundation but because it is a self-correcting enterprise which can put any claim in jeopardy, though not all at once.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"No episode is non-inferential knowledge that presupposes no other knowledge: even an observation report expresses knowledge only if the perceiver also knows that such reports are reliable indicators of what they report.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Concepts of thoughts and sense impressions can be seen as posited by proto-scientific theories of behaviour and perception, modelled respectively on overt speech and inner replicas; privileged access is a reporting role built on their intersubjective use.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"Myth of the Given","definition":"The idea that observation \"strictly and properly so-called\" is constituted by certain self-authenticating nonverbal episodes, the authority of which is transmitted to verbal and quasi-verbal performances when these performances are made \"in conformity with the semantical rules of the language\"","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"psychological nominalism","definition":"all awareness of sorts, resemblances, facts, etc., in short, all awareness of abstract entities -- indeed, all awareness even of particulars -- is a linguistic affair","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"“looks” talk","aliases":["looks"],"definition":"the statement \"X looks green to Jones\" differs from \"Jones sees that x is green\" in that whereas the latter both ascribes a propositional claim to Jones' experience and endorses it, the former ascribes the claim but does not endorse it.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"standard conditions","aliases":["condition"],"definition":"condition in which things look what they are","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"Rylean language","aliases":["language"],"definition":"a language of which the fundamental descriptive vocabulary speaks of public properties of public objects located in Space and enduring through Time","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"[John] works in a necktie shop. … he has never looked at an object in other than standard conditions. … [After electric lighting is installed,] one of his neighbors, Jim, comes in to buy a necktie. \"Here is a handsome green one,\" says John. \"But it isn't green,\" says Jim, and takes John outside. \"Well,\" says John, \"it was green in there, but now it is blue.\" … [Later,] he makes such statements as \"It looks green, but take it outside and see.\"","phenomenon":"logical priority of 'is' talk over 'looks' talk","aliases":["looks"],"near":["inverted spectrum","dispositional analysis of color","color constancy"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"once our fictitious ancestor, Jones, has developed the theory that overt verbal behavior is the expression of thoughts, and taught his compatriots to make use of the theory in interpreting each other's behavior, it is but a short step to the use of this language in self-description. … Dick can be trained to give reasonably reliable self-descriptions … without having to observe his overt behavior. … What began as a language with a purely theoretical use has gained a reporting role.","phenomenon":"theory-theory of mind","aliases":["theory","theoretical"],"near":["simulation theory of mind","analytical behaviorism","argument from analogy for other minds"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"the picture of a great Hegelian serpent of knowledge with its tail in its mouth (Where does it begin?)","phenomenon":"coherentism about justification","near":["classical foundationalism","infinitism","epistemic contextualism"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"One is committed to a stratum of authoritative nonverbal episodes (\"awarenesses\"), the authority of which accrues to a superstructure of verbal actions, provided that the expressions occurring in these actions are properly used. These self-authenticating episodes would constitute the tortoise on which stands the elephant on which rests the edifice of empirical knowledge.","phenomenon":"classical foundationalism","near":["coherentism about justification","infinitism","reliabilism"],"avoid":["Myth of the Given"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"prior1960-runabout-inference-ticket","title":"The Runabout Inference-Ticket","authors":["A. N. Prior"],"year":"1960","area":"logic","topic":"proof theory & logical constants","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"a point not generally noticed, namely that in [the] sense of 'analytically valid' [as valid by meaning alone,] any statement whatever may be inferred … from any other.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"2 and 2 are 4. Therefore, 2 and 2 are 4 tonk 2 and 2 are 5. Therefore, 2 and 2 are 5.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"A connective 'tonk' governed by the rules P ⊢ P-tonk-Q and P-tonk-Q ⊢ Q lets any Q be derived from any P, so stipulating introduction and elimination rules cannot by itself confer meaning or validity.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Satirical reductio of the view that a logical constant's meaning is nothing over and above the inferences its rules license, and that those inferences are therefore valid purely in virtue of meaning.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"analytically valid","aliases":["analytically valid","validity"],"definition":"inferences whose validity arises solely from the meanings of certain expressions occurring in them","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"tonk","aliases":["contonktive"],"definition":"Its meaning is completely given by the rules that (i) from any statement P we can infer any statement formed by joining P to any statement Q by 'tonk' …, and that (ii) from any 'contonktive' statement P-tonk-Q we can infer the contained statement Q.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"old superstitious view","aliases":["old superstitious view","superstitious view","superstitious"],"definition":"that an expression must have some independently determined meaning before we can discover whether inferences involving it are valid or invalid","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"any statement whatever may be inferred, in an analytically valid way, from any other. '2 and 2 are 5', for instance, from '2 and 2 are 4'. It is done in two steps, thus: 2 and 2 are 4. Therefore, 2 and 2 are 4 tonk 2 and 2 are 5. Therefore, 2 and 2 are 5.","phenomenon":"non-conservative extension","near":["explosion (ex falso quodlibet)","Curry's paradox","paradox of material implication"],"avoid":["failure of harmony","disharmony","proof-theoretic definition of a logical constant","conventionalism about analyticity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"[The meaning of 'and'] is said to be completely given by saying that (i) from any pair of statements P and Q we can infer the statement formed by joining P to Q by 'and' …, that (ii) from any conjunctive statement P-and-Q we can infer P, and (iii) from P-and-Q we can always infer Q. … there is simply nothing more to knowing the meaning of 'and' than being able to perform these inferences.","phenomenon":"proof-theoretic definition of a logical constant","near":["truth-functional definition","explicit definition","non-conservative extension"],"avoid":["meaning as use","inferential role semantics","inferentialism","implicit definition","harmony","conjunction introduction","conjunction elimination","conventionalism about analyticity","Ramsey-style simultaneous implicit definition"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1093/analys/21.2.38"},{"id":"kripke1963-semantical-considerations-modal","title":"Semantical Considerations on Modal Logic","authors":["Saul Kripke"],"year":"1963","area":"logic","topic":"modal logic","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"A is necessary in H iff A is true in all worlds H′ possible relative to H.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[Quantified M, S4, S5 and B] are a straightforward extension of the modal propositional logics … and nevertheless neither the Barcan formula nor its converse is derivable.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Necessity is truth in every world possible relative to the given one, and each world has its own domain of existing individuals; quantifiers range over that domain, so neither the Barcan formula nor its converse is valid.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Two-world S5 countermodels, in which the second world gains or loses an individual, refute the Barcan formula and its converse; the apparent proof of the converse misapplies necessitation to a formula with free variables.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"model structure","aliases":["m.s."],"definition":"A model structure (m.s.) is an ordered triple (G, K, R) where K is a set, R is a reflexive relation on K, and G ∈ K. … K is the set of all ‘possible worlds’; G is the ‘real world’.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"relative possibility","aliases":["possible relative to","relatively possible"],"definition":"If H₁ and H₂ are two worlds, H₁ R H₂ means intuitively that H₂ is ‘possible relative to’ H₁; i.e., that every proposition true in H₂ is possible in H₁.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"quantificational model structure","aliases":["q.m.s.","model structure"],"definition":"a model structure (G, K, R), together with a function ψ which assigns to each H ∈ K a set ψ(H), called the domain of H. Intuitively ψ(H) is the set of all individuals existing in H.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"closure","aliases":["closures"],"definition":"If A is a formula containing free variables, we define a closure of A to be any formula without free variables obtained by prefixing universal quantifiers and necessity signs, in any order, to A.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"ψ(G) = {a}, ψ(H) = {a, b}, where a and b are distinct. We then define, for a monadic predicate letter P, a model in which φ(P, G) = {a}, φ(P, H) = {a}. Then clearly □P(x) is true in G when x is assigned a; and since a is the only object in the domain of G, so is (x)□P(x). But, (x)P(x) is clearly false in H …, and hence □(x)P(x) is false in G.","phenomenon":"failure of the Barcan formula (expanding domains)","aliases":["domain"],"near":["failure of the converse Barcan formula (shrinking domains)","necessitism (constant domains)","failure of necessitation for open formulas"],"avoid":["merely possible individual","de dicto vs de re modality","quantifying into a modal context"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"ψ(G) = {a, b}, ψ(H) = {a}, where again a ≠ b. Define φ(P, G) = {a, b}, φ(P, H) = {a}, where P is a given monadic predicate letter. Then clearly (x)P(x) holds in both G and H, so that φ(□(x)P(x), G) = T. But φ(P(x), H) = F when x is assigned b, so that, when x is assigned b, φ(□P(x), G) = F. Hence φ((x)□P(x), G) = F.","phenomenon":"failure of the converse Barcan formula (shrinking domains)","near":["failure of the Barcan formula (expanding domains)","necessitism (constant domains)","failure of the T axiom (non-reflexive accessibility)"],"avoid":["de dicto vs de re modality","quantifying into a modal context"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"should φ(P(x), H) be given a truth-value when x is assigned a value in the domain of some other world H′ and not in the domain of H? Intuitively, suppose P(x) means ‘x is bald’—are we to assign a truth-value to the substitution instance ‘Sherlock Holmes is bald’? Holmes does not exist, but in other states of affairs, he would have existed. Should we assign a definite truth-value to the statement that he is bald, or not?","phenomenon":"predication of non-existent objects","near":["truth in fiction","problem of negative existentials","rigid designation"],"avoid":["empty name","truth-value gap from reference failure","incompleteness of fictional objects","incompleteness of fiction","varying domains (contingent existence)","presupposition failure","reference failure","merely possible individual"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"in worlds other than the real one, some actually existing individuals may be absent, while new individuals, like Pegasus, may appear.","phenomenon":"varying domains (contingent existence)","near":["necessitism (constant domains)","counterpart theory","possibilist quantification"],"avoid":["merely possible individual"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"dummett1975-philosophical-basis-intuitionistic","title":"The Philosophical Basis of Intuitionistic Logic","authors":["Michael Dummett"],"year":"1975","area":"logic","topic":"non-classical logic","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Any justification for adopting one logic rather than another as the logic for mathematics must turn on questions of meaning.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"We must … replace the notion of truth, as the central notion of the theory of meaning for mathematical statements, by the notion of proof","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Since meaning is exhausted by use, grasp of meaning must be manifestable; knowledge of recognition-transcendent truth-conditions cannot be manifested, so proof, not truth, must be central to the meaning of mathematical statements, leaving some classical inferences unjustified.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"The thesis that numbers are mental creations can ground rejecting classical mathematical reasoning only for someone denying any present truth about what unperformed computations would yield; otherwise the case must rest on general meaning theory, reaching beyond mathematics.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"harmony","definition":"the requirement of harmony between [the grounds for asserting a statement and its inferential consequences] in respect of some type of statement is the requirement that the addition of statements of that type to the language produces a conservative extension of the language","exact":true,"avoid":["belnap1962-tonk-plonk-plink/t1"]},{"id":"t2","term":"molecular view of language","aliases":["molecular"],"definition":"any view on which individual sentences carry a content which belongs to them in accordance with the way they are compounded out of their own constituents, independently of other sentences … not involving those constituents","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"canonical proof","aliases":["proof"],"definition":"a restricted type of proof … in terms of which [the explanation of implication] is given, and which does not admit modus ponens save in subordinate deductions","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"demonstration","aliases":["demonstrations"],"definition":"the sort of argument which will normally appear in a mathematical article or textbook … A demonstration is just as cogent a ground for the assertion of its conclusion as is a canonical proof … a demonstration of a proposition provides an effective means for finding a canonical proof.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"if Fidel Castro were to meet President Carter, he would either insult him or speak politely to him; but it might not be determinately true, of either of those things, that he would do it, since it might depend upon some so far unspecified further condition, such as whether the meeting took place in Cuba or outside.","phenomenon":"failure of conditional excluded middle","aliases":["condition"],"near":["simplification of disjunctive antecedents","failure of transitivity (hypothetical syllogism)","failure of contraposition"],"avoid":["excluded middle without bivalence","counterexample to Conditional Excluded Middle","subjunctive (counterfactual) conditional","non-counterfactual subjunctive conditional"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"The platonist metaphor assimilates mathematical enquiry to the investigations of the astronomer: mathematical structures, like galaxies, exist, independently of us, in a realm of reality which we do not inhabit but which those of us who have the skill are capable of observing and reporting on.","phenomenon":"platonism","aliases":["platonist"],"near":["constructivism","formalism","fictionalism"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"to say that fictional characters are the creations of the imagination is to say that a statement about a fictional character can be true only if it is imagined as being true, that a fictional character can have only those properties which it is part of the story that he has","phenomenon":"incompleteness of fiction","aliases":["fictional"],"near":["imaginative resistance","problem of negative existentials","the paradox of fiction"],"avoid":["incompleteness of fictional objects","predication of non-existent objects"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"the meaning of a statement consists solely in its rôle as an instrument of communication between individuals, just as the powers of a chess-piece consist solely in its rôle in the game according to the rules.","phenomenon":"meaning as use","near":["truth-conditional semantics","intention-based semantics","semantic externalism"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"lewis1983-new-work-theory","title":"New Work for a Theory of Universals","authors":["David Lewis"],"year":"1983","area":"metaphysics","topic":"universals, laws & supervenience","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Let us say that an adequate theory of properties is one that recognises an objective difference between natural and unnatural properties; preferably, a difference that admits of degree.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Two things are qualitative duplicates if they have exactly the same perfectly natural properties.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Besides abundant properties (any classes of possibilia), philosophy needs an objective, graded natural/unnatural distinction among properties, even if taken as primitive, to analyse duplication, laws, causation and mental and linguistic content, and to formulate supervenience theses and materialism.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Laws are the regularities of the true system best balancing simplicity and strength when its primitive predicates denote only perfectly natural properties; likewise, interpretation favours natural, eligible referents and contents, answering Putnam's model-theoretic argument and Kripke's quaddition puzzle.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"avoid":["lewis1984-putnams-paradox"],"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"natural properties","aliases":["natural property"],"definition":"Natural properties would be the ones whose sharing makes for resemblance, and the ones relevant to causal powers.","exact":true,"avoid":["lewis1984-putnams-paradox/t3"]},{"id":"t8","term":"abundant properties","aliases":["abundant property","abundant","property","properties"],"definition":"Any class of things, be it ever so gerrymandered and miscellaneous and indescribable in thought and language, and be it ever so superfluous in characterising the world, is nevertheless a property.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"intrinsic property","aliases":["intrinsic"],"definition":"Property P is intrinsic iff, for any two duplicate things, not necessarily from the same world, either both have P or neither does.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"supervenience","aliases":["supervenience thesis","supervenes"],"definition":"A supervenience thesis is a denial of independent variation… To say that so-and-so supervenes on such-and-such is to say that there can be no difference in respect of so-and-so without difference in respect of such-and-such.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"alien property","aliases":["alien"],"definition":"a property is alien to a world iff (1) it is not instantiated by any inhabitant of that world, and (2) it is not analysable as a conjunction of, or as a structural property constructed out of, natural properties all of which are instantiated by inhabitants of that world.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"eligible interpretation","aliases":["eligible"],"definition":"Say that a set from the domain is eligible to be the extension of a one-place predicate iff its members are just those things in the domain that share some natural property… An eligible interpretation is one that assigns none but eligible extensions to the predicates.","exact":true,"avoid":["lewis1984-putnams-paradox/t3"]},{"id":"t7","term":"lawhood","aliases":["law","laws"],"definition":"[A suitable system] must be as simple in axiomatisation as it can be without sacrificing too much information content… A law is any regularity that earns inclusion in the ideal system… let the primitive vocabulary that appears in the axioms refer only to perfectly natural properties.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Compare Bruce with the cat-shaped chunk of miscellaneous and ever-changing matter that follows him around, always a few steps behind. … Bruce, unlike the cat-shaped chunk, has a boundary well demarcated by differences in highly natural properties. Where Bruce ends, there the density of matter, the relative abundance of the chemical elements … abruptly change. Not so for the chunk.","phenomenon":"reference magnetism","near":["indeterminacy of reference","causal theory of reference","rigid designation"],"avoid":["degrees of naturalness"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"A well-educated person working arithmetic problems intends to perform addition when he sees the '+' sign. He does not intend to perform quaddition, which is just like addition … for small numbers but which yields the answer 5 if any of the numbers to be quadded exceeds a certain bound. Wherefore does he intend to add and not to quadd? Whatever he says and whatever is written in his brain can be perversely (mis)interpreted as instructing him to quadd.","phenomenon":"rule-following paradox","near":["Goodman's new riddle of induction","Lewis Carroll's regress","sorites paradox"],"avoid":["indeterminacy of interpretation","indeterminacy of reference","indeterminacy (inscrutability) of reference","indeterminacy of translation"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"You put a lump of butter on a skillet, and the butter melts. What event causes this? There is one event that we can call a moving of molecules. … In fact, but contingently, it is molecular motion that occupies the heat-role. … Now consider an alleged second event, one that we may call a having-the-occupant-of-the-heat-role. This second event occurs just when and where the first does … Certainly the first event causes the melting of the butter, but shall we say that the second event does so as well?","phenomenon":"causal exclusion problem","near":["causal preemption","epiphenomenal side-effect","backtracking counterfactual"],"avoid":["multiple realizability of a functional role","determinable–determinate relation between events","proportionality of cause to effect"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"If [copying machines] were more perfect than they are, the copies they made would be perfect duplicates of the original. Copy and original would be alike in size and shape and chemical composition of the ink marks and the paper, alike in temperature and magnetic alignment and electrostatic charge, alike even in the exact arrangement of their electrons and quarks. … But they would occupy different spatio-temporal positions; and they might have different owners, be first examined in different centuries, and so on.","phenomenon":"intrinsic versus extrinsic properties","near":["essential versus accidental properties","primary versus secondary qualities","determinable versus determinate properties"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1080/00048408312341131"},{"id":"derose1992-contextualism-knowledge-attributions","title":"Contextualism and Knowledge Attributions","authors":["Keith DeRose"],"year":"1992","area":"epistemology","topic":"skepticism & contextualism","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Subject factors … are best construed, not as affecting the truth conditions of knowledge attributions, but rather as affecting whether those truth conditions are satisfied.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"What the context fixes in determining the “content” of a knowledge attribution is how good an epistemic position S must be in to count as knowing that p.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"The truth conditions of 'S knows that p' vary with the speaker's context (stakes, possibilities raised), so one can truly claim knowledge in a low-stakes case and truly deny it in a high-stakes one, though equally well placed epistemically.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"The speaker's context fixes what 'Does S know?' asks; facts about S fix the answer. So the objection that knowing would depend on speakers' interests confuses whether S knows with the truth conditions of knowledge sentences.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"contextualism","aliases":["contextualist","contextual","context"],"definition":"a theory according to which the truth conditions of sentences of the form “S knows that p” or “S does not know that p” vary in certain ways according to the context in which the sentences are uttered.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"attributor factors","aliases":["attributor factor","attributor"],"definition":"Attributor factors set a certain standard the putative subject of knowledge must live up to in order to make the knowledge [claim] true: They affect how good an epistemic position the putative knower must be in to count as knowing.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"subject factors","aliases":["subject factor","subject"],"definition":"[Subject factors] determine whether or not the putative subject lives up to the standards that have been set, and thereby can affect the truth value of the attribution without affecting its content: They affect how good an epistemic position the putative knower actually is in.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"sceptical invariantism","aliases":["skeptical invariantism","invariantism","invariantist"],"definition":"the position that the standards for true knowledge attributions remain constant and very high—as high as they can possibly be.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"non-sceptical invariantism","aliases":["non-skeptical invariantism","invariantism","invariantist"],"definition":"a position according to which the standards are held to be constant but relatively low.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Bank Case A. … it is not especially important in this case that [our paychecks] be deposited right away … My wife says, “Maybe the bank won’t be open tomorrow. …” I reply, “No, I know it’ll be open. I was just there two weeks ago on Saturday. …” Bank Case B. … we have just written a very large and very important check. … She then says, “Banks do change their hours. Do you know the bank will be open tomorrow?” … I reply, “Well, no. …”","phenomenon":"shifting standards for knowledge attributions","near":["Gettier case","lottery case","closure-based skeptical argument"],"avoid":["pragmatic encroachment","knowledge without ruling out irrelevant alternatives","epistemic contextualism","subject-sensitive invariantism","interest-relative invariantism","contextual raising of epistemic standards","same character, different content","attributor factors raising the standards for knowledge","context-sensitivity of knowledge attributions","stakes-sensitivity of knowledge attributions","attributor factors in knowledge attributions","accommodation widening the range of relevant possibilities"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"two people who think they are in the same room but are in fact in different rooms and are talking … over an intercom … one claims, “Frank is not in this room” and the other insists, “Frank is in this room—I can see him!”","phenomenon":"same character, different content","aliases":["different"],"near":["same content, different character","lexical ambiguity","faultless disagreement"],"avoid":["indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","indexical (demonstrative) reference","repeated demonstrative with distinct demonstrata"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"[Consider] the two cases in which Henry has a good, clear look at what he takes to be a barn. (In Case C, there are no barn facades around, but in Case D, the fields are filled with barn facades, but Henry is luckily looking at the only actual barn in the area.) In each case, insert two people in the back seat … and have the first say to the second, “Henry knows that that is a barn.”","phenomenon":"subject factors in knowledge attributions","near":["attributor factors in knowledge attributions","same character, different content","false-lemma Gettier case"],"avoid":["fake barn case","barn façade case","barn facade case","environmental epistemic luck","Gettier case","counterexample to the causal theory of knowledge","knowledge without ruling out irrelevant alternatives","subject factors changing a knowledge attribution's truth value but not its content"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2107917"},{"id":"lewis1996-elusive-knowledge","title":"Elusive Knowledge","authors":["David Lewis"],"year":"1996","area":"epistemology","topic":"skepticism & contextualism","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I say S knows that P iff P holds in every possibility left uneliminated by S's evidence - Psst! - except for those possibilities that we are properly ignoring.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Knowledge is closed under implication. … Implication preserves truth - that is, it preserves truth in any given, fixed context. But if we switch contexts midway, all bets are off.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"S knows P iff ∀w (w uneliminated by S's evidence ∧ w not properly ignored by the ascribers → P holds in w); w is uneliminated iff S's perceptual experience and memory in w exactly match those in actuality.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Rules fix which uneliminated possibilities may be properly ignored; Resemblance explains Gettier and lottery cases, and Attention explains the sceptic's pull: attending to a far-fetched possibility makes it relevant, so doing epistemology destroys knowledge, but only temporarily.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"uneliminated possibility","aliases":["uneliminated possibilities"],"definition":"the uneliminated possibilities are those in which the subject's entire perceptual experience and memory are just as they actually are.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"Rule of Actuality","aliases":["actuality","actually"],"definition":"The possibility that actually obtains is never properly ignored; actuality is always a relevant alternative; nothing false may properly be presupposed.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"Rule of Belief","aliases":["belief"],"definition":"A possibility may not be properly ignored if the subject gives it, or ought to give it, a degree of belief that is sufficiently high, and high not just because the possibility in question is unspecific.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"Rule of Resemblance","aliases":["resembles"],"definition":"Suppose one possibility saliently resembles another. Then if one of them may not be properly ignored, neither may the other.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"Rule of Conservatism","definition":"We are permitted, defeasibly, to adopt the usual and mutually expected presuppositions of those around us.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"Rule of Attention","aliases":["attending"],"definition":"No matter how far-fetched a certain possibility may be, no matter how properly we might have ignored it in some other context, if in this context we are not in fact ignoring it but attending to it, then for us now it is a relevant alternative.","exact":true},{"id":"t7","term":"presupposition","aliases":["presuppose"],"definition":"Say that we presuppose proposition Q iff we ignore all possibilities in which not-Q.","exact":true,"avoid":["stalnaker1970-pragmatics/t3","stalnaker1973-presuppositions/t1","stalnaker1974-pragmatic-presuppositions/t1"]}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"I think that Nogot owns a Ford, because I have seen him driving one; but unbeknownst to me he does not own the Ford he drives, or any other Ford. Unbeknownst to me, Havit does own a Ford, though I have no reason to think so because he never drives it … My justified true belief is that one of the two owns a Ford. But I do not know it; I am right by accident.","phenomenon":"Gettier case","near":["fake barn case","veridical hallucination","lottery case"],"avoid":["false-lemma Gettier case","insensitive belief","unsafe belief","veritic epistemic luck","epistemic luck","justified true belief without knowledge"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose you know that it is a fair lottery with one winning ticket and many losing tickets, and you know how many losing tickets there are. The greater the number of losing tickets, the better is your justification for believing you will lose. Yet there is no number great enough to transform your fallible opinion into knowledge - after all, you just might win.","phenomenon":"lottery case","aliases":["lottery"],"near":["preface paradox","fake barn case","pragmatic encroachment"],"avoid":["lottery paradox","Gettier case","lottery problem","lottery proposition","insensitive belief","unsafe belief","justified true belief without knowledge"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"P implies that any evidence against P is misleading. So, by closure, whenever you know that P, you know that any evidence against P is misleading. And if you know that evidence is misleading, you should pay it no heed. Whenever we know - and we know a lot, remember - we should not heed any evidence tending to suggest that we are wrong. But that is absurd.","phenomenon":"dogmatism paradox","near":["preface paradox","lottery paradox","epistemic bootstrapping"],"avoid":["paradox of misleading evidence","dogmatism puzzle","Kripke's dogmatism paradox","rebutting defeater"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"If I say that every glass is empty, so it's time for another round, doubtless I and my audience are ignoring most of all the glasses there are in the whole wide world throughout all of time. They are outside the domain. They are irrelevant to the truth of what was said.","phenomenon":"quantifier domain restriction","aliases":["domain"],"near":["incomplete definite description","quantifier scope ambiguity","scalar implicature"],"avoid":["domain restriction","contextual domain restriction","contextual restriction of quantifiers","conversational impliciture (expansion)","free pragmatic enrichment","saturation of a linguistically mandated slot","semantic underdetermination (completion)","unarticulated constituent","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"Imagine two epistemologists on a bushwalk. As they walk, they talk. They mention all manner of far-fetched possibilities of error. By attending to these normally ignored possibilities they destroy the knowledge they normally possess. Yet all the while they know where they are and where they are going! How so? The compartment in charge of philosophical talk attends to far-fetched possibilities of error. The compartment in charge of navigation does not.","phenomenon":"compartmentalized (fragmented) thinking","aliases":["compartment"],"near":["self-deception","epistemic akrasia","knowledge without belief"],"avoid":["fragmented belief","fragmentation of belief","compartmentalization","divided mind (split consciousness)","shifting standards for knowledge attributions","contextual raising of epistemic standards","accommodation widening the range of relevant possibilities"],"exact":true},{"id":"e6","text":"Hush! [moment of silence] I might have liked to say, just then, 'All of us are silent'. It was true. But I could not have said it truly, or whistled it either. For by saying it aloud, or by whistling, I would have rendered it false.","phenomenon":"self-falsifying utterance","near":["Moore's paradox","Liar paradox","performative utterance"],"avoid":["pragmatic paradox","pragmatically self-refuting assertion","self-defeating utterance"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1080/00048409612347521"},{"id":"derose1995-solving-skeptical-problem","title":"Solving the Skeptical Problem","authors":["Keith DeRose"],"year":"1995","area":"epistemology","topic":"skepticism & contextualism","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The notion of sensitivity, [not in the concept of knowledge], finds its happier home in our contextualist account of how the standards for knowledge are raised","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"we will never have to follow Nozick in accepting the abominable conjunction: that conjunction is false at any epistemic standard.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Asserting or denying that S knows P tends to raise the standards until S's belief in P must be sensitive. Beliefs that skeptical hypotheses are false are insensitive, so the skeptic's denials come out true, but only at inflated standards.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"We are at least as well placed to know we're not brains in vats as to know we have hands, so the skeptic's closure premise holds at every standard, though only the belief that we have hands is sensitive.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"Rule of Sensitivity","definition":"When it's asserted that S knows (or doesn't know) that P, then, if necessary, enlarge the sphere of epistemically relevant worlds so that it at least includes the closest worlds in which P is false.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"insensitive belief","aliases":["insensitive"],"definition":"Let's say that S's belief that P is insensitive if S would believe that P if P were false.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"abominable conjunction","definition":"[Accepting Nozick's treatment] involves embracing the abominable conjunction that while you don't know you're not a bodiless (and handless!) BIV, still, you know you have hands.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"strength of epistemic position","aliases":["stronger a position"],"definition":"The further away one can get from the actual world, while still having it be the case that one's belief matches the fact at worlds that far away and closer, the stronger a position one is in with respect to P.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"warranted assertability maneuver","aliases":["warranted","assertability"],"definition":"although we don't know these [ordinary things], it's often useful for us to claim that we do know them, and we are therefore often warranted or justified in making such claims. … We mistake this useful/justified/warranted assertability of knowledge ascriptions for truth.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"Argument from Ignorance","aliases":["AI"],"definition":"where 'O' is a proposition about the external world one would ordinarily think one knows (e.g., 'I have hands') and 'H' is a suitably chosen skeptical hypothesis … 1. I don't know that not-H. 2. If I don't know that not-H, then I don't know that O. So, C. I don't know that O.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose, to vary Dretske's example, that I am confronted, not by a skeptic, but by a boastful zoologist. He brags, \"Due to my vast knowledge of zebra and mule anatomy, I know that those animals are not mules cleverly painted to look like zebras; so I know that they're really zebras.\" … He certainly seems to be claiming more than the mundane knowledge that even I possess—and claim to possess—in an ordinary zoo setting, where there's no such zoologist telling me what's what.","phenomenon":"shifting standards for knowledge attributions","near":["pragmatic encroachment","Gettier case","fake barn case"],"avoid":["epistemic contextualism","knowledge without ruling out irrelevant alternatives","closure failure","contextual raising of epistemic standards","accommodation widening the range of relevant possibilities","subject factors in knowledge attributions"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"even though I feel inclined to say that I know the Bulls won their game last night because I read the result in a single newspaper, I still feel strongly pulled toward admitting the (mildly) skeptical claim that I don't know that the paper isn't mistaken about which team won: I realize that my belief that the paper isn't mistaken is a belief I would hold even if it were false (even if the paper were mistaken).","phenomenon":"insensitive belief","near":["Gettier case","undercutting (undermining) defeater","fake barn case"],"avoid":["closure-based skeptical argument","knowledge without sensitivity","unsafe belief","lottery case","knowledge without ruling out irrelevant alternatives"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Even where the odds of your being a loser are astronomically high (there are 20 million tickets, only one of which is a winner, and you have but one ticket), it can seem that you don't know that you're a loser of a fair lottery if the winner hasn't yet been announced.","phenomenon":"lottery case","aliases":["lottery"],"near":["preface paradox","Gettier case","fake barn case"],"avoid":["lottery paradox","lottery problem","lottery proposition","insensitive belief","knowledge without sensitivity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"A grandmother sees her grandson is well when he comes to visit; but if he were sick or dead, others would tell her he was well to spare her upset. Yet this does not mean she doesn't know he is well (or at least ambulatory) when she sees him.","phenomenon":"knowledge without sensitivity","near":["Gettier case","lottery case","closure failure"],"avoid":["insensitive belief","unsafe belief","environmental epistemic luck"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2186011"},{"id":"mcgee1985-counterexample-modus-ponens","title":"A Counterexample to Modus Ponens","authors":["Vann McGee"],"year":"1985","area":"logic","topic":"conditionals","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Sometimes the conclusion of an application of modus ponens is something we do not believe and should not believe, even though the premises are propositions we believe very properly.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Our [nested-conditional] examples show that modus ponens is not strictly valid. They do nothing to dissuade us from our entrenched belief that modus ponens is valid for simple conditionals.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Given classical Boolean connectives, any conditional ⇒ that obeys modus ponens and exportation ((φ ∧ ψ) ⇒ χ ⊨ φ ⇒ (ψ ⇒ χ)) and is no stronger than strict implication is logically indistinguishable from the material conditional ⊃.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Rational belief in 'If A, then if B then C' and in A need not justify believing 'If B then C'. Modus ponens can fail when the major premise has a conditional consequent, though it seems unexceptionable for simple conditionals.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"law of exportation","aliases":["exportation"],"definition":"The principle that 'If φ and ψ, then θ' entails 'If φ, then if ψ then θ'. Since we accept a conditional with a conditional consequent whenever we accept the matching one with a conjoined antecedent, it appears to be a feature of English usage.","exact":false},{"id":"t2","term":"modified Stalnaker conditional","aliases":["modified Stalnaker","Stalnaker conditional","Stalnaker"],"definition":"⌜φ ⇒ ψ⌝ is true in w under a set of hypotheses Γ iff ψ is true in w under Γ ∪ {φ}; an atomic sentence is true in w under Γ iff true in the world most similar to w where all of Γ hold.","exact":false}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Opinion polls taken just before the 1980 election showed the Republican Ronald Reagan decisively ahead of the Democrat Jimmy Carter, with the other Republican in the race, John Anderson, a distant third. Those apprised of the poll results believed, with good reason: If a Republican wins the election, then if it's not Reagan who wins it will be Anderson. A Republican will win the election. Yet they did not have reason to believe If it's not Reagan who wins, it will be Anderson.","phenomenon":"counterexample to modus ponens","aliases":["modus ponens"],"near":["counterexample to hypothetical syllogism","counterexample to contraposition","counterexample to strengthening the antecedent"],"avoid":["revisability of logic"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"I see what looks like a large fish writhing in a fisherman's net a ways off. I believe If that creature is a fish, then if it has lungs, it's a lungfish. … Yet, even though I believe the antecedent of this conditional, I do not conclude … If that creature has lungs, it's a lungfish. … It is more likely that, even though it does not look like one, the animal in the net is a porpoise.","phenomenon":"counterexample to modus ponens","aliases":["modus ponens"],"near":["counterexample to hypothetical syllogism","counterexample to contraposition","counterexample to strengthening the antecedent"],"avoid":["revisability of logic"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"If Juan hadn't married Xochitl and Sylvia hadn't run off to India, Juan and Sylvia would have become lovers. entails If Juan hadn't married Xochitl, then if Sylvia hadn't run off to India, Juan and Sylvia would have become lovers.","phenomenon":"law of exportation","aliases":["exportation"],"near":["law of importation","strengthening the antecedent","simplification of disjunctive antecedents"],"avoid":["subjunctive (counterfactual) conditional"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2026276"},{"id":"belnap1962-tonk-plonk-plink","title":"Tonk, Plonk and Plink","authors":["Nuel D. Belnap Jr."],"year":"1962","area":"logic","topic":"proof theory & logical constants","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"one can define connectives in terms of deducibility, but one bears the onus of proving at least consistency (existence); and … it is necessary to prove uniqueness as well.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"even on the synthetic view [that meaning arises from inferential role], we are not defining our connectives ab initio, but rather in terms of an antecedently given context of deducibility","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A connective's defining rules, viewed as extending a base deducibility system, are admissible only if conservative: a statement A₁, …, Aₙ ⊢ B not containing the new connective is provable with the rules only if provable without them.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Inferential roles can define connectives without an antecedent truth-table meaning, but to speak of 'the' connective one must show uniqueness: any connective given formally identical rules plays exactly the same inferential role.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"conservative extension","aliases":["conservative","conservativeness","extension"],"definition":"although the extension may well have new deducibility-statements, these new statements will all involve [the new connective]. The extension will not have any new deducibility-statements which do not involve [the new connective] itself.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"uniqueness","aliases":["unique"],"definition":"at most one inferential role is permitted by the characterization of [the connective]; i.e., that there cannot be two connectives which share the characterization given to [it] but which otherwise sometimes play different roles.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"synthetic mode","aliases":["synthetic"],"definition":"the analytic mode, which tends to explain wholes in terms of parts, and the synthetic mode, which explains parts in terms of the wholes or contexts in which they occur","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"plonk","definition":"define a connective plonk by specifying that B ⊢ A-plonk-B. The extension is easily shown to be conservative … the conditions on plonk do not determine a unique connective.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"the connective tonk, where tonk is defined by specifying the role it plays in inference. Prior characterizes the role of tonk in inference by describing how it behaves as conclusion, and as premiss: (1) A ⊢ A-tonk-B, and (2) A-tonk-B ⊢ B … We are then led by the transitivity of deducibility to the validity of A ⊢ B","phenomenon":"non-conservative extension","near":["failure of uniqueness","explosion (ex falso quodlibet)","Curry's paradox"],"avoid":["failure of harmony","disharmony","proof-theoretic definition of a logical constant","meaning as use"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose, for example, that I propose to define a connective plonk by specifying that B ⊢ A-plonk-B. The extension is easily shown to be conservative … given only: B ⊢ A-plonk-B, and B ⊢ A-plink-B, we cannot show that plonk and plink invariably play the same role in inference.","phenomenon":"failure of uniqueness","near":["non-conservative extension","circular definition","explosion (ex falso quodlibet)"],"avoid":["failure of harmony","disharmony","proof-theoretic definition of a logical constant","meaning as use","indeterminacy of reference","indeterminacy of interpretation"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"define and as that connective such that (1) A-and-B ⊢ A, (2) A-and-B ⊢ B, and (3) A, B ⊢ A-and-B.","phenomenon":"proof-theoretic definition of a logical constant","near":["truth-functional definition","explicit definition","non-conservative extension"],"avoid":["meaning as use","inferential role semantics","implicit definition","harmony","conjunction introduction","conjunction elimination","inferentialism","conventionalism about analyticity","Ramsey-style simultaneous implicit definition"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1093/analys/22.6.130"},{"id":"haslanger2000-gender-race-are","title":"Gender and Race: (What) Are They? (What) Do We Want Them to Be?","authors":["Sally Haslanger"],"year":"2000","area":"metaphysics","topic":"social metaphysics","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"On my analysis women are those who occupy a particular kind of social position, viz., one of sexually-marked subordinate.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"My priority in [defining race and gender] is not to capture what we do mean, but how we might usefully revise what we mean for certain theoretical and political purposes.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Woman(S) ↔df S is systematically subordinated ∧ marked as a target by bodily features presumed to evidence a female reproductive role. Racialized groups likewise: subordinated or privileged, marked by features presumed to evidence ancestral geographic origins.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"The primary question is not what our ordinary concepts of gender and race mean or track, but what concepts would best serve critical feminist and antiracist theory, even if adopting them revises ordinary meaning.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"woman","aliases":["women"],"definition":"S is a woman iffdf S is systematically subordinated along some dimension (economic, political, legal, social, etc.), and S is “marked” as a target for this treatment by observed or imagined bodily features presumed to be evidence of a female’s biological role in reproduction.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"race","aliases":["races","racial"],"definition":"races are those groups demarcated by the geographical associations accompanying perceived body type, when those associations take on evaluative significance concerning how members of the group should be viewed and treated.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"analytical project","aliases":["analytical approach","analytical"],"definition":"On this approach the task is not to explicate our ordinary concepts … What is the point of having these concepts? … Are they effective tools to accomplish our (legitimate) purposes; if not, what concepts would serve these purposes better?","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"descriptive project","aliases":["descriptive"],"definition":"a descriptive project is not concerned with exploring the nuances of our concepts (or anyone else’s for that matter); it focuses instead on their extension. Here, the task is to develop potentially more accurate concepts through careful consideration of the phenomena, usually relying on empirical or quasi-empirical methods.","exact":true},{"id":"t8","term":"ethnicity","aliases":["ethnicities","ethnic"],"definition":"it might be useful to employ the notion of “ethnicity” for those groups that are like races as I’ve defined them except that they do not experience systematic subordination or privilege in the context in question.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"commonality problem","aliases":["commonality","in common"],"definition":"the commonality problem questions whether there is anything social that females have in common that could count as their “gender”. If we consider all females—females of different times, places, and cultures—there are reasons to doubt that there is anything beyond body type (if even that) that they all share.","exact":true},{"id":"t7","term":"normativity problem","aliases":["normativity","norms"],"definition":"The normativity problem raises the concern that any definition of “what woman is” is value-laden, and will marginalize certain females, privilege others, and reinforce current gender norms","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"in the contemporary US, there are contexts in which being Black and male marks one as a target for certain forms of systematic violence (e.g., by the police). In those contexts, contrary to Frye’s suggestion, being male is not something that a man “has going for him”; though there are other contexts (also in the contemporary US) in which Black males benefit from being male.","phenomenon":"intersectionality","near":["double consciousness","stereotype threat","tokenism"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Blacks, Whites, Asians, Native Americans, are currently racialized … insofar as these are all groups defined in terms of physical features associated with places of origin, and insofar as membership in the group functions socially as a basis for evaluation. However, some groups are not currently racialized in the US, but have been so in the past and possibly could be again (and in other contexts are), e.g., the Italians, the Germans, the Irish.","phenomenon":"context-relativity of racialization","aliases":["racialized","racialization"],"near":["racial essentialism","hypodescent (one-drop rule)","colorism"],"avoid":["intrinsic versus extrinsic properties"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Alternative groupings could include: “pregnant persons,” “lactating persons,” “menstruating persons,” “infertile persons,” … we might imagine that “after the revolution” gender is a component of one’s overall social position because, for example, there are legal protections or medical entitlements granted to individuals classified as having a certain sort of “sexed” body; but this need not have broad implications for psychological identity or everyday social interactions, for the “sex” of bodies might not even be publicly marked.","phenomenon":"non-hierarchical gender","aliases":["gender"],"near":["gender identity","gender performativity","gender essentialism"],"avoid":["institutional fact"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1111/0029-4624.00201"},{"id":"gettier1963-justified-true-belief","title":"Is Justified True Belief Knowledge?","authors":["Edmund L. Gettier"],"year":"1963","area":"epistemology","topic":"analysis of knowledge","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"it is possible for a person to be justified in believing a proposition that is in fact false.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[Two] examples show that [the definition of knowledge as justified true belief] does not state a sufficient condition for someone's knowing a given proposition.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"One can be justified in believing a falsehood, and justification carries over to what one deduces from it; so a belief deduced from a justified falsehood can be justified and true by sheer luck, yet not knowledge.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Counterexamples refute the right-to-left (sufficiency) direction of 'S knows that P ↔ (P is true ∧ S believes P ∧ S is justified in believing P)', and likewise Chisholm's 'adequate evidence' and Ayer's 'right to be sure' variants.","formal":true}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"justification","aliases":["justified"],"definition":"it is possible for a person to be justified in believing a proposition that is in fact false. … if S is justified in believing P, and P entails Q, and S deduces Q from P and accepts Q as a result of this deduction, then S is justified in believing Q.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Smith has strong evidence for the following conjunctive proposition: (d) Jones is the man who will get the job, and Jones has ten coins in his pocket. … (e) The man who will get the job has ten coins in his pocket. … Smith sees the entailment from (d) to (e), and accepts (e) … unknown to Smith, he himself, not Jones, will get the job. And, also, unknown to Smith, he himself has ten coins in his pocket.","phenomenon":"Gettier case","near":["lottery paradox","closure failure","new evil demon problem"],"avoid":["insensitive belief","unsafe belief","safety violation","false-lemma Gettier case","veritic epistemic luck","epistemic luck","justified true belief without knowledge"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Smith has strong evidence for the following proposition: (f) Jones owns a Ford. … Smith selects three place-names quite at random, and constructs the following three propositions: … (h) Either Jones owns a Ford, or Brown is in Barcelona; … Smith is therefore completely justified in believing each of these three propositions. … Jones does not own a Ford … by the sheerest coincidence … the place mentioned in proposition (h) happens really to be the place where Brown is.","phenomenon":"Gettier case","near":["preface paradox","veridical hallucination","lottery paradox"],"avoid":["insensitive belief","unsafe belief","safety violation","false-lemma Gettier case","veritic epistemic luck","epistemic luck","justified true belief without knowledge"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1093/analys/23.6.121"},{"id":"goldman1967-causal-theory-knowing","title":"A Causal Theory of Knowing","authors":["Alvin I. Goldman"],"year":"1967","area":"epistemology","topic":"analysis of knowledge","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"S knows that p if and only if the fact p is causally connected in an \"appropriate\" way with S's believing p.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I think my analysis shows that the question of whether someone knows a certain proposition is, in part, a causal question","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Gettier's subject lacks knowledge because the fact making his belief true is causally unconnected to it. Knowledge requires an appropriate causal link between fact and belief: perception, memory, or warranted inferences correctly reconstructing the chain.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"The connection between known fact and belief need not be the fact's causing the belief: a common cause of both suffices, permitting knowledge of the future; nor must knowers be able to cite their evidence, as with forgotten sources.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"Pattern 2","definition":"p and S's belief of p can also be causally connected in a way that yields knowledge if both p and S's belief of p have a common cause.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"appropriate","definition":"\"Appropriate,\" knowledge-producing causal processes include the following: (1) perception (2) memory (3) a causal chain, exemplifying either Pattern 1 or 2, which is correctly reconstructed by inferences, each of which is warranted … (4) combinations of (1), (2), and (3)","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"correct reconstruction","aliases":["correctly reconstructed","reconstructed","reconstruct","reconstruction"],"definition":"S knows p because he has correctly reconstructed the causal chain leading from p to the evidence for p that S perceives … Such a correct reconstruction is a necessary condition of knowledge based on inference. … he must reconstruct all the important links.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose S perceives that there is solidified lava in various parts of the countryside. … S concludes that a nearby mountain erupted many centuries ago. Let us assume that this is a highly warranted inductive inference … suppose that, after the mountain has erupted, a man somehow removes all the lava. A century later, a different man (not knowing of the real volcano) … puts lava in appropriate places. Still later, S comes across this lava …","phenomenon":"Gettier case","near":["fake barn case","lottery case","veridical hallucination"],"avoid":["insensitive belief","unsafe belief","veritic epistemic luck","epistemic luck","false-lemma Gettier case"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"A newspaper reporter observes p and reports it to his newspaper. When printed, however, the story contains a typographical error so that it asserts not-p. When reading the paper, however, S fails to see the word 'not', and takes the paper to have asserted p. Trusting the newspaper, he infers that p is true.","phenomenon":"deviant causal chain","near":["causal overdetermination","causal preemption","backward causation"],"avoid":["Gettier case","false-lemma Gettier case","counterexample to the causal theory of knowledge","wayward causal chain","causal deviance","veritic epistemic luck","epistemic luck","insensitive belief"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"T intends to go downtown on Monday. On Sunday, T tells S of his intention. Hearing T say he will go downtown, S infers that T really does intend to go downtown. And from this S concludes that T will go downtown on Monday. Now suppose that T fulfills his intention … by going downtown on Monday. … If we ever can be said to have knowledge of the future, this is a reasonable candidate for it.","phenomenon":"knowledge via a common cause","near":["backward causation","causal overdetermination","Gettier case"],"avoid":["knowledge of the future","knowledge without sensitivity","insensitive belief","inductive knowledge","Pattern 2 knowledge","counterexample to the causal theory of knowledge"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"I originally came to know [that Lincoln was born in 1809], let us suppose, by reading an encyclopedia article. … I remember that Lincoln was born in 1809, but not that this is stated in a certain encyclopedia. I no longer have any pertinent beliefs that highly confirm the proposition that Lincoln was born in 1809. Nevertheless, I know this proposition now.","phenomenon":"problem of forgotten evidence","near":["Gettier case","undercutting (undermining) defeater","epistemic bootstrapping"],"avoid":["reliable but unjustified belief","problem of stored beliefs","stored beliefs","preservative memory","memory knowledge","forgotten evidence","knowledge despite forgotten evidence"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2024268"},{"id":"goldman1976-discrimination-perceptual-knowledge","title":"Discrimination and Perceptual Knowledge","authors":["Alvin I. Goldman"],"year":"1976","area":"epistemology","topic":"analysis of knowledge","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"An alternative that disqualifies a true perceptual belief from being perceptual knowledge must be a \"perceptual equivalent\" of the actual state of affairs.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I suggest that a person is said to know that p just in case he distinguishes or discriminates the truth of p from relevant alternatives.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"S noninferentially perceptually knows b has F iff b has F, b perceptually causes a percept noninferentially causing that belief, F ∈ K for every relevant perceptual equivalent ⟨c,K,R*⟩ of the actual ⟨b,J,R⟩, and the belief-forming propensity has appropriate genesis.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"A true belief is knowledge only if produced by a mechanism reliable enough to tell the actual situation apart from relevant situations where the belief would be false; Cartesian justification from self-warranting propositions is unnecessary.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"perceptual equivalent","aliases":["perceptual equivalents","perceptual equivalence","perceptual","percept"],"definition":"If the percept produced by the alternative state of affairs would not differ from the actual percept in any respect that is causally relevant to S's belief, this alternative situation is a perceptual equivalent for S of the actual situation.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"relevant alternative","aliases":["relevant alternatives","alternative","alternatives","relevant"],"definition":"A possible state of affairs in which p is false that a knower must be able to distinguish from the actual one: only 'serious' possibilities, not all logical ones. Which ones count is left open; likelihood, similarity and the speaker's context sway the choice.","exact":false},{"id":"t4","term":"reliability","aliases":["reliable","reliability"],"definition":"Roughly, a cognitive mechanism or process is reliable if it not only produces true beliefs in actual situations, but would produce true beliefs, or at least inhibit false beliefs, in relevant counterfactual situations.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Henry is driving in the countryside with his son. … “That's a barn,” … he has no doubt that the last-mentioned object is a barn, which indeed it is. … unknown to Henry, the district he has just entered is full of papier-mâché facsimiles of barns. These facsimiles look from the road exactly like barns … Henry has not encountered any facsimiles … if the object on that site were a facsimile, Henry would mistake it for a barn.","phenomenon":"fake barn case","aliases":["barn","barns"],"near":["false-lemma Gettier case","radical skeptical hypothesis","lottery case"],"avoid":["Gettier case","barn facade case","barn façade case","undercutting (undermining) defeater","counterexample to the causal theory of knowledge","insensitive belief","unsafe belief","veritic epistemic luck","epistemic luck","environmental epistemic luck"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose Oscar is standing in an open field containing Dack the dachshund. Oscar sees Dack and (noninferentially) forms a belief in (P): (P) The object over there is a dog. … wolves are frequenters of this field. Further suppose that Oscar has a tendency to mistake wolves for dogs … The mere fact that he would erroneously take a wolf to be a dog hardly shows that he doesn't know a dachshund to be a dog!","phenomenon":"knowledge without sensitivity","near":["false-lemma Gettier case","fake barn case","closure failure"],"avoid":["unsafe belief","safety violation","knowledge without ruling out irrelevant alternatives","insensitive belief"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"In a dark room there is a candle several yards ahead of S which S sees and believes to be ahead of him. But he sees the candle only indirectly, via a system of mirrors (of which he is unaware) that make it appear as if he were seeing it directly. We would surely deny that S knows the candle to be ahead of him.","phenomenon":"Gettier case","near":["veridical hallucination","lottery case","radical skeptical hypothesis"],"avoid":["environmental epistemic luck","deviant causal chain","perceptual de re belief","false-lemma Gettier case","counterexample to the causal theory of knowledge","insensitive belief","unsafe belief","veritic epistemic luck","epistemic luck"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2025679"},{"id":"stine1976-skepticism-relevant-alternatives","title":"Skepticism, Relevant Alternatives, and Deductive Closure","authors":["G. C. Stine"],"year":"1976","area":"epistemology","topic":"skepticism & contextualism","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"my account holds the set of relevant alternatives constant from beginning to end of the deductive closure argument … to do otherwise would be … akin to equivocation.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"one does know what one takes for granted in normal circumstances. I do know that [the animal] is not a mule painted to look like a zebra.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Relevant-alternatives theorists need not deny closure: saying someone knows p only pragmatically, cancellably presupposes that not-p is a relevant alternative, so 'I know it is not a painted mule' is odd to say yet true.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Relevance requires reason to think an alternative true, not just reason to think it could be true; since standards for such reasons vary with context, the skeptic has a legitimate foothold but cannot show ordinary knowledge claims are loose.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"relevant alternative","aliases":["relevant","alternative","alternatives","relevant alternatives"],"definition":"an alternative is relevant only if there is some reason to think that it is true.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"epistemic deductive closure","aliases":["deductive closure","closure"],"definition":"The validity of the argument form: a knows that p; a knows that p entails q; therefore a knows that q (as opposed to the rejected form whose second premise is merely 'p entails q').","exact":false},{"id":"t4","term":"relevant alternative presupposition","aliases":["relevant alternative","presupposition","presupposing","presuppose"],"definition":"it is the speaker, not the sentence (or proposition) itself, who does the presupposing. Thus, the presupposition falls in the category of those which Grice labels 'cancellable'. It is possible for 'John knows that p' to be true even though a pragmatic presupposition, that not-p is a relevant alternative, is false.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"I know that the striped animal I see in the zoo is a zebra. I know this despite the fact that I have no particular evidence that it is not a mule painted to look like a zebra … In this context - under normal circumstances, in zoos of integrity, etc. - that an animal on display has been deliberately disguised to fool trusting zoo-goers is just not a relevant hypothesis, one that I need trouble myself about rejecting.","phenomenon":"knowledge without ruling out irrelevant alternatives","aliases":["relevant"],"near":["Gettier case","fake barn case","lottery case"],"avoid":["closure failure","knowledge without sensitivity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"if on the basis of visual appearances obtained under optimum conditions while driving through the countryside Henry identifies an object as a barn, normally we say that Henry knows that it is a barn. Let us suppose, however, that unknown to Henry, the region is full of expertly made papier-mache facsimiles of barns. In this case, we would not say Henry knows that the object is a barn … what if no such facsimiles exist in Henry's surroundings, although they do in Sweden?","phenomenon":"fake barn case","aliases":["barn","barns"],"near":["false-lemma Gettier case","radical skeptical hypothesis","lottery paradox"],"avoid":["environmental epistemic luck","Gettier case","barn façade case","barn facade case","knowledge without ruling out irrelevant alternatives","counterexample to the causal theory of knowledge"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"If you know it is a zebra, and you know its being a zebra entails its not being a painted mule, then you know it is not a mule painted to look like a zebra. But you do not know the last, so you do not know the first - i.e., you do not know it is a zebra.","phenomenon":"closure-based skeptical argument","near":["Moorean proof","argument from illusion","epistemic regress problem"],"avoid":["argument from ignorance","skeptical paradox","closure failure"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00411885"},{"id":"zagzebski1994-inescapability-gettier-problems","title":"The Inescapability of Gettier Problems","authors":["Linda Zagzebski"],"year":"1994","area":"epistemology","topic":"analysis of knowledge","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"no account of knowledge as true belief plus something else can withstand Gettier objections as long as there is a small degree of independence between truth and the other conditions","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"What generates the problem for JTB … is that an accident of bad luck is cancelled out by an accident of good luck.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Whether the extra component is internalist justification, reliability, Plantinga's warrant or undefeated justification, any analysis of knowledge as true belief plus something closely but not inviolably tied to truth admits Gettier counterexamples.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Gettier cases can be generated mechanically: start with a belief justified strongly enough for knowledge yet false through bad luck, then add an independent piece of good luck that makes it true while leaving justification unchanged.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"Gettier problem","aliases":["Gettier problems","Gettier"],"definition":"Gettier problems arise in the theory of knowledge when it is only by chance that a justified true belief is true. … Since justification does not guarantee truth, it is possible for there to be a break in the connection between justification and truth, but for that connection to be regained by chance.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"defeasibility condition","aliases":["defeasibility conditions","defeasibility"],"definition":"a way to handle [Gettier cases] is to add what are commonly called 'defeasibility conditions' … [This move] adds to the requirement that knowledge be justified true belief the restriction that the belief in question must also be justified in certain counterfactual situations.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"[Mary's eyesight] is good enough to allow her to identify her husband sitting in his usual chair in the living room … in somewhat dim light … Suppose Mary simply misidentifies the chair-sitter who is, let us suppose, her husband's brother. … Mary's husband could be sitting on the other side of the room, unseen by her. In that case her belief 'My husband is sitting in the living room' is true and has sufficient warrant for knowledge … but she does not have knowledge.","phenomenon":"Gettier case","near":["fake barn case","veridical hallucination","lottery case"],"avoid":["false-lemma Gettier case","misidentification","insensitive belief","unsafe belief","veritic epistemic luck","epistemic luck","undercutting (undermining) defeater"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"you are driving through a region in which, unknown to you, the inhabitants have erected three barn facades for each real barn … Your eyesight is normal and reliable enough in ordinary circumstances to spot a barn from the road. But in this case the fake barns are indistinguishable from the real barns at such a distance. As you look at a real barn you form the belief 'That's a fine barn'. The belief is true and justified, but is not knowledge.","phenomenon":"fake barn case","aliases":["barn","barns","fake"],"near":["false-lemma Gettier case","veridical hallucination","lottery case"],"avoid":["Gettier case","barn facade case","barn façade case","counterexample to the causal theory of knowledge","insensitive belief","unsafe belief","veritic epistemic luck","epistemic luck","undercutting (undermining) defeater","environmental epistemic luck"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Suppose Dr Jones, a physician, has very good inductive evidence that her patient, Smith, is suffering from virus X. … no false belief figures causally or evidentially in her justification … Smith's symptoms are due to a distinct and unknown virus … Smith has very recently contracted virus X, but so recently that he does not yet exhibit symptoms caused by X … Dr Jones' belief that Smith has virus X is true, justified and undefeated, but it is not knowledge.","phenomenon":"Gettier case","near":["false-lemma Gettier case","lottery case","reliable but unjustified belief"],"avoid":["environmental epistemic luck","undercutting (undermining) defeater","undercutting defeater","insensitive belief","unsafe belief","veritic epistemic luck","epistemic luck"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2220147"},{"id":"feldman1985-evidentialism","title":"Evidentialism","authors":["Richard Feldman","Earl Conee"],"year":"1985","area":"epistemology","topic":"justification & evidence","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Doxastic attitude D toward proposition p is epistemically justified for S at t if and only if having D toward p fits the evidence S has at t.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"We hold the general view that one epistemically ought to have the doxastic attitudes that fit one's evidence. We think that being epistemically obligatory is equivalent to being epistemically justified.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Believing, disbelieving or suspending judgment is justified just when that attitude fits the evidence one has — regardless of voluntary control, normal cognitive limits, responsible inquiry, or the reliability of the process producing it.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Justification (an attitude's fitting one's evidence) is distinct from well-foundedness, which further requires holding the attitude on the basis of fitting, undefeated evidence; basing objections and simple reliabilism concern well-foundedness, not justification.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"evidentialism","aliases":["evidentialist"],"definition":"What we call evidentialism is the view that the epistemic justification of a belief is determined by the quality of the believer's evidence for the belief.","exact":true,"avoid":["fantl2002-evidence-pragmatics-justification/t2"]},{"id":"t2","term":"well-founded","aliases":["well-foundedness","well founded"],"definition":"The term 'well-founded' is sometimes used to characterize an attitude that is epistemically both well-supported and properly arrived at. … its application depends on two matters of evidence - the evidence one has, and the evidence one uses in forming the attitude.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"doxastic voluntarism","aliases":["doxastic","voluntary","voluntarily"],"definition":"the view that a doxastic attitude can be justified for a person only if forming the attitude is an action under the person's voluntary control","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Bonjour's example is of a person who is clairvoyant. As a result of his clairvoyance he comes to believe that the President is in New York City. The person has no evidence showing that he is clairvoyant and no other evidence supporting his belief about the President.","phenomenon":"reliable but unjustified belief","near":["new evil demon problem","Gettier case","generality problem for reliabilism"],"avoid":["clairvoyance case","improperly based belief","counterexample to the causal theory of knowledge"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose that one evening Jones looks out of his window and sees a bright shining disk-shaped object. The object is in fact a luminous frisbee … But Jones is attracted to the idea that extraterrestrials are visiting the Earth. He manages to believe that he is seeing a flying saucer. Is the process that caused this belief reliable? Since the sequence of events leading to his belief is an instance of many types of process, the answer depends upon which of these many types is the relevant one.","phenomenon":"generality problem for reliabilism","near":["new evil demon problem","Gettier case","epistemic bootstrapping"],"avoid":["cognitive penetration","undercutting (undermining) defeater","reliable but unjustified belief","improperly based belief","misidentification"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Suppose Alfred is justified in believing p, and justified in believing if p then q. Alfred also believes q. … Kornblith argues that Alfred's belief in q may still not be justified. It is not justified, according to Kornblith, if Alfred has a strong distrust of modus ponens and believes q because he likes the sound of the sentence expressing it rather than on the basis of the modus ponens argument.","phenomenon":"propositional justification without doxastic justification","near":["doxastic justification without propositional justification","Gettier case","reliable but unjustified belief"],"avoid":["reason acted on vs. reason merely had","improperly based belief","undercutting (undermining) defeater","higher-order evidence","defeat by misleading higher-order evidence"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Sally has fairly good evidence that her husband Burt has been seeing another woman. Their marriage is in a precarious condition. It would be best for Sally if their marriage were preserved. Sally forsees that, were she to believe that Burt has been seeing another woman, her resulting behavior would lead to their divorce.","phenomenon":"conflict between epistemic and prudential reasons for belief","near":["pragmatic encroachment","Pascal's wager","self-fulfilling belief"],"avoid":["state-given reason for an attitude","conflict of pro tanto reasons","conflicting normative requirements"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00372404"},{"id":"pryor2000-skeptic-dogmatist","title":"The Skeptic and the Dogmatist","authors":["James Pryor"],"year":"2000","area":"epistemology","topic":"perception & foundations","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"My view is that whenever you have an experience as of p, you thereby have immediate prima facie justification for believing p.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"it is not a precondition for having perceptual justification for believing p that one have other, antecedently justified beliefs that one could appeal to without begging the question","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"The skeptic's strongest argument is best reconstructed not via closure but via a principle requiring antecedent, non-question-begging grounds for rejecting 'bad' hypotheses like demon deception; that principle is the premise to deny.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Having an experience that represents a perceptually basic proposition gives defeasible justification for believing it that rests on no other justification, so one can justifiably believe there is a hand without first ruling out skeptical scenarios.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"dogmatism","aliases":["dogmatism","dogmatist","dogmatists","dogmatic"],"definition":"when it perceptually seems to you as if p is the case, you have a kind of justification for believing p that does not presuppose or rest on your justification for anything else, which could be cited in an argument … for p.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"immediate justification","aliases":["immediately justified","immediate","immediately","justification"],"definition":"you're justified in believing p, and this justification doesn't rest on any evidence or justification you have for believing other propositions.","exact":true,"avoid":["pryor2000-skeptic-dogmatist/t1","bonjour1980-externalist-theories-empirical/t2"]},{"id":"t3","term":"modest anti-skeptical project","aliases":["modest","anti-skeptical project"],"definition":"to establish to our satisfaction that we can justifiably believe and know such things as that there is a hand, without contradicting obvious facts about perception.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"epistemic priority","aliases":["epistemic priority","epistemically prior"],"definition":"Your justification for believing p1 is antecedent to your justification for believing p2 just in case your reasons for believing p1 do not presuppose or rest on your reasons for believing p2. Your reasons for believing p1 can not beg the question whether p2.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"perceptually basic","aliases":["perceptually basic","basically represent","basically"],"definition":"those propositions we seem to perceive to be so, but not in virtue of seeming to perceive that other propositions are so","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"Skeptical Principle about Knowledge (SPK)","aliases":["SPK","Skeptical Principle about Knowledge"],"definition":"If you're to know a proposition p on the basis of certain experiences or grounds E, then for every q which is “bad” relative to E and p, you have to be in a position to know q to be false … antecedently to knowing p on the basis of E.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"I'm driving my car. I look at my gas gauge and it appears to read “E.” This gives me justification for believing that I'm out of gas. However, for no good reason, I suspect that I'm hallucinating the gas gauge. So I do not actually form the belief that I'm out of gas, nor do I form the belief that my gas gauge reads “E.”","phenomenon":"propositional justification without doxastic justification","aliases":["justification"],"near":["doxastic justification without propositional justification","immediate justification","self-justifying belief"],"avoid":["propositional justification","mediate justification","undercutting (undermining) defeater","higher-order evidence","defeat by misleading higher-order evidence","reason acted on vs. reason merely had"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"the skeptical hypothesis that you're dreaming is compatible with many of your perceptual beliefs. … The dreaming hypothesis does however introduce a nonstandard explanation of your experiences. And this explanation would undermine the support your experiences give you for your perceptual beliefs—in the sense that, if you were to learn that you are dreaming, then you would have reason to doubt that your experiences were a trustworthy basis for beliefs about the external world.","phenomenon":"undercutting (undermining) defeater","aliases":["undermine","undermining","undercutting","underminer"],"near":["rebutting defeater","transmission failure","epistemic circularity"],"avoid":["higher-order evidence","Cartesian dream argument","dreaming argument","skeptical hypothesis","radical skeptical hypothesis","closure-based skeptical argument","defeat by misleading higher-order evidence"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Two subjects given the same sensory stimulation but with different background beliefs can differ with respect to what perceptually seems to them to be the case. There is a substantial amount of empirical evidence that this is so. For instance, if you believe that the object you're looking at is a familiar object like a carrot, you're likely to experience it as being more orange than you would if you lacked that belief.","phenomenon":"cognitive penetration","aliases":["cognitively penetrated"],"near":["perceptual learning","color constancy","inattentional blindness"],"avoid":["theory-ladenness of observation","theory-laden observation","memory color","cognitive penetrability of perception","top-down effect on perception","seeing-as (aspect perception)"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1111/0029-4624.00277"},{"id":"christensen2007-epistemology-disagreement-good","title":"Epistemology of Disagreement: The Good News","authors":["David Christensen"],"year":"2007","area":"epistemology","topic":"disagreement","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"In evaluating competing explanations of disagreement about P, my evaluation should be independent of the reasoning which supports my current assessment of P.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"adjusting our beliefs in the direction of those peers with whom we disagree should be welcomed as a valuable strategy for coping with our known infirmities.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"When a peer with the same evidence and equal competence disagrees, one should move one's credence substantially toward hers, roughly splitting the difference, unless reasons independent of the disputed reasoning locate the error in her.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Even someone who has evaluated the shared evidence correctly must revise on learning that a peer disagrees: the disagreement is evidence that she herself erred, and rationality requires heeding evidence of one's own possible error.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"Rational Uniqueness","aliases":["Uniqueness","unique","rational"],"definition":"the assumption that there is only one maximally rational response to a given evidential situation","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"Cognitive Parity","definition":"I had good reason to think her and myself to be equally good at responding to that kind of evidence","exact":true,"avoid":["elga2007-reflection-disagreement/t2"]},{"id":"t4","term":"live-and-let-live attitude","aliases":["live-and-let-live"],"definition":"my friend and I might both be maximally rational in our responses to the evidence, even if our degrees of confidence in the relevant proposition differ","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose that five of us go out to dinner. … We can all see the bill total clearly… I do the math in my head and become highly confident that our shares are $43 each. Meanwhile, my friend does the math in her head and becomes highly confident that our shares are $45 each. … we've been equally successful in our arithmetic efforts: the vast majority of times, we agree; but when we disagree, she's right as often as I am.","phenomenon":"peer disagreement","aliases":["peer","peers","disagree","disagreement"],"near":["faultless disagreement","merely verbal dispute","deference to an epistemic superior"],"avoid":["rebutting defeater","undercutting (undermining) defeater","defeat by misleading higher-order evidence"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose you are a doctor, used to determining appropriate drug dosages for your patients. You are extremely reliable at this… Now suppose you've just determined the dosage for one of your patients when you're informed that you've earlier been given a judgement-distorting drug that has been shown through many trials to cause 99% of the people who take it to make mistakes in tasks like dosage-determination.","phenomenon":"higher-order evidence","aliases":["evidence"],"near":["rebutting defeater","pragmatic encroachment","preface paradox"],"avoid":["undercutting (undermining) defeater","reliable but unjustified belief","defeat by misleading higher-order evidence"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"One of my patients is in very serious condition, and my friend and I both examine him, study his medical records, read the relevant literature--and come to conflicting conclusions. … Theory A is somewhat simpler, but Theory B fits a bit better with the data. My friend has about 65% credence in theory B and 35% in A, while for me, the two figures are reversed. … she is more moved by the fit with reported data, while I'm more moved by simplicity.","phenomenon":"epistemic permissivism","aliases":["permissive","permissiveness"],"near":["imprecise credences","washing out of priors","epistemic conservatism"],"avoid":["failure of uniqueness","conflict of pro tanto reasons","defeat by misleading higher-order evidence"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1215/00318108-2006-035"},{"id":"elga2007-reflection-disagreement","title":"Reflection and Disagreement","authors":["Adam Elga"],"year":"2007","area":"epistemology","topic":"disagreement","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Upon finding out that an advisor disagrees, your probability that you are right should equal your prior conditional probability that you would be right.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"with respect to many controversial issues, the associates who one counts as peers tend to have views that are similar to one's own.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Treat one's future self as a guru, not an expert: for any H and credence function P′ one may have at future t, P(H | I have P′ at t) = P′(H | X), X being one's present extra information.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Giving one's own verdict extra weight against someone one counted as a peer would license bootstrapping: becoming confident one is the better judge merely by noting disagreements. So peers' verdicts get equal weight.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"equal weight view","aliases":["equal"],"definition":"Upon finding out that an advisor disagrees, your probability that you are right should equal your prior conditional probability that you would be right. Prior to what? Prior to your thinking through the disputed issue, and finding out what the advisor thinks of it. Conditional on what? On whatever you have learned about the circumstances of the disagreement.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"epistemic peer","definition":"you count your friend as an epistemic peer with respect to an about-to-be-judged claim if and only if you think that, conditional the two of you disagreeing about the claim, the two of you are equally likely to be mistaken.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"guru","definition":"I treat the advisor as a guru if P (H | advisor has P′) = P′ (H |X), where X is my extra information, supposing that the advisor has probability function P′.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"right-reasons view","definition":"when you find out that your peer has arrived at the opposite conclusion as you, how much should you be moved? According to the right-reasons view, the answer depends on how good your initial evaluation of the evidence was.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"spinelessness","definition":"the problem that an egalitarian view on how to respond to disagreement will recommend suspension of judgment on virtually all controversial issues.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"suppose that you and your friend independently judge the same long series of races. … (You are given no outside information about the race outcomes.) Suppose for reductio that in each case of disagreement, you should be 70% confident that you are correct. It follows that over the course of many disagreements, you should end up extremely confident that you have a better track record than your friend. As a result, you should end up extremely confident that you are a better judge.","phenomenon":"epistemic bootstrapping","near":["Condorcet jury theorem","preface paradox","epistemic akrasia"],"avoid":["bootstrapping problem","peer disagreement","higher-order evidence","defeat by misleading higher-order evidence"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"consider Ann and Beth, two friends who stand at opposite ends of the political spectrum. Consider the claim that abortion is morally permissible. … Ann and Beth have discussed claims closely linked to the abortion claim. They have discussed, for example, whether human beings have souls, … and whether rights figure prominently in a correct ethical theory. By Ann's lights, Beth has reached wrong conclusions about most of these closely related questions.","phenomenon":"deep disagreement","near":["faultless disagreement","merely verbal dispute","metalinguistic negotiation"],"avoid":["peer disagreement"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Joe may reasonably be confident that this morning he had sushi for breakfast, even if he expects that in a year, he will have forgotten the truth of that claim","phenomenon":"anticipated forgetting","aliases":["forgotten"],"near":["anticipated impairment of judgment","loss of self-locating information","diachronic Dutch book"],"avoid":["counterexample to van Fraassen's Reflection Principle","anticipated forgetting (information loss)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"suppose that you are waiting for a train. You are only 50% confident that the train will ever arrive, but are sure that if it does arrive, it will arrive in exactly one hour. Since you have no watch, when fifty-five minutes have in fact elapsed you will be unsure whether an hour has elapsed. As a result, at that time you will have reduced confidence—say, only 40% confidence—that the train will ever arrive.","phenomenon":"loss of self-locating information","near":["anticipated forgetting","anticipated impairment of judgment","diachronic Dutch book"],"avoid":["counterexample to van Fraassen's Reflection Principle","cognitive dynamics: losing track of time","temporal self-locating ignorance","self-locating (de se) uncertainty","self-locating belief","self-locating knowledge","irreducibly de se ignorance","de se belief","decision under self-locating uncertainty","essential indexical","losing track of time"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1111/j.1468-0068.2007.00656.x"},{"id":"elga2000-self-locating-belief","title":"Self-Locating Belief and the Sleeping Beauty Problem","authors":["Adam Elga"],"year":"2000","area":"rationality","topic":"credence & probability","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Before being put to sleep, your credence in [Heads] was 1/2. I've just argued that when you are awakened on Monday, that credence ought to change to 1/3.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[Belief change] can happen to a perfectly rational agent during a period in which that agent neither receives new information nor suffers a cognitive mishap.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Waking, one is in H1 (Heads, Monday), T1 (Tails, Monday) or T2 (Tails, Tuesday). Indifference gives P(T1) = P(T2); a coin tossed only after Monday's waking gives P(H1|H1 ∨ T1) = 1/2; so P(H1) = P(T1) = P(T2) = 1/3.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Credence can rationally change with no new information or cognitive mishap when one comes to count one's temporal location as relevant; this is a counterexample to van Fraassen's Reflection Principle and shows centred worlds raise a new question about updating.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"new information","definition":"To say that an agent receives new information (as I shall use that expression) is to say that the agent receives evidence that rules out possible worlds not already ruled out by her previous evidence.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"counting one's temporal location as relevant","aliases":["counts her temporal location as relevant","located"],"definition":"To say that an agent counts her temporal location as relevant to the truth of a certain proposition is to say that there is a time t such that … her credence in the proposition, conditional on her being located at t, differs from her unconditional credence in the proposition.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"Reflection Principle","aliases":["Reflection"],"definition":"Any agent who is certain that she will tomorrow have credence x in proposition R (though she will neither receive new information nor suffer any cognitive mishaps in the intervening time) ought now to have credence x in R.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Some researchers are going to put you to sleep. During the two days that your sleep will last, they will briefly wake you up either once or twice, depending on the toss of a fair coin (Heads: once; Tails: twice). After each waking, they will put you to back to sleep with a drug that makes you forget that waking. When you are first awakened, to what degree ought you believe that the outcome of the coin toss is Heads?","phenomenon":"Sleeping Beauty problem","aliases":["sleep"],"near":["absent-minded driver problem","Doomsday argument","Monty Hall problem"],"avoid":["temporal self-locating ignorance","irreducibly de se ignorance","self-locating belief","de se belief","self-locating knowledge","decision under self-locating uncertainty","cognitive dynamics: losing track of time","loss of self-locating information","anticipated forgetting (information loss)","anticipated forgetting","losing track of time","essential indexical"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Initially you were certain that the coin was fair, and so initially your credence in the coin's landing Heads was 1/2. Upon being awakened, you receive no new information (you knew all along that you would be awakened). So your credence in the coin's landing Heads ought to remain 1/2.","phenomenon":"halfer answer to Sleeping Beauty","aliases":["halfer"],"near":["thirder answer to Sleeping Beauty","self-indication assumption","long-run frequency argument"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"At the start of the experiment, you had credence 1/2 in [Heads]. But you were also certain that upon being awakened on Monday you would have credence 1/3 in [Heads] – even though you were certain that you would receive no new information and suffer no cognitive mishaps during the intervening time.","phenomenon":"counterexample to van Fraassen's Reflection Principle","aliases":["Reflection"],"near":["lottery paradox","preface paradox","base-rate fallacy"],"avoid":["loss of self-locating information","anticipated forgetting (information loss)","anticipated forgetting","temporal self-locating ignorance","cognitive dynamics: losing track of time","losing track of time"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Then when you wake up, you're certain that you're in one of three 'predicaments': H1 HEADS and it is Monday. T1 TAILS and it is Monday. T2 TAILS and it is Tuesday. Notice that the difference between your being in T1 and your being in T2 is not a difference in which possible world is actual …","phenomenon":"self-locating (de se) uncertainty","aliases":["self-locating","de se"],"near":["logical uncertainty","higher-order uncertainty","imprecise credences"],"avoid":["temporal self-locating ignorance","irreducibly de se ignorance","self-locating belief","de se belief","self-locating knowledge","decision under self-locating uncertainty","cognitive dynamics: losing track of time","loss of self-locating information","losing track of time","essential indexical"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1093/analys/60.2.143"},{"id":"joyce1998-nonpragmatic-vindication-probabilism","title":"A Nonpragmatic Vindication of Probabilism","authors":["James M. Joyce"],"year":"1998","area":"rationality","topic":"credence & probability","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"degrees of belief which violate the axioms of probability are less accurate than they otherwise could be relative to any reasonable measure of accuracy.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"My position is that a rational partial believer must aim not simply to accept truths and reject falsehoods, but to hold partial beliefs that are gradationally accurate","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"If an inaccuracy measure I satisfies Structure, Extensionality, Dominance, Normality, Weak Convexity and Symmetry, then every credence function c violating the probability axioms has a probability function c* with I(c, ω) > I(c*, ω) at every world ω.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Degrees of belief are estimates of truth-values, judged by a gradational 'closeness counts' standard rather than full belief's all-or-nothing one; so probabilistic coherence can be defended as a requirement of accuracy itself, not of prudence or betting.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"gradational accuracy","aliases":["gradational","gradationally accurate","gradationally","accuracy","accurate"],"definition":"partial beliefs are appropriately evaluated on a gradational, or “closeness counts,” scale that assigns true beliefs higher degrees of accuracy the more strongly they are held, and false beliefs lower degrees of accuracy the more strongly they are held.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"Norm of Gradational Accuracy","aliases":["NGA","gradational accuracy"],"definition":"An epistemically rational agent must evaluate partial beliefs on the basis of their gradational accuracy, and she must strive to hold a system of partial beliefs that, in her best judgment, is likely to have an overall level of gradational accuracy at least as high as that of any alternative system she might adopt.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"Dominance","aliases":["dominance"],"definition":"[Dominance] says that the accuracy of b(X) always increases as it approaches ω(X). Thus, moving one's degree of belief for X closer to X's truth-value improves accuracy no matter what one's other degrees of belief might be.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"Weak Convexity","definition":"Weak Convexity is motivated by the intuition that extremism in the pursuit of accuracy is no virtue. It says that if a certain change in a person's degrees of belief does not improve accuracy then a more radical change in the same direction … should not improve accuracy either.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"Extensionality","definition":"Our next constraint stipulates that the “facts” which a person's partial beliefs must “fit” are exhausted by the truth-values of the propositions believed, and that the only aspect of her opinions that matter is their strengths.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Copernicus (let us suppose) was exactly as confident that the earth's orbit is circular as Kepler was that it is elliptical. However, both were wrong since the gravitational attraction of the moon and the other planets causes the earth to deviate slightly from its largely elliptical path. … Still Kepler was obviously nearer the mark","phenomenon":"verisimilitude (truthlikeness)","near":["pessimistic meta-induction","underdetermination of theory by evidence","incommensurability of theories"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"imagine that an agent with degrees of belief [(9/10, 9/10, 1/2, 1/2) for X1–X4] who has strong evidence for X1 and X2, somehow learns that exactly two of the Xj hold … The best way for our agent to improve his calibration score (indeed to ensure that it will be zero) is to keep his estimates for X3 and X4 fixed, ignore all his evidence, and lower his estimates for X1 and X2 to 1/2.","phenomenon":"violation of truth-directedness","near":["violation of Extensionality","problem of old evidence","verisimilitude (truthlikeness)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"When one tries to guess, say, the number of hits that a baseball player will get in his next ten at-bats, one aims to get the value exactly right. Guessing two hits when the batter gets three is just as wrong as guessing two hits when he gets ten.","phenomenon":"categorical (all-or-nothing) accuracy","near":["gradational ('closeness counts') accuracy","calibration","expected-value estimation"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"imagine a person who reports previsions (0.6, 0.2) for (X, ¬X) when losses are given by the Brier score. This agent will incur a 10¢ penalty if X is true, and a 50¢ penalty if X is false. Figure 1 shows how she could have saved a sure penny by reporting the previsions (0.7, 0.3).","phenomenon":"violation of finite additivity","near":["violation of non-negativity","conjunction fallacy","failure of conditionalization"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"In the same way that it makes no sense to define “temperature” as “the quantity measured by thermometers” because it is impossible to know a priori either that such a quantity tracks any important physical property or that different thermometers will always assign similar values in similar circumstances, so too it makes no sense to define “degree of belief” as “the prevision reported in a quadratic-loss game”","phenomenon":"operational definition (operationalism)","near":["implicit definition by axioms","ostensive definition","persuasive definition"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1086/392661"},{"id":"nozick1969-newcombs-problem-two","title":"Newcomb's Problem and Two Principles of Choice","authors":["Robert Nozick"],"year":"1969","area":"rationality","topic":"decision theory","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"if the actions… do not affect… which state obtains, then whatever the conditional probabilities (so long as they do not indicate an influence), one should perform the dominant action.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the explanation of the money's being in [a] box (or not) refers to the [chooser's predicted] action only in a nonextensional belief context","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"When acts cannot influence which state obtains, choose a dominant act whatever the conditional probabilities. So take both boxes: one's choice figures in explaining their contents only inside a near-infallible predictor's belief, whose accuracy makes that look like influence.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"If acts influence states, maximize expected utility using P(Sᵢ|A); if not, a dominant act wins; failing dominance, choose A when EU(A) > EU(B) under every distribution r with each rᵢ between P(Sᵢ|A) and P(Sᵢ|B).","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"Dominance Principle","aliases":["dominates"],"definition":"If there is a partition of states of the world such that relative to it, action A weakly dominates action B, then A should be performed rather than B.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"betweenness","aliases":["between"],"definition":"A probability distribution r1, ..., rn, summing to 1, is between p1, ..., pn and q1, ..., qn iff for each i, ri is in the closed interval [pi, qi] or [qi, pi].","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose a being in whose power to predict your choices you have enormous confidence. … There are two boxes, (B1) and (B2). (B1) contains $1000. (B2) contains either $1000000 ($M), or nothing. … (I) If the being predicts you will take what is in both boxes, he does not put the $M in the second box. (II) If the being predicts you will take only what is in the second box, he does put the $M in the second box.","phenomenon":"Newcomb's problem","near":["Kavka's toxin puzzle","Death in Damascus","Prisoners' Dilemma"],"avoid":["Newcomb problem","Newcomb's paradox"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"The $M is either already sitting in the second box, or it is not, and which situation obtains is already fixed and determined. … Therefore, whether the money is there or not, … I get $1000 more by taking what is in both boxes rather than taking only what is in the second box.","phenomenon":"dominance argument for two-boxing","near":["evidential expected-utility argument for one-boxing","\"Why ain'cha rich?\" argument","Dutch book argument"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"You know that many persons like yourself … have gone through this experiment. All those who took only what was in the second box … ended up with $M. And you know that all the shrewdies, all those who … took what was in both boxes, ended up with only $1000. You have no reason to believe that you are any different, vis-a-vis predictability, than they are.","phenomenon":"\"Why ain'cha rich?\" argument","near":["dominance argument for two-boxing","Dutch book argument","sunk cost fallacy"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"P knows that S or T is his father … If S is his father, P will die of [a terrible inherited] disease … there is a well-confirmed theory … about the genetic transmission of the tendency to decide to do acts which form part of an intellectual life. … S had this tendency …, T did not … P is now deciding whether (a) to go to graduate school and then teach, or (b) to become a professional baseball player.","phenomenon":"medical Newcomb problem","near":["Death in Damascus","backward causation","Simpson's paradox"],"avoid":["Newcomb problem","Newcomb's problem","smoking lesion problem","smoking lesion","common-cause problem"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/978-94-017-1466-2_7"},{"id":"lewis1981-causal-decision-theory","title":"Causal Decision Theory","authors":["David Lewis"],"year":"1981","area":"rationality","topic":"decision theory","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"My main thesis is that we should maximise expected utility calculated by means of dependency hypotheses.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"It is essential to define utility … using the unconditional credences C(K) of dependency hypotheses, not their conditional credence C(K/A).","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Rational choice maximises U(A) = Σ C(K)·V(AK), the sum taken over dependency hypotheses K (maximally specific propositions about how what the agent cares about causally depends on his acts), weighted by unconditional C(K), not C(K/A).","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Gibbard–Harper's counterfactual, Skyrms's factor-partition and Sobel's imaging decision theories share one common idea, differing mainly in formulation; but with chancy outcomes plain counterfactuals fail, so dependency hypotheses are built from counterfactuals with chance propositions as consequents.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"dependency hypothesis","aliases":["dependency hypotheses"],"definition":"a maximally specific proposition about how the things [the agent] cares about do and do not depend causally on his present actions","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"U-maximising","aliases":["U-maximal","maximise"],"definition":"What should you do if you spread your credence over several dependency hypotheses? You should consider the expected value of your options under the several hypotheses; you should weight these by the credences you attach to the hypotheses; and you should maximise the weighted average.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"V-maximising","aliases":["V-maximal"],"definition":"[Noncausal decision theory] prescribes the rule of V-maximising, according to which a rational choice is one that has the greatest expected value. … You make the news, so make the news you like best.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"Tickle Defence","aliases":["tickle","Tickle Defense"],"definition":"The Tickle Defence of noncausal decision theory questions whether Newcomb problems really can arise. … if you are fully rational, you know your own mind. … You will have [the bad news] already when you feel that tickle in the tastebuds … Your consequent choice tells you nothing more.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"probabilistic full pattern","aliases":["full pattern"],"definition":"Now consider the causal counterfactuals A □→ [P=p] from the agent's options to the chance propositions. Define a probabilistic full pattern as a set containing exactly one such counterfactual for each option. I claim that the conjunction of the counterfactuals in any probabilistic full pattern is a causal dependency hypothesis.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose you like eating eggs, or smoking, or loafing … You are convinced … that these pleasures will do you no harm at all. … But also you think you might have some dread medical condition: a lesion of an artery, or nascent cancer … you are convinced that it has some tendency … to cause you to eat eggs, smoke, or loaf. So if you find yourself indulging, that is at least some evidence that you have the condition","phenomenon":"medical Newcomb problem","aliases":["medical"],"near":["Death in Damascus","Allais paradox","Kavka's toxin puzzle"],"avoid":["Newcomb problem","Newcomb's problem","smoking lesion problem","smoking lesion"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose I am convinced that stroking has no influence over purring, but that I wouldn't stroke Bruce unless I were in a mood that gets him to purr softly by emotional telepathy. Then I give credence to I stroke Bruce □→ he purrs softly","phenomenon":"backtracking counterfactual","aliases":["back-tracking","backtracking"],"near":["causal counterfactual","counterpossible conditional","might-counterfactual"],"avoid":["back-tracking counterfactual","subjunctive (counterfactual) conditional"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Suppose you are one player in a two-person game. Each player can play red, play white, play blue, or not play. If both play the same colour, each gets a thousand dollars; if they play different colours, each loses a thousand dollars; if one or both don't play, … no money changes hands. … But you have been persuaded that you and the other player are very much alike psychologically and hence very likely to choose alike … Is it rational for you to play?","phenomenon":"Hunter–Richter problem","aliases":["Hunter-Richter"],"near":["Prisoners' Dilemma","Battle of the Sexes","Death in Damascus"],"avoid":["coordination game","pure coordination game","coordination problem","Newcomb problem","twin Prisoner's Dilemma","Newcomb's problem"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"[The principle] says that the way things would be on a false but possible supposition X is no less specific than the way things actually are. … But surely some questions about how things would be if X have no nonarbitary answers: if you had a sister, would she like blintzes?","phenomenon":"failure of conditional excluded middle","near":["failure of antecedent strengthening","failure of contraposition","backtracking counterfactual"],"avoid":["counterexample to Conditional Excluded Middle","subjunctive (counterfactual) conditional","excluded middle without bivalence"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1080/00048408112340011"},{"id":"lewis1988-desire-belief","title":"Desire as Belief","authors":["David Lewis"],"year":"1988","area":"rationality","topic":"reasons & motivation","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"By imposing the [Anti-Humean thesis] as a new constraint on Decision Theory, we have overconstrained it, and made it exclude what can perfectly well happen.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"We are within our rights to construe 'desire' inclusively, to cover the entire range of states that move us … Humeanism understood in this inclusive way is surely true","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Anti-Humeans tie desiring A necessarily to believing A would be good—quantitatively, desire for A equals credence that A would be good; since that equation collides with decision theory, it is the anti-Humean thesis that must go.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Given Jeffrey kinematics, additivity and invariance, requiring V(A)=C(A°) (A°: 'it would be good that A') after updating makes Vₓ(A) both linear and a ratio of linear functions of the shift size x; so no update changes both C(A) and V(A).","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"Desire-as-Belief Thesis","aliases":["Desire-as-Belief","desire as belief"],"definition":"To any ordinary proposition A, there corresponds another proposition: A°, the proposition that it would be good that A. Frederic's expected value for A, which represents the degree to which he desires that A, equals the degree to which he believes that A°.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"Invariance Assumption","aliases":["Invariance"],"definition":"When credences change by probability kinematics, expected values may change also, but only in response to the exogenous redistribution of credence over the Eᵢ's. … this cannot happen if A is compatible with only one of the Eᵢ's. In that case, its value remains unchanged.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"cold desire","aliases":["cold","desire","desires"],"definition":"Some desires, for instance your desire to have Neiss for a colleague, are warm—you feel enthusiasm, you take pleasure in the prospect of fulfilment. Other desires, for instance your desire to hire the best available candidate, are cold. Nobody ever said that only the warm desires can move us.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"The Department must choose between two candidates for a job, Meane and Neiss. Neiss is your old friend, affable, sensible, fair-minded, co-operative, moderate, … . Meane is quite the opposite. But it is clear that Meane is just a little bit better at philosophy. Gritting your teeth and defying all desire, you vote for Meane, because you believe that Meane getting the job instead of Neiss would, all things considered, be good.","phenomenon":"continence (strength of will)","near":["weakness of will (akrasia)","the amoralist (evaluative judgment without motivation)","Buridan's ass"],"avoid":["executive virtue"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Stanley has often escaped the anger of the pirates by claiming to be an orphan. [Frederic] now learns that Stanley is in fact no orphan. … he thinks that the pirates will soon be very angry with Stanley … he believes at least somewhat more than he did before that it would be good for the pirates to be angry with Stanley; and so … he desires at least somewhat more than he did before that the pirates be angry with Stanley.","phenomenon":"counterexample to the Desire-as-Belief thesis","near":["wishful thinking","adaptive preference formation (sour grapes)","Newcomb's problem"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Suppose Frederic single-mindedly pursues one goal: that the proposition G be true. … For this special case, we have managed at least to equate desire with conditional credence. … For any proposition A, let A° be the proposition that A conduces to G: A → G, in some appropriate sense of the conditional arrow. It seems that Frederic's desire that A should always equal his degree of belief that A conduces to achieving his goal G.","phenomenon":"Stalnaker's thesis (probability conditional)","aliases":["probability conditional","conditional"],"near":["material conditional","import-export principle","Principal Principle"],"avoid":["instrumental (means-end) reasoning"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1093/mind/xcvii.387.323"},{"id":"korsgaard1986-skepticism-practical-reason","title":"Skepticism about Practical Reason","authors":["Christine M. Korsgaard"],"year":"1986","area":"rationality","topic":"reasons & motivation","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Motivational skepticism must always be based on content skepticism. … I want only to establish the fact that motivational skepticism has no independent force.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[The internalism requirement] does not require that rational considerations always succeed in motivating us. All it requires is that rational considerations succeed in motivating us insofar as we are rational.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Doubts about reason's power to motivate have no independent force: they always presuppose a narrow view of what reason can say about action, so the requirement that reasons be able to motivate cannot itself limit which principles are rational.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Against Williams: if pure practical reason exists then, by the internalism requirement, every rational agent's subjective motivational set includes the capacity to be moved by it; what the set contains depends on which kinds of reasoning are possible, not conversely.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"internalism requirement","aliases":["internalism"],"definition":"Practical-reason claims, if they are really to present us with reasons for action, must be capable of motivating rational persons.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"true irrationality","definition":"knowing the truth about the relevant causal relations in the case, we might nevertheless choose means insufficient to our end … This would be what I will call true irrationality, by which I mean a failure to respond appropriately to an available reason.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"content skepticism","definition":"doubt about the bearing of rational considerations on the activities of deliberation and choice; doubts, that is to say, about whether \"formal\" principles … can give substantive guidance to choice and action.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"motivational skepticism","definition":"doubt about the scope of reason as a motive","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"You are outraged at the mocking things you heard me say about you, but I was talking about somebody else. You are terrified by the burglars you hear whispering in the living room, but in fact you left the radio on.","phenomenon":"passion based on a false belief","near":["true irrationality","weakness of will (akrasia)","imprudence (preferring an acknowledged lesser good)"],"avoid":["misidentification"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"[Kant] is talking about what the world would have been like if nature had had our happiness as her end. Our actions would have been controlled entirely by instincts designed to secure our happiness … The favored creature is portrayed as able to see that his actions are rational in the sense that they promote the means to his end (happiness); but he is not motivated by their reasonableness; he acts from instinct.","phenomenon":"reasoning theoretically but not practically","aliases":["reason","reasonableness"],"near":["weakness of will (akrasia)","self-deception","passion based on a false belief"],"avoid":["disappearing agent (no causal deviance)","reason acted on vs. reason merely had","wanton agency","instrumental (means-end) reasoning"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"'Tis not contrary to reason to prefer the destruction of the whole world to the scratching of my finger. 'Tis not contrary to reason for me to chuse my total ruin, to prevent the least uneasiness of an Indian or person wholly unknown to me. 'Tis as little contrary to reason to prefer even my own acknowledg'd lesser good to my greater","phenomenon":"instrumentalism about practical reason","aliases":["reason"],"near":["rational egoism","passion based on a false belief","externalism about moral motivation"],"avoid":["instrumental (means-end) reasoning"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"A person in whom [the motivational path from end to means] is, from some cause, blocked or nonfunctioning may not respond to argument, even if this person understands the argument in a theoretical way. Aristotle thinks of the incontinent person as being in a condition of this sort: this happens to people in fits of passion or rage, and the condition is actually physiological.","phenomenon":"weakness of will (akrasia)","aliases":["incontinent"],"near":["passion based on a false belief","self-deception","rationalization"],"avoid":["will at odds with one's second-order volition","reason acted on vs. reason merely had","disappearing agent (no causal deviance)"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2026464"},{"id":"makinson1965-paradox-preface","title":"The Paradox of the Preface","authors":["D. C. Makinson"],"year":"1965","area":"rationality","topic":"rational requirements","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Even though each individual belief expressed by [an] author or philosopher [who believes he errs somewhere] is rational, the collection of all his beliefs is not.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"To say that a person's position as a whole is rational is to say more than that each of the beliefs which make it up is rational.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Each belief in a set one knows to be inconsistent can be rational: an author taught by past corrections rationally believes each claim in his book, and rationally believes that at least one of them is false.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"When an author believes his book's claims s₁, …, sₙ and sₙ₊₁ = ¬(s₁ ∧ … ∧ sₙ), each belief bᵢ in sᵢ can be rational without the set {b₁, …, bₙ₊₁} being rational; conflating these generates paradox.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"paradox of the preface","aliases":["preface","paradox"],"definition":"The author who writes and believes each of s1, . . . , sn and yet in a preface asserts and believes ~(s1 & . . . & sn) is, it appears, behaving very rationally. Yet clearly he is holding logically incompatible beliefs","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose that in the course of his book a writer makes a great many assertions… Given each one of these, he believes that it is true. If he has already written other books, and received corrections from readers and reviewers, he may also believe that not everything he has written in his latest book is true. His approach is eminently rational; he has learnt from experience.","phenomenon":"preface paradox","aliases":["preface","paradox"],"near":["lottery paradox","Moore's paradox","dogmatism paradox"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"The philosopher who says \"All my present philosophical views are correct\" would be regarded as rash and over-confident. The one who refrains from such a view is playing safe. The one who says \"At least some of my present philosophical beliefs will turn out to be incorrect\" is simply being sensible and honest.","phenomenon":"preface paradox","aliases":["preface","paradox"],"near":["lottery paradox","Moore's paradox","concessive knowledge attribution"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1093/analys/25.6.205"},{"id":"kolodny2005-why-rational","title":"Why Be Rational?","authors":["Niko Kolodny"],"year":"2005","area":"rationality","topic":"rational requirements","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"there are no reasons to comply with rational requirements in general. … rationality is only apparently normative, and the normativity that it appears to have is that of reasons.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"From the first-person standpoint, the ‘ought’ of rationality is transparent. It looks just like the ‘ought’ of reasons.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"The requirement to follow beliefs about reasons is narrow-scope — Bel(conclusive reason for A) → Req(A), not Req(Bel(…) → A) — since lacking A has no content to reason from; so Req(Z) → ConclusiveReason(Z) would make such beliefs self-justifying.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Telling someone he 'ought rationally' to have an attitude just points out that, by his own lights, he has conclusive reason for it; the felt pressure comes from that apparent reason, so no second primitive 'ought' is needed.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"Transparency Account","definition":"rational requirements govern our responses to our beliefs about reasons. The normative ‘pressure’ that we feel, when rational requirements apply to us, derives from these beliefs: from the reasons that, as it seems to us, we have.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"core requirements","definition":"If one believes that one has conclusive reason to have A, then one is rationally required to have A. … If one believes that one lacks sufficient reason to have A, then one is rationally required not to have A.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"process-requirement","aliases":["process-requirements"],"definition":"We should, accordingly, distinguish between ‘state-requirements,’ which simply ban states in which one has conflicting attitudes, and ‘process-requirements,’ which say how, going forward, one is to form, retain, or revise one’s attitudes so as to avoid or escape such conflict-states.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"Reasoning Test","aliases":["reason"],"definition":"[The requirement] governing the conflict between A and B is wide scope … only if, from a state in which one has conflicting attitudes A and B, (i) one can reason from the content of A to dropping B and (ii) one can reason from the content of B to dropping A.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"Generalized Internalism Requirement","aliases":["GIR"],"definition":"That p is a reason for A to R only if it is possible for A to reason from the content of the recognition that p is a reason for A to R to R-ing.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"From the fact that you believe that p, it follows that you are rationally required to believe what p entails. If we suppose that we have conclusive reasons to comply with rational requirements, then it follows that you have conclusive reason to believe what p entails. And since p entails p, it follows that if you believe that p, then you have conclusive reason to believe that p.","phenomenon":"bootstrapping problem","near":["Lewis Carroll's regress","preface paradox","Moore's paradox"],"avoid":["epistemic bootstrapping"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"a state-given reason for intending to drink a toxin might be that an eccentric billionaire will give me a million dollars if I intend to drink it—whether or not I actually drink it. And a state-given reason for believing that I am good looking might be that I will be more confident if I believe it—which does nothing, alas, to show that it is true.","phenomenon":"state-given reason for an attitude","aliases":["state-given reason","state-given"],"near":["object-given reason","self-fulfilling belief","doxastic voluntarism"],"avoid":["conflict between epistemic and prudential reasons for belief"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"We admire the courage that the mafioso displays in lunging for power, even though we don’t believe that it’s something that he ought to do. Likewise—the suggestion would go—we admire the rationality that someone displays in intending to X, because he believes that he ought to X, even though we do not believe that in fact he ought to X.","phenomenon":"executive virtue","near":["reasons-responsive virtue such as kindness","weakness of will (akrasia)","moral luck"],"avoid":["continence (strength of will)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"An atheist might say to a racist believer: ‘Look: I think you’re nuts to believe in God, let alone that He created anyone. But given that you believe that God created all people equal, and given that you agree that people whose skin is a different colour from yours are people, you ought to believe that He created them equal too. It would be irrational of you not to.’","phenomenon":"rational advice (subjective advice)","aliases":["irrational"],"near":["objective advice (citing a real reason)","appraisal (grading) of someone's rationality","purely classificatory norm"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1093/mind/fzi509"},{"id":"broome1999-normative-requirements","title":"Normative Requirements","authors":["John Broome"],"year":"1999","area":"rationality","topic":"rational requirements","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Rationality does not consist entirely in acting for good reasons, as is commonly supposed. To a large extent it consists in following normative requirements.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"In general, intending an end normatively requires you to intend what you believe to be a necessary means. It does not give you a reason to [do so].","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Besides reasons and oughts there is a strict, non-detaching normative relation: it entails O(p → q) but not p → Oq or p → Rq, so neither an ought nor a reason to q can be detached from p.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Correct reasoning, theoretical or practical, does not supply reasons for its conclusion: believing the premises, or intending an end and believing some means necessary, strictly calls for the conclusion even if you ought not to hold those premises.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"normative requirement","aliases":["normatively requires","normative requirements","normatively","requires","requirement","normative"],"definition":"p requires q … and translate it as 'p normatively requires you to q'. If this relation holds, one consequence is that O(p → q) … Consequently, a normative requirement does not permit a normative conclusion to be detached by modus ponens.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"strictness test","aliases":["strictness","strict","test"],"definition":"a very useful test for distinguishing a normative requirement from a reason. The reasons relation is slack, so if a normative relation is strict (and it is not the oughts relation) it must be normative requirement.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"subjective ought","aliases":["subjectively","subjective","ought"],"definition":"I think 'subjective ought' is really just an alternative term for 'normative requirement'. If so, it is an unsatisfactory term … it conceals the logical structure of the situation, because it does not make the 'ought' govern a conditional.","exact":true,"avoid":["broome1999-normative-requirements/t1"]}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"suppose you believe that platypuses produce milk and all creatures that produce milk are mammals. These beliefs normatively require you to believe that platypuses are mammals. But suppose you also believe platypuses lay eggs and no mammals lay eggs. These beliefs normatively require you not to believe platypuses are mammals.","phenomenon":"conflicting normative requirements","aliases":["normatively require","conflicting requirements"],"near":["conflict of pro tanto reasons","preface paradox","moral dilemma"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Suppose you intend to open the wine, and then someone tells you the corkscrew is in the kitchen. This information imparts to you the belief that in order to open the wine, you must fetch the corkscrew. By reasoning, you form the intention of fetching the corkscrew.","phenomenon":"instrumental (means-end) reasoning","aliases":["instrumental","reasoning"],"near":["theoretical reasoning","enkrasia","deliberation about final ends"],"avoid":["instrumentalism about practical reason"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"First, suppose [believing you ought to r gave you a reason to r], and suppose you believe you ought to r. Then you would have a reason to r. So if you had no contrary reason not to r, it would be the case that you ought to r. Therefore, in the special case where you have no contrary reason, your belief would make it the case that you ought to r.","phenomenon":"bootstrapping problem","near":["weakness of will (akrasia)","Moore's paradox","wishful thinking"],"avoid":["epistemic bootstrapping"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"We say, for instance, 'If it is raining, it must be thawing'. We do not mean, 'If it is raining, necessarily it is thawing', but, 'Necessarily, if it is raining, it is thawing'. Moreover, the antecedent may be implicit and not even stated: we notice the rain and say simply, 'It must be thawing'.","phenomenon":"wide-scope reading of a modal conditional","near":["narrow-scope (detachable) reading of a modal","counterfactual conditional","paradox of material implication"],"avoid":["if-clause restricting a modal","epistemic modality","context-supplied conversational background (modal base)","circumstantial (root) modality"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1111/1467-9329.00101"},{"id":"carroll1895-tortoise-said-achilles","title":"What the Tortoise Said to Achilles","authors":["Lewis Carroll"],"year":"1895","area":"rationality","topic":"rational requirements","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[‘If A, B, C, then Z’ is] another Hypothetical … if I failed to see its truth, I might accept A and B and C, and still not accept Z","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"a race-course, that most people fancy they can get to the end of in two or three steps, while it really consists of an infinite number of distances","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Treating an inference step as a premise starts an endless regress: whoever grants A, B and the conditional C ('if A and B, then Z') may still refuse Z, so D ('if A, B and C, then Z') is needed.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Adding conditionals that link premises to conclusion never closes the gap between accepting the premises and accepting the conclusion; the step licensed by a rule like modus ponens must be taken, not written in as another premise.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"Hypothetical Proposition","aliases":["Hypothetical","Hypotheticals"],"definition":"I accept as true the Hypothetical Proposition that, if A and B be true, Z must be true; but, I don't accept A and B as true … I accept A and B as true, but I don't accept the Hypothetical","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"'I accept A and B and C and D. Suppose I still refused to accept Z?' 'Then Logic would take you by the throat, and force you to do it!' … 'Whatever Logic is good enough to tell me is worth writing down,' said the Tortoise. … 'We will call it (E) If A and B and C and D are true, Z must be true. Until I've granted that, of course I needn't grant Z.'","phenomenon":"Lewis Carroll's regress","aliases":["Tortoise"],"near":["rule-following paradox","epistemic regress problem","Zeno's paradox of motion"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Achilles had overtaken the Tortoise, and had seated himself comfortably on its back. 'So you've got to the end of our race-course?' said the Tortoise. 'Even though it does consist of an infinite series of distances? I thought some wiseacre or other had proved that the thing couldn't be done?' 'It can be done,' said Achilles. 'It has been done! Solvitur ambulando. You see the distances were constantly diminishing; and so—'","phenomenon":"Zeno's Achilles paradox","aliases":["Achilles","Tortoise"],"near":["Zeno's arrow paradox","Thomson's lamp","Zeno's moving rows paradox"],"avoid":["Zeno's dichotomy paradox","supertask","Zeno's paradox of motion"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1093/mind/iv.14.278"},{"id":"davidson1963-actions-reasons-causes","title":"Actions, Reasons, and Causes","authors":["Donald Davidson"],"year":"1963","area":"action","topic":"action & intention","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I want to defend the ancient—and common-sense—position that rationalization is a species of ordinary causal explanation.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Central to the relation between a reason and an action it explains is the idea that the agent performed the action because he had the reason.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Explaining an action by the agent's reason is causal explanation: the primary reason—a pro attitude toward actions with some property plus the belief that this action has it—causes the action.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Rationalizations need no laws couched in the vocabulary of reasons: a singular causal claim entails only that some law covers the events under some description, perhaps neurological or physical, so reasons can be causes.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"primary reason","aliases":["primary reasons"],"definition":"R is a primary reason why an agent performed the action A under the description d only if R consists of a pro attitude of the agent toward actions with a certain property, and a belief of the agent that A, under the description d, has that property.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"pro attitude","aliases":["pro attitudes","attitudes"],"definition":"desires, wantings, urges, promptings, and a great variety of moral views, aesthetic principles, economic prejudices, social conventions, and public and private goals and values in so far as these can be interpreted as attitudes of an agent directed toward actions of a certain kind.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"rationalization","aliases":["rationalizations","rationalizes"],"definition":"What is the relation between a reason and an action when the reason explains the action by giving the agent's reason for doing what he did? We may call such explanations rationalizations, and say that the reason rationalizes the action.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"intention with which","aliases":["intention"],"definition":"The expression 'the intention with which James went to church' has the outward form of a description, but in fact it is syncategorematic and cannot be taken to refer to an entity, state, disposition, or event. Its function in context is to generate new descriptions of actions in terms of their reasons","exact":true,"avoid":["davidson1967-logical-form-action/t4"]}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"I flip the switch, turn on the light, and illuminate the room. Unbeknownst to me I also alert a prowler to the fact that I am home. Here I do not do four things, but only one, of which four descriptions have been given.","phenomenon":"one action under different descriptions","aliases":["descriptions"],"near":["fine-grained individuation of actions","basic action","deviant causal chain"],"avoid":["referential opacity (action under a description)","coarse-grained individuation of actions","referential opacity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"we cannot treat what was done simply as a term in sentences like 'My reason for flipping the switch was that I wanted to turn on the light'; otherwise we would be forced to conclude, from the fact that flipping the switch was identical with alerting the prowler, that my reason for alerting the prowler was that I wanted to turn on the light.","phenomenon":"intensionality of rationalizations","near":["extensionality of singular causal statements","logical connection argument","deviant causal chain"],"avoid":["referential opacity","referential opacity (substitution failure)","referential opacity (action under a description)","substitution failure in belief contexts","de re / de dicto ambiguity","de dicto vs de re reading","one action under different descriptions"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"you may err about your reasons, particularly when you have two reasons for an action, one of which pleases you and one which does not. For example, you do want to save Charles pain; you also want him out of the way. You may be wrong about which motive made you do it.","phenomenon":"reason acted on vs. reason merely had","aliases":["reasons"],"near":["weakness of will (akrasia)","deviant causal chain","Buridan's ass"],"avoid":["alienation from one's own motives"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Suppose a hurricane, which is reported on page 5 of Tuesday's Times, causes a catastrophe, which is reported on page 13 of Wednesday's Tribune. Then the event reported on page 5 of Tuesday's Times caused the event reported on page 13 of Wednesday's Tribune. Should we look for a law relating events of these kinds?","phenomenon":"extensionality of singular causal statements","near":["intensionality of rationalizations","causal overdetermination","causal preemption"],"avoid":["referentially transparent context","one action under different descriptions"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2023177"},{"id":"frankfurt1969-alternate-possibilities-moral","title":"Alternate Possibilities and Moral Responsibility","authors":["Harry G. Frankfurt"],"year":"1969","area":"action","topic":"free will & responsibility","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Even though a person is subject to a coercive force that precludes his performing any action but one, he may nonetheless bear full moral responsibility for performing that action.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"there may be circumstances that make it impossible for a person to avoid performing some action without those circumstances in any way bringing it about that he performs that action.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"An agent who acts on his own while someone stands ready to make him act that way if needed cannot do otherwise, yet is as responsible as if no one were waiting: the inability played no role.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Inability to do otherwise excuses only when it explains the action; the correct principle is that an agent is not responsible for what he did if he did it only because he could not have done otherwise.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"principle of alternate possibilities","aliases":["principle"],"definition":"This principle states that a person is morally responsible for what he has done only if he could have done otherwise.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"coercion","aliases":["coerced","coercive"],"definition":"it is incorrect to regard a man as being coerced to do something unless he does it because of the coercive force exerted against him.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"revised principle of alternate possibilities","aliases":["principle of alternate possibilities","revised","revision"],"definition":"What I have said may suggest that the principle of alternate possibilities should be revised so as to assert that a person is not morally responsible for what he has done if he did it because he could not have done otherwise.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose someone—Black, let us say—wants Jones₄ to perform a certain action. … If it does become clear that Jones₄ is going to decide to do something else, Black takes effective steps to ensure that Jones₄ decides to do, and that he does do, what he wants him to do. … Let Black give Jones₄ a potion … Now suppose that Black never has to show his hand because Jones₄, for reasons of his own, decides to perform and does perform the very action Black wants him to perform.","phenomenon":"Frankfurt-style case (counterfactual intervener)","near":["manipulation case (implanted values)","coercion (duress)","will at odds with one's second-order volition"],"avoid":["moral responsibility without freedom of the will"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Jones decides for reasons of his own to do something, then someone threatens him with a very harsh penalty … unless he does precisely that, and Jones does it. … Another possibility is that Jones₂ was stampeded by the threat. … The threat upset him so profoundly, moreover, that he completely forgot his own earlier decision and did what was demanded of him entirely because he was terrified of the penalty with which he was threatened.","phenomenon":"coercion (duress)","near":["Frankfurt-style case (counterfactual intervener)","manipulation case (implanted values)","weakness of will (akrasia)"],"avoid":["excusing condition","reason acted on vs. reason merely had"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2023833"},{"id":"frankfurt1971-freedom-will-concept","title":"Freedom of the Will and the Concept of a Person","authors":["Harry G. Frankfurt"],"year":"1971","area":"action","topic":"free will & responsibility","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"it is having second-order volitions, and not having second-order desires generally, that I regard as essential to being a person.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"It is in securing the conformity of his will to his second-order volitions … that a person exercises freedom of the will.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Persons differ from wantons not in reason but in the structure of their will: they care which of their desires moves them to act, wanting some desires to be effective, whereas wantons do not care about their will.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Modeled on free action (the freedom to do what one wants), free will is the freedom to have the will one wants; it is not required for moral responsibility, as the willing addict shows.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"second-order volition","aliases":["second-order volitions","volitions of the second order","second-order"],"definition":"Someone has a desire of the second order either when he wants simply to have a certain desire or when he wants a certain desire to be his will. In situations of the latter kind, I shall call his second-order desires “second-order volitions” or “volitions of the second order.”","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"wanton","aliases":["wantons"],"definition":"I shall use the term ‘wanton’ to refer to agents who have first-order desires but who are not persons because … they have no second-order volitions. … The essential characteristic of a wanton is that he does not care about his will.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"will","definition":"An agent's will, then, is identical with one or more of his first-order desires. … it is the notion of an effective desire—one that moves … a person all the way to action.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"freedom of the will","definition":"the statement that a person enjoys freedom of the will means … that he is free to want what he wants to want.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose that a physician engaged in psychotherapy with narcotics addicts believes that his ability to help his patients would be enhanced if he understood better what it is like for them to desire the drug to which they are addicted. Suppose that he is led in this way to want to have a desire for the drug. … although he wants to be moved by a desire to take the drug, he does not want this desire to be effective.","phenomenon":"mere second-order want (not a volition)","near":["second-order volition","first-order desire","decisive identification"],"avoid":["second-order desire","higher-order desire","state-given reason for an attitude"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"One of the addicts hates his addiction and always struggles desperately, although to no avail, against its thrust. He tries everything that he thinks might enable him to overcome his desires for the drug. But these desires are too powerful for him to withstand, and invariably, in the end, they conquer him. He is an unwilling addict, helplessly violated by his own desires.","phenomenon":"will at odds with one's second-order volition","near":["wanton agency","volitional ambivalence","moral responsibility without freedom of the will"],"avoid":["weakness of will (akrasia)","psychological compulsion","loss of self-control","lack of freedom of the will","alienation from one's own motives","disappearing agent (no causal deviance)","unwilling addiction"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"[The addict's] actions reflect the economy of his first-order desires, without his being concerned whether the desires that move him to act are desires by which he wants to be moved to act. If he encounters problems in obtaining the drug … his responses to his urges to take it may involve deliberation. But it never occurs to him to consider whether he wants the relations among his desires to result in his having the will he has.","phenomenon":"wanton agency","near":["will at odds with one's second-order volition","weakness of will (akrasia)","volitional ambivalence"],"avoid":["psychological compulsion","lack of freedom of the will","disappearing agent (no causal deviance)","instrumental (means-end) reasoning"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Suppose that his addiction has the same physiological basis and the same irresistible thrust as the addictions of the unwilling and wanton addicts, but that he is altogether delighted with his condition. He is a willing addict, who would not have things any other way. If the grip of his addiction should somehow weaken, he would do whatever he could to reinstate it.","phenomenon":"moral responsibility without freedom of the will","near":["excusing compulsion","will at odds with one's second-order volition","wanton agency"],"avoid":["psychological compulsion","Frankfurt-style case","Frankfurt-style case (counterfactual intervener)","overdetermination","causal overdetermination"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"Consider a person who, without reservation or conflict, wants to be motivated by the desire to concentrate on his work. … Suppose the person is asked whether he wants to want to want to concentrate on his work. He can properly insist that this question concerning a third-order desire does not arise.","phenomenon":"decisive identification","aliases":["decisive"],"near":["volitional ambivalence","infinite regress of higher-order desires","wanton agency"],"avoid":["second-order volition"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2024717"},{"id":"davidson1970-weakness-will-possible","title":"How Is Weakness of the Will Possible?","authors":["Donald Davidson"],"year":"1970","area":"action","topic":"action & intention","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"a judgement that a is better than b, all things considered, is a relational, or [prima facie], judgement, and so cannot conflict logically with any unconditional judgement.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"What is special in incontinence is that the actor cannot understand himself: he recognizes, in his own intentional behaviour, something essentially surd.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Moral principles relate a judgement to its ground—'lying is prima facie wrong' is pf(Wx, Lx), not ∀x(Lx → Wx)—so, as with probabilistic support, no unconditional verdict detaches and conflicting principles yield no contradiction.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Even if agents do what they most want and most want what they judge better, akratic action is possible; its fault is irrationality—violating the principle of continence—since the agent has no reason to act against his best judgement.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"incontinence","aliases":["incontinently","incontinent"],"definition":"In doing x an agent acts incontinently if and only if: (a) the agent does x intentionally; (b) the agent believes there is an alternative action y open to him; and (c) the agent judges that, all things considered, it would be better to do y than to do x.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"principle of continence","aliases":["principle"],"definition":"[A] principle the rational man will accept in applying practical reasoning: perform the action judged best on the basis of all available relevant reasons.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"prima facie","definition":"The concept of the prima facie, as it is needed in moral philosophy, relates propositions. In logical grammar, 'prima facie' is not an operator on single sentences, much less on predicates of actions, but on pairs of sentences related as (expressing) moral judgement and ground.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"I have just relaxed in bed after a hard day when it occurs to me that I have not brushed my teeth. Concern for my health bids me rise and brush; sensual indulgence suggests I forget my teeth for once. … Everything considered I judge I would do better to stay in bed. Yet my feeling that I ought to brush my teeth is too strong for me: wearily I leave my bed and brush my teeth.","phenomenon":"weakness of will (akrasia)","near":["succumbing to temptation","psychological compulsion","self-deception"],"avoid":["conflict of pro tanto reasons","reasoning theoretically but not practically","will at odds with one's second-order volition"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"A man might hold it to be wrong, everything considered, for him to send a valentine to Marjorie Morningstar. Yet he might send a valentine to Marjorie Eveningstar, and do it intentionally, not knowing that Marjorie Eveningstar was identical with Marjorie Morningstar.","phenomenon":"referential opacity","near":["weakness of will (akrasia)","deviant causal chain","self-deception"],"avoid":["referential opacity (substitution failure)","substitution failure in belief contexts","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","Frege's puzzle about co-referring names","Frege's puzzle about identity statements","de re / de dicto ambiguity","intensional (non-extensional) adjective","one action under different descriptions","intensionality of rationalizations","referential opacity (action under a description)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"I am very partial to ice cream, and a bombe is served divided into segments corresponding one to one with persons at High Table: I am tempted to help myself to two segments and do … But do I lose control of myself? Do I raven, do I snatch the morsels from the dish and wolf them down, impervious to the consternation of my colleagues? Not a bit of it.","phenomenon":"calm succumbing to temptation","aliases":["tempted"],"near":["loss of self-control","psychological compulsion","self-deception"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"we cannot reason from: (M4) If the barometer falls, it almost certainly will rain / (m4) The barometer is falling / to the conclusion: (C4) It almost certainly will rain / since we may at the same time be equally justified in arguing: (M5) Red skies at night, it almost certainly won't rain / (m5) The sky is red tonight / (C5) ∴ It almost certainly won't rain","phenomenon":"Hempel's statistical ambiguity","near":["lottery paradox","base-rate fallacy","paradox of the ravens"],"avoid":["reference class problem"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"vaninwagen1975-incompatibility-free-will","title":"The Incompatibility of Free Will and Determinism","authors":["Peter van Inwagen"],"year":"1975","area":"action","topic":"free will & responsibility","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"if anyone can (i.e., has it within his power to) render some proposition false, then that proposition is not a law of physics. This I regard as a conceptual truth","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"If Q is a true proposition that concerns only states of affairs that obtained before S's birth, and if S can render [Q∧R] false, then S can render R false.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"If the laws plus the world's state at any one instant entail its state at every instant, no one can act otherwise; if the conditional analysis of 'can' falsifies a premise of the argument for this, the analysis is refuted.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Under determinism, P₀ ∧ L ⊨ P (P₀ the pre-birth world-state, L the laws, P the state at T). Being able to act otherwise at T requires being able to render P, hence P₀ ∧ L, hence L false: impossible.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"determinism","definition":"(a) For every instant of time, there is a proposition that expresses the state of the world at that instant. (b) If A and B are any propositions that express the state of the world at some instants, then the conjunction of A with the laws of physics entails B.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"law of physics","definition":"Let us, then, understand by 'law of physics' a law of nature that is not about the voluntary behavior of rational agents.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"render false","aliases":["render","rendered","rendering","false"],"definition":"An idiom for ascribing abilities: S can render P false just in case S can produce some condition sufficient for P's falsity; so 'He could have reached Chicago by midnight' becomes 'He could have rendered the proposition that he did not reach Chicago by midnight false'.","exact":false},{"id":"t4","term":"conditional analysis","aliases":["conditionalists","conditionalist"],"definition":"what statements of the form: (8) S could have done X mean is: (9) If S had chosen to do X, S would have done X.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"a judge who had only to raise his right hand at a certain time, T, to prevent the execution of a sentence of death … the judge - call him 'J' - refrained from raising his hand … 'L' to denote the conjunction into a single proposition of all laws of physics … (1) If determinism is true, then the conjunction of P₀ and L entails P. … (6) J could not have rendered L false. (7) If determinism is true, J could not have raised his hand at T.","phenomenon":"consequence argument","near":["Frankfurt-style case (counterfactual intervener)","manipulation argument","luck objection to libertarianism"],"avoid":["incompatibilism","ability to do otherwise","fixity of the laws","fixity of the past"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Consider, for example, the propositions expressed by 'The Spanish Armada was defeated in 1588' and 'Peter van Inwagen never visits Alaska'. The conjunction of these two propositions is quite possibly true. … Given that it is true, it seems quite clear that I can render it false if and only if I can visit Alaska. If, for some reason, it is not within my power ever to visit Alaska, then I cannot render it false.","phenomenon":"fixity of the past","near":["fixity of the laws","logical fatalism","backward causation"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"(A) Nothing ever travels faster than light. (B) Jones, a physicist, can construct a particle accelerator that would cause protons to travel at twice the speed of light. … whatever the reason for Jones's failure to act on his ability to render (A) false, it is clear that (A) and (B) are consistent, and that (B) entails that (A) is not a law of physics.","phenomenon":"fixity of the laws","near":["fixity of the past","local miracle compatibilism","ceteris paribus law"],"avoid":["accidental generalization","natural (causal) impossibility"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"There would probably be fairly general agreement that a proposition cannot be a law of nature unless it is true and contingent, and that no proposition is a law of nature if it entails the existence of some concrete individual, such as Caesar or the earth. But the proposition that there is no solid gold sphere 20 feet in diameter (probably) satisfies these conditions, though it is certainly not a law of nature.","phenomenon":"accidental generalization","near":["ceteris paribus law","fixity of the laws","necessary a posteriori truth"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"Suppose … that we see someone's arm rise and it later turns out that there was no cause whatsoever for his arm's rising. Surely we should have to say that he did not really raise his arm at all. Rather, his arm's rising was a mere chance happening, that, like a muscular twitch, had nothing to do with him, beyond the fact that it happened to involve a part of his body.","phenomenon":"luck objection to libertarianism","near":["consequence argument","manipulation argument","deviant causal chain"],"avoid":["disappearing agent (no causal deviance)","Mind argument"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf01624156"},{"id":"strawson1962-freedom-resentment","title":"Freedom and Resentment","authors":["P. F. Strawson"],"year":"1962","area":"action","topic":"free will & responsibility","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"we cannot, as we are, seriously envisage ourselves adopting a thoroughgoing objectivity of attitude to others as a result of theoretical conviction of the truth of determinism","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the general framework of attitudes itself is something we are given with the fact of human society. As a whole, it neither calls for, nor permits, an external 'rational' justification.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Moral responsibility is grounded not in libertarian metaphysics but in reactive attitudes such as gratitude, indignation and guilt: a natural framework of human life that the truth of determinism, even if accepted, could neither dislodge nor rationally undermine.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Pleas like 'he didn't mean to' deny ill will, leaving the agent a full participant; pleas like 'he's only a child' invite an objective stance. Neither works by judging behaviour determined, so determinism licenses no universal objectivity.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"participant reactive attitudes","aliases":["reactive attitudes","reactive attitude","participant reactive attitude","personal reactive attitudes","reactions","attitudes"],"definition":"essentially natural human reactions to the good or ill will or indifference of others towards us, as displayed in their attitudes and actions","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"objective attitude","aliases":["objective attitudes","objective","objectivity of attitude","objectivity","object"],"definition":"To adopt the objective attitude to another human being is to see him, perhaps, as an object of social policy; as a subject for what, in a wide range of sense, might be called treatment; … to be managed or handled or cured or trained","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"moral indignation","aliases":["moral","indignation","indignant","morally indignant"],"definition":"resentment on behalf of another, where one's own interest and dignity are not involved; and it is this impersonal or vicarious character of the attitude, added to its others, which entitle it to the qualification 'moral'.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"self-reactive attitudes","aliases":["self-reactive attitude","self-reactive","reactive attitudes"],"definition":"self-reactive attitudes associated with demands on oneself for others. And here we have to mention such phenomena as feeling bound or obliged (the 'sense of obligation'); feeling compunction; feeling guilty or remorseful or at least responsible; and the more complicated phenomenon of shame.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"pessimist","aliases":["pessimists","pessimism","pessimistic"],"definition":"hold that if [determinism] is true, then the concepts of moral obligation and responsibility really have no application, and the practices of punishing and blaming, of expressing moral condemnation and approval, are really unjustified.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"If someone treads on my hand accidentally, while trying to help me, the pain may be no less acute than if he treads on it in contemptuous disregard of my existence or with a malevolent wish to injure me. But I shall generally feel in the second case a kind and degree of resentment that I shall not feel in the first.","phenomenon":"quality of will","near":["moral luck","doctrine of double effect","exempting condition"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"situations in which one person is offended or injured by the action of another … such expressions as 'He didn't mean to', 'He hadn't realized', 'He didn't know'; and also all those which might give occasion for the use of the phrase 'He couldn't help it', when this is supported by such phrases as 'He was pushed', 'He had to do it', 'It was the only way', 'They left him no alternative'","phenomenon":"excusing condition","near":["exempting condition","Frankfurt-style case (counterfactual intervener)","manipulation case (implanted values)"],"avoid":["blameless (excusable) norm violation"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"'He's only a child', 'He's a hopeless schizophrenic', 'His mind has been systematically perverted', 'That's purely compulsive behaviour on his part'. … The second and more important subgroup of cases allows that the circumstances were normal, but presents the agent as psychologically abnormal—or as morally undeveloped. The agent was himself; but he is warped or deranged, neurotic or just a child.","phenomenon":"exempting condition","near":["excusing condition","Frankfurt-style case (counterfactual intervener)","weakness of will (akrasia)"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"velleman1992-happens-when-someone","title":"What Happens When Someone Acts?","authors":["J. David Velleman"],"year":"1992","area":"action","topic":"action & intention","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the desire to act in accordance with reasons can perform the functions that are attributed to its subject in his capacity as agent","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"In [the standard] story, reasons cause an intention, and an intention causes bodily movements, but nobody—that is, no person—does anything.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"The standard belief–desire–intention causal story omits the agent: behavior can flow from motives in perfectly normal causal ways without the agent participating, so no account of causal normality can repair it.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Agent-causation can be reduced to event-causation: the agent's role is played by a motive functionally identical to him, the desire to act in accordance with reasons, which drives practical reflection and reinforces whichever motive offers the strongest reason.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"standard story","definition":"[The agent's] desire for the end, and his belief in the action as a means, justify taking the action, and they jointly cause an intention to take it, which in turn causes the corresponding movements of the agent's body.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"full-blooded action","aliases":["full-blooded human action","full-blooded"],"definition":"Our concept of full-blooded human action requires some event or state of affairs that owes its occurrence to an agent and hence has an explanation that traces back to him.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"functionally identical to the agent","aliases":["functionally identical"],"definition":"mental events and states that are functionally identical to the agent, in the sense that they play the causal role that ordinary parlance attributes to him","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"problem of agency","definition":"the problem of agency is that of finding an agent at work amid the workings of the mind","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose that I have a long-anticipated meeting with an old friend …; but that as we talk, his offhand comments provoke me to raise my voice in progressively sharper replies, until we part in anger. Later reflection leads me to realize that accumulated grievances had crystallized in my mind … into a resolution to sever our friendship over the matter at hand, and that this resolution is what gave the hurtful edge to my remarks. … it was my resentment speaking, not I.","phenomenon":"disappearing agent (no causal deviance)","near":["deviant causal chain","will at odds with one's second-order volition","Frankfurt-style case (counterfactual intervener)"],"avoid":["alienation from one's own motives"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"A frequently cited example is the assassin whose decision to fire on his target so unnerves him as to make his trigger-finger twitch, causing the gun to fire. In such a case, the agent's intention has caused corresponding movements of his body, but it has done so without the agent's participation.","phenomenon":"deviant causal chain","near":["consequential deviance","Frankfurt-style case (counterfactual intervener)","weakness of will (akrasia)"],"avoid":["wayward causal chain","deviant (wayward) causal chain","basic (primary) deviance"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1093/mind/101.403.461"},{"id":"lewis1968-counterpart-theory-quantified","title":"Counterpart Theory and Quantified Modal Logic","authors":["David Lewis"],"year":"1968","area":"metaphysics","topic":"modality & essence","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The counterpart relation is our substitute for identity between things in different worlds.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"… an essential attribute of something is an attribute it shares with all its counterparts.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Modal sentences translate into an extensional first-order theory of worlds (W), inhabitants (I) and counterparts (C): □φα becomes ∀β∀γ(Wβ ∧ Iγβ ∧ Cγα → φᵝγ), i.e. φ holds of every counterpart of α in every world.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Because counterparthood, a similarity relation, need not be transitive, symmetric, one-one or total, Becker's and Brouwer's principles (for open sentences), necessity of identity and the Barcan formula fail; of eight principles tested, only the converse Barcan formula holds.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"counterpart","aliases":["counterparts"],"definition":"Your counterparts resemble you closely in content and context in important respects. They resemble you more closely than do the other things in their worlds. But they are not really you.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"counterpart theory","definition":"We could stick to our standard logic … and provide it with predicates and a domain of quantification suited to the topic of modality. … The new predicates required, together with postulates on them, constitute the system I call Counterpart Theory.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"essence","aliases":["essential attributes","essential attribute","essential"],"definition":"The whole of [something's] essence is the intersection of its essential attributes, the attribute it shares with all and only its counterparts.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"relative modality","aliases":["relative modalities"],"definition":"Just as a sentence φ is necessary if it holds in all worlds, so φ is causally necessary if it holds in all worlds compatible with the laws of nature … These, and many more, are relative modalities, expressible by quantifications over restricted ranges of worlds.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose x1 in world w1 resembles you closely in many respects, far more closely than anything else in w1 does. And suppose x2 in world w2 resembles x1 closely, far more closely than anything else in w2 does. So x2 is a counterpart of your counterpart x1. Yet x2 might not resemble you very closely, and something else in w2 might resemble you more closely. If so, x2 is not your counterpart.","phenomenon":"non-transitivity of counterparthood","near":["non-symmetry of counterparthood","multiple counterparts in a single world","transworld identity"],"avoid":["failure of transitivity (hypothetical syllogism)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose x3 in world w3 is a sort of blend of you and your brother; x3 resembles both of you closely, far more closely than anything else in w3 resembles either one of you. So x3 is your counterpart. But suppose also that the resemblance between x3 and your brother is far closer than that between x3 and you. If so, you are not a counterpart of x3.","phenomenon":"non-symmetry of counterparthood","near":["non-transitivity of counterparthood","multiple counterparts in a single world","transworld identity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Suppose x4a and x4b in world w4 are twins; both resemble you closely; both resemble you far more closely than anything else in w4 does; both resemble you equally. If so, both are your counterparts.","phenomenon":"multiple counterparts in a single world","aliases":["counterparts","world"],"near":["non-transitivity of counterparthood","non-symmetry of counterparthood","transworld identity"],"avoid":["fission case"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Suppose there is something x5 in world w5—say, Batman—which does not much resemble anything actual. If so, x5 is not a counterpart of anything in the actual world.","phenomenon":"merely possible individual","near":["impossible object","actual individual lacking counterparts elsewhere","transworld individual"],"avoid":["failure of the Barcan formula (expanding domains)","varying domains (contingent existence)"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2024555"},{"id":"yablo1992-mental-causation","title":"Mental Causation","authors":["Stephen Yablo"],"year":"1992","area":"metaphysics","topic":"causation","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"With events as with properties, physical determinates cannot defeat the causal pretensions of their mental determinables.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Although determinables and determinates do not compete for causal influence … they do compete for the role of cause, with the more commensurate candidate prevailing.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Supervenience plus multiple realizability entail □∀x(Mx ↔ ∃P(Px ∧ □∀y(Py → My) ∧ ◇∃y(My ∧ ¬Py))), M mental and P physical: the determinable–determinate equation, suggesting that mental properties are determinables of physical determinates, which cannot causally preempt them.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Causes should be proportional to their effects, not overladen with detail the effect doesn't require; since a doorbell's ringing would survive a different neural realization of the decision to ring, the decision, not its physical realizer, causes it.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"determination","aliases":["determines"],"definition":"P determines Q iff: for a thing to be P is for it to be Q, not simpliciter, but in a specific way. … Metaphysically … P determines Q … only if: (i) necessarily, for all x, if x has P then x has Q; and (ii) possibly, for some x, x has Q but lacks P.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"proportionality","aliases":["proportional"],"definition":"When all of the conditions are met—that is, y is contingent on x, and requires it, and x is adequate, and enough, for y—x will be called proportional to y.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"exclusion principle","aliases":["exclusion"],"definition":"If an event x is causally sufficient for an event y, then no event x* distinct from x is causally relevant to y (exclusion).","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"dualism","aliases":["dualist"],"definition":"all I mean by the term is that mental and physical phenomena are, contrary to the identity theory, distinct, and contrary to eliminativism, existents.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Imagine a pigeon, Sophie, conditioned to peck at red to the exclusion of other colors; a red triangle is presented, and Sophie pecks. Most people would say that the redness was causally relevant to her pecking, even that this was a paradigm case of causal relevance. But wait! I forgot to mention that the triangle in question was a specific shade of red: scarlet.","phenomenon":"proportionality of cause to effect","near":["causal overdetermination","trumping preemption","epiphenomenalism"],"avoid":["causal exclusion problem"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"If p is the bolt's suddenly snapping, for example, and q is its snapping per se, then for p to occur is for q to occur in a certain way, namely suddenly; and my slamming the door consists in my shutting it, not simpliciter, but with significant force.","phenomenon":"determinable–determinate relation between events","near":["type–token relation between events","causal relation between events","mereological composition of events"],"avoid":["one action under different descriptions","adverb-dropping entailment (modifier drop)","material constitution (coincident objects)","metaphysical priority (grounding)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Suppose we stipulate that it contributed nothing to Socrates' demise that he guzzled the hemlock rather than simply drinking it. Then Xanthippe is mistaken when, disgusted at Socrates' sloppy habits, she complains that his guzzling the hemlock caused his death. Assuming that the drinking would still have occurred, if the guzzling hadn't, … Even without the guzzling, the death would still have followed on the drinking.","phenomenon":"failure of contingency (overly determinate candidate)","near":["failure of adequacy (insufficiently determinate candidate)","failure of enoughness","causal preemption"],"avoid":["failure of requirement","one action under different descriptions"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Safety valves are designed to open quickly under extreme pressure … This particular valve has begun to operate as advertised when a freak molecular misalignment stiffens the mechanism; this decelerates the opening to just past the point of endurance and the boiler explodes. … we can arrange it so that the explosion was contingent on the opening … given the unlikelihood of the molecular mishap, had the … opening failed to occur, it might easily have been quicker if it had.","phenomenon":"failure of adequacy (insufficiently determinate candidate)","near":["failure of contingency (overly determinate candidate)","causal preemption","causal overdetermination"],"avoid":["failure of enoughness"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2185535"},{"id":"schaffer2010-monism-priority-whole","title":"Monism: The Priority of the Whole","authors":["Jonathan Schaffer"],"year":"2010","area":"metaphysics","topic":"ontology & metaontology","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the world has parts, but the parts are dependent fragments of an integrated whole.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I will be defending the thesis that the cosmos is the one and only fundamental actual concrete object, prior to all of its proper parts.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"With P parthood, D dependence, u the cosmos, and Bx ≝ Pxu ∧ ¬∃y(Pyu ∧ Dxy): (∃!x)Bx ∧ Bu. If dependence is a well-founded partial order, equivalently ∀x((Pxu ∧ x≠u) → Dxu): every proper part depends on the cosmos.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Only the cosmos is fundamental: it is one entangled quantum system, and entangled systems are irreducible wholes; and since atomless gunk is possible but a world without a maximal whole is not, the whole alone is guaranteed as ground.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"tiling constraint","aliases":["tiling"],"definition":"the basic actual concrete objects collectively cover the cosmos without overlapping. In a slogan: no gaps, no overlaps.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"priority monism","aliases":["Priority Monism","Monism","monism","priority monist","monist"],"definition":"Monism can thus be thought of as the conjunction of the numerical thesis that there is exactly one basic object with the holistic thesis that the cosmos is basic.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"existence monism","aliases":["Existence Monism","existence monist","monism"],"definition":"Existence Monism is the doctrine that there is one and only one actual concrete object.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"asymmetry of supervenience","aliases":["supervenience","supervene","asymmetry"],"definition":"the proper parts must supervene on their whole … but the whole need not supervene on its proper parts. In other words, though emergence is metaphysically possible, submergence—the converse of emergence—is metaphysically impossible.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"worldless junk","aliases":["junk","junky","worldless"],"definition":"though atomless gunk is metaphysically possible, worldless junk—the converse of gunk, in which everything is a proper part of something—is metaphysically impossible.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"metaphysical foundationalism","aliases":["metaphysical foundationalism","metaphysical foundationalist","foundationalism","foundationalist"],"definition":"The assumption of a well-founded partial ordering may be understood as a kind of metaphysical foundationalism … the metaphysical foundationalist thinks all being must originate in basic being and rejects limitless chains of dependence … and circular dependence","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e2","text":"Pascal's hypothesis is conceivable, on which there is an endless nested sequence of microcosms, in which every physical \"atom\" of the universe houses a miniature replica universe, every \"atom\" of this miniuniverse houses its own miniuniverse, and so on without limit.","phenomenon":"atomless gunk","aliases":["gunk","gunky","atomless"],"near":["worldless junk","extended simple","mereological nihilism"],"avoid":["infinite divisibility","infinite regress"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"A given whole might, for instance, have the property of being polka-dotted. There would be no question of the whole being \"different from itself\" or having any other problematic status. The claim that the whole is polka-dotted is a coherent claim, which would entail heterogeneity among its derivative dots and background.","phenomenon":"distributional property","aliases":["distributional"],"near":["regionalized property","regionalized instantiation","extrinsic property"],"avoid":["heterogeneity","qualitative heterogeneity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Consider Socrates. Given that he exists, the proposition <Socrates exists> must be true. And conversely, given that the proposition <Socrates exists> is true, there must be Socrates. Yet clearly there is an asymmetry. The proposition is true because the man exists and not vice versa. … Further, given that Socrates exists, his singleton {Socrates} must exist. And conversely, given that {Socrates} exists, there must be Socrates. Yet … there is an asymmetry. {Socrates} exists in virtue of Socrates and not vice versa.","phenomenon":"metaphysical priority (grounding)","aliases":["priority","prior","grounding","grounded"],"near":["causal dependence","numerical identity","mereological composition"],"avoid":["grounding","ontological dependence","metaphysical dependence","asymmetric dependence","truthmaking","supervenience","groundedness"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1215/00318108-2009-025"},{"id":"sider1997-four-dimensionalism","title":"Four-Dimensionalism","authors":["Theodore Sider"],"year":"1997","area":"metaphysics","topic":"persistence & composition","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I say that all assignments have minimal D-fusions, because of an argument parallel to the argument for unrestricted composition.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"anyone who accepts that logic is non-vague must also accept the principle of unrestricted composition. … everyone who shares this assumption about vagueness must also accept four dimensionalism.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Every assignment f has a minimal D-fusion y: a fusion-at-t of f(t) for every t ∈ dom(f), existing only at those times. With f(t) = {x} on T1, y is an extended temporal part of x throughout T1.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Every object has a temporal part at each moment it exists, because diachronic composition is unrestricted: any restriction would be either vague—making the number of concrete objects indeterminate, impossible if logic is precise—or an implausibly sharp cutoff.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"Thesis of Temporal Locality","aliases":["Temporal Locality","temporal"],"definition":"Applied to time, the idea is that to any way of dividing up the lifetime of an object into separate intervals of time, there is a corresponding way of dividing the object into temporal parts that are confined to those intervals of time.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"instantaneous temporal part","aliases":["instant"],"definition":"x is an instantaneous temporal part of y at instant t =df i) x exists at, but only at t, ii) x is part of y at t, and iii) x overlaps at t everything that is part of y at t","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"wholly present","definition":"x is wholly present at t =df everything that is at any time part of x exists and is part of x at t","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"minimal D-fusion","definition":"In an intuitive sense, a minimal D-fusion of some objects at various times consists of those objects at those times, and nothing more.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"count indeterminacy","aliases":["indeterminacy"],"definition":"Count indeterminacy is indeterminacy in how many objects there are, not merely in how many of the objects there are that exist at some specified time.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose we make a model of the Π-shaped part of Stonehenge out of three toy blocks, b1, b2, and b3, by placing one on top of two of the others at time t1; suppose a few minutes later at t2 we separate the blocks. Is there something that we brought into existence at the first time and destroyed at the second?","phenomenon":"diachronic composition question","near":["problem of the many","Ship of Theseus puzzle","problem of temporary intrinsics"],"avoid":["special composition question","material constitution (coincident objects)","fusion case"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose I undergo amnesia in such a way that we feel indeterminacy in whether “Young Man Ted is identical to Old Man Sider” is true.","phenomenon":"indeterminate identity over time","aliases":["indeterminacy","identical"],"near":["fission case","problem of temporary intrinsics","Ship of Theseus puzzle"],"avoid":["psychological continuity without connectedness","successive selves","indeterminacy of reference","ontic vagueness"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"the end of my fingernail is part of me today, but is not part of me tomorrow if I clip it off; a certain plank may be part of the ship of Theseus at one time but not another","phenomenon":"temporary parthood","near":["atemporal parthood","mereological essentialism","material constitution (coincident objects)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"imagine a lump of clay that gets made into a statue-shape for only an instant (by a god, say). It seems to me that some three dimensionalists might want to say that in that instant, a statue comes into being, but immediately goes out of existence.","phenomenon":"material constitution (coincident objects)","near":["mereological essentialism","fission case","intermittent existence"],"avoid":["diachronic composition question"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"we want to accept some objects with discontinuous lives (e.g. Hirsch’s example of a watch that is taken apart for repairs and then reassembled)","phenomenon":"intermittent existence","aliases":["discontinuous lives","discontinuous"],"near":["fission case","material constitution (coincident objects)","problem of temporary intrinsics"],"avoid":["diachronic composition question"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2998357"},{"id":"parfit1971-personal-identity","title":"Personal Identity","authors":["Derek Parfit"],"year":"1971","area":"metaphysics","topic":"personal identity","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The relation of [a person who divides] to each of the resulting people contains all that interests us—all that matters—in any ordinary case of survival.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I have tried to show that what matters in the continued existence of a person are, for the most part, relations of degree.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"When a person divides into two psychologically continuous successors, each relation is as good as survival though identity, being one-one, cannot hold to both; so identity is not what matters in survival.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Survival is a matter of degree: psychological connectedness (direct memory, intention and character links), unlike transitive continuity (overlapping chains), is non-transitive and comes in degrees, licensing talk of past and future selves and stripping self-interest of special authority.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"q-memory","aliases":["q-remembering","q-remember","q-memories","q-remembered"],"definition":"I am q-remembering an experience if (1) I have a belief about a past experience which seems in itself like a memory belief, (2) someone did have such an experience, and (3) my belief is dependent upon this experience in the same way (whatever that is) in which a memory of an experience is dependent upon it.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"psychological connectedness","aliases":["connectedness","Psychological connectedness","psychological"],"definition":"Let us say that the relation between a q-memory and the experience q-remembered is a \"direct\" relation. Another \"direct\" relation is that which holds between a q-intention and the q-intended action. … \"Psychological connectedness,\" as I define it, requires the holding of these direct psychological relations. \"Connectedness\" is not transitive, since these relations are not transitive.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"psychological continuity","aliases":["continuity","Psychological continuity","psychological"],"definition":"\"Psychological continuity,\" in contrast, only requires overlapping chains of direct psychological relations. So \"continuity\" is transitive.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"descendant self","aliases":["ancestral self","descendant self"],"definition":"A can think of any individual, anywhere in his \"tree,\" as \"a descendant self.\" This phrase implies psychological continuity. Similarly, any later individual can think of any earlier … individual on the single path which connects him to A as \"an ancestral self.\"","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"My brain is divided, and each half is housed in a new body. Both resulting people have my character and apparent memories of my life. What happens to me? There seem only three possibilities: (1) I do not survive; (2) I survive as one of the two people; (3) I survive as both.","phenomenon":"fission case","aliases":["fission"],"near":["fusion case","teletransportation case","Ship of Theseus puzzle"],"avoid":["indeterminate identity over time","multiple counterparts in a single world"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose that the bridge between my hemispheres is brought under my voluntary control. This would enable me to disconnect my hemispheres as easily as if I were blinking. … I am near the end of a maths exam, and see two ways of tackling the last problem. I decide to divide my mind, to work, with each half, at one of two calculations, and then to reunite my mind and write a fair copy of the best result.","phenomenon":"divided mind (split consciousness)","aliases":["divide my mind","divide","divided"],"near":["fission case","multiple personality (dissociative identity)","teletransportation case"],"avoid":["compartmentalized (fragmented) thinking"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Two people come together. While they are unconscious, their two bodies grow into one. One person then wakes up. … I like Palladio and intend to visit Venice. I am about to fuse with a person who likes Giotto and intends to visit Padua. I can know that the one person we shall become will have both tastes and both intentions. … I hate red hair, and always vote Labour. The other person loves red hair, and always votes Conservative.","phenomenon":"fusion case","aliases":["fusion","fuse"],"near":["fission case","teletransportation case","body swap (brain transplant)"],"avoid":["indeterminate identity over time"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"These beings are just like ourselves except that they reproduce by a process of natural division. … A has q-intentional control over B + 2, B + 2 has q-intentional control over B + 6, and so on up to B + 30. … A is as close psychologically to B + 1 as I today am to myself tomorrow. A is as distant from B + 30 as I am from my great-great-grandson.","phenomenon":"psychological continuity without connectedness","aliases":["continuous","connected","psychological","psychologically"],"near":["fusion case","divided mind (split consciousness)","Ship of Theseus puzzle"],"avoid":["indeterminate identity over time"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"we are incapable, while we are in love, of acting as fit predecessors of the next persons who, when we are in love no longer, we shall presently have become","phenomenon":"successive selves","near":["fission case","fusion case","multiple personality (dissociative identity)"],"avoid":["indeterminate identity over time"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2184309"},{"id":"quine1948-on-what-there","title":"On What There Is","authors":["W. V. O. Quine"],"year":"1948","area":"metaphysics","topic":"ontology & metaontology","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"To be assumed as an entity is, purely and simply, to be reckoned as the value of a variable.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Our acceptance of an ontology is, I think, similar in principle to our acceptance of a scientific theory, say a system of physics","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A theory is committed to Fs iff its affirmations can be true only if some values of its bound variables are Fs; so '∃x(Prime(x) ∧ x > 1,000,000)' commits one to numbers, while names alone commit to nothing.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Denying that Pegasus is does not presuppose that Pegasus somehow is: any name can be recast as a description ('the thing that pegasizes') and analysed away Russell-style, so a singular term need not name anything to be meaningful.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"ontological commitment","aliases":["committed","commitment","commitments","commit","commits"],"definition":"a theory is committed to those and only those entities to which the bound variables of the theory must be capable of referring in order that the affirmations made in the theory be true.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"Plato's beard","definition":"Nonbeing must in some sense be, otherwise what is it that there is not? This tangled doctrine might be nicknamed Plato's beard; historically it has proved tough, frequently dulling the edge of Occam's razor.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"pegasizes","aliases":["is-Pegasus","Pegasus","pegasize","pegasizing"],"definition":"we could have appealed to the ex hypothesi unanalyzable, irreducible attribute of being Pegasus, adopting, for its expression, the verb 'is-Pegasus', or 'pegasizes'. The noun 'Pegasus' itself could then be treated as derivative, and identified after all with a description: 'the thing that is-Pegasus'","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"convenient myth","aliases":["myth","myths"],"definition":"from a phenomenalistic point of view, the conceptual scheme of physical objects is a convenient myth, simpler than the literal truth and yet containing that literal truth as a scattered part.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"If Pegasus were not, McX argues, we should not be talking about anything when we use the word; therefore it would be nonsense to say even that Pegasus is not. Thinking to show thus that the denial of Pegasus cannot be coherently maintained, he concludes that Pegasus is.","phenomenon":"problem of negative existentials","near":["Frege's puzzle about informative identity","problem of universals","paradox of analysis"],"avoid":["negative existential statements","empty name","reference failure","presupposition failure","truth-value gap from reference failure"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"The phrase 'Evening Star' names a certain large physical object of spherical form, which is hurtling through space some scores of millions of miles from here. The phrase 'Morning Star' names the same thing, as was probably first established by some observant Babylonian. But the two phrases cannot be regarded as having the same meaning; otherwise that Babylonian could have dispensed with his observations and contented himself with reflecting on the meanings of his words.","phenomenon":"Frege's puzzle about informative identity","near":["Kripke's puzzle about belief","analytic truth","empty name"],"avoid":["Frege's puzzle about identity statements","Frege's puzzle about co-referring names","necessary a posteriori truth","coextensive but non-synonymous predicates","referential opacity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Take, for instance, the possible fat man in that doorway; and, again, the possible bald man in that doorway. Are they the same possible man, or two possible men? How do we decide? How many possible men are there in that doorway? Are there more possible thin ones than fat ones? How many of them are alike? Or would their being alike make them one?","phenomenon":"individuation problem for mere possibilia","near":["problem of the many","impossible object","Ship of Theseus puzzle"],"avoid":["merely possible individual","transworld identity problem","identity of indiscernibles"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"There are red houses, red roses, red sunsets; this much is prephilosophical common sense in which we must all agree. These houses, roses, and sunsets, then, have something in common; and this which they have in common is all I mean by the attribute of redness.","phenomenon":"one-over-many argument for universals","near":["third man argument","indispensability argument","resemblance nominalism"],"avoid":["problem of universals","platonism"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"tarski1944-semantic-conception-truth","title":"The Semantic Conception of Truth and the Foundations of Semantics","authors":["Alfred Tarski"],"year":"1944","area":"logic","topic":"truth & paradox","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"X is true if, and only if, p. … we shall call a definition of truth \"adequate\" if all these equivalences follow from it.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the condition of the \"essential richness\" of the meta-language proves to be, not only necessary, but also sufficient for the construction of a satisfactory definition of truth","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Truth is defined via satisfaction, specified recursively (e.g. s satisfies φ ∨ ψ iff s satisfies φ or s satisfies ψ); a sentence, having no free variables, is true iff every sequence s satisfies it.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"The Liar shows that a language with ordinary logic, names of its own sentences, and a truth predicate for them obeying every T-biconditional is inconsistent; so truth must be defined in a distinct metalanguage, yielding a hierarchy of languages.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"material adequacy","aliases":["adequate","materially adequate"],"definition":"we wish to use the term \"true\" in such a way that all equivalences of the form (T) can be asserted, and we shall call a definition of truth \"adequate\" if all these equivalences follow from it.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"semantically closed","definition":"[a language that] contains, in addition to its expressions, also the names of these expressions, as well as semantic terms such as the term \"true\" referring to sentences of this language; … all sentences which determine the adequate usage of this term can be asserted in the language.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"meta-language","aliases":["metalanguage"],"definition":"the language in which we \"talk about\" the [object-language], and in terms of which we wish, in particular, to construct the definition of truth for the [object-language].","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"essential richness","aliases":["essentially richer","essentially rich"],"definition":"If we restrict ourselves to languages based on the logical theory of types, the condition for the meta-language to be \"essentially richer\" than the object-language is that it contain variables of a higher logical type than those of the object-language.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"satisfaction","aliases":["satisfy","satisfies","satisfied"],"definition":"a relation between arbitrary objects and certain expressions called \"sentential functions.\" … We indicate which objects satisfy the simplest sentential functions; and then we state the conditions under which given objects satisfy a compound function … a sentence is true if it is satisfied by all objects","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"consider the following sentence: The sentence printed in this paper on p. 347, l. 31, is not true. For brevity we shall replace the sentence just stated by the letter 's.' … we establish empirically the following fact: (2) 's' is identical with the sentence printed in this paper on p. 347, l. 31. … (3) 's' is true if, and only if, 's' is not true.","phenomenon":"Liar paradox","near":["truth-teller sentence","Grelling's heterological paradox","Curry's paradox"],"avoid":["contingent Liar paradox"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"our present historical knowledge does not give us any possibility of eliminating the word \"true\" from the following sentence: The first sentence written by Plato is true.","phenomenon":"blind truth ascription","near":["Tarskian T-sentence","truth-teller sentence","redundant (eliminable) use of 'true'"],"avoid":["statements seeking their own levels","groundedness","attributive use of a definite description"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Consider the sentence \"snow is white.\" We ask the question under what conditions this sentence is true or false. … if the definition of truth is to conform to our conception, it must imply the following equivalence: The sentence \"snow is white\" is true if, and only if, snow is white.","phenomenon":"Tarskian T-sentence","near":["Liar paradox","truth-teller sentence","blind truth ascription"],"avoid":["Convention T","disquotational schema","proper name theory of quotation","instance of the T-schema"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2102968"},{"id":"fantl2002-evidence-pragmatics-justification","title":"Evidence, Pragmatics, and Justification","authors":["Jeremy Fantl","Matthew McGrath"],"year":"2002","area":"epistemology","topic":"justification & evidence","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"(PC) S is justified in believing that p only if S is rational to prefer as if p.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"We do not merely refute evidentialism. We show that, why, and how a subject's pragmatic situation may affect her justification.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Justified(S, p) → ∀A ∀B [S is rational to prefer A∧p to B∧p ↔ S is rational to prefer A to B]: one's rational preferences conditional on p must coincide with one's actual rational preferences.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"A condition linking justification to rational preference, derived from the closure of knowledge rather than from case intuitions, refutes evidentialism: keeping justification fixed by evidence would demand evidence adequate at any stakes, disqualifying ordinary testimonial and inductive justification.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"preferring as if p","aliases":["rational to prefer as if p","prefer as if p","preferring as if","prefer as if"],"definition":"for any states of affairs A and B, S is rational to prefer A to B, given p, iff S is rational to prefer A to B, in fact","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"evidentialism","aliases":["evidentialist"],"definition":"For any two subjects S and S', necessarily, if S and S' have the same evidence for/against p, then S is justified in believing that p iff S' is, too.","exact":true,"avoid":["feldman1985-evidentialism/t1"]},{"id":"t3","term":"EPC","definition":"S is justified in believing that p only if anyone with S's evidence for p, no matter what the stakes, would be rational to prefer as if p.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"justified in believing","aliases":["justified"],"definition":"We use 'S is justified in believing that p' throughout the paper, in a standard way, to mean that S has good enough evidence to know that p. … if S fails to know, it is not for S's lack of evidence.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"At a Boston station you hear a stranger casually say that the train makes all the little stops, including Foxboro. If it hardly matters to you, you seem to have good enough evidence to know that it stops there. But if your career depends on reaching Foxboro, the very same evidence seems insufficient: you think he might be wrong and that you had better check.","phenomenon":"pragmatic encroachment","near":["Gettier case","lottery paradox","moral encroachment"],"avoid":["knowledge without ruling out irrelevant alternatives","epistemic contextualism","contextual raising of epistemic standards","shifting standards for knowledge attributions","accommodation widening the range of relevant possibilities","subject factors in knowledge attributions"],"exact":false},{"id":"e2","text":"It would be extremely hard for you to go on if you believed your son was guilty of the crime of which he is accused. The belief would result in intense misery and pain, whether or not he is in fact guilty. You have good evidence that he is guilty.","phenomenon":"conflict between epistemic and prudential reasons for belief","near":["pragmatic encroachment","Pascal's wager","epistemic akrasia"],"avoid":["state-given reason for an attitude","conflict of pro tanto reasons","conflicting normative requirements"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1215/00318108-111-1-67"},{"id":"goldman1979-justified-belief","title":"What Is Justified Belief?","authors":["Alvin I. Goldman"],"year":"1979","area":"epistemology","topic":"justification & evidence","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The justificational status of a belief is a function of the reliability of the process or processes that cause it","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[Justificational status] is not only a function of the cognitive processes actually employed in producing [a belief]; it is also a function of processes that could and should be employed.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Base: belief from an unconditionally reliable belief-independent process, where no available reliable process would have prevented the belief → justified. Recursion: belief from a conditionally reliable belief-dependent process operating on justified beliefs → justified. Closure: nothing else is.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Since classical base clauses (indubitability, self-evidence, self-presentation, incorrigibility) ignore how a belief was caused, they wrongly certify beliefs produced by faulty processes; justification is instead historical, depending on causal ancestry, not wholly on the believer's current state.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t2","term":"conditional reliability","aliases":["conditionally reliable","conditional reliability"],"definition":"A process is conditionally reliable when a sufficient proportion of its output-beliefs are true given that its input-beliefs are true.","exact":false},{"id":"t3","term":"Historical Reliabilism","aliases":["Historical","Reliabilism","reliability"],"definition":"An Historical theory makes the justificational status of a belief depend on its prior history. Since my Historical theory emphasizes the reliability of the belief-generating processes, it may be called 'Historical Reliabilism'.","exact":false},{"id":"t4","term":"Current Time-Slice theory","aliases":["Current Time-Slice","Time-Slice"],"definition":"A Current Time-Slice theory makes the justificational status of a belief wholly a function of what is true of the cognizer at the time of belief.","exact":false},{"id":"t5","term":"ex ante justification","aliases":["ex ante","ex ante justified","ex ante justifiedness"],"definition":"S is ex ante justified in believing p at t just in case his total cognitive state at t is such that from that state he could come to believe p in such a way that this belief would be ex post justified.","exact":false}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Humperdink has been studying logic … from Elmer Fraud, whom Humperdink has no reason to trust as a logician. Fraud has enunciated the principle that any disjunctive proposition consisting of at least 40 distinct disjuncts is very probably true. Humperdink now encounters the proposition p, a contingent proposition with 40 disjuncts, the 7th disjunct being 'I exist'. … he doesn't notice that it is entailed by 'I exist'. … Bearing this rule in mind, Humperdink forms a belief in p.","phenomenon":"propositional justification without doxastic justification","aliases":["proposition"],"near":["Gettier case","new evil demon problem","epistemic akrasia"],"avoid":["reliable but unjustified belief","improperly based belief","reason acted on vs. reason merely had"],"exact":false},{"id":"e2","text":"We can imagine a possible world where a benevolent demon so arranges things that beliefs formed by wishful thinking usually come true. This would make wishful thinking a reliable process in that possible world, but surely we don't want to regard beliefs that result from wishful thinking as justified.","phenomenon":"reliable but unjustified belief","aliases":["reliable"],"near":["new evil demon problem","generality problem for reliabilism","Gettier case"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Suppose that Jones is told on fully reliable authority that a certain class of his memory beliefs are almost all mistaken. His parents fabricate a wholly false story that Jones suffered from amnesia when he was seven but later developed pseudo-memories of that period. Though Jones listens to what his parents say and has excellent reason to trust them, he persists in believing the ostensible memories from his seven-year-old past.","phenomenon":"undercutting (undermining) defeater","near":["Gettier case","new evil demon problem","problem of forgotten evidence"],"avoid":["rebutting defeater","defeat by misleading higher-order evidence","higher-order evidence","reliable but unjustified belief","dogmatism paradox","counterexample to the causal theory of knowledge"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"One plausible suggestion, though, is that the relevant processes are content-neutral. It might be argued, for example, that the process of inferring p whenever the Pope asserts p could pose problems for our theory. If the Pope is infallible, this process will be perfectly reliable; yet we would not regard the belief-outputs of this process as justified.","phenomenon":"generality problem for reliabilism","aliases":["problems"],"near":["new evil demon problem","swamping (value) problem for reliabilism","Gettier case"],"avoid":["reliable but unjustified belief"],"exact":false}],"doi":"10.1007/978-94-009-9493-5_1"},{"id":"dretske1971-conclusive-reasons","title":"Conclusive Reasons","authors":["Fred Dretske"],"year":"1971","area":"epistemology","topic":"analysis of knowledge","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"if S knows that P, and he knows this on the basis (simply) of R, then R would not be the case unless P were the case.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Anything short of conclusive reasons, though it may provide one with justified true beliefs, fails to give the kind of support requisite to knowledge.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Knowledge that P on basis R requires: (A) ¬◇(R ∧ ¬P) in the actual circumstances; (B) S believes P without doubt on the basis of R; (C) S knows R, or R is S's experience. Properly qualified, these suffice.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Knowledge requires evidence that, in the circumstances actually prevailing, objectively excludes the possibility of error; but the knower need not know that it does, so sceptics who show we cannot certify our reasons do not show we lack knowledge.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"conclusive reason","aliases":["conclusive reasons"],"definition":"Let us call R a conclusive reason for P if and only if R would not be the case unless P were the case.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"particularized impossibility","aliases":["particularized impossibilities","particularized"],"definition":"R's being unable to obtain without P given the circumstances actually prevailing on the occasion (those logically and causally independent of P held fixed), though not in general: it is compatible with R's obtaining without P in other circumstances, as with a broken thermometer.","exact":false,"avoid":["dretske1971-conclusive-reasons/t1"]}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"S believes that a nearby mountain, M, erupted many years ago, on the basis of the solidified lava spread through the surrounding countryside, and M's eruption did produce that lava. But not far from M is another mountain, N, and the geology was such that something had to give: had M not erupted, N would have, leaving the lava, in all respects relevant to S's belief, exactly as it is. S does not know that M erupted.","phenomenon":"counterexample to the causal theory of knowledge","near":["deviant causal chain","knowledge via a common cause","lottery case"],"avoid":["Gettier case","environmental epistemic luck","fake barn case","barn façade case","barn facade case","insensitive belief","unsafe belief","epistemic luck","veritic epistemic luck","causal preemption"],"exact":false},{"id":"e2","text":"Imagine a blind dog's being 'taken in' by a thief in his master's clothing while the master lies unconscious in the corner. The dog, taken in as he is by the thief, certainly thinks his master is in the room, and he is right","phenomenon":"Gettier case","near":["fake barn case","veridical hallucination","lottery case"],"avoid":["false-lemma Gettier case","veritic epistemic luck","epistemic luck","insensitive belief","unsafe belief","misidentification"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"[If someone says,] 'James would not have come without an invitation', we can deny this without supposing that James would have come without an invitation. For suppose we know that if James had not received an invitation, he would have flipped a coin to decide whether to go or not. In such a case, it is not true that he would not have come without an invitation, but neither is it true that he would have come without an invitation.","phenomenon":"failure of conditional excluded middle","near":["epistemic use of 'might'","backtracking counterfactual","failure of antecedent strengthening"],"avoid":["might-counterfactual","would/might duality","subjunctive (counterfactual) conditional"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"A dog may know that his master is in the room, and I (at least) want to say that he can know this in a straightforward sense without (necessarily) possessing the conceptual understanding which seems to be required to say of him that he believes he knows this, believes that he has good reasons, or believes anything about his epistemic relation to the fact that his master is in the room.","phenomenon":"failure of the KK principle","near":["knowledge without belief","closure failure","Moore's paradox"],"avoid":["anti-luminosity","luminosity failure","animal knowledge","reliable but unjustified belief","epistemic externalism","knowing without knowing that one knows","reliable differential responsiveness without conceptual understanding"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1080/00048407112341001"},{"id":"bonjour1980-externalist-theories-empirical","title":"Externalist Theories of Empirical Knowledge","authors":["Laurence BonJour"],"year":"1980","area":"epistemology","topic":"justification & evidence","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the rationality or justifiability of Norman's [clairvoyant] belief should be judged from Norman's own perspective, rather than from one that is unavailable to him.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"external or objective reliability is not enough to offset subjective irrationality.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Reliability alone cannot make a basic belief justified: someone who accepts a reliably produced belief with no reason to think it reliable, like a clairvoyant lacking any evidence of his power, believes irresponsibly by his own lights, hence unjustifiedly.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Externalist foundationalism is ad hoc: the regress arises because justification requires that the believer himself can deploy a justifying argument, and externalism simply waives that access requirement for basic beliefs without explaining why.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"epistemic regress problem","aliases":["regress problem","regress"],"definition":"how to avoid an infinite and presumably vicious regress of justification in one's account of the justification of empirical beliefs.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"basic belief","aliases":["basic beliefs","basic"],"definition":"empirical beliefs (a) that are genuinely justified, but (b) whose justification is not inferentially dependent on that of any further empirical belief(s) … These non-inferentially justified beliefs, or basic beliefs as I shall call them, are claimed to provide the foundation upon which the edifice of empirical knowledge rests.","exact":true,"avoid":["pryor2000-skeptic-dogmatist/t2"]},{"id":"t3","term":"externalism","aliases":["externalist","external"],"definition":"though there must in a sense be a reason why a basic belief is likely to be true, the person for whom such a belief is basic need not have any cognitive grasp of this reason. … all these matters may be entirely external to the person's subjective conception of the situation.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"epistemic justification","aliases":["justification","epistemic"],"definition":"the distinguishing characteristic of this particular species of justification is, I submit, its internal relationship to the cognitive goal of truth … the concept of epistemic justification is fundamentally a normative concept. It has to do with what one has a duty or obligation to do, from an epistemic or intellectual standpoint.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Norman, under certain conditions that usually obtain, is a completely reliable clairvoyant … He possesses no evidence or reasons of any kind for or against the general possibility of such a cognitive power, or for or against the thesis that he possesses it. One day Norman comes to believe that the President is in New York City, though he has no evidence either for or against this belief. In fact the belief is true and results from his clairvoyant power …","phenomenon":"reliable but unjustified belief","aliases":["reliable"],"near":["new evil demon problem","Gettier case","undercutting (undermining) defeater"],"avoid":["clairvoyance case","counterexample to the causal theory of knowledge"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"One day [Samantha] comes to believe, for no apparent reason, that the President is in New York City. She maintains this belief, appealing to her alleged clairvoyant power, even though she is at the same time aware of a massive amount of apparently cogent evidence … indicating that the President is at that time in Washington, D.C. Now the President is in fact in New York City … Moreover, Samantha does in fact have completely reliable clairvoyant power","phenomenon":"rebutting defeater","near":["undercutting (undermining) defeater","Gettier case","new evil demon problem"],"avoid":["reliable but unjustified belief","misleading evidence","clairvoyance case","dogmatism paradox","counterexample to the causal theory of knowledge"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Maud believes herself to have the power of clairvoyance, though she has no reasons for this belief. She maintains her belief despite being inundated by her embarrassed friends and relatives with massive quantities of apparently cogent scientific evidence that no such power is possible. One day Maud comes to believe, for no apparent reason, that the President is in New York City … Now in fact the President is in New York City, and Maud does … have completely reliable clairvoyant power.","phenomenon":"undercutting (undermining) defeater","near":["rebutting defeater","epistemic bootstrapping","Gettier case"],"avoid":["reliable but unjustified belief","misleading evidence","higher-order evidence","clairvoyance case","defeat by misleading higher-order evidence","dogmatism paradox","counterexample to the causal theory of knowledge"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Agatha … believes herself to be perceiving a cup on the desk. She also knows … that she is one of a group of 100 people who have been selected for a philosophical experiment by a Cartesian evil demon. … though 99 of the people will be perceiving a cup in the normal way, the last one will be caused by the demon to have a complete hallucination … she does not have any further information as to whether she is the one who is hallucinating, though as it happens she is not.","phenomenon":"lottery case","near":["preface paradox","veridical hallucination","new evil demon problem"],"avoid":["lottery paradox","environmental epistemic luck","fake barn case","barn façade case","barn facade case","Gettier case","radical skeptical hypothesis","reliable but unjustified belief","undercutting (undermining) defeater","higher-order evidence","closure-based skeptical argument","defeat by misleading higher-order evidence","insensitive belief","counterexample to the causal theory of knowledge"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1111/j.1475-4975.1980.tb00396.x"},{"id":"moore1939-proof-external-world","title":"Proof of an External World","authors":["G. E. Moore"],"year":"1939","area":"epistemology","topic":"skepticism & contextualism","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I can prove now … that two human hands exist. How? By holding up my two hands, and saying … 'Here is one hand' … 'and here is another'.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I can know things, which I cannot prove; and among things which I certainly did know … were the premisses of my two proofs [of things outside us].","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"One can rigorously prove that things outside us exist by exhibiting one's hands: the premiss 'here is one hand, and here is another' is known, differs from the conclusion that two hands exist, and entails it.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Commonsense proofs of external things remain conclusive even though their premisses cannot themselves be proved (that would require, among other things, proving one is not dreaming), because one can know what one cannot prove.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"to be met with in space","aliases":["met with in space","things to be met with in space"],"definition":"It is natural so to understand the phrase 'to be met with in space', that to say of anything which a man perceived that it was to be met with in space is to say that it might have been perceived by others as well as by the man in question.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"external to our minds","aliases":["external to my mind","external to our mind"],"definition":"to say of anything that it is external to our minds, will mean … that from a proposition to the effect that it existed at a specified time, it in no case follows that any of us were having experiences at the time in question.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"rigorous proof","aliases":["proof","prove","rigorous"],"definition":"it would not have been a proof unless three conditions were satisfied; namely (1) unless the premiss … was different from the conclusion I adduced it to prove; (2) unless the premiss which I adduced was something which I knew to be the case … and (3) unless the conclusion did really follow from the premiss.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"presented in space","aliases":["presented"],"definition":"Perceived at a particular place (as an after-image seen on a white background, each of a pair of double images, or a pain felt in a tooth is), even when no one else could perceive that very thing; only what is actually perceived qualifies.","exact":false}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"I can say: 'I held up two hands above this desk not very long ago; therefore two hands existed not very long ago; therefore at least two external objects have existed at some time in the past, QED'.","phenomenon":"Moorean proof","near":["transcendental argument","Cartesian dream argument","argument from illusion"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"I took the trouble to cut out of a piece of white paper a four-pointed star, to place it on a black ground, to 'look steadfastly' at it, and then to turn my eyes to a white sheet of paper: and I did find that I saw a grey patch for some little time","phenomenon":"negative after-image","aliases":["after-image","after-images","after-sensation"],"near":["positive after-image","double vision","phantom limb pain"],"exact":true}],"doi":null}]};
